        StaticBook("Bill of Sale", "Smith & Son", "      Smith & Sons\n    Luxury Tailors\n------------------- =     Bill of Sale        = 1/2 Cloth, plain\n= 1/2 Cloth, blue\n= Pauldron x2\n= Rerebrace x2\n= Vambrace, metal x2\n= Gauntlet x2\n= Leather cowl x1\n-------------------     Total: M2,034.2", ""),
        StaticBook("Bio Theories", "Leyu", "Contrary to the magic theory of life being so diverse due to magic, I have observed something quite different. The following will be my notes and observation on reality.\nThe offspring seem to have traits dirived from their parents. The calf of a piebald doe and a solid coat stag had approximately half the spots its mother had. The following is a sketch:\n*a sketch of a spotted doe, a solid color stag, and half of each calf\nI thought that maybe that if one was to build on one specific trait, it would become more pronounced. I took 50 mice from the wilderness, each of more or less the same physical appearance, and caged them in my room. I selected the most approachable of the\nmice, being the ones that did not instantly recoil from my presense. I took those mice and bred them together. After a few weeks, I selected the most approachable of the babies. These too, I bred. I continued this for five generations. \nGeneration 1: These mice did not instantly recoil when I approached them. They also accepted food I gave them. I deemed these mice, out of the original batch, to be the more approchable of the batch.\nGeneration 2: The more approachable mice out of this generation were as follows: They were less hesitant to accept food from me, they did not scurry away from my hand, and they were unphased by my presence. \nGenereation 3:\nThese mice were astoundingly strange. They seemed to be joyous when I arrived to check on them, and they did not recoil at all. They did not hesitate to accept my food. \nGeneration 4:\nThese mice were very different. They were extremely overjoyed to see me, and they were like dogs. They snuggled agaisnt my finger, and kept to me for warmth. When I touched them, they were in ecstacy. These were tame.\nGeneration 5:\nThese mice are as tame as any household pet! They scamper towards me and attempt to cuddle with my hand. They seek my presence and are saddened by my absense. I believe I have successfully produced a pet, and tamed mice.\nI decided to test if the obverse was true. Out of the original batch I captured from the forest, I bred the most unruly ones. The results are as follows.\nGeneration 1:\nThese mice were the unruly ones I selected. They ran away from my finger faster than the rest, they did not sniff the food I offered, and they seemed a bit afraid.\nGeneration 2:\nThese mice were even scarder. They took less time to run away from my finger, they did not even go near the food I offered, and they occasionally got defensive.\nGeneration 3:\nThese mice were even more defensive. When I put my finger near, they hissed and ran away. They ran away from the offered food and did not even attempt to reconcile with me.\nGeneration 4: \nThese mice were even more ferocios. When they saw me, they got defensive, and attempted to bite my finger several times. They snarled at the food I presented.\nGeneration 5:\nThese mice were as aggresive as a mother bear! They actively attacked my finger when I put it in there, and I have the scars to prove it. They hissed and snarled at the food, and they were alerted by my presence and were violent.\nThe results of the Mice experiment were very fruitful! It proved that if one were to exploit the traits and capitilize on particular traits, in this case, tameness and aggression, one could change the entire race of animal! We have proven that traits are \ndependant on parental traits and that they can be artificially manipulated and be used to make new races of animals, with different behaviors and physical descriptions. \nI have developed an extraordinary theory! The results of the mice experiment proved that traits can be artificially manipulated and selected, but could nature do this as well!? It may go against everything that we are taught, but perhaps the diversity of \nlife in the wild is a product of nature, not magic! Could nature dictate that the creatures with the better traits more suited to its environment survive to pass their traits to their kin, thus making that trait prominent in the race. Eventually, this\nwould accumulate and become a new race! This could explain the diversity of life far better than magic ever could! I dub this theory Draug's Theory of Natural Manipulation of Traits.\nDraug's Theory of Natural Manipulation:\nMany people would object to the past statement, announcing it as heresy and idiocy, but there is enough proof to atleast make an argument for it. I realize that I have not really explained the Theory of Natural Man-\n-ipulation. Basically, nature plays the role I play. The beasts with the desirable traits, ones better suited to the land they live in, will be able to reproduce, thus passing the trait that allowed them to survive to their kin. Nature weeds out the unde-\n-sirable traits through illness, predation, food accessability, and famine. The beasts that have the better traits will pass the trait to the kin, and so on. Eventually this will manifest into new beasts, with distinctly different traits than its\nancestors. Overtime, these beasts become specialized to their environment, and have developed specific traits for their lands. For example, creatures of the desert have ways to conserve fluids and keep cool, like larger extremities and needing less water\nto survive. I bet that these started out as, a beast needed slightly less water to survive, and it survived, with its water consuming peers dying of thirst. Eventually the trait would become prominent. We have not yet discovered how these changes come-\nabout, but we know they exist. Given enough time, a beast will become specially accustomed to its land, eventually thriving in its habitat. Life will prevail and overcome obstacles through Natural Manipulation. \nThe Theory of Dominance:\nWith each generation traits are passed down from parent to offspring. It seems that some traits mask other traits. The red petals of flowers are more dominant than white. Yet in the next generation,\nthe plants were red and white. It seems the trait was not destroyed, yet hidden away. Such stands the theory of specific traits being dominant over other traits.\n\n-Xanthur Altraza\nPlant Luminescense Theory:\n\nAfter planting flower cultures and observing their growth, I noticed a strange phenomenon. When I first planted the flowers, some were planted, accidentally, on a hump in the dirt, making them face more horizontally then\nverically, like the others. However, after three more days of observations I noticed that these plants has started to tilt. They eventually, throughout another week of my spectating them, aligned themselves with the original vertical plants, making now\nboth groups face vertically, albeit one group's bases are planted horizontally. I concluded from this discovery that plants require something from the Sun, (Through Artificial Manipulation they achieved this characteristic/ability, since a plant which is\nable to tilt itself towards its source of nourishment, the sun as I concluded, would be more efficient than one which could not, making it more likely to thrive and prosper, thus through nature the plant has been manipulated so as now the whole species\nshares this ability), This something required by the plants could either be the sun's warmth or the light it emits. So as to test this, I created a new experiment. I planted a few flowers under the shadow of a tree, thus making them not at all exposed to\nsun's light.  I planted a few more flowers next to the trees shadow, where it was exposed to light. After two weeks, the leaves of the plants, under the tree, began to shrivel and yellow. After about another week, the plants completely died. On the other\nhand, the plants exposed to light were green and healthy, prospering. After several retrials of this test, I can safely say that plants in fact, do require light to survive, as well, plants have developed a method of adjusting themselves so as to be\nexposed to light, this through Artificial Manipulation.\n\n- Sil Iyat\nWhales\nThis may be something different from the rest in this journal, but I believe it is necessary. I will observe the habits of whalers. I can see what can drive a beast to threatening status.", ""),
        StaticBook("Birth & Fall V.1", "Tyrell Blavier", "The Birth and Fall of\n    -=;Tahu'nor;=-\n\nAs to the account of \nthe High Overseer of the Syndicate and last owner of Tahu'nor before it's dissolution and abandonment, Tyrell Blavier.\n\n\n\n         Volume 1\n-Sections\n\n-Foreword (Pg. 4-5)\n-On Tahu'nor (Pg. 6-10)\n-On Salvus (Pg. 11-15)\n-On The Syndicate (Pg. 16-18)\n-Summary (Pg. 19)\n-Addition\n\nI ask that you, dear reader, from King to farmer alike, read this story with no bias what so ever. I write this with no bias in hand so I expect the same from your reading. I cared for both Tahu'nor and Salvus, but the truth favors\nSalvus.\n-Foreward\n\nWhy would I speak of Tahu'nor, one may ask. Well by all facts I, Tyrell Blavier, am probably the most knowledgeable non-Tahu'norian who knows about Tahu'nor! Since I did my best to keep the Syndicate as an entirely neutral-\n-Foreword (cont.)\n\n-entity in the whole incident, I was able to see the story unfold with an almost omninicient and removed view on both Salvus and Tahu'nor.\n\nThis is where my critique of Tahu'nor starts.\n-On Tahu'nor\n\nTahu'nor was born from a lack of understanding, blind ambition and temper tantrums. Oft or not you can see the nation wished to make itself sound like the helpless little child being pushed by big bad Salvus.\n-On Tahu'nor (cont.)\n\nLet me tell you it here. This is not true. Tahu'nor used pity play at a max while one could say that this whole situation was largely their fault, if not all.\n\nNo less than a few months after it's-\n-On Tahu'nor (cont.)\n\n-declared \"freedom\" did they commit acts of agression and war to innocents and it's own allies! Tahu'nor's entire reason for being was to spoil the south, Ante'ame told me such herself. What kind of \"farmer\" is she?\n-On Tahu'nor (cont.)\n\nIf these people were humble farmers, they would of just left Salvus, made their own city and forget about us. Nay, she wanted power and the love of a man named Arzota Shadeleaf. Ante'ame was insane and Tahu'nor, sadly,-\n-On Tahu'nor (cont.)\n\n-was but a simple tool in all of this.\n\nA nation founded like this was doomed to failure, as it has been proven from the events show in it's history. This book uphors truth and a neutral view.\n-On Salvus\n\nNow I couldn't really say I was neutral if I didn't speak of how Salvus handled this situation either. Diplomatics was poor at the start, very poor. People were making deals with them left and right, voiding one and then-\n\n-On Salvus (cont.)\n\n-making another deal that too would be voided. A part of this was due to the love between Ante'ame and Arzota. Arzota claimed she listned to him only (albiet not true, as she did listen to me) and made his own deals. He had no-\n-On Salvus (cont.)\n\n-power to do so and gave the entire valley to the company of Windshear farms (Windshear was a proto-Tahu'nor.)\n\nAn entire valley? Not to mention the weird acts of aggression and late moves in the game of-\n-On Salvus (cont.)\n\n-diplomacy. Salvus oft or not had to use violence when Windshear didn't obey, as many got tired of them being so unreasonable and easly agitated when spoken to. I say that some violence could of been spared.\n-On Salvus (cont.)\n\nWhat should of happened is that I and Arzota primarily should of spoken to Windshear. We should of collaborated as mediators and worked through this with Salvus and the \"farmers\". Yet this didn't happen. All of it was disorganized.\n-On The Syndicate\n\nA self critique is required here as much as a critique on Tahu'nor and Salvus. I should of done more to provide a buffer between the States. The Syndicate could of done better to protects it's assets and It's a situation i've-\n-On The Syndicate (cont.)\n\n-come to study, as I need to learn for future application of my skills in diplomacy. Not only that but I nearly made the relation between Ante'ame and I non-platonic. Love and other feelings were a great-\n-On The Syndicate (cont.)\n\n-mover in this whole incident, so much so that even I was caught in it all. Perhaps maybe I shouldn't of even got involved and let the Inner Chamber do it's own plan. The Syndicate was but a budding entity at that time anyways.\n-Summary\n\nThis Volume was ment to express my views. The next Volume is purely historical documentation. Both Volumes go hand in hand and one shouldn't be skipped over.\n\n-Tyrell Blavier", ""),
        StaticBook("Birthday Card", "Ramza Mantisuku", "I wish you a happy birthday little one I hope you live londer than most. We are all here for you I really hope you enjoy another day and many more days  to come. If you ever need someone to talk to I'll be here.. -Ramza  Mantisuku ", ""),
        StaticBook("Black Petal Wine", "Izier Vincrute", "Black Petal Wine\n\nThis is a fine black rose petal wine. The Rose Petals where fermented with grape juice and sugar. The sweet taste of the grapes blend well with the sweetness of the petals.\nThe wine was brewed and bottled in Aegis. Limited uses to 24 bottles.", ""),
        StaticBook("Black Rose", "Telperian Faroar", ">---------------------<\nAtrim:Is a blonde man with green eyes. “Hey Louise, do you like like me?” He like likes me who tried to help me out with my mind. [!] A sketch of Atrim is here with a heart next to it. [!]\n>---------------------<\n\nCareful: William.\n>---------------------<\nTuvya: Is the Emperor of the lands of Orenia, can help me settle. Met him near the Cloud Temple.\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nJakir: Blue eyes, handlebar moustache, light brunette, man. Helped with the fight in the Standing Inn. [!] A sketch of Jakirs’ face is here. [!] Unloyal to a lover?\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nArtemis Carlyle: A man with darker brunette hair and dark blue eyes. Met him at the Standing Inn. Wants to help me find my admirer.\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nDaren Bonehill: Count of Nerezza. Is my boss. I work at his castle. From the start of the Stone Road, head towards Northern Wilderness. Then, take a left, then a right. [!] A sketch of Daren is here. [!]\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nTeleia Index: Supplied me 2 notebooks as I had lost mine. [!] A sketch of Teleia is here. [!]\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nKatari Stirling: Conversed with me in the Standing Inn. [!] A sketch of Katari is here. [!]\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nGilraen: [!] An image is here of her. [!] She helped me with monsters and hunger one night.\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nThor Faolain: Owner of Nerezza. Is also my boss. I work the farm for him. [!] Thors’ face is sketched here. [!]\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nJack: I gifted him 3 pancakes and he placed a kiss on the back of my hand. I think he fancies me. [!] A quick sketch of his face is here. [!]\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nCptn Harrison Shard: Is a privateer Captain that has a fancying of me. [!] A sketch of his head & Shoulders. [!]\n>---------------------<\n\n>---------------------<\nRagnar Faolain: 3rd leader of the Faolain along with Daren and Thor.\n>---------------------<\n\nX--------------------X\nI saw a dwarf get murdered. An accused thief took an axe to his head. I am still bad with blood. Rest In Peace. I then played DnD with Ulfrik Faolain. It was fun. \nX--------------------X\nIf he fixes my mind, I must play DnD with him\nX--------------------X\nToday, I received a fancy, brass-buttoned green dress, from “A secret admirer.” I think Ulfrik sent it but he was being cryptic about the answer.\nX--------------------X", ""),
        StaticBook("Blackfoot Plague", "Lelien", "\n\n\n\n \n Blackfoot Plague\n\n    by Lyuin Avern\n22nd of Grand Havest, 1461\nSymptoms:\n- Coughing\n- Blurred vision\n- Inability to clot blood\n- Internal acid burns\n- Haemorrahage of lungs\n\nThis has been dubbed 'Blackfoot plague'.\nThe Mali of Than’siol seek a cure, these have come in several forms.\nThe latest of which are thanhium pillars which attempt to kill plague particles.\nThe masls are somewhat effective, but not plague-proof.\n\n1st of Amber Cold, 1461\nIt has been discovered that strands of the plague can latch on to wheat crops, infected them and making ingestion of the infected bread to cause the plague. Furthermore, it has been adivsed and tried to see if this plague can\nnfect other food-stuffs.\nIn better news, Clerics have discovered they are able to rid the plague from its host. Thus creating an efficient method to remove plague from a host. However many Clerics are not immune themselves and thus choose not to involve\nthemselves in such matters.\n\nThe plague is both transmitted through consumption and through ingestion of air and infected foodstuffs.\n20th of Snow's Maiden, 1461\nA cure has been found! Discovered by the Valah withing the Fringe. This is an odd solution made up of several components including milk, carrot, tippen's root and other materials.\nThe filter-masks have proven useful and\nwith the cure having cleansed many of the people of their ailments, it is safe once more for our people.\n\n‘Blackfoot’ plague cure\n- A single gound carrot\n- A ground carrot of golden complexion\n- A single unit of Tippen’s root.\n- A measure of plague blood extracted from a victim of the plague\n- A single egg (remove the shell)\n- A single bucket of milk (should be fresh)\n- Oddly, a feather plucked from a chicken.\n- Several units of fertilizer", ""),
        StaticBook("Blacksmithing V1", "Lark Steelwall", "\n      Blacksmithing\n         Volume 1\n\n  \n\n            By\n\n      Lark\n           Steelwall\n   Introduction to\n     Blacksmithing\n\nBlacksmithing is the art of using heat and brute force to change the shape, hardness, sharpness, and occasionally the color of metal to one's will.\nThis art takes many years to master, and cannot be learned\neasily. As with other trades, one start off on the lowest rung of the metaphorical ladder of trade work and climbs their way up through hard work and dedication. One does not simply try to forge without any experience. Thankfully, you have this book.\nChapter 1-\nForging 101: Operating the forge.\n\nAssuming you have found a forge suitable to work with, you will require the following things: Gloves to prevent burns on the hands, an apron to keep sparks off your clothing, and if you\nhave long hair, something to tie back the hair to keep it from catching on fire.\nIf you have these clothing materials, next on your list should be tools for the forge, a basic set of tools for the beginner blacksmith should consist of a hammer, a \npair of tongs, a poker to stoke the fire, a chisel to clean up the metal and add engravings and such,\nand billows to get the heat of the forge up. When you have these basic tools you are ready to begin smithing. \nOperating the forge is not quite hard, a fuel source such as wood or coal will work, and the heat can be further brought up through the use of billows or poker, arousing the wood or coal to keep circulation of heat going.\nOnce the heat has reached the desired temperature you can finally begin smithing. Placing an ingot into the furnace with the tongs will allow the ingot to heat up. When the metal has become a bright yellow or red it is malleable and able to be forged into\nthe desired shape or object. When you have hammered out your metal object you should dip it in a water source to quickly cool it off. It is also advised to reheat the metal to strengthen it. This process is known as tempering. \nTo improve the metal even more, one should sharpen it on a grindstone. \n\nIf you wish to learn more about Blacksmithing, look for Volume 2 of this many volumed series.\n\n-Lark", ""),
        StaticBook("Blah Dictionary", "Shreck", "Diz buuk tranzlatez wurds in blah tew kommon, writtun bi Shreck.\n\n-Ug ¦ Hello, Hi, Hail\n-Throm'ka ¦ Formal greeting\n-Yub ¦ Yes\n-Nub ¦ No\n-Gug'ye ¦ Goodbye\n-Lat ¦ You\n-Mi ¦ Me, I\n-Wi ¦ We, Us\n-Hi ¦ He\n-Rulg ¦ Thanks\n-Blah ¦ Talk, Speak, Orchish Language\n-Blah'er ¦ Mouth\n-Peepers ¦ Eyes\n-Skah'er ¦ Male Reproductive Organ\n-Gruk ¦ Think, Know, Understand\n-Dabu ¦ Yes, My Pleasure\n-Bubhosh ¦ Great, Big\n-Nubhosh ¦ Bad, Small\n-Snaga ¦ Slave\n-Buub ¦ Pig\n-Pushdug ¦ Stinky\n-Glob ¦ Fool, Shit, Poop\n-Klomp ¦ Fight, Attack\n-Flat ¦ Dead, Kill\n-Blarg ¦ Home\n-Brudda ¦ War Uzg Orc\n-Stout ¦ Dwarf\n-Twiggeh, Treehugger ¦ elf\n-Darkie ¦ Farfolk, Dark elf\n-Squeals, Snaaks ¦ Halflings\n-Quickspawn, Breeder, Shara, Pinkii ¦ Human\n-Nuutshara ¦ Ascended\n-Nubded ¦ Undead\n-Howlur ¦ Dog, Wolf\n-Skah ¦ Fuck\n-Agh ¦ And\n-Buurz ¦ Dark\n-Mojo ¦ Magic\n-Bi ¦ By\n-Krimp ¦ Stun, Capture\n-Uzg ¦ World\n-Goi ¦ City\n-Lusk ¦ Axe\n-O'lig ¦ Bow\n-Ligz ¦ Arrow\n-Zult ¦ Sword\n-Stik ¦ Staff\n-Steemiez ¦ Redstone \n   Numburz!\n\nAsh ¦ One\nDub ¦ Two\nGakh ¦ Three\nFuth ¦ Four\nH' ¦ Five\nH'ash ¦ Six\nAshety ¦ Ten\nAshty ¦ Eleven\nDubty ¦ Twelve\nDubty ¦ Twenty", ""),
