beautiful element in the creation of an oil to coat a weapon, or to coat and strengthen armor. This is not the same as enchanting armor or weaponry, mind you, for doing that is often permanent and requires magic. Through oils, one may\nreap similar benefits of enchantment but instead it is only temporary and anyone with basic sense can apply them. The purest base, and often representation of water, is Aqua Vitae or “Water of Life,” which is best made by distilling wine or other liquors.\nAir\n===\nThe Nimble, the Traveller, the All-Seeing.\n\nOur final element, the element of Air, is not the least by any stretch of imagination but is often the hardest one to put into representation and use in a potion. Now this element is often associated with agility, quickness, travel, feet, perception, and\neven flight, it can be quite difficult to embody the element of Air into a physical item due to our basic fault of lacking understanding of what we cannot see. But with reason and the opening of one’s eyes, the ways in which to represent Air are quite \nobvious in our everyday world.\nItems that can be used to represent air, and subsequently put through your tools to produce a potion, are rather easy to find during your average day but of course, as with all the elements, purer ingredients are often quite\nrarer. One representation that you may have already thought of is a mere feather, which is often the most common representation of Air. This, of course, is not the purest representation of the element but it will often due for your more basic potions\ncentered around this element. Another, more outlandish representation, are rabbits’ feet. Rabbits are often seen as quick and agile critters, and as Air takes on the aspect of agility and quickness, it becomes quite obvious how the feet of a rabbit and\nAir intermingle. With that said, it becomes rather easy to find out representations of Air through simply looking and observing the world around you. But do not fret if you are still hard-pressed to find these symbols, the next chapter will explain \nherbs that will best aid you in the process of finding symbols for all the elements.\nThe Tales of the Traveling Alchemist, Vol I-2\n\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Elemental Thiik", "Anonymous", "         Thiik  \n\n        Terra\n\nTerra is one of the most commonly used of the Thiik within the Dialect of the Elements. Representing the firm, unmoving Land beneath our feet some of the longest lasting Thiik are Terra Runes.\nBenefits\n\nThe positives of Terra runes are many as they can be used to do anything from fix an item to the ground or add reinforcement for support of Carved structures. Many of our halls boast these Thiik along the walls.\nDrawbacks\n\nTerra Thiik have issues due to the fact that Stone is naturally abrasive to suggestion. Due to this it's impossible to really summon stone or move it signifigantly or in large portions with the Terra symbol. It can be used to modify\nexisting Stone and Land near it, however.\nCreation\n\n*Outlined here is a symbol painstakingly drawn in Ink. It seems to be somewhat circular in shape but rough on the outline.*\n           Igni\n\nThe rune of Fire this is perhaps one of the most useful of the base runes. Fire, being free willed, is open to suggestion. Be warned however! Fire always needs a source, and if you fail to provide... it will draw\nfrom you! Caution, young smith!\nBenefits\n\nIgni is commonly used around forges and for weapons due to it's useful nature. Some of the greatest forges of the Great Hall bear Ograhad's own Igni and are thus able to forge metals that we could not previously\nreach a melting point of.\nDrawbacks\n\nFire is hard to control by nature and as such demonstrates it when you attempt to force it into particular functions. It will fight the entire time and may even cause signifigant damage if you attempt to force it to hold back too much\nAs stated before, be wary that you do not overload the Heart rune or it will begin to draw it's toll from you, the Original creator of the Ruhn. This is a swift death.\nCreation\n\n*An upsidedown triangle is drawn here, the sides seem rough and somewhat uneven but it was obviously drawn very precisely.*\n         Wulgim\n\nThe Ruhn of Wulgim is one of the more subtle of the Elemental Dialect. Wulgim at it's heart isn't something that causes Khrum to shake in their boots. It has it's uses and I have seen many clever Thinkadre using this in\nKadrel to cool Stailininn quicker, as well as power wheels to stoke a furnace effortlessly.\nBenefits\n\nWuglim has many smaller uses over the obvious of Igni or Kaas but is no less powerful than either. Often used in ports or in Kadrel it's become something of a symbol for clever thinking amonst Thinkadre.\nDrawbacks\n\nMuch less than the previous, Wuglim does things in a subtle way and therefore doesn't draw too much energy when it does so or have the potential for sudden death. The only drawback to this Ruhn is while it can effect bodies of\nWuglim it has a certain limit - the more desired to be effected will draw more Manok than Amoruk can handle and will do the same as Igni - Draw Manok from your Anym.\nCreation\n\n*Carefully drawn here is something of a 'I' but the middle seems to be broken so the two halves aren't bridged.*\n          Kaas\n\nKaas is another subtle of the Elemental Ruhn but can be used in big ways... Although be cautious young Thinkadre, Kaas does not take so kindly to being forced to tasks. Much like Karaad in manners Kaas is a bit more violent\nin it's escapades for freedom. Some more foolhardy Dwedmar have found themselves suddenly caught in a whirlwind summoned before them and it has been the death of many good Thin'kadre.\n\n\nBenefits\n\nKaas is a strange Ruhn, it can manipulate Kaas around it or even summon more... but it comes at a heavy risk. Kaas manipulated must be consistantly manipulated - If you were to break a Kaas rune all the Kaas\nit has touched will lash out violently and instantly. Do not leave these Ruhn active for long. However, Kaas can be used for long-distance travelling and barriers - it is a potent tool for combat.\nDrawbacks\n\nAs stated, Kaas is violent when released and summoned Kaas is hard if not impossible to control. Sudden winds inside of caverns can be deadly... or Kaas can receed entirely, leaving a foolhardy Dwed dead in \nthe most painful of ways. Use caution with this Ruhn young Kadre'ruhn.\nCreation\n\n*A diagonal U is written here, with a slight extention on the bottom of the arch.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Elements of Mind", "Aris", " Elements of Mind\n           /\\\n         /\\ /\\\n         ><  ><\n         \\/ \\/\n           \\/\n     /\\         /\\\n     \\/         \\/\n\nThoughts on the mind and its most basic concepts.\n             Aris, 1461.\nEvery living being has a soul and a body, but what makes them unique, is their Mind. Mind itself is what defines you, and your actions. However, mind is different, it can be viewed in many ways, but it isn't as simply explained the body or a soul. But\nA simplistic way to explain would be this;\n If a being was a ship\n\n Your body would be the hull,\n\n Your soul would be the sails,\n\nBut the ship would never sail without its Captain, the Mind.\nAn example simple as that makes everything easier to understand, yet the Mind is more than that, a Mind can be described and divided in different ways. You can say a mind is inferior or superior, but you cannot say a mind is right or wrong. Continuing\nwith the same example from before, Mind, the Captain, can fit in three categories;\n  The Captain can be..\nCurious, jumping into adventure in search of answers.\nIntelligent, a person of logic and reason.\nKnowing, from experience and books\n\nCuriosity, Intelligence and Knowledge are the key points of the Mind, but ultimately, there is  Wisdom. Now, let me break down the four.\n\nCuriosity, the most innocent of the elements of mind for it is the base of all knowledge. A child is curious, \nbecause it is important and natural for them to gain experience from the world they have stepped their foot in. Curiosity is the desire, the craving to gain information or learn something. Curiosity is important to me, because it is what activates me, \nand more importantly, motivates me. It's very\nimportant for me to be\nopen to try new things\nand find out more, as there is an endless\namount of information.\n\nKnowledge is, in my opinion, the outcome of curiosity. It the term used for skills and\ninfromation gained via experience and / or education. Knowledge is something many seek, without truly knowing what they are looking for; knowledge isn't only facts laid out by letters in a book, but the prize of feeding one's curiosity for they can\nbecome truly aware of how things are. Knowledge covers familiarity, awareness and understanding of\nsomeone and something\nbut simply put, it doesn't mean you grasp the concept.\nOne can say they know\nor understand evocation, yet never be able to actually\nevoke a single droplet of water, a lone spark, or a tiny huff of wind.\nKnowledge is something\nthe mali'aheral, the High Elven, hold very dear. It could even be\ncalled the national\ntreasure of the race.\n \"Kae maehr evar\"\nKnowledge protects me\nCommon-\nphrase from the High Elven, is something that also fits me. Knowledge is what I seek, and something I already hold in some topics. It is also something that has saved me many times in seemingly helpless situations. For me, it is important to know the \nright people, and know\nthe right things. Knowledge is also the very reason I originally picked up\nthe graceful art of\nMental Magic, because for me, knowledge is a seemingly endless goal\nas new information keeps surfacing, and\nmental magic is inevitably \nthe door and also the key to new knowledge.\nBefore I started learning, it was a door\nthat remained locked for me, but now the door is opening to me\nagain, slowly, bit by bit.\n\nIntelligence, the ability to acquire and apply new information and knowledge.\nAn intellectual mind adds and overwrites\nold knowledge. You can not learn to become intelligent, for you are someone who can grasp concepts, or you will never be such.\nWhat makes intelligence different from knowledge, is that you\ncan be born\nintelligent, but you cannot be born knowledgeable. An intelligent person has great mental capacity for judgement, reasoning and logical thinking. A person that knows alot doesn't necessarily have all this, sadly, superior minds are often thought to be\nsly, only becasue they are vastly more intelligent than the\npeople around them.\nTo me, intelligence isn't something you can train, but something you do or do not have,\nshould it be the latter,\nI have trouble taking\nsuch people seriously.\n\nUltimately, \nWisdom is the prize of attaining the most of these three key elements of the mind.\nBecoming wise isn't something you can decide yourself, it is more like a title you\ngain from your surroundings. The Wise\nis intellectual, knowledgeable, yet still curious\nfor more. In my opinion wisdom is what an individual should truly\ntry to reach for, but\nmany are simply unfit.\nWisdom is the quality of having experience, vast amounts of knowledge, good judgement and logical thinking, and that isn't something you can\nattain within a short time frame; it is the\nultimate achievement.\nA wise person to me, is someone to look up to,\nand someone who should receive endless respect, but unfortunately the truly wise are scarce.\nThough, you can be wise without knowing\na lot, as the only true wisdom is in knowing you know nothing.. and one must beware of false knowledge for it is more dangerous than ignorance itself.\nThe path to wisdom is\nendless and eternal.\n------------------- \n-------------------", ""),
        StaticBook("Elements of Mind", "Farley III Stafyr", "Elements of Mind\n\nWritten by Godwein Stafyr\nUpon the writing of the Theorem of Wisdom, my very own mind came to the conclusion that there must be more to it. Every person knows of elements such as fire, earth, water, wind or air and lightning.  But aside from external elements which we\ncan feel and use, there are also other elements, in a completely different range, of which we unknowingly make use and into which we tap, without realizing it.\n\nThe Elements of Mind.\nThe mind is a strange fabric, giving us the ability to think, to consider, to act, react, to learn, to remember and many things more. But the mind itself, such as the outer world we experience, has its own elements. To underline this, an example will be\ngiven.\n\nFeelings. Feelings, such as love, guilt, hate, happiness and many more. This is one of the elements of the mind, we are able to use it, express ourselves with it, to tap into the power of this element. \nFurther explanation is not necessary, I presume, as everyone has at least one feeling within him.\n\nOther elements do exist. Wisdom is one of them, as I aforementioned. Also knowledge and experience are\nelements of the mind. But there are more!\n\nConcentration, Purity, Peace and Balance, and Common Sense.\n\nHow each of these work, will be explained in further, upcoming books. e\nFor the element of wisdom, I refer myself to my work “Theorem of Wisdom” for further information. The book “Knowledge and Experience – Elaborated” may give additional hints about two further elements.\n-: Primary Edition :-", ""),
        StaticBook("Elephant Inn", "Ser Paul Ryan Jr.", " + The Elephant Inn + -------------------\n\n       Foodstuffs:\n\nBread - 1 mina\nBaked Potatoe - 2 mina Steak - 5 mina\n\nVarious specials - ask \n\n + The Elephant Inn + -------------------           Drinks \n\nAle - 2 Mina\nWine - 3 Mina\nSpecial - Ask\n\n+ The Elephant Inn +\n-------------------         Services \n\nRooms - 10 minas \nMassage - 5 minas \nEntertainment - varies \nSpecials - ask ", ""),
        StaticBook("Elinor's Trial", "Grand Druid Verden", "Travel to the three groves and inquire about the life and personalities of the Druids. Ask about their totem, why it's special to them, and how they serve Nature and the Aspects. Be kind, courteous, and friendly; this is your future family after all.\nThe Malinor circle sits comfortably at the back of the city. Just look for the absurdly large tree. The Wayward circle sits outside Rosewyn Hollow, outside former Salvus. It's very secluded, so you might want to ask for directions.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ellian - 8", "Elwin", "Ellian Sandru was born in Adunia to Annabella and Gavin Douglas, though Annabella was missing for awhile, leaving her with Gavin and her older brother, Alexander. At this time, her name was Ellian Douglas, and his Alexander Douglas. She made many friends \nin Adunia, one named P'ol, who took care of her whenever Gavin couldn't. P'ol was a kind blacksmith who lived in the area at the time. Eventually,  things went very bad for her, but she doesn't remember much from that time. She returned to Adunia a short \ntime after to discover that her brother was seriously injured from whatever had happened, and healing in the clinic, unconscious. Annabella was around more, and decorated the house and such. Everything was calm for awhile, until one day she was walking\nthrough the streets and saw a crowd gathered around someone, and Gavin. She ran over to him, and Annabella showed up, picking her up. Gavin was fighting, and he lost, and was dragged away, Ellian and her mother watching in horror, unable to help.\nEllian and Annabella ran home and tried to escape, and the people pursued her, but with help they escaped through a window. They ran and took refuge in Lenfarthing, away from everything. For awhile, she didn't understand what had happened.\nOne day in Malinor (before it's corruption and evacuation, Old Malinor), she was with a dark elf when an Adunian named Jon spoke to her, and told her what had happened; he'd been taken and made a Dread Knight.\nShe didn't believe it at first, but trusted him and returned to Adunia with him and Annabella, safe. Eventually her brother Alex, who had been missing, returned to Adunia and offered to take her in. He'd been living in Gronkkston with orcs,\nand she accepted. But they were attacked by someone, and Alex was injured, but healed and taken to Gronkkston. Everything went surprisingly well, the orcs being nice to them, and raising them as their own. Then, Alex got hurt again... this time worse.\nHis injury left him unable to move for a long time, paralyzed and likely very traumatized by whatever caused it. Eventually a cleric was found to heal him, but when he was healed, he wasn't the same as before. He didn't talk, and he didn't\nlike to play anymore. Most of the time, when everyone went out and did things in the area, he'd stay alone in the hut, quiet. Ellian got quite lonely and worried about him. One day, she went to Abresi and was told -yet again- that she was silly to be\nliving in Grokkston. She thought it might be a nice place, but when she got there it was loud and scary, and the Krugsmas tree was on fire at the time.\nShe made a friend named Lili, and met someone named Raigeki, who wanted to put out the fire, and the\ntwo girls helped. He said he was part of an Order with lots of Adunians like her, and she went to take a look. The people there knew who she was, and what happened to her father. So, she stayed the night, and after that, kept coming back. The orcs and the\nOrder near Abresi were both nice to her, but she began to stay with Raigeki and Annie in their room, though Annie didn't know at first. Raigeki was the chef of the place, so he'd often bring Ellian cookies and other baked goods. Alex had been busy or \nsomething, so she took him and showed him to the Order. She didn't have the key to Raigeki's room, so the two went to a festival near the Crossing. Alex went to do something, and she went over to the flower shop. There, she ran into\nsomeone she'd never met before- Cymrych Douglas, Alex and Ellian's half brother. It turned out Cymrych had a twin, also their half brother, who is a Dread Knight as well. The three of them went to Gronkkston, then Ellian and Cymrych went to the inn next\nto the Crossing, Alex staying behind in Gronkkston. There, the two of them talked, and Cymrych convinced her to return to Adunia.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lesson V2", "Zahir Darthirii", "Just as in common, pronouns may be used to replace nouns. The pronouns in elven are as follows: \n\nKae - Me \nLae - Him/He \nNae - You \nLye - We - Used to speak of elves, people or the world. \nHae - Her/She \n\nFor example the sentence 'elvulnir van ayla' (The trickster fared well) may be transformed into 'Lae van ayla' (He fared well). \n\nIn addition to this, other good language supplements to anyone learning elven include basic affixes. These affixes may be added to create further detail or form critical grammatical structures in a sentence.\n\nThe dash denotes location of seed word. Affixes and seed words are separated by an apostrophe.\nac- (aca-) - Honorific for accursedness. \nah- (ahe-) - Honorific for reverence. Often used as a prefix for scholars. For example: Ah'Lucion. \nfi- - New or anew.\ncer- (Slang from Ceru) - Honorific for strength. Often used as a prefix for those of high military rank. For example: Cer'Silir.\n-ante (-nte) - Last \n\n-ento (-nto) - Next \n-onn - From or born. \nu- - Or \n\nvul- (cule) - Clever or deceptive. \n-ir - Designates purpose of someone or something. \n-sae - The spreading, development or management. \n-ii - Diminutive form. For example: Malii, meaning little elf.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons 1", "Arinislia", "Elven Lessons\n\nOriginally By:\n\nKalenz Uradir\n\nRescribed after the burning of the Eternal Library by:\n\nArinislia\nIn Elven, grammatical structure is often different to that of common. For one, instead of placing an adjective before a noun, it is placed after said noun. This becomes particularly important for the most basic of greetings.\nKarin’alya-Goodday\nKer’ayla – Goodnight\nVan’ayla –Goodbye\n\nEach places the time of day before the adjectives describing it. Noun adjective are separated by an apostrophe.\nOther important words of the beginner in Elven include race names:\n\nMali-Elf\nUruk- Orc\nValah-Human\nBortu-Dwarf\n\nIn order to form sub-races of Elves, adjectives are taken to add more detail to the word Mali.\n\nWood Elf-Mali’ame\nHigh Elf-Mali’aheral\nDark Elf-Mali’ker\n\nIn order to form sub-races of other cultures, the suffix tali- may be used. This word directly means ‘part of’.\nGoblin-Tali’uruk (Lit: Part Orc)\nHalfing-Tali’valah (Lit: Part Human)\n\nOther basic phrases include exclamations, including:\n\nGood-Ayla\nBrilliant-Sul’leyun (Lit: Beautiful Light)\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons 2", "Arinislia", "Elven Lessons 2\n\nOriginally By:\n\nKalenz Uradir\n\nRescribed after the burning of the Eternal Library by:\n\nArinislia\nJust as in common, pronouns may be used to replace nouns. The pronouns in Elven are as follows:\n\nKae-Me\nNae-You\nLae-He/Him\nHae- She/Her\nLye-We\n(used to speak of Elves, people, or \nworld)\n\nfor example, the sentence 'elvulnir van ayla' (The trickster fared well) may be transformed into 'Lae van ayla' (he fared well).\n\nIn addition to this, other good languages supplements to \nanyone learning Elven include basic affixes. These affixes may be added to create further detail of form critical grammatical structures in a sentence. The dash denotes location of a seed word. Affixes and seed words are seperated by an apostrophe.\nac-(aca-) - Honorific for accursedness\n\nah-(ahe-) Honorific for reverence. Often used as a prefix for scholar.\nEx.: Ah'Lucion\n\nfi- New or anew\ncer- (Slang from Ceru) -Honorific for strength. Often used as a prefix for those of high military rank. Ex. : Cer'Silir\n\n-ante (-nte) - Last\n\n-ento (-nto) - Next\n\n-onn - from or born\n\nu- - Or\n\nvul- (cule) - Clever or deceptive\n\n-ir -purpose\n\n-sae - The spreading, development, or management\n\n-ii - deminuitive form\nAs always, a full list of affixes may be found in the Elven Dictionary.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons 3", "Arinislia", " Elven Lessons 3\n\nOriginally By: \nKalenz Unradir\n\nRescribed after the burning of the Eternal Library by: \n\nArinislia\nTo conjugate verbs in elven, one must note precisely what person is being referred to in the sentence. Then the verb is conjugated: \n\n-e - 1st sing\n\n-a - 2nd sing\n\n-e - 3rd sing\n-ae - 1st pl\n\n-an - 2nd pl\n\n-eyae - 3rd pl\n\nTo create past and future tenses is quite simply in elven. However, it takes specific forms depending on the time of day.\nFor these two tenses, the following four words are used:\n\nKarin'ento- Next sunrise\n\nKarin'ante- Last sunrise\n\nKer'ento- Next sunset\n\n\nKer'ante- Last sunset\n\nIf it is night at the time the elf refers to the past or present, the two forms with the 'Ker' are used. If it is day at the time the elf refers to the past or present, the two forms with 'Karin' are used. These endings are \nsimply added on the end of sentences to designate the tense. Ex. : Kalenz iyathe (Kalenz thinks) becomes Kalenz iyathe ker'ante (Kalenz thought). Assuming the time in which it is spoken is the night.\nIn spoken elven, particularly more casual speech, these endings are often shortened into the slang of karinto, karinte, keno, and kente.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons 4", "Arinislia", "Elven Lessons 4\n\nOriginally By :Kalenz Uradir. Rescribed by: [Ghost] The Eternal Librarien.\n\nRescribed once more after the burning of the Eternal Library by: \n\nArinislia\nIn elven, numbers are formed by the compostition of multiple words. Each word representing the magnitude of the number. To note a number between one to ten, the simple word in elven to use. The express a multiple of ten, an elven number is placed before\na multiple of ten. For example: niut'matelu means 200.\n\n1- cem\n2-niut\n3-hael\n4-vailu\n5-kulin\n6-banih\n7-laier\n8-esun\n9-moiel\n10-telu\n100-matelu\n1000-marmatelu\n\nAnother particularly useful prefix not mentioned before is 'fer'. Fer is used in combination with seed words for form the names of handheld tools.\nSome such examples include those below. However, to those more advanced in elven, others may be thought up. \n\nFer'ame- axe\nFer'thuln- pickaxe\nFer'nor- shovel\nFer'sul -torch\nFer'bilok- hammer\nFer'norsae- hoe", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons 5", "Arinislia", "Elven Lessons 5\n\nOriginally by: Kalenz Uradir. Rescribed by: [Ghost] The Eternal Librarian.\n\nRescribed after the burning of the Eternal Library by:\n\nArinislia\nIn elven. In order to denote affiliation, the suffix -leh is used. It is placed after the object and designates the possession of said object by the subject of the sentence. However, it also has a use when no object in the sentence can be found and means \nin such a case 'spirit'. this also contributes to the common farewell blessing \"kaenan'leh evareh\" which means \"may it protect us\".\n\nIt is also worth learning the 'operative' which is also seen in the sentence above on in\nword 'evareh'. In elven, it serves the purpose of turning a verb into a wish. It is conjugated as below:\n\n-eh -operative sing.\n-erih-operative pl.\n(-arih if the verb ends in -ar)", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons I", "Kalenz Uradir", "\n\n\n\n     New Elven\n      Lessons:\n     Volume 1\n\n   by Kalenz Uradir\nIn Elven grammatical structure is often different to that of Common. For one instead of placing an adjective before a noun it is placed after said noun. This becomes particularly important for the most basic of greetings.\nKarin'ayla - Goodday\n\nKer'ayla - Goodnight\n\nVan'ayla - Goodbye\n\nEach places the time of day before the adjective describing it. Noun and adjective are separated by an apostrophe.\nOther important words of the beginner in Elven include race names:\n\nMali - Elf\n\nUruk - Orc\n\nValah - Human\n\nBortu - Dwarf\nIn order to form sub-races of Elves, adjectives are taken to add more detail to the word Mali.\n\nMali'ame - Wood Elf\n\nMali'aheral - High Elf\n\nMali'ker - Dark Elf\nIn order to form sub-races of other cumtures the suffix tali- may be used. This word most directly means 'part of'.\n\nTali'uruk - Goblin \n(Lit: Part Orc)\n\nTali'valah - Halfling\n(Lit: Part Human)\nOther basic phrases include exclamations including:\n\nAyla - Good\n\nSul'leyun - Brilliant\n§i(Lit: Beautiful Light)", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons II", "Kalenz Uradir", "\n\n\n\n     New Elven\n     Lessons:\n     Volume I\n\n  by Kalenz Uradir\n\nJust as in common, pronouns may be used to replace nouns. The pronouns in Elven are as follows:\n\nKae - Me\nNae - You\nLae - He/Him\nHae - She/Her\nLye - We (used to speak of Elves, people or world)\nFor example the sentence 'elvulnir van ayla' (The trickster fared well) may be transformed into 'Lae van ayla' (He fared well).\n\n\nIn addition to this, other good language supplements to anyone learning Elven include basic affixes. These affixes may be added to create further detail of form critical grammatical structures in a sentence.\nThe dash denotes location of seed word. Affixes and seed words are separated by an apostrophe.\n\nac- (aca-) - Honorific for accursedness\nah- (ahe-) - Honorific for reverence. Often used as a prefix for scholar. Ex.: Ah'Lucion\nfi- - New or anew\ncer- (Slang from Ceru) - Honorific for strength. Often used as a prefix for those of a high military rank. Ex.: Cer'Silir\n-ante (-nte) - Last\n-ento (-nto) - Next\n-onn - From or born\nu- - Or\nvul- (cule) - Clever or deceptive\n-ir - purpose\n-sae - The spreading, develpment or management\n-ii - diminuitive form\n\nAs always, a full list of affixes may be found in the Elven Dictionary.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons IV", "[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "\n\n\n\nNew Elven Lessons:\n     Volume 4\n\n   by Kalenz Uradir\n\nIn elven, numbers are formed by the composition of multiple words. Each word representing the magnitude of the number. To note a number between one to ten the simple word in elven to use. To express a multiple of ten, an elven number is placed before \na multiple of ten.\n\nFor example: niut'matelu means 200.\n\n1 - oem\n2 - niut\n3 - hael\n4 - vailu\n5 - kulin\n6 - banih\n7 - laier\n8 - esun\n9 - moiel\n10 - telu\n100 - matelu\n1000 - marmatelu\n\nAnother particularly useful prefix not mentioned before is 'fer'. Fer is used in combination with seed words to form the names of handheld tools.\nSome such examples include those below. However, to those more advanced in elven others may be thought up. \n\nFer'ame - axe\nFer'thuln - pickaxe\nFer'nor - shovel\nFer'sul - torch\nFer'bilok - hammer\nFer'norsae - hoe", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Lessons V", "[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "\n\n\n\nNew Elven Lessons:\n      Volume 5\n\n   by Kalenz Uradir\n\nIn elven in order to denote affiliation the suffix -leh is used. It is placed after the object and designates the possession of said object by the subject of the sentence. However, it also has a use when no object in the sentence can be found and\nmeans in such a case 'spirit'. This also contributes to the common farewell blessing \"kaean'leh evareh\" which means \"may it protect us\". \n\nIt is also worth learning the 'operative' which is also seen in the sentence above on in \nword 'evareh' . In elven it serves the purpose of turning a verb into a wish. It is conjugated as below:\n\n-eh - operative sing.\n-erih - operative pl. (-arih if the verb ends in -ar)", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven LessonsIII", "Kalenz Uradir", "\n\n\n\n     New Elven\n     Lessons:\n     Volume 3\n\n   by Kalenz Uradir\n\nTo conjugate verbs in elven one must note precisely what person is being referred to in the sentence. Then the verb is conjugated:\n-e - 1st sing\n-a - 2nd sing\n-e - 3rd sing\n-ae - 1st pl\n-an - 2nd pl\n-eyae - 3rd pl\nTo create past and future tenses is quite simple in elven. However, it takes specific forms depending on the time of day. For these two tenses the following four words are used: \nKarin'ento - Next sunrise\n\nKarin'ante - Last sunrise\n\nKer'ento - Next sunset\n\nKer'ante - Last sunset\nIf it is night at the time the elf refers to the past or present the two forms with the 'Ker' are used. If it is day at the time the elf refers to the past or present the two forms with 'Karin' are used. These endings are simply added on to the end \nof sentences to designate the tense. \n\nEx.: Kalenz iyathe (Kalenz thinks) becomes Kalenz iyathe ker'ante (Kalenz thought). Assuming the time in which it is spoken is the night. \nIn spoken elven, particularly more casual speech these endings are often shortened into the slang of karinto, karinte, keno and kente.", ""),
        StaticBook("Elven Survey", "Beranabus Seregon", "            To be an Elf\nOverview: I have been pondering the concept for a long time as to what it is to be an elf and have devised a set of questions inwhich I shall go about asking both what folk consider elves and other descendants these questions to \nget a general consensus of what it is to be an elf to those around the realm.\nThe questions include\nTo elves:\nName:\nDo you believe in the classification of elves with subtypes a/e Mali'ame, mali'aheral, and Mali'ker:\nDo you believe in Malin:\nWhat is an ideal elven nation (Unified, individual states, or something else):\nWhat is your opinion on Haelun'or:\nWhat is your opinion on Laureh'lin:\nWhat is it to you, to be an elf:\n\n    To 'non' elves\nName:\nDo you consider yourself a friend enemy, or ally of elves as a whole:\nOpinion of Haelun'or:\nOpinion of Laureh'lin:\nWhat would you consider an elf to be:\nAnything else you wish to say:\n                  1.\n                  Elf\nDwyn Tinuvial\n1. I don't see anything wrong with it, as there is an obvious physical difference, though do not let it shield my opinions they are still my kin regardless.\n2. Aye\n3. Equality in seperate states\ndifferent nations, though a council of leaders inwhich they may make decisions for all the people. Though still seperate.\n4. I have friends there, great people I would like to visit though as a whole I wish they would just leave us alone.\n5. We've got our\nissues, and we are working on them. But we can't do anything with everything else breathing down our neck.\n6. Well just to be like anyone else. A hard working, a father, a mother. Part of a family. My race doesn't define me my actions do.\n              2\n             Elf\nAnarane Flormai\n1. I believe in subtypes as they are very different from one another\n2. Yes\n3.Unified, I was born in Malinorian times and can see it working.\n 4. They are murderers and killers who hide behind their walls.\n5. They are alright I guess, they aren't as bad as the high elves.\n6. To live long and have pointy ears. (She said jokingly) Love nature which high elves neglect.\n              3.\n             Elf\nGwyneviere Ikur'onn\n1. Yes I do\n2. I believe he was real, but we do not need to copy him.\n3. Impartial, just have one person incharge.\n4. Filled with snobby people\n5. I like people inside but poor government\n6. A person with pointy ears and calls themself an elf.\n              4\n           Human\nArchibald Vallero\n1. Friend, moreso with Haelun'or but still somewhat with cerulin. Fuck Dark elves.\n2. Peak of elven perfection\n3. Disorganized, A pot of Chaos, and too integrated.\n\n4. Long ears, tall and lithe, and.. All that shit.\n5. None\n              5\n            Elf\nAsh'ya Norodiir\n1. Yes, we all look so different though it is a difficult question. We are all mali though.\n2. Yes\n3. Unified, we have enough war as it is we needn't have more within our own race.\n4. Impartial, I haven't met anyone of their people before.\n5. Seems to be more willing to fight than I had imagined. Many talk of war and fighting not peace.\n6. Malin created us as brothers and sisters. We live alongside nature our beliefs I believe are \nthat we care more for Athera. For me to be an elf is to live how Malin wanted us to be. We are cursed with the inability to produce many children so we should care for those who live now. Ideally we do not fight between ourselves.\n              6\n            Elf\nCuruven Haler'sul\n1. I do believe we should, all Mali are children of Malin but our cultures differ from each different name.\n2. Yes\n3. Different districts, but with one leader.\n4. Haelun'or is a powerful state, moreso than Cerulin sadly, but the haelun'orians are harsh when it comes to silver law. But most won't be effected if they don't disturb them.\n5. Cerulin is quite a good nation. I suppose it is a heathen for Mali'ame\nand I respect that. I do hope this new government reform will grow the nation even more however.\n6. My definition of a Mali is not a halfbreed of another race. Mixbreeding with other mali is fine but not of other races..", ""),
        StaticBook("ElvishforDummies", "[Avatar] Chikachu", "\n\n\n       Elvish for Dummies\nIf you really need a \"For Dummies\" book to learn Elvish..........\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n...\nDon't play an elf.\n\n:D", ""),
