        StaticBook("How2KillMonsters", "[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "\n\n\n\n     Monsters\n        and\n   How to Defeat\n        Them\n\n    by Seth Calith\n\nMonsters roam our lands, and a good fighter has to know how to repel them as well as their strengths and weaknesses. In this book, the reader will find a guide on how to defeat each beast using either a bow or a sword.\nZombies\nZombies are creatures that, wanting to attack, follow you around. With that knowledge, a bow is a useful tool to defeat these monsters. The key to this fight is charging a shot in your bow while running. Once it is charged, shoot the arrow to \nhit it while still charging another arrow.\nIf it is of your preference to use a sword, you can take advantage of the Zombie’s low terrain-calculation properties. Try to stand higher than its head so that it is unable to strike you.\nSkeletons\nThe average skeleton is not as strong as the zombie, yet deadlier because of his fine use of the art of archery and bowmastery. It is recommended to use a sword against such creatures since they are bad in close combat and can only\nshoot at a determined rate.\nTo do such, circle around it fast enough so you can avoid its arrows, which is not recommended unless you are wearing good armor. When it fires, sprint towards it and slash him as hard as you can. With an average iron sword\nand a good reaction, you can defeat it while only taking one arrow, probably to the knee, due to its position upon death. If you can’t dispose of these requirements, you will have to study its rapidness of shot or withstand some more arrows, which is not\nrecommended unless you are wearing good armor.\nFor profficient bow-wielders, a duel can be had against one of these creatures. One should find a place to cover oneself and do so. When it shoots, wait for the sound of the arrow to crash against your\nhiding place and leave it. Shoot it once with a good aim and hide again. It will surrender sooner or later.\nBlazes\nThe blazes’s combat is not very different of the skeleton's, and with the blaze shooting fireballs instead of arrows, three at a time. If using a sword, follow the steps for the skeleton with a slight change. Wait for three shots to be fired,\nnot one, and make sure you don’t step on the fires set ablaze by the blaze itself. Do a similar thing with the bow. You can know when he is about to shoot because it fires up. \nSlimes\nSlimes come in various sizes, and upon death they divide into smaller ones. Use a bow to defeat all but the smallest, that are inoffensive and can be easily defeated with a sword. \nEnderbeings\nThese are strange beasts. They can move from a place to another in a blink, known as teleporting. When fighting one, the only tip I can give you is to jump in some water. Oddly, these beasts are hurt by it, so it will die in its chase.", ""),
        StaticBook("Howl for Love", "Damien Menrow", "Silent scream under a grinning moon,\nsings out it's voiceless call.\n\nA hallowed ground echos its nearing doom,\nearth beneath catches your fall.\nBirds take flight to cry their fear,\nas the heart beats its final few.\n\nI think of the one that I hold dear,\nand howl for the love of you.\nLetters written with the sweetest musk,\nbirds carry your darkened lies.\n\nI wait for Dawn to give to Dusk,\nwhen the light fades and the shadows rise.\nMy sweetest sleeps in the ebony night,\nbut I feel you move to our lightened space.\n\nEncrouching on my loves kind sight,\nI'll make sure you will know your place.\nFollow your heart yet it leads you here,\nclutched tight and still after I'm through.\n\nThe silence is shattered by a cry so clear,\nas I howl for the love of you.", ""),
        StaticBook("Human Babies", "Rosso", "\n\n\nWhere do human babies come from?\n\nThe mother and father or the soon to be parents, shag a lot until the woman is pregnant. Human shag so much, sometimes the father is not even the husband of the mother. They shag everywhere they can, in a bar, on their home, in other’s home, in a brothel,\noutside, and so forth.\n\nWhen a woman gets pregnant, it takes 9 seeds for the baby to be born in the most VILE of experiences for the woman, where it seems like the Nether has been opened in their wombs, and rivers of blood and slime flow from it, until a\nbloody crying newborn comes out.\n\nEven those men that have been in the Nether itself, get disgusted by the sight that is the human birth.\n\nWhere do Human babies come from\n\nOriginal author unknown\nCoppied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Hwaldar", "Thurak", "Rescribed by the Farseer Thurak, 9\nth of Sun's Smile, 1484.\nThe following scrawl and musings regard the wizard Hwaldar, who, upon his expulsion from the Mage’s Guild and subsequent return, has created a great stir within the ranks of magi and Undead alike.\nFurthermore, these notes reflect on recent events before and up to Ahand, 15’th of Sun’s Smile, 1305, and inquire into the actions and mental state of Hwaldar, the fallen Archmage.\nHwaldar and Whisperer are two names that cannot be said without the other being brought up, especially in a conversation regarding the Mage’s Guild. Both men are responsible for, if not at least held in relation to, the recent strife within \nand surrounding the council and academy of magi. This first connection, their shared ire for the Guild, is clear, but there are others, and they all build to strange conclusions. Hwaldar once told me that he and the Whisperer are close \nfriends, and, indeed, that may be true, but I believe there may be other explanations. In short, the two men may be the same, yet splintered mind.\nLet me begin with some backstory. I first met Hwaldar outside the iron and diamond portcullis to the Tower in the north. He, of course, was on the other side, telling me only mages were allowed inside. That didn’t stop me, but that’s an irrelevant \nmatter for another time. Our conversation ended with me adding that one day, Hwaldar may just welcome me inside, if only for company. Many weeks passed, and though I had achieved welcome into the Tower, it was not by Hwaldar. In fact, the man \nhad vanished and was expelled from the Guild. For a while, I thought that would be the last I heard of the Archmage. \nSometime after my first conversation with Hwaldar, I met with one who calls himself “Whisperer”. He’s a strange, albeit interesting character with supposed ties with the Undead, who he has qualms with, along with the mages. Several times \nwhile travelling with the Whisperer, he remarked on mages, the Undead, and the whole of Aegis.\nDespite his world knowledge, many experiences seemed very much new to him. The concept of names and naming was beyond him, and he frequently used the terms \"Disfigured\" or \"Disfigurement\" to describe and indicate people\nor the environment. However, he specifically stopped outside of a Mage’s Guildhouse in Laurelin. His hatred towards the mages wasn’t some abstract story. It was specific to the Mage’s Guild. Since then, Whisperer tasked me with setting up a \nmeet between he and Bell; a seemingly popular figure in Aegis, to discuss \"The Truth\".\nNot long after the Whisperer’s unrest, Hwaldar returned to me, with a similar hatred for the Guild. Interestingly, he admitted that he and Whisperer are close friends, and even went so far as to imply that he created Whisperer\nwith his mind, if not several minds. Since then, Hwaldar has been brewing mischief in attempts to raise himself up or splinter the Guild. Thusly, the connections find themselves. \nBoth men have always contacted me separately, as in there was only one, and never both. However, while the men never share obvious company, they share beliefs, dislikes, and circumstances. For example, both have some arcane and rueful connection\nto Bell, who gladly reciprocates her frustrations. Whisperer claimed she was a “sister”, as she was once Ascended. As far as Hwaldar goes, she has taken up a position as Archmage at the Guild, much to his anger. Both whispere and Hwaldar claim\nto have been tricked and stolen from by the mages. In Whisperer’s case, he claims that the mages are responsible for many lost lives, and the theft of the Tower after he himself rebuilt it. As for Hwaldar, he was tricked out of his position as Archmage,\nagain, by the mages. Also, word has slipped that Hwaldar may be a sleeper agent of the Undead, thus connecting him with the ties Whisperer claimed to have, despite Whisperer having supposedly severing them. In fact, \nWhisperer may remain allied with the Undead, by the is irrelevant to the subject at hand.\nIt is clear that both Whisperer and Hwaldar are tied closely together, but in what manner remains to be seen. Is Whisperer a dissociative splinter of a once sane mind? Has Hwaldar suffered some persona cataclysm that helped spawn this alter?\nMaybe Whisperer is a clever ruse meant to serve some mysterious gain. Maybe they are truly two different entities, despite ever-stacking evidence. If Hwaldar survives cleanly through the spate of wrath he faces from the Mage’s Guild, time may just \ntell. However, if Hwaldar’s mind has truly torn, and all irrational behavious can be explained in him, the situation poses on last question: How would the fevered mind of an old mage be dealt with?", ""),
        StaticBook("Hydrium Testing", "Camo Shadd", "5th of Sun’s Smile, 1505\nWeather: Rain\n\nToday I am planning on conducting tests of which would be a better material to use for a potential hydrium wall.\nI decided today would be a good day to test things by the river \nsince it is raining and the water will be high. I am planning on conducting the test by the sheep farms.\n\nTest details\nTest: To see what are the statistics of certain material under the pressure of a stream through water.\nFor this test I will use \n8 different materials, oak wood, spruce wood, cobblestone, stone bricks, bricks, dirt, glass, stone. I will make a small body of water flowing down river. I will test to see how much of said material is used to create the water to flow a different \ndirection. I will also note any other issues with the chosen material.\n\nHypotheses: I predict that glass will be one of the strongest out of the 8 tested items. My reasoning for is that glass is used for things like windows and cups. I think it \nshould be strong and durable. The weight of it should keep it firm.\nI think that dirt shall be the weakest out of all the tested things. My main reasoning for this is that I think it will eroded away.\n\n\nResults\n\nDirt: As predicted dirt took the most material to change water flow. It also held it up for the least amount of time. The dirt would slowly get pushed over or moved to the side by the water. This is not surprising given its permeable nature.\nDirt is not meant to hold up water, it should stay in the ground where it belongs.\n\nOak Wood: Oak wood did surprisingly well. It took a lot of material to change water flow which is disappointing. The most noteworthy thing about wood is \nthat it is very durable. It did not degrade. This is probably due to the hardy nature of bark and trees. From this I expect that heavier and harder wood should do a lot better.\n\nSpruce Wood: This material is like oak wood but heavier and more durable. It \ndid everything oak wood did but better. It took a little less of this to block off the stream compared to standard oak. This material was also more durable than oak. It took longer to chip away. The main issue with this type of wood is that it is not \nheavy so it does move around after a while.\n\nGlass: This material acted differently from what I thought it would do. It was not as strong as I would have guessed. The only thing that I thought was good was that is was heavy so it did not get moved.\nThe main problem is that is was not durable, every once in a while a piece would chip off. This is not a good sign for a proper building material. \n\nCobblestone: This material had good and bad things about it.  It did not take very much of it to change\nwater flow but it was difficult to get it to that point. There is little holes in the stone that lets water through it. Even though this is a huge issue it can be solved by reinforcing it with some sort of stone mix. The best thing about this material is \nits weight. The weight also makes it naturally durable.\n\nStone: Stone acted similarly to cobblestone. One thing to note is that cobble and normal stone each have a different benefits. Stone is lighter than cobble but \nit seem to be more durable. If they were both used in the construction cobble would make a better foundation. It took roughly about the same amount as stone and cobblestone to change the water flow.\n\nBricks: This and stone bricks are by far the heaviest things on the list. Individual bricks are not extremely durable. Stone would be more durable. They good things about bricks are that they are strong and heavy. They could make good walls and good \nfoundations for any structure. I am certain they will be used for the wall.\n\nStone Bricks: This, like normal bricks, are very heavy. This material is the most durable thing tested. It would be excellent for walls. A nice thing about this is testing \nit did not take up very many bricks to change water flow. This material would be the one of choice for building walls. Another note about this is that is is not difficult to make.\n\n\nConclusion\nA hydrium wall should have a structure made out of cobblestone since it is very strong. For the outer wall since it needs to be strong and durable it should either be made out of bricks or normal stone bricks. Everything decorative \nof the build should be attach to the outer brick wall.", ""),
        StaticBook("Hydrology.", "Poe Grace", "-------------------\nSociology upon Hydromany and Hydromancers.\n-------------------\nWritten by,\nPoe\nGrace\n\nDedicated to,\nSelf Reliance.\nUpon looking at Sociology, we must ask ourselves, what is Sociology, and how does this word link into that of Hydromancy and Hydromancers.\nUpon reasearching the definition of Sociology, One must conclude that Sociology can be defined by the few words,\nSuch as:\n   \"Sociology is the academic study of social behaviour, including its origins, development, organization, and institutions.\"\n\nNow, knowing what sociology means, we now must look into the correlation between\nsociology, and Hydromancy/Hydroma-ncers.\nWith our definition of Sociology, we are presented with the word \"Social\", to truly understand, in depth the correlation between the two subjects we must look to the definiton of one of our \"sub-subjects\nThe word social, can be defined as;\n   \"The term social refers to a characteristic of living organisms as applied to populations of humans and other animals.\"\n\nThis definition of our sub-subject, 'Social' supports our cause,\nthat infact, Hydromany and Hydromancers, can be correlated to the word Social, of which Sociology stems from.\nFrom our defintion of the word \"Social\" we are given the word \"Living organism.\"\nThis word directly describes a person.\nOf which all Hydromancers\nare, Dirrectly linking our 2 subjects in 2 different ways.\nBy the plural form, and noun form.\n\nNow that we understand, that our two subjects have been linked clearly, we may now define and expand upon our already basic \nknowledge, from what we have gleaned from the definitions.\nOne now knows that one can explain the two terms, Hydromancy and Hydromancer, using the word Sociology. In our tense, we would correlate the matter, to the fact that \"Hydromancers\" is \nthe plural of \"Hydromancy.\"\nThis now defines that a collection of able bodied Hydromancy can be defined as Hydromancers, of which the singular form of this word is Hydromancer.\nNow must must correlate directly between the word\nSociology, and Hydromancers.\nAs now knowing that Hydromancers is a group of subjects, we have gleaned from the definitions that these two words do infact correlate, and can, and perhaps may well be used in conjuction with eachother in the same sentence.\nNow refering to our Hyrdomancers, as our 'Group'\nThis our correlation between the two subjects even more potentency. Throught the defention of the word Sociology, we glean that a group contains social behaviour, as the group is made from\nliving organisms.\nWe can now extend our reasearch into that of our group's habits, behaviour and institutions ( A fine example of a Hydromancer's institution would infact be the Mage's guild.)\nWe have now gathered enough information to glean from our \nsituation, that the word sociology and Hydromancy/Hydromn-cers, can be used in conjunction absoloutley.\nFor one to look at the sociology of a Hydromancer, would be to look at their behaviour, habits and instituations (as stated before, an\nexample would of course be, The Mage's guild.)\nTheir habits would perhaps include how often a hydromancer had to rely on his power to connect to the void, and retrieve water from the void.\nAn example of behaviour could possibly include what \nthis water is used for, or how it is used, being used with a centripital tone, or infact a soothed one.\nThese examples directly show us how the term 'Sociology' and the generalisation term of 'Hydromancy' can infact be used in a sentence together, and\nstill be gramaticly correct, as the two words correlate absoloutley, with what could be defined as a postive correlation.\n\nOne know must understand, the defintions of these root words, and their stems. To fully grasp the idea that these \ntwo words, can infact correlate, and be used within a sentence.\n\n-Poe Grace.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Hydromancy", "Rosso", "Water: seemingly the simplest of elements, and yet incredibly complex. Water is a necessity of live, needed by all living things, plants and beasts alike. Just as it is the patron of life, water can also pose as the harbinger of eradication.\nDespite our best attempts; we are mortal. A single wave can take everything we own. A flood can contaminate food. A particularly harsh winter can be inescapable. Water contains raw power, a power that a hydromance yearns to wield.\nA hydromancer's training stems from the evocation and control of water itself but not the water that we know. This water can only be found in the void and some say it is not water at all. As all evocationists know, their power comes from the ability\nto control \"mana,\" but I'll leave that topic for others to discuss. After much dedication and training, a hydromancer is able to pull mana directly from the void, shaping it into a water-like substance in our world. This connection to the void is\ndifficult to put into words, as connecting to the void is more of a sensation than something that can be explained. As a mage's training progresses, connecting to the void and evoking the mana within it become more simpler tasks. \nMany mages use a staff when evoking the elements, but the truth is that the staff is not necessary. I, for instance, use a staff topped in mage-gold and carved from elderly jungle wood. This staff is fitting for me because of some of the properties\nthat is possesses, and also because it has been personalized for me. The purpose of my use of a staff is to create a simple, close point for me to concentrate on and evoke water from. When the water is pulled forth, it forms a stream from the end of my\nstaff that maintains itself midair. From here, I can move it, project is somewhere, or allow it to dissipate.\n\nOne thing about water evocation that differs from the other branches of evocation is that water can change states in our world. Novice\nwater evocationists begin with controlling pure water in its liquid form. As a hydromancer becomes more skilled ((approximately late tier 3)), he is able to change the water into ice. Ice is much more suited for battle and defence than liquid water is,\nas it can be used to create walls, freeze doors shut, block oncoming projectiles, as a form of short-distance defense, and as a method of harming others. I have personally mastered a technique of blasting an enemy with water, and then freezing\nthem in their tracks should they be hit. Magical ice is not like that which can be found in our world; void ice does not melt from the heat of the sun or a torch. To break void ice, the hydromance must either completely lose concentration, or another\nmage must do something to break the ice (i.e. a pyromancer melts the ice with magical flames). Water is not often formed into the vapor state, as the wisps of steam are too difficult for even the most trained evocationists to control.\nWater, being able to form any conceivable shape, is only limited by the caster's imagination and fatigue. With proper training, water can be suited for nearly any purpose.\nIntroduction to Hydromancy\n\nWritten by Ev Ar'ahern\nCopied by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n*Strange Numbers\n((15/6/13))\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Hydromancy", "daelaris", "Introduction to Hydromancy\n\nEv Ar'ahern\n\n\nWater: seemingly the simplest of elements, and yet incredibly complex. Water is a necessity of live, needed by all living things, plants and beasts alike. \nJust as it is the patron of life, water can also pose as the harbinger of eradication.\n Despite our best attempts; we are mortal. A single wave can take everything we own. A flood can contaminate food.\nA particularly harsh winter can be inescapable. Water contains raw power, a power that a hydromance yearns to wield. A hydromancer's training stems from the evocation and control of water itself but not the water that we know. \nThis water can only be found in the void and some say it is not water at all. As all evocationists know, their power comes from the ability to control \"mana,\" but I'll leave that topic for others to discuss.  After much dedication and training, -\n- a hydromancer is able to pull mana directly from the void, shaping it into a water-like substance in our world. This connection to the void is difficult to put into words, as connecting to the void is more of a sensation than something that can be -\n- explained. As a mage's training progresses, connecting to the void and evoking the mana within it become more simpler tasks.\n\n Many mages use a staff when evoking the elements, but the truth is that the staff is not necessary. \nI, for instance, use a staff topped in mage-gold and carved from elderly jungle wood. This staff is fitting for me because of some of the properties that is possesses, and also because it has been personalized for me. The purpose of my use -\n-of a staff is to create a simple, close point for me to concentrate on and evoke water from. When the water is pulled forth, it forms a stream from the end of my staff that maintains itself midair.  From here, I can move it, -\n- project is somewhere, or allow it to dissipate.\nOne thing about water evocation that differs from the other branches of evocation is that water can change states in our world. Novice water evocationists begin with controlling pure water in its liquid form. As a hydromancer becomes more skilled -\n- ((approximately late tier 3)), he is able to change the water into ice. Ice is much more suited for battle and defense than liquid water is, as it can be used to create walls, freeze doors shut, block oncoming projectiles, as a form  -\n- of short-distance defense, and as a method of harming others. I have personally mastered a technique of blasting an enemy with water, and then freezing them in their tracks should they be hit. Magical ice is not like that which can be found -\n- in our world; void ice does not melt from the heat of the sun or a torch. To break void ice, the hydromance must either completely lose concentration, or another mage must do something to break the ice -\n- (i.e. a pyromancer melts the ice with magical flames). Water is not often formed into the vapor state, as the wisps of steam are too difficult for even the most trained evocationists to control.\n\nWater, being able to form any conceivable shape, is only limited by the caster's imagination and fatigue. With proper training, water can be suited for nearly any purpose.", ""),
        StaticBook("Hydromancy", "Arayon Drakon", "=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=       Hydromancy\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=   By Arayon Drakon\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=  A tome about being able to create a small droplet of water all the way to creating monstorous spikes of ice!\n    Table Of Contents\nPage 3: The Void\nPage 4-5:Mana\nPage 6-9:Hydromancy\n        The Void\nThe void is the realm from one in which someone pulls their arcane elements from. The void is a seperate plane of existance that is seemingly nothing and everything at the same time. Endless potiential rests there.\n          Mana\nMana is the form of energy used to create an element from the void. Mana exists within each descendant, it is believed to originate from the soul. Everyone has a limited source of mana, once one runs out of it, it typically takes an\nelven hour to regenerate all of the mana. \n      Hydromancy\nHydromancy is the process of producing any form of water from the void. Some examples are:\n-Water\n-Ice\n-Steam\n-Boiling Water\nHydromancy is largely a form of limitless possibilitys.\nIn order to produce water from the void one must study the form one wishes to produce for long periods of time. Water produced from the void does not follow the typical terms of natural water. One can not drink produced water for example. The water will\nnot stay permanently when produced, as it is a drain on mana while it is there. The effects of the elements stay though. Once one understands the element and the void they should be able to create it within the void and pull it into reality through large \namounts of practice. Limited control is available for elements pulled from the void. If one wants to use fine control you have to learn primordial conjuration, which allows you to make beings of an element.", ""),
        StaticBook("i", "Elisa Balor", "", ""),
        StaticBook("I like Droods", "The Pants bandit", "\"I like druids very much. They are very nice and do not kick me out just because I am a clothomaniac. They are very nice and I like them, and Dryads.\" \n[!] A strange inscription was written, #Ambrosisbestsourdoughforlife.", ""),
        StaticBook("Iblees Journal", "Annabella", "I have taken a break form my studies to examine the various Iblees eye structures that have been apearing.\nThe first one appeared to be harmless. Showing major corruption, but it did not cause harm form what I hear. The second structure found in forn of Dwarven lands has various creatures which are unknown to me.\nIt is confirmed, they are undead.\n\nA Drake has spawned, crashing into the city before it dies.\nThe general chaos is over, everyone is healing each other. I will stay for a slight time longer, but I should leave soon.\nConclusion: It appears our hope for peace on this land is far from the case. A traveler warns me it is Iblees, and the structures formed show the infamous 'Eye of Iblees'. \nI will make another journal should I hear of other incidents.", ""),
        StaticBook("IHBH:SA", "Ser Paul Ryan", "- - - - - - - - - - - -         It's Hard\n    Being Handsome:\n\n  How to survive in \n  Athera, and other\n        life tips\n- - - - - - - - - - - - \n   By: Ser Pavl Ryan\nWith special foreword         by Emperor \n   Tuvya Carrion I \n TABLE OF CONTENTS: \n\n- Foreword\n- Authors Note\n- Chapters I-V\n- About the Author\n        FOREWORD\n-------------------\n\nMeeting Ser Paul Ryan, much less becoming good personal friends with him, will most certainly be one of the most defining moments of my life. I've seen much in my time as the Emperor of Athera's greatest nation, but\nnever have I seen such strict dedication to greatness as I have in Paul. Whether it was making him part of my privy council, calling upon him to lead a heroic charge in times of war, or simply asking the favor of his attendance to my young daughters \nnameday party, I knew I could count on a man as loyal and reliable as Paul. Reading his first book, as well as getting a sneak peak at his upcoming biography, granted me a different outlook on life, and how I live it. Never before have I experienced such\na thrilling page turner as I have when reading \"It's Hard Being Handsome\". I recommend this fantastic encyclopedia of majesty to all men and women, regardless of age, race or gender, who one day wish to become sucessful. \n     AUTHORS NOTE\n-------------------In my times of travel around the land of Athera, I've come to learn a great deal of things, both through speaking to some of the oldest and wisest masters of \nmany skills, and trial and error as I blundered \nmy way through city after city, town after town. I've learnt how to fight great mythical beast, how to drink a \ndwarf under the table, how to hide from halflings, sell my legitimate wares to all kinds of illigitimate folk, and a great deal of other skills\n. But if there's \none thing I've learned above all else, it's this; \nIt's hard \nbeing handsome. \n\nHi, I'm Ser Paul Ryan, and this is my book.\nMany times through the years, I've been approached by great kings and lowly merchants, all desperate for my advice. I've been the honored guest in many foriegn \ncourts, and handed out justice in my own lands.\n I recieved many great awards, both for my continued service to House Carrion and Oren as a whole, and for helping\nout the distressed through all kinds of situations. Yet I soon found out that, no matter how great I was (and trust me, I was great),\n my life always seemed....\nempty. By the time I had done all of these great things and achieved knighthood and gentry status, all at the ripe old age of 18, I decided to write down my \nadventures, and, more importantly,\nthe rules with which I've come to live life. In the following pages, you too will learn to live by these rules. Good luck, friend,\nand I hope you enjoy it! \nCHAPTER ONE (I)\n-------------------Find friends:\nOne of the earliest lessons I learned in life was this; EVERYTHING'S better with friends, friend. Becoming close acquaintance to esteemed men and women like the \nruling class of Oren,\nor simply the pal of the local drunk and homeless, friends help you through all sorts of trouble. Need a place to stay? Fine a friend. Need\na loan? Find a friend. Need help breaking Goldsteins' stinking knees, because he still hasn't paid you back \nfor that time in the brothel? Find a friend. No matter\nwhere I go in all of Athera, I can always count on finding room and board, courtesy of my good pals. If you really think you're a step above the rest, you can even\ntry to become one of MY friends,\nand join Paul's Pals™, my adventuring group of heroes. For better or for worse, helping out someone in need of a hand can result \nin some good luck coming your way down the road. Remember my original and trademarked saying: \n A friend in need is a friend indeed, who, in the future, will owe you™. \n\nCHAPTER TWO (II)\n-------------------Find fortune\n\nOne of the greatest assets any man has in lifes' adventure is his coin purse.\nMany a schlub can be bribed, and it never hurts to have the pocket coinage to buy that\nfancy new automatic crossbow, or perhaps donate to your favorite local political party. But how does one obtain such vast, impressive wealth like that owned by \nthe plutocrats of our age, such as Emperor Tuvya, or Ser Paul Ryan? Well, friend, let me tell you; hard work, creativity, and a good bit of honest borrowing can go far. I myself made my first fortune when I founded CapCo™, Oren's premier\nentreprenuerial company, which now is valued at roughly 500,000 minas. By working hard to achieve\nyour dream, and marketing your products correctly, you'll soon build the capital needed to further your endeavors, like \nperhaps launching a new, better company. Good\nbusiness partners are a must have, and I myself continue to hold close correspondance with my good friend and investor, Jacob Goldstein, of Goldstein & Company. After I safely handed over CapCo™\nto the care of my board of directors, my next startup companies, Paul's Pawn & Picking™, Paul's Tonics & Powders™, Paul's Ghost Hunting™, \nand Paul's Travelling Stall™all quickly flourished under my careful experience. Need some help starting down the \npath to vast wealth? Try finding a local investor\nto give you a startup fee, and bleed that sucker dry! If you need some immediate cash, perhaps to pay back the loan shark who threatened to break your fabulous \ncheekbones, consider\nborrwing someone \nsomeone elses valuables and using those to pay. Remember, friends: It's not illegal if you're not caught. \n\nCHAPTER THREE (III)\n-------------------Find your talents\n\nIt never helps to have good qualities, friend. \n I myself am incredibly handsome and creative, and possess a myriad of skills that would make a jack of all trades cry\nuncle. But how can you exploit the talents you have? Simple; by deceptively manipulating others. Are you a heavy hitter? Consider \nstrong arming your way into the \nlocal tavern, and borrowing some of his ale to sell later at a profit. Small and sneaky? Deceptively sneak into a store after hours, and help yourself to the register. \nOr perhaps you're like me,\na reknown inventor throughout the lands, sought after for his political savviness and charm. Remember that in this life, NOTHINGS free. Make\nsure you charge the right amount to give that little girl the location you saw her lost dog in, or to hide that \nhalfling running from some hungry orcs. If you don't \ncapitalize on all of your chances to make yourself more marketable, you'll never make it in our dog-eat-dog world. \n\n\nCHAPTER FOUR (IV)\n-------------------Find adventure\n\nSure, friend, you may be able to talk the talk, but can you walk the walk? It doesn't matter if you've accomplished all of these other steps to becoming great, if you don't USE them! Go out on the \nroad, and venture into lands previously uncharted. Have a drink at that edgy tavern, or consider stopping by your good dwarf friends\nto see what they've been up to lately. Seeing the world is its own lesson, one that anyone can take, and for free. \n Learn how to fight or talk your way out of \nany situation by haggling with the Vanirs over the price of a fish, or wrestling with Orcs in their sand pits. Awe the local milkmaid with tales of your completely\nlegitimate exploits, or spend a night sweeping\nat a tavern to cover your room and board. Life in our world is uncertain, friend, but it's always worth checking around the \ncorner to see what kind of trouble you can get into. Fall down and break a leg, or wake up without memory in the temple of those \nmonks who definitly enchanted this\nholy water I can sell you at a discount price? Get back up, and try again. If you're ever torn between relative safety, or a possible adventure, ask yourself one \nthing: What would Paul do? \nCHAPTER FIVE (V)\n-------------------Putting it all together, and surviving the process\n\nYes, friend, it's challening to learn how to be as great as me, and even more challenging to try, but the results will always pay off. By combining\nmy advice with\na little salt-of-experience you gain on your own, you'll quickly find yourself developing into the man or woman you only dreamed of becoming. Go out there, learn\nyour good qualities, get rid of your bad ones, and make\nsome good memories while you're at it. Work your way from the gutter to a palace, and go from using that\nrusty dagger to defend yourself to summoning the most powerful of magics. You will fail at first, friend. After all, no one is perfect. \n(well, actually, speak for yourself, bub)\nbut the lessons you learn in your failure will help you in years to come. Inevtiably, the great success you'll achieve will make the lesser peons\nof this great realm jealous of you. They'll wish they had your \ncreativity, your work ethic, and above all, your dashing good looks. There's a great deal I've learned\nmyself in my many adventures, most of all I've found that, yes, it really IS hard, being handsome. But would I ever give it up?\nNot for anything in this world, friend. \n\n- Ser Paul Ryan\nSome Quick Tips! \n\nWant to get better at those strange moments of your life, when everyone suddenly has magical symbols above their heads, and people start hitting each other? Try \npracticing in your free time with some pals and a wooden\nsparring sword, or, if worse comes to worse, learn how to flee effectively! To fight another day, of course. \n\n\nDon't try so hard to win all of the time, friend! In gambling, tussling, or even a friendly debate, losing can often yield more than winning. \n\nIf you see Paul or one of his many friends throughout the land, consider stopping for a chat or challenge with them, rather than strangely running and jumping by. \n\nPick a skill or service, become talented in it, and learn how to make a living through it. Sell your paintings or swords in a stall, or on the road, like me! Get a job as a petty laborer to garner experience, before ultimately starting a rival \ncompany up with stolen secrets, and crush your former boss!\n\nGo outside every once in a while, friend. And I don't just mean leaving your hovel in Athera.\n\nHave fun, friend. What else are we here for?\n - About the Author - -------------------Ser Paul Ryan is a successful warrior, statesman, and inventor of great reknown. Founder and CEO of CapCo™, and a number of other popular brands, such as \nPaul's Pawn & Picking™, \n Paul's Travelling Stall™, Paul's Pals™, and Paul's World Fair™, he was born in the small village of Wesconsen, before squiring under Boris Carrion III. \nKnighted by the late Emperor Peter Chivay for valor extraordinare in the field of battle. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Ikuras", "Skale", "[!] This tome contains extensive script detailing the teaching and knowledge of Ikuras, his magic, and the events that lead to his release.", ""),
        StaticBook("Illusion", "Rameriz", "Illusion.\n\nBy \"Vlad.\"\nChapter 1: Illusion itself\n\nIllusion is one of the most useful forms of magic allowing the mage to be able to look into another mind. Illusion takes a large amoutn of energy and consentration from the mage as it needs to consentrate on the \nopponant for it to work. Illusion is also very dangerous as the side-effects from an interaction with Illusion could be Damaging for a slight moment or for life.\nChapter 2: Uses.\n\nIllusion mages have the ability to look into anothers mind and either speak or command them to do something or think something. This could be fatal as an Illusionist could make the opponant think of something devestating\nand send them into shock. Illusionists also have the power to blind or scar people, this can be done physically or mentally. Mental Scaring and Blindness could be something like a trigger word which could make the person do or say certain things that coul\n-d endanger them. Illusionists can also physically blind a person or scar or harm someone making them self-harm or making them think they can do things that just aren't possible.\nFinally they have the power to read minds or get people to tell them secrets\nChapter 3: Conclusion.\n\nOverall Illusion is a difficult magic to turn and a dangerous magic to use. To read more on the magic itself continue on the remaining chapters.\n*This page is ripped out.*\nThis page is ripped out.*\nThankyou for Reading.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\"Vlad.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Immortal", "Sofetios Jayamen", "       Immortal\n\n          ~By Sofetios\n                Jayamen\n\nThere is a man in the town,\nWho knows that Power is the source of good,\nHe controls the town,\nBut his people do frown,\nBut the man doesn’t notice and never would.\n\nAs he goes through the town,\nAnd buys with a word,\nThe people do stray away,\nAnd for their company he cannot pay,\nBut to him that thought is absurd.\n\nAs the man drinks his wealth,\nEven to him none was dealt,\nThe man gets word one day,\nThat someone had passed away,\nAnd there came a new feeling he’d never felt.\n\nHe traveled very far,\nAnd as he walked to the place,\nHe couldn’t bear to look,\nAt this grave by the brook,\nWhere his life had been another case.\n\nAs he returned home,\nThe Home that never was,\nHe looked into the clouds,\nHe sought an answer he knew he wasn’t allowed.\nAs nearly every person does.\n\nHe walked into the town,\nSure the answer was there,\nWith his wealth here but gone,\nTo other people he was drawn,\nBut he realized the connection he did tear.\n\nAlone in a crowd,\nHe then sat down,\nEveryone just passed by,\nAnd the man began to sigh,\nUntil a small boy came around.\n\nThe boy looked around,\nAnd saw him alone,\nThe boy sat next to him,\nAgainst the man’s own whim,\nAnd said in an easy tone.\n\n“Sorry to bug you now,\nBut you look a bit sad,\nI’ve been following a while,\nAnd you never seem to smile,\nSo I brought something that might help a tad.”\n\nThe boy reached in a bag,\nAnd he pulled out a small ball,\nHe gave it to the man,\nThe man took it in his hand,\nHe dropped it and it bounced tall.\n\nThe man began to smile,\nAs his heart felt sore,\nFor the man’s answer was clear,\nThe very thing he did fear,\nThe immortal things in life you can’t pay for. \n\nThe End\nThank you for taking the time to read this.\nI'll be writing more in the future.\n\nIf you like my writing please send me a bird or tell me in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
        StaticBook("Immortal Blood", "Domainoft", "Immortal Blood\nOriginally Written in Aegis\nRetold by Leyu'Maehr\nWondrous Writtings\nVaerhaven\n*Strange Numbers\n((9/9/13))\nThe moons and stars were hidden from sight, making that particular quiet night especially dark.\nThe town guard had to carry torchlight to make their rounds ; but the man who came to my chapel that night carried no light with him. I came to learn that Movarth Piquine co uld see just as well in the night as in the day - an excellent talent,\nconsidering his int erests were exclusively nocturnal. One of my acolytes brought him to me, and from the look of him, I at first thought he was in need of healing. He was pale to the point of opalescence with a face that looked like it had once been\nvery handsome before some unspeakable suffering. The dark circles under his eyes bespoke exhaustion, but the yes themselves were alert, intense , almost insane. He quickly dismissed my notion that he himself was ill, though he did\nwant to discuss a specific disease. \"Vampirism\" he said, and then paused at my quizzical look, \"I was told that you were someone I should seek for help understanding it.\" \"Who told you that?\" I asked with a smile. \"Tissina Gray\", I immediately\nremembered her. A brave, beautiful knight who needed my assistance on the subject of Vampirism. It had been two years, and I had not heard from her since. \"You;ve spoken to her? How is her ladyship?\" I asked. \"Dead\" Movarth replied coldly. And\nthen, responding to my shock, he added, perhaps to soften the blow. \"She said your advice was invaluable. She was tracking a Vampire\" a pause, \"It killed her.\" \"Then my advice was not enough\" I sighed. \"Who says it would be enough for you?\" \"I know\nthat if a student doesn't ask the right questions, the teacher cannot be responsible for his failure. I intend to ask you the right questions.\" And that he did. For hours, he asked questions and I answered. but he never volunteered any information\nabout himself. He never smiled. He only studied me with those intense eyes of his, commiting every word I said to memory. Finally, I could detect some weariness in his eyes. I would like to commit this tomorrow night. I must sleep and absorb this. \"You\nsleep during the day\" I smiled. To my surprise, he returned the smile, though it was more of a grimace. \"When tracking your prey, you adapt their habits.\" The next day he returned, with more questions. I told him of the Vampires of Eastern Malinor,\nthe Volkihar, paranoid and cruel, whose very breath could freeze their victim's blood in their very veins. I explained to them how they lived amongst the tree trunks, never venturing into the world of men except to feed. He left, and returned\nwithin a few days. True to his word, the man returned to my chapel. There was a fresh scar on his cheek, but he was smiling that grim but satisfied smile of his. \"Your advice helped me very much\" he said. \"But you should know that the Volkihar have an\nability you did not mention. They can reach through the bark of their trees without breaking it. It was a nasty surprise, being grabbed from behind.\" \"Terrifying\", I said, \"You were lucky you survived\", \"I don't believe in luck\" he replied, \"I\ncan land a thousand blows without losing my balance, providing I get the first strike\" \"So you must never be surprised\", \"That is why I came to you. Again, I answered his questions, they taxed my knowledge. There was much to cover. The Bonsamu, who\nwere indistuingishable from men except when seen by candelight. The Keerilth who could disintegrate into mist. The Yekef who swallowed men whole. The dread Telboth who preyed on children, eventually taking their place in the family, waiting\npatiently for years before murdering them all in their unnatural hunger. Once again, he bade me farewell. Promising to return in a few weeks. I nodded, knowing then that this man was on an eternal quest. He wouldn't be satisfied but with the\nbarest hint of how things were. He needed to know it all. He did not return for a month, and on the night that he did, I could see his frustration and despar, though there were no lights burning in my chapel. \"I failed\" he said as I lit a candle. \"You\nwere right. I could not find a single one.\" \"I brought the light up to my face and smiled. He was surprised, even stunned by the pallor of my flesh, the dark hunger in my ageless eyes, and the teeth. Oh, yes, I think the teeth definitely surprised the\nman who could not afford to be surprised. \"I haven't fed in seventy-two hours\" I explained, as I fell on him. ::He did not land the first blow or the last.", ""),
        StaticBook("Immortal Spirits", "Thurak'Yar", "Immortal Spirits\n\nA list of the Greater Spirits inhabiting the Immortal Realm and what they represent. This may change, given time, and will be updated as new spirits come to power.\nVotar- The spirit of the hunt, commonly shown as a giant, bloodied wolf. Praised by the Phrase Glougaz Votar.\n\n      /\\_==_/\\\n      | /o   o\\ |\n      \\         /\n       \\       /\n        \\ *.* /\n          \\ /   \nEnrohk- The spirit of bloodlust, savagery and war, often shown as a bastard sword covered in blood. \n        \n         /\\\n         |  |\n         |  | \n         |....|\n         |. .|\n      `==8=='\n          ||\nShezept- The spirit of revenge, plots and stealth, depicted as a woman with a lower body of a snake. \n      \n        O\n       -|-\n   ^\\_\\\\_\n   \\____/\nJevex- Spirit of order, hard work and self sacrifice, shown as a giant hammer. He's a deadly enemy to the spirit Shezept.\n \n     [======]\n     [==|  |==]\n         ==\n         ==\n        |.--.|\n         ==     \nIxli- The spirit of forbidden knowledge, truth and judgement. Depicted as an eye, he provides knowledge as the cost of sanity. \n\n       |/  \\|\n      |  (.)  |\n       |\\  /|\nOgrol- Spirit of despair, sapped strength and entrapement, shown as a cage full of writhing bodies. He tries to lock up all who summon him.\n\n       ______\n       | | |  | | |\n       =<< >>=\n       | | |  | | |\n      ========\nKor- The spirit of the dead, depicted as a cloaked man with an open hand.\n          _\n          |O\\\n           |-''\n          /\\\nAnkrus- Spirit of the sea, fishermen and safe travels, often shown as an aquatic woman wielding a spear. \n\n           () |\n           oo/\n           \\/\n           /\\\n           \\/\nArwa- Spirit of fertility, harvest and farming, commonly shown as a plump female with over seven hundred breasts.\n \n           ()\n         /.oo.\\\n        /o.oo.o\\\n        |o.o.o.o.o|\n        \\_____/\n         [|]    [|]\nAnyhuluz- Spirit of destruction, malicious intent and internal strife, shown as a warrior covered in open wounds.\n\n         O   ^\n       /(`)-`|\n         [|]\n         /\\  \nAkezo- Spirit of health, vitality and healing, depicted as a winged whale surrounded by a blue aura.\n\n   (\\./)\n     \\\\_/--^--.\n      =.    \"~\"   )\n        \\______/\nPaxahru- Spirit of arrogance and stupidity, depicted as a boasting fool.\n\n        /**\\\n       ( ^^ ) \n        /--\\ \n        |`~~'|\n        ====\n        \\/\\/\n         |   |   \nUblulhar- Spirit of hope an new beginnings, shown as a tree sapling bursting from the ground. \n\n       _____\n      {~~~~}\n     {~~~~~}\n      {~~~~}\n       {~| |~}\n          | |\n       ~~~~~\nGhorza- Spirit of travel, depicted as an ever turning wheel.\n\n       /----\\\n      / /--\\ \\\n      |  |<:::::)|  |\n      \\ \\--/ /\n       \\----/\nRolfizh- Spirit of assassinations and poison, depicted as a dagger with poison dripping from it.\n\n        /\\\n        |  | \n        |  |\n       =||||=\n         ||\n        \nTrokorl- The spirit of engineering, machinery and building. Depicted as a goblin with a goblin with goggles and a hammer.\n\n       {o o}\n        \\-/P\n         |. |/\n         |. |\n         ==\n        /  \\\nLuara- The spirit of the moon, often shown by the symbol of a pale, white wolf.\n\n        \\___(>\n         |===/\n         |    |  \nRamakhet- Spirit of the desert, barren lands and sand. He is never seen, and therefore is rarely ever depicted as something other than his name. He has sworn to scour the earth of all life for all time.\n       ~~~~~\n      Ramakhet\n       ~~~~~\nKrathol- The spirit of pain, suffering and starvation. Depicted as a pile of dry bones.\n   \n      (------)\n      (------)   \nVeist- Spirit of illusion, tricks and thievery, depicted as a goblin with four eyes.\n\n      (:_:)\n      \\| |/\n       /\\\n       |  |\nFreygoth- Spirit of the wind and wild animals. He takes any form which he may desire. \n \n       ~~~~~\n        ~~~~\n         ~~~\n         \nGlutros- The spirit of greed and obesity. He is depicted as a cake.\n   \n        , , , ,\n       |====|\n       |====|\n       \nThulezia- Spirit of lust, pleasure and beauty, depicted as a naked slave girl. \n\n        (_)\n        |00|\n      ,/|   |\\,\n        !__!       \n       /   \\   \n     \n    \nIsuz- Spirit of love, caring, and tranquility, depicted as an open-armed woman.\n\n       { . }\n       \\| |/\n         ||\n         ||\n        \nOrgon- Spirit of Disease, Pestilence, and failure. Depicted as a festering, starving man with six arms, each holding a rusted and broken weapon.\n            (.)\n           |-||-|\n           i-||-i\n           !-||-!\n            /\\ \nUrin- The spirit of rain and hail, depicted as a dark rain cloud.\n\n         *****\n       ********\n         *****\n         ` ` ` ` \n          ` ` `\n           ` ` `\n            ` ` \nKesaroth- Spirit of envy and jealousy, usually shown as a golden bull.\n \n                 |__|\n                 ('')\n     /`-------\\/\n    / |         | |\n  '~  | |-----| |\n      ^^       ^^\nLeyd- Spirit of dominance and physical strength, symbolised by a closed fist.\n\n       ()()()\n        (   )\n         |**|\n         |**|\nScorthuz- The spirit of cleansing and purging, depicted as a vial of clear water.\n\n          (-)\n           | |\n         /--\\\n         |~~~|\n         |~~~|\n         ----\nTheruz- Spirit of intelligence and learning, shown as a wise old teacher. Brother to Betharuz.\n\n        ----\n       /     \\\n       | _  _ |\n     ('| O  O |`)\n       |  (.)  |\n       `/=.=\\'\n        \\/\\/\nBetharuz- Spirit of alcohol, cactus green and celebration, often depicted as a young dim man, in direct contrast to his brother, Theruz.\n\n       *, (.) U\n        \\_||_/\n           ||\n          /\\\nGentharuz- Spirit of smithing, smelting, industry and forging, depicted as a man with no legs, hammering an anvil.\n\n          (,)\n           ||--P\n         || ||   <==->\n         ||===| |==|\n         ||    || |++|\nDrelthok- Spirit of sleeping and dreams, often shown as a dark cloud.\n\n        %%%%%%\n       %%%%%%%\n        %%%%%%\n        \nEathruz- Spirit of dawn and dusk, shown by the symbol of a red sun.\n\n       . .       . .\n       .\\       /.\n         \\\\ //\n         /|/\\|\\\n         \\|\\/|/\n         // \\\\\n      . /       \\ .\n      . .         . .\nKotrestruu- The spirit of memory and the recording of knowledge, depicted as an endless book.\n   \n       ______\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/|\n       ||=====/\nLetrothak- The spirit of hindsight and excuses, shown as a goblin with eyes in the back of his head.\n\n        /----\\\n     \\\\| |  ||  | |//\n      \\,| (i)(i) |,/\n        \\      /\n         |      |\n       /        \\\n   __/ |       | \\__", ""),
