    private fun _ATHERA_4(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("Love", "Lielle Des'nox", "Though the parting was far too soon,\nNothing can change the love held.\nOne glance at him shows everything.\nThey were made perfectly to be together. \n\nLove that burned as passionately as the sun,\nSo strong that I only\nhad to hear\nThe way he spoke of her to understand\nJust how deep his love for her ran.\n\nA parting that was far too soon,\nBut a love that will carry on even in death.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Love Poem", "Celeion", "Shall I compare thee to a summer’s day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate.\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer’s lease hath all too short a date.\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimmed; \nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance, or nature’s changing course, untrimmed;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow’st,\nNor shall death brag thou wand’rest in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to Time thou grow’st.\nSo long as men can breathe, or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee.", ""),
        StaticBook("Lumber & You", "Maehr'Celia", " Lumberjacking and You\n\nSome people think that they can just go out into a forest with a axe, find a tree, and just swing away. Well, they can, but do they really know what they are doing? Do they know the proper way to swing an axe, which trees to cut, \nhow to make a cut, how to do it safely, or how to remove the tree and process it? I thought not. So lets begin.\n :Axes and You:\nFirst off, I recomend getting a hold of a axe made of stone or some form of metal. Swing away with 'wooden axe' (really\njust a pointy stick) is just pointless, the wood chips and becomes dull, and they break quick, so if you must use them bring plenty of them. As for swinging the axe, plant your face firmly on the ground and swing at full force to achieve a proper cut.\n:Felling a Tree:\n1- Cut a wedge into the tree on the side you want it to fall, as this will be where it leans when cutting\n2-Go to the direct otherside of the wedge and swing horizontally to the tree\n3- Yell 'TIMBER!\"\n4-Step away from falling tree\n:Selecting a Tree:\n A proper tree has to be atleast 10 years or more, healthy, and preferably 2 or more 'hugs' around. But thats just my preference\n:Removing said Tree: \nRemoving the tree is simple, clear a path to where you want it, and drag away\n Le fin, \n  You now know the basics to begin your career as a lumberjack", ""),
        StaticBook("Lumi'drimWarrent", "Warden Eldwehn Strongbow", "This man, Elrolas Hawksong, has been given the right of up holding the laws of Malinor within her forests, alongside the Lumi'drim as the rank of Sentinel...\n\n*signed, Warden Eldwehn Strongbow*", ""),
        StaticBook("M&P Fin (XII)", "Jan Altimir Vták", "I'm sorry I haven't written...I haven't come back in a while and....I got sick.\n\nI still am...it's from eating something....bad....\nDont worry I'm okay...but I didnt know what to write and...I have been very tired lately...\n\nPlease dont worry...I'll come home soon...I promise\n\n(( Yeah got sick and he's got papers to write T_T ))", ""),
        StaticBook("Mage's Guild", "Bellamy Ceux les Sombres", "I am Bellamy Ceux les Sombres. I am looking to join your guild and take the mantle of magic upon myself one day to help others. Thank you for reading this if you do.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mages Guild Inq.", "Ralotan Osprius", "Name: Ralotan Osprius\n\nIntentions: Become a student learning about Electrical Evocation.\n\n(MC Name: veal12)", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic Dictionary", "PiercingDarkness", "The Magic Dictonary:\n\nYir'sari - True Wizard, powerful magic masters.\n\nAscended - The demi-god children of the aenguls.\n\nUndead - The evil fists of Iblees, they destroyed Aegis.\nArcane Transportation Pad - \n\nStaff - a tool used to conduct mana, as well as aid you in Void connections.\n\nWand - Akin to the staff, doesn't work as well.\n\n\nCleric - Powerful magicians based mostly in healing, they are fueled by aenguls.\n\nNecromancer - Users of Necromany, a deadly magic having to do with life drain.\n\nPyromancer - Fire evocationist, magician of fire.\nElectromancer - Users of electric magic, mostly evocationist of electricity, they are common in Anthos.\n\nEvocation - The use of summong elements from the Void.\nFire-Evocation - The summoning of mana from the Void to create fire.\n\nWater-Evocation - The summoning of mana from the Void to create water or other water related things.\nElectric-Evocation - The evoking of mana from the Void to form electricity.\n\nWind-Evocation - The use of void mana to create wind and it's similiar elements.\n\n\nEarth-Evocation - The evocation of mana to form earthen elements.\n\nShamanism - Magic having to do with the use of spirits.\n\nElemental control - A shaman magic having to do with spirits.\nMagic(k) - The use of mana to conjure things, and even to control things.\n\nMana - Life force and energies around us, used to form complex spells.\n\nNecromancy - The use of life drain to get mana to form complex \nspells.\n\nShade-Magic - Fueled by a gem, it's a shadow magic having to do with the art of tendrils, and other shadow elements.\n\n\nIllusion - A arcane magic, the use of mana to form false images and feelings, and project them upon others, it's impossible to illusion yourself.\n\nConjuration - The conjuring of living beings.\nWizard - Common term name for the Yir'sari, true wizards.\n\nArch-Mage - Ruler of the mage's guild.\n\nMage - Practicer of magic.\n\nMagician - Mage is it shortened.\nAscended Magic - Magic fueled by a patron aenguel, it is a light magic, and holy, it is akin to cleric.\n\nUndead-Magic - Fueled by Iblees, the Undead used their necromancy to begin their conquest of Aegis.\nThe Void - A place where mages draw power from to fuel their spells.\n\nDragon - A beast of huge size and intelligence, they roam Northern Anthos, hunting.\nYellow Stone Material ((Endstone))\n\nA heavily weird material, it spreads corruption of many kinds.\n\nDruidism - Magic over nature, has to do with the spirits known as the Aspects. \nAlteration - Magic that alters the properties, of real world things.\n\n-------------------\n\nMagic sayings:\n\n\"Magic seeks those who don't seek it.\"\n\"Magic is the path of ths scholar, not a insolent warrior of heavy brains and no mindset.\" \n\n\"Magic is the path of destruction, insolent mage!\" - Gavin the Druid\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n\n\"Good and evil doesn't decide how magic works, only the acts of the user.\"\n\n- The Arcane Mage\n\n\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\nWritten by, Mythras Ardere.\n\nSources:\n\n- history of magic\n- The Clerical Order\n- The Mage's Guild\n- Salamandra's spirit\n- Many assorted books on magic, and knowledge from other mages.", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic License", "ski_king3", "\n    An Official Magic\n  License of the Holy\n  Magistrate of Oren:\n   It is hereby proclaimed that from this day forward, the elf known as Kalameet may practice magic lawfully within Oren under the guidance of the Creator.\n   The license is legally granted by the Holy Magistrate of Oren, specifically by the High Magister\nRose Valois with the approval of King William Horen III.\n   This license hereby approves the use of illusion magic by the elf known as Kalameet, and only illusion magic.\n\n\n   If this license is lost and found by anyone other than Kalameet\nor a member of the Holy Magistrate, it is the responsibility of any citizen of Oren to see this license be returned to the High Magister or the holder of this license.\n\n\nSigned,\nRose Valois, High Magister of Oren", ""),
        StaticBook("Magic School", "Gavin Headsted", "At The School we teach a wide range of fun and magic things like:\n\nMAGIC\nFighting\nFarming\nCooking\nWoodwork\nStonework\nReading and Writing\nMining", ""),
        StaticBook("Mail", "The Mailman", "Mail", ""),
        StaticBook("Making Babies", "[Masked] Marlyn Hardwood", "\n\nHow to make Babies\n\nby, Marlyn Hardwood\n((It would FTB as you start reading this book", ""),
        StaticBook("Mali of Thorns", "Anonymous", "Deliver us, oh savior, from the life that we have formerly known.\n\nConvert us to a new age of empty foliage and hushed laughter.\n\nBring to us, the might of a nation frequently knocked against our doorstep.\nTurned from the anti-mali ways of the White Rose, he has come to bring us enlightenment with a great coat of thorns.\n\nForever are we to serve our valah master, under their rightous boot.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Mali'Lumi Ethic", "Sentinel Elrolas", "http://www.lordofthecraft.net/topic/98989-the-malilumi-ethic/", ""),
        StaticBook("Malin", "Signature Trading Card", "  One of the four brothers from the start of time, Malin was the father of the Elves. While blessed with a long life, his descendents suffer the curse of limited fertility.", ""),
        StaticBook("Malinor's Fall", "Lucion Sullas", " A Treatise upon the\nCollapse of the Unified        Elven State\n\n\n  -By Lucion Sullas\n\n\n\n\n\n\n ~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~\nThe ancient land of our forebears, Aegis, is historically the only period of a singular Elven state ruled over by a united authoratative body. One can cite multiple reasons for the success of said state, and few would be able to refute their truthfulness;\nthe success and power of the Warden military, the stability of governance brought by Prince Native and many of his subordinates, and the political respect afforded to their nation by the world-at-large to name but a few. Furthermore,\nand most importantly of all, the elven population had a single cultural heritage from which to draw upon--the father Malin at it's head, and his protective forests that embraced our ancestors. With the fall of Aegis and the migration to Asulon, \nthe remnants of Malinor did what they could to emulate the nation they lost. Their arrival on the Emerald Peninsula had them seek out what was familiar, that is, a grand forest to protect them. In this they founded their first settlement of Elandriel they\nthey were driven from it by the pressing of the sinister Mori. After this reversal of fortunes, they took to living in the nearby city of Normandor, and named it their capital. It was in this isolated pocket of Asulon where Malinor was broken, due to six\ndegenerations that occurred within a relatively short period of time.\n1. The Secession of\n       Haelun'or\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~With the settlement of ancient ruins at the far west of the peninsula, the high elves preferred to rule themselves. This was generally accepted by the Malinor High Council, and no official\ntreaty was ever deemed necessary. The high elves themselves held a referendum to gauge the desire for true independence, and the result was overwhelmingly in favour, despite the grumbling of a small number of loyalists. Thus Malinor lost a\nlarge number of its high elven population.\n2. The Hostilities of \n       the Mori\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~The natives of the land were openly hostile to all Elven elements save for those who served them. Slavers, murderers, and open degenerates in both mind and flesh, they attacked the elven colonists\nwith unbridled fervour. Their religious institutions may have been the cause of this, but the purpose of this aggression has never been confirmed to any degree of satisfaction.\n3.   The Dark Elven\n        Servitude\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~Many dark elves left Malinor to join their supposed ebony-skinned brethren. Considering themselves kin to the Mori creatures, many of the dark elves wished to learn from them, and adopt their\nways. Not only did it lose Malinor a swathe of its dark elven population, but it also made these departed dark elves actively hostile.\n4. The Departure of\n      the Druids\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~Displeased at the felling of trees for construction, the druids left Malinor lands in protest of the 'humanised elves.' Many have refuted that the druids were ever a true part of Malinor, as they were\nnever exclusively elven themselves, though the seats given to them on Malinor's High Council suggest that they had influence in its affairs despite themselves. Regardless, the loss of this link to the natural world left a cultural gap in the nation.\n5. The Isolation of the\n    Elven Population\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~So far away from the conflicts of the humans, dwarves and orcs, Malinor never truly needed to test themselves upon the battlefield. The population of dedicated guardsmen (already in decline\nduring late Aegis) dwindled significantly with personal conflicts settled by individuals amongst the population. Raiders were avoided through a closed gate, or simply by being ignored. With no threats, what martial legacy they had died away.\n6. The Degeneracy of\n     the Malinorian\n        Population\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~Though not truly flowering into its most putrid form until mid-Anthos, Malinor's population turned often to lawlessness. Though this was abated late-Asulon somwhat with a\npopulation boom in civilians and guardsmen, the seeds of chaos were already planted. And an increase of political refugees from the growing Oren Empire simply made an unstable situation worse.\n\nAsulon, overall, was an abysmal time for the old Princedom, and these issues never were addressed with any real success. It finally succumbed to the Conclave of Malin and collapsed close to the end of Anthos. The cultural divides within the Elven populous\nare so wide that any unified state that wished to exist would require significant co-operation and trust between the Elven factions--a trust that does not exist--to stave off inevitable despotism. Many would tout compromise as a way to bridge gaps,\nbut one must only look upon the Conclave of Malin to see the success of compromise on a vastly diverse state.\n\nThere will never be a true 'Malinor' again. It will be for future historians to argue the implications of this.\n\n     \n\n\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~  Maehr'sae Hiylun'ehya\n\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~", ""),
        StaticBook("Malog's Proverbs", "Malog'Yar", "*As you open the book, you see in its pages a collection of proverbs written by the shaman, Malog'Yar. Each one is individually numbered for ease of memorization, it seems. They don't appear to have a particular order to them, but must \nhave been written as they came to the shaman's mind.\n((As in my other books, only those words which are actually different in the Blah are written in said language. The rest are in Common for ease of reading.))\n\n1. The lust for power is the beginning of shame. It concieves, agh brings forth arrogance. Arrogance, when it reaches maturity, brings forth humiliation.\n2. The path to honor is paved with sacrifice.\n3. Gold is a heavy thing, agh bub'hosh is the burden of he who hoards it. It will crush the heart, agh bring low the spirit.\n4.He who forgets his ancestors of the past will have nub future for his descendents.\n5.Food, water,\nshelter, praise; these things are wasted, when given to ash with nub honor.\n6.All is vanity for the whitewash, for he has abandoned his ancestors. He has departed from his purpose in life.\n7.Find that skill which lat are hosh\nat. Do it with honor, for even the lowly chicken farmer can glorify Krug.\n8.Do nub scorn simplicity. It is a chest of many treasures.\n9.Hosher is a small blarg with a mate agh cubs, than an empty palace.\n10.Integrity, \nuprightness, honor, discipline agh wisdom are the bones of the spirit. Without these, lat can nub stand.\n11.Hosher to be a blind man with wisdom, than a fool with many eyes.\n12.Luxury is an open gate to complacency.\n13.Complacency is a downhill path to the flatting of the spirit. It is an easy path to descend, but returning uphill is strenuous, requiring diligence.\n14.Bring up lats cubs in the way of honor, for they will take lats place in the future.\n15.This year's swineherd may be next year's Rex.\n16.Remember your brother, agh be quick to share his burden.\n17.The hardship of life is a refining furnace. It hurts for the time, but melts away that which would make \nlat weak.\n18.Lats body is a gift. Use it hoshly. Do nub abuse it, agh do nub let it wither.\n19.Life is work. Work hard or starve.\n20.The cactus lives in the dry sands, needing little nourishment. It is stronger for it.\n21.Hosher is a bag of rotten flesh, than a feast in the blarg of the dishonorable.\n22.He who lives with nub honor is like a fortress made of dry twigs or a zult made of still air: useless.\n23.Taking a title simply for the \nsake of taking a title is like eating sharp blades simply for the sake of eating.\n24.A wise blah is more precious than all the gems beneath the earth.\n25.Hosher to be flat for what is honorable, than to abandon lats \nintegrity simply to extend lats life.\n26.Honor is nub a currency to be traded. Do nub treat it as such.\n27.A life with nub honor is as meaningless as letters with nub order.\n28.Do nub tolerate weakness in body, \nmind or spirit.\n29.He who works hard will have his bread.\n30.Feeding the lazy is like planting the wind. It accomplishes nubthing.\n31.Be satisfied with food agh a cave. It may be all lat have ash day.\n32.Do nub envy the rich. Their decadence will make them soft.\n33.Do nub peep down on the poor. They are nub blinded by the glimmer of gold.\n34.Minas often do little more than disappear.\n35.Deceit agh\ntwisted words only clutter agh clog the mouth. Eventually, lat will choke on them.\n36.Blah what lat mean, nub what others want to hear.\n37.A slimy heart will slip, agh fall to its destruction.\n38.Rulers, do nub \nbe haughty. Ash day, lats reign will end, agh another will take lats place.\n39.He who is wise heeds the truthful rebuke.\n40.The fool surrounds himself with flatterers, agh is nub aware when ruin comes.\n41. Hosh is the \nfemale who bears strong cubs, agh works tirelessly to nourish their bodies agh spirits.\n42.Happy is the male who finds her, agh keeps her.\n43.Hosher is ash honorable lifemate, than ash hundred weak mates.\n44.As a blarg will \nnub stand on mist, cloud or vapor, neither will a family stand on weakness, foolishness of dishonor.\n45.Walk as Krug walked, agh honor the spirits. This will keep lats path straight.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mana Gem S/R-1", "Tyrell Blavier", "Mana Gem Research:\n\n-A mana gem is part of enchanting, and supplies mana to an enchantment. It must be charged by an enchanter. \n-After awhile it loses it's charge, and needs to be charged with mana again.\nI talked to Mark Volaire xXMark123Xx about these gems and the theory of mana gems and being used for energy distribution back into us. He suspected this would not be possible, however suggested the idea of using them on Golems.", ""),
        StaticBook("Marked Contract", "Kyral Winterleaf", "The Task:\nTo protect the Druids\nwhile they purge the Father Grove, working along the Scions to do so. Killing any monster that emerges before a Druid is felled.\n\nThe Payment:\n1500 per man, 5 will be called, 7500 total will\nbe payed upon completion\n*Kyral's signature seems to be written on the bottom of the paper, rather neat and easy to be read*", ""),
        StaticBook("Marriage Vows", ":", "I, Kane swear to.\n\n- Be faithful until the end of my days.\n\n- Share my property and possessions and titles with Ceren-Lumi.\n\n- Protect my wife's honor at all costs.\nI, Ceren-Lumi swear to.\n\n-Be faithful until the end of my days.\n\n-Share my property and possessions and titles with Kane.\n\n-Protect my husband's honor at all costs.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mathic Code", "Rosso", "From a note written by a friend in code, of many pages, she had no memory of writing it, or what it did mean.\nI decided to try and decipher it as if it were a secret note between armies, and I came with results. The code is copied first, then the meaning of the code is told, then the translation.\n\n\n\n           Code\n\n{t, ƒ:ªº£ ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t.£ ûe:ç :º e, ñ.ô, ñ.nü| ƒ'ª t, u':ô .nô t, ,ñö£:n,ºº d,i'û t, u':ô û,ª, e:º £' c'ññ.nô]\n[t,ü û,ª, ô\"º£ .nô e, û.º .º . º\"nd,.ñ| ô,ƒ:n:nø .£ i.º£ t,:ª ê.ö,]\n\n[.º û.º d,ƒ'ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º e, ñ.ô, .ø.:n| û:t t, º'ªª'û 'ƒ º\"ni:øe£ ª,ñ.:n:nø \"n£ª,.£,ô .nô \"nû,ic'ñ, £' t, ô,,ö c\"ªª,n£º 'ƒ t, º,.]\n[º\"ç ô:ª,c£:'ni,ººn,ºº :º .n.t,ñ. £' e:ñ| ûe' :º i:n,.ª]\n[t,º, .ª, £' d, E:º £ª:\"ñöeº .nô E:º ƒ.:i:nøº| ñ.nü £:ñ,º 'u,ª]\n\n{t, º,c‘nô ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ƒª.c£:‘nº ‘ƒ t.£ ûe:ç :º e, .ª, d.ªª,ô ƒª‘ñ ,n£ªü]\n[t, ‘t,ªº e.u, t,:ª ô,u:c,º ‘ƒ û.ª .nô ô:º“n:‘n| ô,ƒ:n:æ t,ñ .º ûe.£ t,ü .iû.üº e.u, d,,n| t, c.ª,£.*,ªº ‘ƒ t,:ª ƒ.£,]\n\n[t,ü ê‘“£ .nô öi,.ô .nô ‘ƒƒ,ª t,:ª u‘:c,º £‘ t, e,.u,nº| d“£ t,ü .ª, .º t, ô,,ö c.u,º d,øø:æ ƒ‘ª º“ni:øe£]\n[t, û.ü :º ê“£]\n\n{t, t:ªô ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[. ‘n, £,.ªº ƒª,, t, di:nôƒ‘iô ‘ƒ t, .düºº ƒª‘ñ t, ,ü,º ‘ƒ t, ö.ª£º]\n[:£ :º .º :£ û.º]\n[t, i:øe£ .nô i:øe£º .nô i:øe£:æ ‘ƒ t,:ª êª:n,º ƒª,, t,:ª º:øe£| t.£ t,ü ñ.ü di:nô ‘t,ªº]\n\n[û.ª .nô ô:º“n:‘n .ø.:n º“øø,º£º :£º,iƒ| .nô t, i:n, :º ñ.ô, .º .n “nc‘“n£.di, º,ª:,º ‘ƒ ö‘:n£º| .ii :n c‘nƒi:c£ û:t t,:ª ƒ,ii‘ûº]\n[.ii û:,iô:æ t,:ª i.û]\n\n{t, ƒ‘“ªt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t,:ª i.û :º t, ö‘û,ª ‘ƒ t, ñ.nü]\n[ºi‘ûiü t, i:øe£º .ª, ,æª.u,ô û:t t, ºüñd‘iº ‘ƒ t, ,nº,ñdi,º ‘ƒ t, ñ.nü]\n[t, º:øe£º ‘ƒ n,û ç.:nº .ª, ƒ.ñ:i:.ª]\n\n{t, ƒ:ƒt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ƒª.øñ,n£º .ª, ñ.ô, .º £‘ø,t,ª .º û.º d,ƒ‘ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º e,]\n[t, ñ.nü ç‘‘º, t,:ª ö.t| £‘ t, ª,ö,£:£:‘n .º t, º“n ª:º,º .nô ƒ.iiº ‘ª £‘ t, dª,.*:æ ‘ƒ :£| t.£ t, º“n ê.ii º£.nô º£:ii]\n\n{t, º:xt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[t, ñ.nü d,c‘ñ, .º ‘n, .º û.º t.£ ûe:ç :º e,]\n\n{t, º,u,nt ñ.t:c .ø,}\n\n[.º û.º d,ƒ‘ª,| t.£ ûe:ç :º ñ.nü ñ.ô, .ø.:n]\n\n\n\n\n         Meaning\n\n. - a\n, - e\n: - i\n‘ - o\n“ - u\n\nt - th\nƒ - f\nª - r\nº - s\n£ - t\nñ - m\nc - c\nø - g\nû - w\ne - h\nç - ch\nô - d\nn - n\nü - y\nu - v\nö - p\nd - b\ni - l\nê - sh\n* - k\næ - ng\n\n| - ,\n\n[] - sentance\n{} - title\n\n\n\n\n      Translation\n\nThe first mathic age.\n\nThat which is He made many, for the void and the emptiness below the void were His to command.\nThey were dust and He was as a sunbeam, defining at last their shape.\n\nAs was before, that which is He made again, with the sorrow of sunlight remaining untreated and unwelcome to the deep currents of the sea.\nSuch directionless is anathema to Him, who is linear.\n\nThese are to be His triumphs and His failings, many times over.\nThe second Mathic age\n\nThe fractions of that which is he are barred from entry.\nThe others have their devices of war and disunion, defining them as what they always have been, the caretakers of their fate.\n\nThey shout and plead and offer their voices to the heavens, but they are as the deep caves begging for sunlight.\nThe way is shut.\n\nThe third Mathic age\n\nA one tears free the blindfold of the abyss from the eyes of the parts.\nIt is as it was.\nThe light and lights and lighting of their shrines free their sight, that they may blind others.\n\nWar and disunion again suggests itself, and the line is made as an uncountable series of points, all in conflict with their fellows.\nAll wielding their law.\n\nThe Fourth Mathic Age\n\nTheir law is the power of the many.\nSlowly the lights are engraved with the symbols of the ensembles of the many.\nThe sights of new chains are familiar.\n\nThe Fifth Mathic Age\n\nThe fragments are made as together as was before, that which is he\nThe many choose their path, to the repetition as the sun rises and falls or to the breaking of it, the sun shall stand still.\n\nThe Sixth Mathic Age\n\nThe many become as one as was that which is he.\n\nThe Seventh Mathic Age\n\nAs was before, that which is he many made again.\n\nMathic Code\n\nCoppied and Decoded by Rosso\nVaerHaven\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Mauscript", "Count Lucius Tython", "I am Horen, the father of man, and most loved song of the Creator.", ""),
        StaticBook("Meat Cuts", "Jistuma", "\n\nBeef cuts:\n\nChunk (lower Neck)- Chuck Beef is located in the lower neck region of the animal. It is by far the best cut to be used in a stew or to be ground due  to its large amount of connective tissue\nBeef Rib (Ribs) – The Beef rib is part of the rib cage of the animal. It contains prime cuts such as the prime rib, as well as rib-eye. So far the best cooking methods I have found are cooking it on a grill or in an oven because of its tender nature.\nBrisket (lower front, underneath Chunk) – Brisket is another tough cut of beed, it is located directly under Chuck. It is best prepared as a pot roast, or covered in spices and smoked.\nPlate (Short Ribs) –The Plate is the part where both Short Ribs and Skirt Steak are made. Usually good for braising, they are also great if marinated and grilled. This cut also contains a lot of cartilage and care needs to be made cutting it.\nShank (Legs) – Shanks derive from the upper left portion of the animal. It is tough and not very edible unless braised and slow cooked.\nShort Loin (behind the Ribs)  – The Short Loin is a piece that is usually cut into steaks. It contains the Porterhouse, Tenderloin, and Strip Steak. All of them seem to be really tender and are derived from the rear part of the ribs.\nFlank (Utter Area)  – Flank is usually marinated before cooking and its preparation usually includes marinating and careful cooking. This cut is found near the utter and usually best when ground.\nRound (Rear end)  – Round is usually a tougher, but leaner piece of meat. It is best using moist heating methods such as stewing or pot roast. This part of the animal is where cubed meat traditionally derives from.\n\n\nPork Cuts\n\nPork Butt (shoulder)  – Despite the /unfortunate/ name, the Pork Butt is actually the upper shoulder. Although it is great for braising, it can also be made into sausages. Right above this is a fatty layer great for making lard.\nShoulder (Above the legs)  – The Shoulder is a pretty self explanatory name. This is another tough cut of meat however I find it good in making sausages as well. However it is usually cured or smoked.\nPork Loin (Ribcage)  – This part of the pig is mostly ribcage. It can be cut into Tenderloin located in the back and the whole Loin can be roasted or chopped into individual pieces. Like the Butt the Loin also has a fatty layer above for making lard.\nHam (actual Butt)  – The Ham is the part of the pig which is a deceptive name. This cut comes from the back of the pig and is saved for salted meats, and smoked ham. In addition the bottom portion, the Ham hock can be braised as opposed to curing.\nSide (Bottom)  – The side of a pig is where many can enjoy the simple breakfast treat of bacon. As well this part can be cut into steaks o made into pancetta, this is by far one of the tastiest parts of a pig and it takes much effort to avoid eating.\nSpare Rib (Under the Loin)  – The Spare rib is located where the breast connects to the ribs, it can be prepared by braising, slow cooking, or even grilling.\nJowl (Head)  – The Jowl is mostly used for sausages, as with any area this part is located. However it is not uncommon to see this part cured. Although not common it has been done.\nMeat Cuts\n\nUnknown author\nCoppied by Rosso\nCloud Temple of Anthos.", ""),
        StaticBook("Meat Cuts", "Lilliana", "Beef cuts:\n\nChunk (Lower Neck)- Chunk Beef is located in the lower neck region of the animal. It is by far the best cut to be used in a stew or to be ground due to its large amount of connective tissue.\n\nBeef Rib (Ribs)- The Beef rib is part of the rib cage of the animal. It contains prime cuts such as the prime rib, as well as rib-eye. So far the best cooking methods I have found are cooking it on a grill or in an oven because of its tender nature.\n\n\nBrisket (lower front, underneath Chunk)- Brisket is another tough cut of beef, it is located directly under Chunk. It is best prepared as a pot roast, or covered in spices and smoked.\nPlate (Short Ribs)- The Plate is the part where both Short Ribs and Skirt Steak are made. Usually good for braising, they are also great if marinated and grilled. This cut also contains a lot of cartilage and care need to be made cutting it.\n\nShank (Legs)- Shanks derive from the upper left portion of the animal. It is tough and not very edible unless braised and slow cooked.\n\nShort Loin (behind the Ribs)- The Short Loin is a piece that is usually cut into steaks. It contains the Porterhouse, Tenderloin and Strip Steak. All of them seem to be really tender and are derived from the rear part of the ribs.\n\nFlank (Utter Area)- Flank is usually marinated before cooking and its preparation usually includes marinating and careful cooking. This cut is found near the utter and usually best when ground.\n\nRound (Rear end)- Round is usually a tougher, but leaner piece of meat. It is best using moist heating methods such as stewing or pot roast. This part of the animal is where cubed meat traditionally derives from.\nPork Cuts:\n\nPork Butt (shoulder)- Despite the /unfortunate/ name, the Port Butt is actually the upper shoulder. Although it is great for braising, it can also be made into sausages. Right above this is a fatty layer great for making lard.\n\nShoulder (Above the legs)- The Shoulder is pretty self explanatory name. This is another tough cut of meat however I find it good in making sausages as well. However it is usually cured or smoked.\n\n\nPork Loin (Rib Cage)- This part of the pig is mostly ribcage. It can be cut into Tenderloin located in the back and whole Loin can be roasted or chopped into individual pieces. Like the Butt the Loin also has a fatty layer above for making lard.\nHam (actual Butt)- The Ham is the part of the pig which is a deceptive name. This cut comes from the back of the pig and is saved for salted meats, and smoked ham. In addition the bottom portion, the Ham hock can be braised as opposed to curing.\nSide (Bottom)- The side of a pig where many can enjoy the simple breakfast treat of bacon. As well this part can be cut into steaks made into pancetta, this s by far one of the tastiest parts of a pig and it takes much effort to avoid eating.\nSpare Rib (Under the Loin)- The Spare rib is located where the breast connects to the ribs, it can be prepared by braising, slow cooking, or even grilling.\nJowl (Head)- The Jowl is mostly used for sausages, as with any area this part is located. However it is not uncommon to see this part cured. Although not common it has been done.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Medical Std. V1", "The Scribe.", "A Study of Medicine. \nPossibly Volume 1,\nAnatomy.\n\nAssisted by Salazar the Mali'Aheral.\n'One learns to figure out the limits of one's body as one travels.' ~Salazar.\n\nThe cadavers of less fortunate travelers make for good study; \nTheir bodies can be found suffering from illnesses or ailments,\nand studying them was an urge that could not be repressed. \n\n\n\nChapter 1: Circulation of Blood.\n\nThe circulatory system runs throughout the body. It provides blood to various muscles and organs, replenishing them with every beat of the heart. Blood provides Anima to the body, which is the life\nforce that drives us all, and gives us energy. Anima may possibly contain a variety of elements that we require to function in life. As one works more strenuously, the circulatory system does as well. This may be because as the body\nuses energy, it requires more anima to function at the previous levels. \nAilments of the Circulatory System;\n\nA disease of the circulatory system causes the blood to not coagulate where it may normally do so. This causes the patient afflicted with such a disorder to bleed profusely, until magic is introduced.\nThis disease is highly rare.\n\nAnother disease is caused by the blood vessels themselves being left open to the air or to water for a long time. This causes blood-poisoning, an infection of the blood itself.\nThis can be treated a variety of ways, though none of which are easy. Amputation is the most rudimentary form of this. The next is quick cleansing of the wound with pure water and alchemically based healing potions. The third is to use clerical magic, but\nsuch can be considered a panacea.\n\nThis condition is uncommon, as most open wounds are wrapped. \n\n\nChapter Two; Digestive.\n\nThe digestive system consists of the stomach, smaller intestine, large intestive, and the excrementory system. \n\nThe stomach contains within it a large amount of naturally produced\nacid which dissolves food that is consumed through the mouth. After the food is broken down, it seems to be passed through both intestines, where it is absorbed into the body. Of course, these broken down materials most likely filter into the blood system\nwhere the blood and the body converts the food to anima. This is why death by starvation can take place. However, the body itself can overstock these necessary nutrients, and cause fatness and obesity. \nDigestive diseases. \n\nThe stomach acid can become too concentrated and can break down the lining of the stomach. This can cause severe pain. The most common cure for this is the direct consumption of tippens root, as it brings the pH of \nthe stomach acid down to natural levels. \n\nAnother digestive issue is an illness that causes constant vomiting, or constant bowel movements. While vomitting is the bodies natural defense against bad food, this vomit is \nvery common even with high quality food. The best way to deal with this sickness is to consume mass quantites of salted water, as such replenishes the bodies well-being after their body exterts it. Sugar should also be added to the water.\nThis disease can be common or rare depending on its severity. \n\nOne may also vomit if they exert themselves to a point of exhaustion.\nChapter 3; Respiratory.\n\nThe Respiratory system contains the lungs and the windpipe. The windpipe is located behind the thick protective cover at the front of the throat. The lungs suction air from the environment\ninto them through use of a muscle underneath the lungs called the diaphragm. The lungs expand to hold the inhaled air, and then the air is pushed out. However, the body somehow utilizes the gas, most likely to produce more efficient anima.\nIf blood is deprived of the air, it turns purple and sickly looking. When re-exposed to the air, it turns bright red. My belief is that blood uses one of the gases that comprises air to convert nutrients into anima.\nBetween both of our knowledge, no diseases or ailments of the respiratory system were known, but the respiratory system can be damaged through the inhalation of coal fumes, causing 'Black-Breath'. This cannot be cured except by \nthe magic of the monks.\nChapter 4; Muscular.\n\nThe muscles are connected to the bones by tendons, stretchy bits of yellow 'flesh' that allows for the full range of motion. The muscles of the body tend to work in groups of two, one to push, and the other to pull.\nThe larger the muscle, the greater the force exerted by the movement of the muscle. As muscles are damaged through use, they heal and grow larger, thusly more powerful. This is why one is sore after strenuous exercize.\nNo ailment of the muscle is cureable. The degrading of muscle is either through use of magic, or by some ailment. Both of these result in the eventual loss of the majority of muscle.\nThere appear to be three types of muscle, involuntary, mixed, and voluntary. Involuntary muscles can not be controlled at all, such as the heart or the digestive muscles. \n\nMixed can be controlled some of the time, but for the \nmost part, operate on their own. This includes breathing, blinking, and swallowing. \n\nVoluntary muscles are only able to be used with input, such as the arms or the legs.\nChapter Five; Bones.\n\nThe bones are the structure of the body. They also offer protection. Bones are incredibly dense, most are stronger than steel. The amount of bones in a body is 206, with six tiny, tiny bones in the ears that some\nhow are linked directly to balance. The bones can heal themselves if set correctly, though clerical magic allows this process to finish faster.\nThere are no known ailments of the bone.\nAddendums; \nBruises are the pooling of blood from broken blood vessels.\n\nThe heart is an involuntary muscle.\n\nThe circulatory system involves vessels that take blood to the heart to replenish in anima,\nand arteries that take anima-enriched blood away from the heart to the muscles and other parts of the body.", ""),
        StaticBook("Melunis the Mad", "Sofetios", "   Melunis the Mad\n\n          ~By Sofetios\n               Jayamen\nI was travelling to a place called Geedin in search of a philosopher called Melunis. This man was said to be the wisest in the land, according to my friend who went there a while back. So I figured I would see what wisdom he could offer me. \nI arrived in the town and walked up to a guard. I said,\n\n“Excuse me sir, could you direct me to the man named Melunis?” The guard just laughed and said,\n\n“The only Melunis I know is a madman, you can find him over by the alley.” I thought to myself, this madman couldn’t be the one that my friend was talking about. My friend was a very smart person and wouldn’t confuse a wise man with a madman. \nSo to satisfy my knowing of whether or not this was the correct man, I went to the alleyway and saw an old man in rags. I said to the man.\n\n“Are you the man known as Melunis?” to which he looked at me, smiled, and said,\n\n“Indeed so! How can I help you this fine day!” The day was overcast, and cold. I couldn’t see how it was fine.\n\n“Would you happen to know my friend, James Linson?”  The man looked overjoyed at the name and said,\n\n“Why yes! I remember the lad. I do hope he is doing well.”\n\n“Well he told me you were the wisest in the land…” I said, to which Melunis replied with a frown,\n\n“Wise? No I’m not wise. I am just a ragged old man in an alleyway. Do I look like I’m Wise?”\n\n“No you don’t.” I said. “You don’t look wise at all.” The man then got a sparkle in his eyes.\n\n”Not wise at all?” said the man. “Well I must agree. You being very well off must know what it means to be wise, as you are in a far better place then I.” The man thought for a second, then said, “Tell me, what does it mean to be wise.”\n“Well it means that you know a lot about things.” I said, “Like a smith knows how to smith well, he is wise in smithing.”\n\n“Ah..” said the man. “ So in a sense, being wise is having knowledge.”\n\n“Yes.” I said.\n\n“ Alright, well what is knowledge.” Said Melunis. “As in how would I be able to obtain it to become more wise.” I thought for a second, then said,\n\n“Knowledge is knowing something, and obtained by learning.”\n\n“I see!” Said the man. “But… how do I know I know something.” At this I was a bit perplexed. I said,\n\n“Could you explain what you mean?” The man nodded and said,\n\n“In other words, How do you know what you know is true.”\n\n“Because what you know it works when applied to things.” I said.\n\n“Alright.” Said the man. “So knowledge is what you know works when applied to things, but how does that make it true?”\n\n“What do you mean?” I said.\n\n“I mean for example, we could see an abstract painting.” Said Melunis. “You could think it’s one thing, I could think its another. Both works when applied, at least to the individual, but who is right?”\n“I guess we would both be right.” I said. “It would be up to the individual.”\n\n“Ah I see now!” Said the man. “So knowledge is what works with the individual. But still, how do we know it is true? Because isn’t the truth universal?” \n I began to get frustrated. But… Then I came to a realization.\n\n“I guess the truth is up to the person.” The man looked at me and said,\n\n“So there is no real truth? But what about common knowledge?” I thought for a second and said,\n\n“The common knowledge is what people have a common belief in.” Melunis nodded, seeming to understand.\n\n“But then what is the real truth?” The man said.\n“The real truth…” I began, “The real truth is… unknowable. Even if we got to it, people still would have different opinions. And those opinions might have more people believing in it than the real truth.” Melunis smiled at me and said,\n“Thank you, I think I understand now what it means to have knowledge.”\n\nSoon after this conversation, I left. Slightly confused, but more understanding. \n Though now I realized what my friend meant by him being the wisest in the land.\n\nThe End\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this!\nI'll be writing more in the future!\nIf you like my writing, Send me a bird or tell me in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))", ""),
