ge.”\n\n“Yes.” I said.\n“ Alright, well what is knowledge.” Said Melunis. “As in how would I be able to obtain it to become more wise.” I thought for a second, then said,\n\n“Knowledge is knowing something, and obtained by learning.”\n“I see!” Said the man. “But… how do I know I know something.” At this I was a bit perplexed. I said,\n\n“Could you explain what you mean?” The man nodded and said,\n“In other words, How do you know what you know is true.”\n\n“Because you know it works when applied to things.” I said.\n“Alright.” Said the man. “So knowledge is what you know works when applied to things, but how does that make it true?”\n\n“What do you mean?” I said.\n\n“I mean for example, we could see an abstract painting.” Said Melunis. “You could think it’s one thing, I could think its another. Both works when applied, at least to the individual, but who is right?”\n“I guess we would both be right.” I said. “It would be up to the individual.”\n\n“Ah I see now!” Said the man. “So knowledge is what works with the individual. But still, how do we know it is true? Because isn’t the truth universal?”\n I began to get frustrated. But… Then I came to a realization.\n\n“I guess the truth is up to the person.” The man looked at me and said,\n“So there is no real truth? But what about common knowledge?” I thought for a second and said,\n\n“The common knowledge is what people have a common belief in.” Melunis nodded, seeming to understand.\n“But then what is the real truth?” The man said.\n“The real truth…” I began, “The real truth is… unknowable. Even if we got to it, people still would have different opinions. And those opinions might have more people believing in it than the real truth.” Melunis smiled at me and said,\n“Thank you, I think I understand now what it means to have knowledge.”\n\nSoon after this conversation, I left. Slightly confused, but more understanding.\n Though now I realized what my friend meant by him being the wisest in the land.\n\nThe End\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please tell me by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
        StaticBook("Memories", "Damien Menrow", "Memories...memories....memories....\n\nIn this world you tired,\nnot leaving me alone...behind...\n\nThere's no other way,\nI'll pray to the gods,\n\"Let him stay.\"\nThe memories ease the pain inside,\nnow I know why!\n\nAll of my memories keep you near,\nin silent moments I imagine you'd be near.\n\nAll of my memories keep you near,\nthe silent whispers,\nsilent tears...\nMade me promise I'd try,\nto find my way back in this life.\n\nI hope there is a way,\nto give me a sign you're okay.\n\nReminds me again it's worth it all,\nso I can go home.\nAll of my memories keep you near,\nin silent moments I imagine you'd be near.\n\nAll of my memories keep you near,\nthe silent whispers,\nsilent tears...\nTogether in all these memories,\nI see your smile.\n\nAll the memories I hold dear,\nDarling you know I'll love you till the end of time!\nAll of my memories keep you near,\nin silent moments I imagine you'd be near.\n\nAll of my memories keep you near,\nin silent whispers,\nsilent tears.\n\nAll of my Memories..*", ""),
        StaticBook("Memory Release", "Sofetios Jayamen", " The Memory Release\n\n             ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n\nIt was the day before the celebration,\nAnd everyone was shopping in stores,\nGetting ready for the holiday,\nAnd doing holiday chores.\n\nRudgar Smitkin,\nWas wondering around,\nHe hated this holiday,\nAnd everything to be found.\n\nHe was walking by a store,\nNear the very edge of town,\nOver in that corner,\nThere wasn’t a soul to be found.\n\nHe walked in the store,\nAnd said “What’s the point!”,\n“The store has not had a sale in weeks,\nMaybe I should just get rid of this joint.”\n\nAll of the sudden,\nThrough the window he did peer,\nAnd saw a boy walking by,\nWho was all full of cheer.\n\nThe boy walked in the shop and said,\n“Good holiday to you sir,\nI brought you a present.\nI know you’ll like it for sure!”\n\nRudgar then said,\n“Good holiday eh?\nGet out of my shop,\nUnless you want to pay!”\n\nThe boy was taken aback and said,\n“But this present is for you,\nI’ve seen you here day in and out,\nJust tapping your shoe!”\n\n“I hate the Holiday!”\nOld Rudgar did shout.\nThen the boy said,\n“Well I’ll tell you what it’s all about!”\n\n“Bah!” said Rudgar,\n“The Holiday is a sham!\nFrom the roast beef,\nRight down to the Ham!”\n\n“So you do know about the Holiday!”\nThe boy said aloud.\nBut of this discovery,\nRudgar wasn’t proud.\n\nSmitkin said,\n“That was long ago,\nAnd if I’ll have that time again,\nI will surely never know.\n\n“Well what did happen back then?”\nSaid the boy to the old guy.\n“I don’t have time for this!”\nAnd then Rudgar did sigh.\n\n“You’re not going to leave.”\nRudgar said with a frown.\nSo he pulled out a chair,\nAnd then he sat down.\n\n“It happened long ago,\nWhen I was a lad.\nOn that Holiday night.\nIs the day I became forever sad.”\n\n“Everything was going merrily.\nWhy I had never felt so free!\nBut I didn’t know,\nWhat was to be.”\n\n“That is when it happened,\nThat holiday night,\nThe night when it broke,\nAnd Gave myself a sight.”\n\n“I was sitting near midnight,\nNext to the holiday vase.\nWhen I tried to get up,\nAt a hurried pace.”\n\n“I tripped,\nAnd I fell on the ground,.\nAnd the vase on it’s stand,\nBroke where it was found.”\n\n“My mother said it was ok,\nBut that was her and not me,\nFor I knew that undoing this night,\nNever could be.”\n\n“I loved that vase,\nAnd also everyone I did see,\nOnly to be,\nBroken by me.”\n\nThen Rudgar,\nTurned around.\nBut where was the boy?\nHe wasn’t anywhere to be found.\n\nThere was only,\nThe present on the floor.\n“I guess my story,\nMust have been a bore.”\n\nHe picked up the present,\nAnd was about to wipe down a case.\nWhen he opened the present up,\nAnd in it… was a replicate of the vase.\n\nOld Rudgar ran to the door,\nAnd as he feared.\nThe boy he had met\nHad dissapeared.\n\nHe looked down,\nAnd what did he see in the snow?\nTwo footprints,\nAnd there were no more that would show.\n\nSo old man Rudgar,\nWent back in his shop,\nNot knowing what to think,\nNot knowing how to stop.\n\nEyes full of tears,\nAnd knowing what he was to say was right.\nHe said, “Good holiday to all,\nAnd to all a good night!”\nTHE END\n((This story is actually a christmas one that I wrote a while back. Made to be suitable for LotC.))\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))\n\nIf you were not sold this book by Sofetios Jayamen, it was done without his permission.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mental Barriers", "Thurak", "Rescribed by the Farseer Thurak, 10th of Sun's Smile, 1484.\nRecognising the Backgrounds of Mental Barriers\n\n1. Birthright\n\n2. Learnt- Emotional\n\n3. Learnt- Intellect\n\n4. Self Aware.\nWhether conscious of the fact or not, all who are of mental capacity for independent thought operate with mental barriers. An avoidance of certain foods, sentimentalities for or against an individual based on previous experiences, a shudder at rain \ndropped upon the bridge of the nose. Whether they serve a point or not is beside the point, but all with functioning minds should also have functioning mental barriers. Granted, some are more useful than others, but none should be ignored.\nConsideration One\n\nWhile minds are capable of being moulded, as in the case of a particular experiment where a disadvantaged goblin child is given the intellectual upbringing of a noble elven child (For scientific experiments only) \nand it is observed that the matter of its circumstance of birth played little consequence in its mental abilities after the experiments completion; however there is equal quantity of proof to suggest the opposite; that particulars\nof birth can suggest better or worse mental ability. So if all with mental function possess mental barriers at some level, then it can also be agreed that some individuals may have naturally more intentional barriers resulting from the \nparticulars of their birth.\nConsideration Two\n\nEmotional experiences are an inevitable by-product of experiencing the company of others. What is not certain is whether the usefulness of such can be realised. It has been observed through various \ntrials in which test subjects experienced the same situation that a similar emotional outcome in each subject came forth. The results were conclusive that the female subjects were more capable of becoming emotionally guarded against a similar circumstance\nwhen the same tests were repeated (and with more definitive results in the older subjects). The outcome of said experiments showed that aged women are more capable of producing emotional based barriers than are young men. Of course this\ndoes not mean it is only the aged woman’s’ game to create emotional barriers, but that their past experiences before commencing tests rendered them more prepared to react in an emotionally defensive manner.\nBut whether young or old, and possessing of either of the genitalia, emotionally driven mental barriers prove to be an easily formed barrier that many people demonstrate. It is the experience that heat means danger, or that a wanting\nlook from the opposite sex means trouble. And such learning becomes the basis of these most simple barriers with the intent to better our future course from further danger.\nConsideration Three \n\nLearning needs by necessity of adapted barriers (the terminology “Adapted” used to refer to any barriers that are reactive in nature) is not limited to that of emotional education, though \nit is certainly the easier and more likely to occur in individuals who have no conscious recognition of mental barriers than those of the next type: intellectual . Mentally capable individuals, typically of a higher learning, are more aware of\nof themselves and the commencement and termination of their own thoughts, such that they might be able to deduce that the outcomes of something will be of ill consequence, and thus establish an intellectual barrier against \nit without requiring the emotional experience of it to draw such a conclusion. Anyone with such an ability would be considered of a more valuable intellectual worth than those who remain unconscious of their circumstance until they experience\nit first hand, because they demonstrate a conscious control over adapted barriers,, knowing the beginnings of danger before the danger is present. As is the results of tests among children in which one observes another \nexperiencing pain and assumes the outcome of the test implement used will result in pain, without having to experience it for themselves. Individuals with intellectual adapted barriers can become conscious of a problem before the problem manifests, and \nthus one who intends to expose them to mental danger will find their defence against it pre-emptive from the first.\nConsideration Four\n\nThe last is definitively the most complicated. Tests on such subjects still remain inconclusive as the data is often to broad an array to speculate commonalities; suggesting that great caution be taken in anticipating\nthe adaptive and individualistic reaction to provocation. \nIf the proposed individual be of such mental awareness as to acknowledge their own thoughts and recognise the thoughts of others within their own murmurings, then any mental intrusion will be \ndetected from the first by these clever adaptive barriers that prevent further intrusion of outside stimuli.\nUnderstanding such mental barriers can only be done on an individual basis, for without \nforeknowledge of the mental, emotional and experiential past of the individual, one has no way of interoperating or supposing their reaction. And no one route will have conclusive success in all cases.\nThe Boldest Conclusion\n\nIf, in any of the previous cases and most cunning of the latter one, an individual were to acknowledge their own conscious existence with relation to a broader knowledge of the universe \nand/or deities, and at the same time comprehends themselves in reference to The Void, then their mental resoluteness may account for intentional barriers of not just emotional and intellectual stimuli, but of all mental stimuli.\nSuch an individual is capable of constructing a deliberate blockade that utilises their own experiences and acknowledging their own experiential or anticipated reaction, mould it into that will require a specifically, and equally intentional, attempt to \nremove it. Of anticipating and overcoming any barriers that the source of which that has been suggested in the previous pages, this book will do no work towards. For the effort to remove a barrier is to discredit the worth\nof it, while the intent of this book has been to acknowledge and more clearly define the circumstance from which mental barriers may have been constructed. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Mental Barriers", "Elindor", "Recognising the backgrounds of\nMental Barriers\n\n1: Birthright\n\n2: Learnt: Emotional\n\n3: Learnt: Intellect\n\n4: Self Aware\nWhether conscious of the fact or not, all who are of mental capacity for independant thought operate with mental barriers. An avoidance of certain foods, sentimentalities for or against an individual based on previous experience, a shudder at rain dropped\nupon the bridge of the nose. Whether they serve a purpose or not is beside the point, but all with functioning minds also have functioning mental barriers.\nGranted, some are more useful than others, but none should be ignored.\nConsideration 1:\nWhile minds are capable of being moulded, as is the case of a particular experiment wherein a disadvantaged goblin child is given the intellectual upbringing of a noble elven child (for scientific purposes only) and it is observed \nthat the matter of its circumstance of birth played little consequence in its mental abilities after the experiment's completion; however there is equal quantity of proof to suggest the opposite: that particulars of birth can suggest better or \nworse mental ability. So if all with basic mental function posess mental barriers at some level, then it can also be agreed upon that some individuals may have naturally more intentional barriers resulting from the particulars of their birth.\nConsideration 2:\nEmotional experiences are an inevitable by-product of experiencing the company of others. What is not certain is whether the usefulness of such can be realised.\nIt has been observed through various trials \nin which test subjects experienced the same situation that arised a similar emotional outcome in each subject. The results were conclusive that the female subjects were more capable of becoming emotionally guarded against a similar circumstance \nwhen the same tests were repeated (and with more difinitive results in the older subjects). The outcome of said experiments showed that aged women are more capable of producing emotional based mental barriers than are young men.\nOf course this does not mean that it is only the aged woman's game to create emotional barriers, but that their past experiences before commencing tests rendered them more prepared to react in an emotionally defensive manner.\nBut whether young or old, and\nposesssing either of the genitalia, emotionally driven mental barriers prove to be an easily formed barrier that many people demonstrate. It is the experience that heat means danger, or that a wanting look from the opposite sex means trouble. And\nsuch learning becomes the basis of these most simple barriers with the intent to better our future course from further danger.\nConsideration 3:\nLearning needs by neccessity of adapted barriers (the terminology \"adapted\" used to refer to any barriers that are reactive in nature) is not limited to that of emotional education, though it is certainly the easier and more likely to\noccur in individuals who have no conscious recognition of mental barriers than those of the next type: intellectual.\nMentally capable individuals, typically of a higher learning, are more aware of themselves and the commencement and termination of their \nown thoughts, such that they might be able to deduce that the outcome of something will be of ill consequence, and thus establish an intellectual barrier against it without requiring the emotional experience of it to draw such a conclusion.\nAnyone with such ability would be concidered of a more valuable intellectual worth than those who remain unconscious to their circumstance until they experience it first hand, because they demonstrate a conscious control over adapted barriers, knowing \nthe beginnings of danger before the danger is present. As is the results of tests among children in which one observes another experiencing pain and assumes that the outcome of the test implement used will result in pain, without having to experience it \nfor themselves.\nIndividuals with intellectual adapted barriers can become conscious of a problem before the problem manifests, and thus one who intends to expose them to mental danger will find their defense against it pre-emptive from the first.\nConsideration 4:\nThe last is difinitively the most complicated. Tests on such subjects still remain inconclusive as the data is of too broad an array to speculate commonalities; supposing that great caution be taken in anticipating this adaptive and \nindividualistic reaction to provocation.\nIf the proposed individual be of such mental self awareness as to acknowledge their own thoughts and recognise the thoughts of others within their own murmurings, then any mental intrusion will be detected from the\nfirst by these clever adaptive barriers that prevent further intrusion of outside stimuli.\nUnderstanding such mental barriers can only be done on an individual basis, for without foreknowledge of the mental, emotional and experiential past of\nthe individual, one has no way of interpreting or supposing their reaction. And no one route will have conclusive success in all cases.\nThe boldest conclusion:\nIf, in any of the previous cases and most cunning in the latter one, an individual were to acknowledge their own conscious existence with relation to a broader knowledge of the universe and/or deity(s), and at the\nsame time comprehends themselves in reference to The Void, then their mental resoluteness may account for intentional barriers of not just emotional and intellectual stimuli, but of all mental stimuli. Such an individual is capable of constructing a \ndeliberate blockade that utilises their own erperience(s), and acknowledging their experiential or anticipated reaction, mould it into a barrier that will require a specifically, and equally intentional, attempt to remove it.\nOf anticipating and overcoming any of the barriers that the source of which has been suggested in the previous pages, this book will do no work towards. For the efforts of removing a barrier is to discredit the worth of it, while the intent of this book \nhas been to acknowledge and more clearly define the circumstance from which mental barriers may have been constructed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mental Magic", "Caedwen Camoryn", "\n\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n              Mental\n              Magic\n=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=\n\n\n\n              >(O)<\nTelepathy\n---\nCommunicating with the will of one’s mind. Not as simple as it seems, though an easier task for matured mental mages. One can accomplish such a goal by envisioning their voice in their own mind while being connected to another’s mind.\nSound simple? Maybe if you speak mentally to yourself on occasion. Though on this spell, one has to hear themself speak; their voice ringing through their own mind, while a mental mage has their connection, or bridge made to another.\nBasic Illusion\n---\nA mental mage can create simple, though effective chimes in another’s mind. Though when working and dealing with basic illusions with another’s mind, it’s a good idea to use your surroundings to your advantage. Now, with saying this,\none can achieve this by acting in a similar way of how telepathy works. In this, a simple effect can be made. A sound, a voice, a certain feeling as if someone was poking the victim. What the caster uses is entirely up to their imagination. With practice,\nthis spell can harness great potential. Though knowing of the effect of the spell is the first and most important step of it. Environment and effect. Two main things to study and master with this spell.\nMental Barriers\n---\nA simple way to say what a barrier is, or what a Mental Mage does. One does not connect to the void in order to counter another mage from entry. You simply cast a barrage of visions, or memories that would startle them.\nEven something created right from your mind, as described in this book. You have to envision what you’re trying to counter them with. Make it vivid, flowing with life. Always keep your focus. Clarity is key.\nAdvanced Illusion\n---\nTo move above just the sense of hearing or smelling for say, this will be based on the sensing of feeling; fooling another’s mind into thinking they’re being touched and even when going further into casting illusions, make another\nsee things. As with what has been said previous, it’s all about focus, and envisioning what you want. As for making another see what you want to fool them with, you must first know what you’re trying to make appear in their mind. Therefore, the more\ndetail you envision, the better. Even putting the physical traits of that item into thought is needed.\nNotes on Mind Melding\n---\nThe mind is totally open in most cases when one sleeps. It allows those of us whom know how to tap into their mind to manipulate even their dreams. You can literally use their mind as a building block on what you want them to\nhear, feel, and sense within their dreams. For the good, or bad. As one sleeps and you enter their mind, you’d most likely be met with little to no resistance. Focus and time are needed to feel out what the other is dreaming, or thinking of while\nsleeping. Once you have an understanding of ‘how’ to find ones thoughts as they dream, you can move to the next step, infiltrating and altering what they percieve within their head. If you can remeber the way of melding your mind to another's, the same\npractice is to be used while entering their dreams. After that’s done and you’re stationed in their mind, you can slowly start to bend what they sense.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mental Sense", "Elindor", "\nMental Sensitivity\n\nA short depiction of such things and how one may endeavour to experience it.\n\nFeaturing an example.\nOur senses are really only the beginning of perception. The simpleton perceives mostly with vision and uses little else of their other senses, and few actually recognise a higher perceptive nature within themselves. But be not fooled by the majority, for \nthe mind shall see far more than what comes before the eyes, if one can but forfeit their pre-conceived ideas that only the seen and experienced can exist, as well as pinpoint their place within an almighty expanse of universes in which knowledge is\nbut a fraction of the truth, and then dive into the opportunities for them whose mind is self actualised, and whose existence is both infinitely small, and omnipotently all.\nLet us embark upon this journey outside of the material things. Let us learn to apprecciate the placement of all things within the universe and begin perceiving without the senses.\nLet us discover ourselves amidst a world that exists just beyond our eyes.\nTo begin with. Ignore your senses. Block out the information that your eyes, ears and skin are attempting to bombard you with at all times. Discover within yourself that small safe place in which dwells just your thoughts, and your sense of self. \nSome people find this a confronting experience as the immediate outcome is an objective view of the being from which you spend all your time trapped within and, in being so, are unable to adequately examine. You may experience a sense of resentment \ninadequacy towards faults. This is not a bad thing, for it means that you have discovered your true nature without the inflated pride that usually skews this view. This is the first most important step towards understanding the world around from \na liberated consciousness. In trying to discover this place, do not allow yourself to slip into such blankness of thought that you discover The Void. Immunity from the sensory overload that is our waking existence is required for locating both \nones self and The Void that sits in wait behind our reality, yet that much emptiness of mind is not required. Thoughts are a good thing in this circumstance. Your thoughts are what will guide you to view yourself from without your eye sockets.\nNow, assuming that you have indeed come to realise yourself objectively; seen your existential being as it were, you may already havcommenced viewing our world from a new viewpoint. Let us take some time before continuing onwards to apprecciate all the \nwonders that surround us that we so eagerly allow to slip by under the weight of our own self importance. Let us aclimatise to this expanding of the mind before we carry on.\nHave we acclimatised?\n\nGood.\n\nNow. Again, block out all the sensory garbage. Cease seeing, feeling, smelling, hearing and tasting your surroundings and again return to the place of just you and your mind in the quiet.\nObviously you cannot also be reading this. So read this first, and then do it. Have you read the rest?\n\nOk, let us continue.\n\nFrom this place of mental freedom, now look beyond yourself. It may be of value to envision an \nappropriate illustration to better comprehend the surrounding reality. For the sake of clarity an example will be used to more accurately define what one is attempting to do.\nOnce you have discovered your place in the universe, allow the universe to\nreveal its place to you. This isn't supernatural druid bollocks but simply the easiest way to explain that you must oepn your mind enough to discover your surroundings for what they are and no longer what they look, feel and seem to be.\nThe Illustration Begins\nYou exist alone. There is nothing. But your eyes are closed, so how can you know that there is nothing? Breath. Allow your mind to drift, it knows what it is doing.\nNow open your mind's eye. See.\nWhat you beleived to be darkness is quite the opposite. For you exist surrounded by endless white. There is no distinctive end. You cannot perceive a difference between land or sky. You know yourself to be sitting, yet there is no surface below.\nNow examine more closely. What is it that takes shape? There is indeed a surface but it is not solid. You can feel it beneath you, perhaps even see it as your eyes focus. There are stones. Smooth stones that fit nicely in the palm. You might pick one up\nso that you can better examine. It seems too perfect to be real. You glance around and see that they are all too perfect. And with each returned glance there are more. Thousands. Countless. The endless white is as an ocean of small white stones.\nYou should look more closely now at the one in your hand, for a detail you missed before now seems more obvious. It is not perfectly smooth afterall, there is one small part that is slightly flatter. You gather another stone, there is an imperfection to\nits colouring. And another, with a small indentation like a dimple. Even as you look about at the stones far in the distance you become aware that they are not all alike. They are each their own, each in its right place, all together creating an expanse\nof white that stretches out beyond the recognition of sight. Further, even, beyond recognition of the mind. You hold tightly the first stone, with its flat side against the skin of your hand. An unexpected feeling as the awakening from a deep slumber \nbeckons you outward. You know not how to give in to it, so you clench your eyes and grip onto the small stone.\n\nBack in the room where you were meditating. All is still. But you feel an odd attraction to a candle upon the desk (or \nsome other inanimate object as is appropriate to your situation). Perhaps you reach out to touch it, though it does already feel like you have it within your grasp, and as your hand rises the thing appears to wobble. For a single instant. So easy to miss.\nConcluding Remarks Such is the beginning. Humble, as most are. \n\nThere is more in store, but self discovery is the only way towards progress from here. Understanding where and how one object exists can bloom into a working appreciation\nfor the placement of all things around. And an ability to realise when something is not in the way that it should. When a stone is overturned, misplaced, or unsettled by something new.\nThere are other sensitivites that are not just mental based. Sensing auras and magical disturbances have proven highly useful, as was the case with an old mage who had lost use of his sight and was able to perceive as though with eyes from sensing \nthe proximity of things emitting aura. \nNoticing magical disturbances is a bit more common, particularly for Void Mages in sensing when something has majorly shifted in the balance of Void energy.\nThe druids too have some connection to the right placement\nof nature from their communion with the aspects, which, in all relativity, is highly likely to operate similarly to the mental sensitivity suggested in the former pages.\nI will write no more upon the uses of such a thing, as any who struggle to see the usefulness therein are blinded by a love of their sight.\nIn fact, I will write no more at this time upon this subject at all.\n\n    ~ - | END | - ~", ""),
        StaticBook("Menu", "Zane Capette", "Menu:\n\nThe following items are sold by piece unless stated in next to the item.\n\nBread: .50 minas\nPancake: 1 mina\nApple Bread: 1 mina\nBanana Bread: 1 mina\nToast: 1.50 minas\nSandwich (meat): 2 minas\nCaramel Bread: 1 mina\nHoney Bread: 4 minas\nBanana Pancake: 1.50 minas\nChoco Pancake: 1 mina\nCookies: 20 for 5 minas\n\n  ~More will be added to the menu as it becomes available. Thank you for shopping with us!~", ""),
        StaticBook("Menu", "Evertlas Treebeard", "~*~*~*~*~*~*~^~*~*~*~*~*~*              The Dwarven\n                  Falcon\n\nEST. 1st Of The First Seed \n                  1480\n\n~*~*~*~*~*~*~^~*~*~*~*~*~*\n\n         ~ * ~ Menu ~ * ~\n \n*Salted Foods 1 Mina More*\n\n*All Soups & Stews 3 Mina*\n\nBread: .5 Mina\n\nCarrots: .5 Mina {Each}\n\nBaked Potatoes: 1 Mina {Each} \n\nCooked Chicken: 2 Mina\n\nGrass Fed Beef:   5 Mina\n\nFresh Fish:    3 Mina\n\nFresh Salmon: 8 Mina\n\nPlain Cookie: 1 Mina\n\nChocolate Cookie: 3 Mina\n\nCaramel Cookie: 1 Mina\n\nFruit Cookie: 3 Mina\n\n*All Pies 4 Mina*\n\nCake: 6 Mina\n\nPork: 4 Mina\n\n~~++ { Golden Food } ++~~\nCarrot: 10 Mina\nApple: 25 Mina", ""),
        StaticBook("Merchant Pack: A", "Ben Avice", "   Merchant Package: A\n\n5x Rocks of Obsidian\n1 Rock = 20-25 minas\n\n10x Ferrum Ingot\n1 Ingot = 10-15 Minas\n\n2x Bundle of Wheat\n1 Bundle = 10 minas\n\n2x Bundle of Spruce Logs\n1 Bundle = 45 Minas\nReturn in two saint days and bring the minas/leftover supplies. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Merchant Pack: T", "Ben Avice", "  Merchant Package: Trials\n\n5x Ferrum Ingot\n1 Ingot = 10-15 minas\n\n1x Bundle of Logs\n1 Bundle = 40-50 minas\n\n1x Rock of Obsidian\n1 Rock = 20-25 Minas\n\n1x Bundle of Wheat\n1 Bundle = 10-15 Minas\nAll prices with a range are haggleable. Start at a higher price for better returns.\n\nReturn in two saints days with all the leftover supplies and minas earned. Your prowess will be evaluated then and you will earn your handbook.\n\nGet to selling!", ""),
        StaticBook("Merchant's Hand.", "Ben Avice", "   Merchant's Handbook\n\nThis handbook contains information on your duties that should be carried out weekly, and the merchant's agreement.\n\nSome duties do not apply every week. (I.e, collecting debts/taxes.)\n          Duties:\n1. Sell wares supplied by the company.\n2. Travel to settlements & speak with business owners.\n- Ask them where they get their supply\n- Ask about becoming an affiliate with us\n- Ask if they need a loan\n- Ask if they are insured (theft, \narson, burglary, failure to pay taxes, animal slaughter, and life insurance)\n3. Collect Taxes\n4. Collect Debts\n5. Recruit other members\n- You will be given a 100 minas bonus everytime you bring in a new member that passes their trials.\n\n6. Operate a store thats owned by the company.\n\nYour pay will be determined by the execution of these duties, bonuses when perfoming well, and bonuses when coming along on company missions.\n  Merchant's Agreement\n\n This serves as rules for the company. Breaking these rules will have proper punishment that follows.\n\n1. Swear to never steal from the company.\n2. Swear to never leak secrets or slander the company. \n\n3. Never use your soulstone on company missions. (Will let you know)\n4. Never use armor better than leather on company missions (Will let you know)\n\n\nAny questions or concerns about the handbook or the company itself may be directed to either Ben Avice ((benben582)) or Mithius Dalma ((jonhan2)). \n\nThere will be more to being a merchant than just these things, all of our services are listed on the \nflyer. ((forum post.))\n\nAs a merchant you are the backbone of the company and we are honored to have you with us. Get out there and get to selling!", ""),
        StaticBook("Merriment a Poem", "Giliath Haldar", "Walking home from the days toil,\nthrough the towns trodden soil.\nCome up to the doors and push aside,\nyour troubles with a smile wide.\n\nCall the girls, call the men,\ngather them so we can then.\nshow this town that fun can vary,\neat drink and let's be merry.\n\nGrab a mug with a sounding cry,\nraise it, raise it to the sky.\nTonight we shall drink our fill,\ntonight we shall be merry still!\nWritten by:\n-Giliath Haldar\n\n((Razorshawl))", ""),
        StaticBook("mfbleh", "Marvin", "An old elf, she sits on her chair,\n\nA woman, her name Violet Blair.\n\nTo me she recounts many a tale,\n\nA land many years away we had sailed\n\nA story of love long\nlost, a knight named Draken Frost,\n\nO', did she dote, and so I had wrote", ""),
        StaticBook("Midg&Plag VIII", "Jan Altimir Vták", "The writing seems horribly scribbled with trembling hands..\n\nSo goods to meeeee.....nips and naps and sweeeeet fishiess.\n\nSleeps yes? Sleeeeps in nestings so snuggle and tights....\nCatnip seems to be smeared across the page and then licked off, leaving wet smears all over.\n\nSooooooo gooooood......comingss homes sooness.....fixings fixings....", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus II", "Jan Altimir Vták", "\"Damien I can't control you. I know I just can't but I don't want to control you either...I want you to be happy being with me like I'm guessing you want me to be too. I realize you're the one who regretted having sex with August, you're the one who \nagreed to not talk to him...And it was to make me happy...I feel like a douche not making the day you came back one of the best days you've had. I got caught up in the conversation...All of whats been going on for months now hasn't exactly made me happy..\nbut I know that's no excuse...I don't expect you to forgive me right after writing because I cried on the page and wrote a sob story...I swear when you come back I'll change my attitude. I'll try and ease your pain as much as I possibly can. Not because\nyou'll leave if I don't but because I want to help you...I love you Damien...I don't want to become what I wanted to save you from in the first place...Please believe me...I'll not talk about this whole thing until you feel comfortable with talking.\nAlso take as much time away from here as you want...I miss you like nothing else but I want you to make your own decision. I'll think over the mental mage idea...If you want to it's your choice but I'm hesitant...We can talk about it when you want. I'll \nunderstand if you don't want to write me for awhile...Just if you do talk about small stuff...I don't want you in pain while you're writing me. Stay safe out there....I love you Damien.\n\nHogarth\n\nP.S. Also consider \ncoming back when it's Amber Cold, you never bit my buns.", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus IV", "Jan Altimir Vták", "A new note lies at the top of the chest, the same handwritting adorning it except a bit less shaky. \n\n\"I'm sorry about that letter...I know you're hurting and I didn't do anything to help, I should be doing the opposite. I don't mean to hurt you Damien,\n\"I love you. I was in a dark place last stone night...I wanted to tell you how I felt so you would understand, I hate this...I'm sure you do too...I didn't want to make you feel worse... I know you leave like this when you're stressed badly but \n\"if you come back I swear I'll make this better for you. We can't hide from this any longer, there's things that we both don't want to tell eachother...They hurt but it doesn't hurt as much as the thought of losing you all together. We don't have to \n\"talk about it at first. I want to spend some time with you just relaxing, we can fish, work on our house or get the tavern up and running...Balek agreed to do the interior of our house in Kal'Karaad but I don't want him to start without your input, now I\n\"don't know if he'll have the time to help...The kingdom has got him designing fortresses, the fighting is tough Damien, it's not just about charging and screaming. It's also about lies and deceit. It's been tough, the kingdom doesn't know it's direction \nbut I do what I can. I was looking forward to seeing you, talking to you more about all that's been going on. I love you Damien...Regardless of all this when I see your face I perk up a bit...Unless you're crying, I don't mean to hurt you either.\n\"I don't know why I started telling you about how things are...I guess if you still won't come back we can communicate in letters. I don't want to ask what that blood was on yours, probably because I'll blame myself for it...I love you Damien, please just\n\"remember that...Come back soon.\"\nA large blank is left with a couple tears staining the page, the hang writing now worse.\n\n\nYour mate, \n\nHogarth\n((Also you should check gaia.))", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus IX", "Jan Altimir Vták", "The note is small and wrinkly as if it's been crumpled up before, the writing barely readable.\n\nAll of my memories keep you near, in silent moments I imagined that you would be near...\n\nTears dot the page.\nDamien...I miss you...", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus V", "Jan Altimir Vták", "Alright, I suppose I'll lay off the letters for a bit. As long as you know you'll be alright. Oh also you should take a bath or something, you got dirt all over the room list time you were here. If you want I can leave a barrel of whiskey out for you to \nuse...It'd be nice seeing you come home smelling like whiskey instead of squirrel waste.\n\nLove you,\nHogarth\n\n((Jeez you get sick often, sucks that you haven't been able to be active in a long \ntime, I guess it can't be helped though.))", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus VI", "Jan Altimir Vták", "Dear Damien,\n\nHello love, I just wanted to write to check on how you are, I saw that you were handling my ring the other night, you got a bit of dirt on it. I'll just wash it off in the river or something. Anyway I didn't know how to respond to \nthe last letter you wrote me, thank you for the honey and fruit though. The saliva mixed in was a bit unexpected but it made me think of you so I think it was a good addition. \n\nAnyway how are you? Hopefully you're alright...I always get\na bit worried when you leave like that, I felt like I was going to have a panic attack back when you were gone for two stone weeks right after we healed you because of how bad the Chimera was getting. I want to be there for you, if there's something you \nwant to talk about feel free to write about it. I'll be there for you. Anyway I suppose I should talk about how I am, I'm holding up, I'm in a fairly good mood compaired to other times but I still wish you were home.\n\nI don't know if I ever told you but \never since you introduced me to Guarger I've been coming to him for advice about you. Hesh isn't around as often as he is and I haven't really spoken to Briar in ages. Plus Guarger's a pretty good guy, he was the one who convinced me I should be nice to \nyou when I was told you cheated on me. I don't know what I would of said had I not talked to him beforehand. Also the letter you gave me was cute, thank you for that meat. I'll cook it up sometime for you once you get back. \nI love you Damien, even if you're not willing to come back full time yet, stop by sometime while I'm around. I might be able to pursuade you with some whiskey and a night to the two of us. \n\nWith love,\n\nHogarth", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus X", "Jan Altimir Vták", "When you unfold the letter a tuft of grayish hair falls out and onto the floor, looking like beard hair. \n\"I'll wait for you Damien, I know it's hard. I'm glad you trusted me with telling me what you did, it might of gotten worse if this waited to pop up.\n\"Thank you for the fish, I've had nothing but bread for the last couple days. Not quality bread either, I make it because I'm in a hurry or a Dwarf needs it before a fight. Be careful out there love, I know you have a bit of bad luck and the main road is \n\"worse than ever because of the war.\"\n\n\"Of course we can get married when you come back...I've been waiting for so long I've lost track of when I proposed to be honest...I hope you still have the ring...\"\n\n\"And thank you for \n\"writing that song for me...It's sad but it helps, I think I'll keep it around for when I feel like giving up so I remember you're out there...I wish I could write a song like that for you but I'm not that good with writing like that so I figured I'd give\n\"you something else of mine...You always talk about how you want my beard, hopefully it keeps you a bit happy for the cold nights...\"\n\n\"Stay strong Damien, I'll be waiting for you. A King isn't a King without his subject...\"\nYour Dwarf,\nHogarth P.S next->\nP.S. Write me when you feel lonely or want me to talk about what's going on or just whenever you feel like it. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus XI", "Jan Altimir Vták", "\"I know you meant well....but Hogarth I feel opressed. I dont like it that you're trying to control me....I'm scared...and I dont want to be...\n\nI'll come home soon.....", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&PlagusIII", "Jan Altimir Vták", "The note is folded in half, clearly Hogarth's bad hand writing.\n\"Damien I don't know where you are but I need you...I barely talk to you anymore...And whenever we do it's about August...I don't want to hurt you but I'm afraid...I love you more than \nanything. I can't keep going like this...I want you to tell me that I'm your only, that you won't betray me like that again... This needs to stop, please realize why I asked you what I did. In the end it's up to you to fix things but I don't know what \nI'll do if you don't...Please just help me, I don't know what I did to deserve this but I'll fix it...I'll be strong like you want me to be and it won't be a facade, it'll be like how it was back in the beginning...I know you were my only back then, I \nTears stain this part of the note, smudging the ink that makes up the words.\n\"could tell you were. I'm sorry if this all makes you mad or it sounds like jiberish...I'm not myself right now.\"\nA small tear stained space is before the last sentence. \nPlease come back...", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&PlagusVII", "Jan Altimir Vták", "* Writing is scribbled all over the pages as if hurried and crazed *\n\nITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me     ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me \n           ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me \nITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me \n    ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me                ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me    ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me        ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me ITS Me     ITS ME ITS ME ITS ME ITS ME\n*The page is dotted with bloody fingerprints and the writing shakey.*\n\nI'm sorry. It hurts. I had to run away. I didn't want to hurt anyone. I had to run. I had to eat. So hungry. I'm sorry.....\nI can't control him anymore. The connection...the Mage broke it when he tried to heal me. She was in my Mind! The sickness....it's making him crazy....I'm sorry....I can't....\n*The page is covered in blood and writing scribbled all over as if crazed.*\n\nITS ME ITS ME CANT YOU SEE IM RIGHT HERE ITS ME CANT YOU SEE I AM HERE I CAN SEE ITS ME ITS ME I CAN SEE YOU I CAN SEE YOU ITS ME....\n§O§LITS NOT HIM\n\nIIIII TTTTTT SSSSS\n III    TT   SSS\n III    TT     SSS\nIIIII   TT   SSSSS\n\nM       M  EEEEE\nMM   MM  E\nM M M M  EEEE\nM  M  M  E\nM     M  EEEEE", ""),
        StaticBook("Midget&Plagus I", "Jan Altimir Vták", "Damien I'm sorry about the other day...You're in a lot of pain and I'm just sorry. I want to just keep it at that. Please come back when I'm around sometime again...We can go fishing or to a tavern and I swear I won't bring all that up. I think both of us\nneed a break from hurting eachother...I know a tavern you'd like, we can catch up more.\n\nYour Dwarf,\nHogarth", ""),
        StaticBook("Mines", "Lheara'sin", "((A roughly drawn map of the world, with mines clearly marked upon it, but not much else.))\n\n((Kha: 452  217\n\n((Temple: 208  392\n\n((Orc: -58  341\n\n((Elf: 589  663\n((Human: 1113  45\n\n((Dwarf (marked with red X): 235  769\n\n((Wilds 1: 835  715\n\n((Wilds 2: 128  772", ""),
        StaticBook("Mira's Drawings", "Almecki", "My ColORing Book\n________________\n*The first few drawings are archaic, however the further you go the skill of the artist seems to improve greatly*\n*This page is a picture of a \nroom with two beds, vines,\nand wooded walls.*\n*This drawing shows a well in the middle of a square with shops around it, and a big tent in the background.*\n*A drawing depicts a wooden bridge across a wide river, with mountains in the background and a pillar on the opposite bank of the river.*\n*This drawing depicts a castle-like ruin structure that is embedded in a mountain, with a few blue-colored stick figures in front of it.*\n*This drawing depicts the same square as before, execpt the ground is covered in ice, despite that the drawing shows rain.  A woman stands next to the well, with the gates to her right, and a shop behind her.*\n*A drawing of a elf with white skin is here, and he seems to be cowering in front of a dark elf child with a dagger.*\n*This drawings shows large, stone walls with tall towers, the tops with red and blue hues.*\n*This drawings shows a stone building surrounded by large pine trees that are covered in a white stringy material.*\n*A woman lays on the ground, with a man sitting next to her, and a ghost standing at her feet.  Their faces show concern, and the woman's eyes are closed.  Their surrounding are of a birch tree, with a small stream flowing around it.  Naturistic people ->\nsurround the group.  This drawing takes up two pages, and is in greater detail than the ones before it.*\n*This drawing depicts a group of elves standing in a group with an odd floating orb in the middle.*\n*This drawing depicts a dark elf boy.  He doesn't have a shirt on, and he seems to be sitting on a bed in the same room that was drawn before.*\n*This drawing is of a gravestone with a tree in front of it.  A dark elf woman and the dark elf boy from before are standing in front of the gravestone.*\n*This drawing shows a horse that is in a small stable squeezed between the roots of a very large tree.*\n*This drawing shows the same brown horse, except with more detail.  It appears to be grazing in an open plain.*\n*This drawing shows two dark elf sisters on the ground, fighting.  The smaller one is on top of the other, and the two are surrounded by a red haze, suggesting a new hatred*\n*This drawing is from the viewpoint of somebody on a horse looking to a tall dark elven woman, sitting on a white horse.  They seem to be on a beach, and the woman is looking out to the sea, the wind flowing through her hair.*\n*This drawings shows the Malinor square.  A weird metal-like creature (golemn) seems to be marching towards a woman who is fleeing.*\n*This drawing depicts a mali'ker man, however he seems to have the same features as the boy in another drawing... perhaps he has grown up.*\n*A large, detailed drawing shows a pair of twins, a child, and a mother separating.. but the drawings shifts and blurs into the same four, however they are embracing eachother.. as one family.*\n*A black bear is walking across a small bridge next to what appears to be the Gauntlet, and a small dark elven child appears to be following it sneakily.*\n*This drawing depicts a group of various elves standing around a white-haired teen who is on the ground, her clothes stained by blood.  The surrounding seem to be those of the Malinor Square.*\n*This drawings is of a brown horse with white spots.  Near the bottom it says 'In memory of Lar'lentos'*\n*This drawing depicts the destruction of Luminaire, the trees dead and the streets filled with corruption.*\n*A drawing shows a dark cave hung with banners of House Z'ress*\n*This drawing shows the rebirth of Luminare after the druid's had recalimed it*\n*This drawing shows a dark and ominous tower in druid-Luminaire.  It is made of dark rocks, ice, and corruption.  Prince Khel and a being stand in front of it, with Wizard Blundermore behind them.  Z'ress sits in the shadows nearby*\n*This drawing depicts New Malinor, however the injured seem to fill the streets- perhaps some sort of raid was carried out recently*\n*This drawing shows a poster that states the banishing of Xel Oussana.*\n*This drawing depicts a small mali'ker woman (Mira) reaching up to a ghostly, tall mali'ker figure that seems to be floating away (Rathina)*\n*This drawing shows a mali'ker woman, sitting alone in a cave*", ""),
        StaticBook("Misfortune Hunt", "The Misfortune Teller", "\n\nDangerous Madness\nFrom the left of the crazy man, down to the right of the road.\n\nTo the grand entrance of the Tree that reaches the sky, from the ones who protect the nature itself.\nDown the road towards the Drood Town.\n\nDown, Down it goes till the bridge is found.\n\nAcross the bridge, run and follow the road into the smelly area, where the trees wither and the water tastes bad.\nInto the Forest of Doom.\n\n\nRun straight, run till you find the bedroom of the dead.\n\nYou see the house and you see the entrance down into the sleepy hallows.\nBe warned, the darkness tricks your mind...", ""),
        StaticBook("Misfortune Hunt", "Misfortune Teller", "The land is small and full of lore.\n\nThe pumpkins grow around your hoe.\n\nThe King of all is next to me, but huge to all that are in his fee.\nRoad Down to the giant Tree where people laugh and drink with me.\n\nStraight into the moon at night, lowering down onto the road.\n\n\nInto the Holy flame and water, Ying and Yang.\n\nRun through the never ending vines and fields.\n\n\nTowards the doors of young and into the water of smelly tears.\n\nYou see the grand one resting on his throne, surrounded by his minions and watching over the town.", ""),
        StaticBook("Missing items!", "Johnathan Castilion", "When I first moved in, i was settled in a very,VERY, temperary room. In that room my belongings were stored. As a farmer it is not unordinary for him to bring his crop with him if he was to move and had not yet sold said crop, right? so when I was givin \na new more permanent room to stay in it seemed natral to want to move my belongings into the new chamber. But alas I could not due to the fact that most of the crop was gone. tis was not just a few harvests of y feild, this was about 20 or 30 full harvest\n. This was over 1600 bales if hay I owned and planed on selling as it was harvested from MY feild. I do not know what happened to it but I am demanding it back, I dont know if it was put into storage because someone thought that it was this places own \nwheat, which I remind you it was not, or if some snake thought they could take it and make a quick profit but this is outrageus. do not even get me started on the hundreds of seeds that have been \"misplaced\" as well. I am demanding the return of my crop \nat the soonest posible date!\n\n-Eamon", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 005 (D)F", "Za'goth Unkai", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 005\n\nAssigned to: Nydari\nBy: Valkelm Za'goth\n\nCatagory:  Guarding\n\nPayout: 50 Minas\n\nTime Alloted:  Till the job is done.\n\nMission Details:  Protect Initiate Charlie Wick during his mission.  \nMission Status: \n\nLost the initiate in a crowd, mission, sadly, failed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 006", "Nydari Angroth", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 006\n\nAssigned to: Nydari + Cal\nBy:  Valkelm Za'goth\n\nCatagory:  (Recruitment)\n\nPayout:\n50 Minas Each\n\nTime Alloted:  One Elven Day\nMission Details:  Recruit one promising man/woman to Mar, bring them to the Tavern for Initiation.\nMission Staus: One human denied, after Cal Marq, former initiate, interupting us. Second human denied, but stated he may have a young friend who may join us. The friend declined our invitation, however, after a talk of philosphy with a druid, I learned of\na disturbing piece of news:\nOren is forcing the druids to pay a tribute in order to not be slaughtered. Perhaps this warrants further investigation.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 007", "Nydari Angroth", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 007\n\nAssigned to: Jayko + Nydari\nBy: Valkelm Za'goth Unkai\n\nCategory: (MISC)\nPayout: 100 Minas\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven Day\nMission Details:  Do three of the five things below:\n\nGather 5 stacks of cobblestone\n\nGather a stack of coal\n\nGather three stacks of logs\n\nRecruit someone\n\nObtain an Iron Sword", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 008", "Nydari Angroth", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 008\n\nAssigned to: Aglareth C.\nBy: Mentor Angroth \n\nCategory: Recruiting \nPayout: 50 minas\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven day\nMission Details: Recruit a Kharajyr or a Halfling.\n\nMission Report: Mission Failed. I found no kha and the one halfling I found turned out to be an elder and a monk.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 009", "Nydari Angroth", "Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 009\n\nAssigned to: Kelomir Bronzebeard\nBy: Mentor Angroth \n\nCategory: Intel\nPayout: 50 minas\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven Day\nMission Details:\nSeek out the town of Ilados, in the province of Lurzan, in the Federation of Mallatir. Learn of their culture and society. I shall guide ye to the path.\nMission Report: \nIlados, as teh name states, is quite ta trade feel to it. Ta place is run by a Govenor, not ah King or Lord, teh place show off it's peaceful nature, but know it cannot always been that way, for it has quite teh wall surrounding it. \nIlados 'as been set up tah be quite organised, wit' ah main road goin' from gate teh coast, an' all teh important stuff alon' ta wayh. Teh citeh's architecture is alike ta Human Citeh's. \n\nTa most common thing ah saw was their importance on peace, \nan ye can go roight up an talk to teh Govenor if ye need be. \n\nDefinetly one who could support our cause.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 010", "Nydari Angroth", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 010\n\nAssigned to: Zanoth + Kelomir\nBy: Mentor Angroth \n\nCategory: Supplies Payout: 50 mina each\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven Day\nMission Details:\n Both of you are to gather at least 3 stacks of cobblestone each for our walls, along with as many ores as possible.\nMission Report:\n\nCobble is collected/stored.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 013", "Nydari Angroth", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 013\n\nAssigned to: Jotohasta\nBy: Mentor Angroth \n\nCategory: Intel\nPayout: 70 Minas\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven Day\nMission Details:\n Locate an alternate entrance into Petrus, preferably for use by multiple men, but any entrance aside from the gate works. Be very stealthy, for getting caught may prove fatal.\nMission Report:\nNo other enterance to Petrus could be found. Apart from one, However only Kha with there abilty to jump great hights would be able to acess this entrance. Petrus has a large sewer system none of this leads to outside of the walls. The Kha \nentrance is located on the walls. A Kha is able to jump and climb over the walls.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 014", "Nydari Angroth", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 014\n\nAssigned to: Zanoth + Aglareth\nBy: Mentor Angroth \n\nCategory: Intel\nPayout: 50 minas\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven Day\nMission Details:\n With the Flaming Rose disbanded, we can focus our attention on other malicious groups. Persons, Guilds, or beings that threaten racial quality and the races are enemies of Mar. We know they are out\nthere, we just need to find them. Scour Athera for those in need of our aid. \nMission Report:\n There are no obvious opposers of racial equality about, at least not yet.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission 015", "Za'goth Unkai", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 015\n\nAssigned to: Aglareth + Gundar\nBy: Mentor Angroth \n\nCategory: Labor\nPayout: 50 Minas\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven Day\nMission Details:\n\n Retrieve six cartloads of spruce logs each from the Forests of Polaris. Do not replant.\nMission Report: \nMission complete. My initiate and I have gathered the 6 cart loads of spruce wood as requested. Even though I had to partially fill his 3rd cart....\n\n-Disciple Aglareth", ""),
        StaticBook("Mission Book", "Volug Irving", "The Covenant of Mar\nMission Book: 002\n\nAssigned to: Cal Marq\nBy: Valkelm Za'goth\n\nCatagory:\nIntelligence Gathering\n\nPayout:\n30 Minas\n\nTime Alotted: 1 Elven Day\nMission Details:\n\nGather information on the Elves, find out one of the three leaders.  Find all three for bonus pay.\n\n\nNames: The Sohear- Kalenz", ""),
        StaticBook("Missive", "Artorius Thannius", "Missive to the People I - The Revolt\nThe Circle of Governance would like to inform all citizens of the Enlightened Caste that the city is now back under control: the revolt of the lesser castes has been suppressed. I would like to\nformally dispel the rumours that this revolt was led by one of the orc beasts. It is laughable that a beast of burden could conceive of a plan of revolt, instruct the lesser caste in the use of tools of labour as weapons, and lead the lesser caste up\nin arms against the Enlightened. It is obvious that this must be have been a coup attempt by one of the Enlightened, and we will spare no expense in hunting down the leader of this insurrection and reeducating them.\nI also wish to reassure the\nenlightened people of Rivel that the recent mass euthanasia of the orc beasts is to prevent the spread of disease amongst them, not, as some have had the lack of wisdom to suggest, to prevent further revolt. Orcs do not think, this is a clearly\nestablished fact. They are simple beasts possessing no capacity for higher thought nor magical skill. Likewise, the recent introduction of “reorientation” of members of the lesser caste via mental magic is simply to improve their\nefficiency and wellbeing. The idea that we are attempting to turn them into “drones” is equally unfounded, and I would request such rumours are ceased: we do not wish to agitate the Lesser Caste and reduce the efficiency of their\nservice to us.\n- Artorius Thannius, Exalted Spellcrafter Archon of Rivel", ""),
        StaticBook("Missive 3", "Tarien Arvius", "Missive to the Spellcrafter Archon III - The Hall\nTo the Spellcrafter Archon,\nWe found the Hall and found it sealed. The Forgotten Dwarves appear to have sealed it from the outside with five keys, and we know not how to open it.\nWhile I felt unease at opening this place, my fears seem to have left me. We must open this hall. It is the will of the Radiant Man. We will assist him in finding the keys, we will open this Hall, and we will find the Seal. And with it, we will strike\ndown the Ravager and ensure the eternal survival of Rivel. I will not be returning to Rivel first as planned: I no longer have any fears about mental control by the Radiant Man. We will find the keys first, there is no reason to delay.\n- Spellcrafter\nTarien Arvius\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Mithras Stuff", "Mithras", "someone is dumb", ""),
        StaticBook("MonkResurrection", "Jistuma", "\n\n\n\n       >|<Monk>|<\n-=~Resurrection~=-\n\nIt is the salvation of many lives, but I wonder why I have seen nothing written on it. The monks, as is very well known, are able to save those that have passed away. While managing my stall at the sanctuary grounds I have been able to see for myself some\nof these happenings, or so I believe. \n\nThis magic, unlike many others, is one I know very little about. Like all holy magics it seems to be manifested as a bright light, mostly used to heal those wounded. Besides the bright light, the monk performing it\nis praying to the Triumvirate. It appears to be like clerical healing, but it has other properties. Sometimes a monk arrives at the sanctuary a bit hurt after travels. It appears bandits and the like do not care that they are mugging what could be their\nsavior. What I have to say about those monks, is that unlike normal folk, their wounds heal faster. This is of course due to their magic, small flecks of light I have seen surrounding and healing the wounds. Then the monks are able to bring back the once \ndeceased fully mortal once more.\n\nThis is not to say they are necromancers. Those simply raise the dead, and they remain in a limbo state living and being deceased. The monks are able to bring the body and soul exactly like it was before the death, in\nnear perfect condition. From those I have talked to over the years in the Sanctuary, there is an always present effect of being saved. Present in all of those that I have seen, none have memories of their death or even of the events leading up to it. \nOther common effects are scars, sometimes showing how the poor soul met its end before, or of the tortures it passed. The ones that worry me the most is that sometimes the ones saved change… I have talked to some before and after saving, and changes can\nbe quite extreme. Some go crazy, others start to live in constant fear, some remain with pains in parts of their bodies. The changes they can go through are incredibly varied. I could conjure up reasons as to why that happens, all of them without means of\nproving so. I just believe death to be a terrible experience, that part of it continues on even after salvation.\n\nAnother interesting fact I have gathered, somehow by luck, is that the saved do not want to return to the location that they had\nperished. This particular thought came to me when one of the saved was hugged dearly by what I assumed was his lover. Before that, his body had been carried by another, a friend perhaps, and he and I had a chat about what happened. I knew of the exact \nlocation of his demise, and as him and the woman were moving away, he asked if they were passing through that exact area when returning home. With the confirmation of the woman, he started giving out other ways to reach their house. A big argument even \nhappened when the woman just wanted to go home as fast as she could with him, and he plainly refused to go through that area, which was the only road to their house. He didn’t know himself why he didn’t want to. Alas he returned to normal about half a day\nlater. Since then I started noticing other strange behaviors such as people staying in the sanctuary for longer times, not using their normal routes out of the sanctuary and returning shortly after leaving with a friend. I simply believe once more, that \nthe experience was so horrible, that those saved gain a subconscious fear of their death place. Besides, what would happen if they saw their own body? \n\nOn the mention of bodies, I do have to state as well, it is not necessary for the \nbody to be brought back to the monks for the resurrection. I have seen many monks bring bodies to the sanctuary, so I believe it might be quite easier with them. This is favorable, as in the lands of Anthos there are many evils. You can be decapitated,\neaten, thrown into lava, cut into little bits and drowned in the deepest of oceans. Your body may disappear and never been found once more, and you may this way still be saved. How they restore the body I do not know, and the monks seem not to answer when\nI ask them. They do answer a few questions. I learned, for instance, that the monks are neutral, and never take side in political views or wars. They also take this neutrality to those they save. I have seen those of evil whose purpose is to kill being \nsaved together with the children that never made any harm. This has brought problems to the monks before, many want others to remain dead.\n\nMost of the stories of the saved start simply with them explaining how they woke up in the monk’s care.\nSeems to always be the same as I stated. Some come back quite soon after their demise, already back before their friends and family run to the sanctuary to check, and others can take elven days. Yet this magic is not all powerful. As the monks mention,\nthey cannot save all. If the deceased does not walk out of the monks rooms an elven week after their death, they are forever lost. The same happens to those that take their own life. I believe that if they think that they don’t want to live anymore, then\nthey wouldn’t return to the land of the living.\n\nThe monks are a blessing to mortals, they are those that save so many from their shortened lives.\n\n\n\nWritten by Rosso, 15th of Malin's Welcome 1452", ""),
        StaticBook("Monks of Charity", "Guru Pinya", "~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\n    \n The Book of Peace\n\n    By: Guru Pinya\n\nThrough experimentation of the arcane, I have become enlightened. Through days of meditation at a time, I have become able to let go of my earthly tether, and enter the void. I have become one with the wind. If you do not understand of what I speak of,\nI shall explain in the simpler of speech. Through meditation my soul has left it's body and enter the void through which the spirits have given me ancient knowledge. In the book I shall write down what I have been taught so that it may be passed on to the\nworld for all the nations to hear.\n   \n         -Guru Pinya\n~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\n\n Concerning the Soul\n\nEverytime we die, our souls leave our body and wander aimlessly around. The Monks of the Cloud Temple then collect our souls and our bodys and take them to be healed back together. However,\nI must say that when one has become a light into the dark world, and their duty has been fulfilled, their spirit will be given a new body and they will be reincarnated. This is called the Natural Order.\n~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\n\n   Concerning the \n   Spiritual Powers\n\nEverything has a life-force inside of it. Some call it energy, some call it life, but I call it Magic. This magic is not from the void, or from a deity, but from onesel! Through\ndiscipline, and patience, one is able to harness their energy an extent, and use it for the wellbeings of others. Some examples of harnessing bodily Magic is using it do increase strength, speed, become unfamished, survive during a thirst,\nset things alight, preserve dying bodies, levitating small things, healing minor wounds, increasing senses, and entering the spirit world.\n~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\n \n   Concerning the\n     Spirit World\n\nThe spirit world is like nothing the mortal eye has seen before. In it lies the Divine Hosts, the angels, the demons, the spirits, the gods, dead spiritual people, and some of the greatest people\nof all time. You might ask how these great people have gotten there. The answer is the Natural Order. When someone has made a great change in the world, their souls do not reincarnate themselves, but they enter into the spiritual world in the peace they\nhad so desired. To enter into peace is to live and die in peace.\n~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\n\n    The Great Path\n\nTo enter into the realm of peace, one must live and die in peace. There are eight rules to peace, and they are:\n\nRight view, viewing reality as it is, and not how it is to be.\nRight intention, intention of renunciation, freedom and harmlessness.\n\nRight speech, speaking in a truthful, and non-hurtful way.\n\nRight action, acting in a non-harmful way.\n\nRight livelihood, living a non-harmful livelihood.\n\nRight effort, making an effort to improve.\n\nRight mindfulness, awareness to see things for what they are with a clear consciousness.\nRight concentration, correct meditation or concentration.\nWith the eight ways to peace said, I will now explain the four ideals. One must always hope that:\n\nAll sentient beings have happiness and it's causes.\n\nAll sentient beings be free of suffering and it's causes.\nAll sentient beings never be separated from bliss without suffering.\n\nAll sentient beings be in equanimity, free of bias, attachment and anger.\nIn the spirit world, for which I have named Tashe, meaning both the spiritual realm and the state of enlightenment, Tashe may be acquired by following the teachings of myself. If you wish to leave the cycle of reincarnation and suffering, please search \nfor me, Guru Pinya.\n((AntoniusLXIV))", ""),
        StaticBook("Monster Manual 1", "Diane Farsight", "       =+-+=\n    The Monster\n       Manual\n       =+-+=\n        \n\n        Volume 1\n\n\n\n\n\nBy: Diane Farsight\n       Foreword\nThe world is a dark place, and it's filled with dark things. This book, and others like it, will hopefully inform you of what is out there, and how to deal with them. After all, Ignorance is a most insidious killer.\n\n      The Blob\n================\nDanger: Low\nDescription: Blobs are generally docile animals shaped like big piles of goo. Their size can vary greatly, but they tend not to surpass 6 feet in height. Their color varies, but will usually be\nbe the color of the enviornment they live in. Furthermore, they are covered in an outer layer of dry skin that protects them from extreme heat. Because of this, they are often found in hot climates. If enveloped by a blob, suffocation is the only way it\nkill you, as their bodies have no acidic properties.\nWeaknesses: Blobs are quite susceptable to cold, making Thanium a perfect weapon against them.\nCreation: Blobs can create more blobs by splitting themselves apart, creating two, smaller blobs.\n     The Ooze\n===================\nDanger: Moderate\nDescription: Oozes are the volitile cousin of blobs. They have the same shape and color, but can be much larger and territorial than their counterpart. Their size can become a staggering 5\nmeters in height, and their body produces a potent acid capable of dissolving flesh and metal alike. Also unlike blobs, Oozes prefer the cold, and lack the outer skin that blobs possess. If you ever need to tell an Ooze apart from a blob quickly, use your\nnose. Oozes will smell much more acrid.\nWeaknesses: Oozes are slow and notably flammable. The best way to deal with one is to set them alight, then run while they burn.\nCreation: Oozes can split themselves into more oozes, just like blobs.\n  The Giant Ooze\n===================\nDanger: EXTREME!\nDescription: On occasion, there will be a sighting of a Gargantuan Ooze. Often they come from forgotten cave systems opened by earthquakes, or humanoids digging too deep. They are like\nOozes, but much larger- usually over 10 meters in size and capable of dissolving massive castle gates in a matter of minutes. Weaknesses: Fire will still kill gargantuan Oozes, but it is imperitave that you do not attempt to hide from them- there is\nalmost no stopping one if it begins chasing you.\nCreation: Unknown. Normal Oozes should split into smaller Oozes before reaching this size. It is hypothesized that extended time in cramped conditions underground allows an ooze to grow to this size.\n  The Flesh Worm\n===================\nDanger: EXTREME!\nDescription: The tunnel worm is a subterranian monster of epic proportions. It's full length is unknown, but it is easily several meters in diameter. Most of it's life is spent boring\nholes in the earth for it to travel in, often straight through stone. A cave created by a Tunnel worm recently will always be perfectly circular, and ribbed to allow the tunnel worm to maneuver within it easily. It is hypothesized that the Underdark\nbeneath the dwarven lands of Anthos was sculpted by the errant burrowing of tunnel worms.\nWeaknesses: Any place that isn't perfectly fitted for it's body, such as large caverns or the surface.\nCreation: Unknown.\n     The Drake\n===================\nDanger: EXTREME!\nDescription: A Drake is, in essence, a corrupted dragon, like those spoken of in legend. Unlike their bretheren, Drakes are much smaller in size and easier to defeat. Despite this, they\nare still capable of flying and breathing fire, and as a result require a large body of able men to be killed safely. When killed, a drake's body will dissolve entirely, but if one can manage to retrieve some of its scales without killing the beast, they\ncan be used to great effect in alchemy.\nWeaknesses: None.\nCreation: Legend has it that drakes are born when a dragon is overcome with taint. This, however, has never been witnessed.\n   The Alchemical\n       Chimera\n===================\nDanger: Moderate\nDescription: An alchemical chimera is a fusion of two animals. It is truely an unnatural beast, created by a devious use of alchemy. Chimera come in all shapes and sizes,\nbut all will resemble the combination of two  natural beasts. They are mindless beasts, virtually untamable, and gluttonous for flesh.\nWeaknesses: : Time. A well created Chimera will last, at max, for 2 to 3 years.\nCreation: Alchemy.\n The Dark Chimera ===================\nDanger: Moderate\nDescription: Through the use of drakkarian magic, the normal conditions for a chimera can be subverted, allowing for fully adult humans to be partially fused with parts hewn from \nanimals. The result is a fully intelligent, remarkably stable chimera. Many dark chimera are no more than victims of dark mages, but this is not always the case. Great care should be taken when dealing with one.\nWeaknesses: None\nCreation: Alchemy \nMixed with Drakkar Magic.\n    The Vodnik\n===================\nDanger: Moderate\nDescription: Vodnik are tiny, fish-like creatures that inhabit rivers and lakes. When vodnik encounter a corpse of any sort, they will begin filling and infesting it. The corpse will begin\nmoving of it's own accord, and begin dragging anything so foolish as to disturb it's water under with it, to become infested as well. Eventually, this process will crescendo until a small army of shambling corpses inhabit an area. This makes clearing\ndifficult.\nWeaknesses: Attacking the arms and legs of a vodnik-infested corpse is your best bet. Cutting off the head should have no effect.\nCreation: The Vodnik reproduce by laying eggs in the corpses they inhabit.\n\n  The Giant Crab\n===================Danger: High\nDescription: An enormous crab, about 15 meters across, 5 meters high, and chitin thicker than plate armor. If they see you, they will try to kill you. . They attack anything that moves,\nmembers of their own species included.\nWeaknesses: Their eyes are vulnerable, but should you lack for arrows you should try attacking their underbelly, or failing that, playing dead.\nCreation: Unknown. They certainly do not reproduce.\n    The Ghost\n===================\nDanger: Low\nDescription: A corporeal entity that is not yet ready to leave this world. Specters have several stages of 'unlife'. Stage one specters are always gray, and flit in and out of existance constantly.\nWhat seperates this stage from the others is the fact that, without fail, the spirit will not be able to come to terms with the fact that they are already dead. They usually need a large amount of persuasion and occaisonally hard evidence to make them \nrealize this. Stage two specters already realize that they are dead, and are able to begin learning to control their abilities more effectively. One of two things happen to stage three specters. They will either turn blue and become good, or turn red and\nbecome pure evil. This is usually in response to how well they were treated in their unlife, or how evil they were when they still lived. Powerful specters are capable of invisibility, minor manipulation of physical objects, and ejecting ectoplasm.\nWeaknesses: Spectres can be banished with a single swing of a weapon, especially holy and gold instruments. They can and will come back to life later, however. They are also unable to cross even the thinnest of gold lines.\nCreation: Spectres are \ncreated when someone dies, but cannot come to terms with their own passing. They become stuck betwixt.\n    The Graven\n===================\nDanger: High\nDescription: Graven are evil spirits who died carrying a great obsession in their hearts. As it drove them in life, so to  does it drive them in death.These spirits will wander the lands,\nendlessly pursuing their unattainable goal, and the pursuit of that goal itself becomes the very fuel that keeps them alive. Above all else, do not look a Graven in the eye. Their eyes are portals into their maddened soul, and will instill a lasting\nmadness into the hearts of those who see it.\nWeaknesses: A graven can be banished easily with any weapon, but is particularly susceptable to gold, and holy objects. Likewise, they are unable to cross any line made of gold. In special\ncircumstances, is also possible to banish them forever by completing the task that drives their madness.\nCreation: Gravens are created when a truely obsessed person meets an untimely demise.\n   The Apparition\n===================\nDanger: EXTREME!\nDescription: An apparition is an amalgation of many, many lost spirits combined for one purpose, and that purpose is pure evil. Apparitions are malicious, and very dangerous to fight.\nThey are capable of incredible acts of telekinesis and illusion, beyond what any living mage could ever accomplish. Normally, apparitions are bound to a central pillar, marked by many skulls and skeletons littering the area around it. If you come across\none such pillar, is vitally important that you DO NOT DAMAGE THE PILLAR IN ANY WAY, SHAPE, OR FORM. Destroying the pillar will weaken the beast temporarily, but afterwards it will be able to roam the world at large, bringing great chaos in it's wake.\nWeaknesses: An apparition is significantly more powerful than a spirit. The use of gold weaponry can keep it at bay, but the banishment itself lasts only mere moments. Only a very thick ring of gold can contain it. The only way to kill them\npermanently is through the aid of a team of Clerics or Shamen\nCreation: The exact origin of Apparitions is murky. From what can be gathered, they are formed from the death of many individuals in one place, at the same time.\n  The Boogeyman\n===================\nDanger: Moderate\nDescription: Yes, the boogeyman is real. Well, was real. The only known boogieman is currently dead. Boogeyman are sentient collections of bugs that swarm around a central corpse. Based on\non the only known boogeyman's example, boogiemen are intelligent, and extremely murderous, taking pleasure in killing innocents. When endangered, the bugs inside the boogieman will scatter in all directions. Because of this, when hunting the Boogiemen it\nis imperative that you take him by suprise.\nWeaknesses: Boogeymen are weak to fire, and crushing implements. Gold hurts it, but a sword will not have much effect on the swarm.\nCreation: Unknown.\n     The Oolog\n===================\nDanger: Moderate\nDescription: The Olog  is roughly twice the size of an orc, and  nearly three times as stupid. A product of large amounts of orcish inbreeding, Ologs were raised for having muscle and size,\nand their bodies show it. Still descendant of Orcs, they also are capable of bloodraging, making them twice as dangerous.\nWeaknesses: Ologs are stupid. I mean, really stupid. Use that to your advantage.\nCreation: These creatures are \n(loosely) considered Natural' beasts, and reproduce normally.\n    Conclusion\n\nThis book has merely scratched the surface of what lurks in the night. Expect more books in the furture.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n  Stay Alive, Atheria.\n\n\n\n\n\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Moon Princess", "Damien Menrow", "Once did an Elf who shone like the Sun,\nlook out on her Kingdom and sigh.\n\nShe smiled and said \"Surely there is no Elf so lovely,\nand so well beloved as I....\"\nSo great was her reign and so brilliant her glory,\nthat long was the shadow she cast.\n\nWhich fell dark upon the young sister she loved,\nand grew only darker as Days and Night passed.\nLulay Moon Princess goodnight sister mine,\nrest now in Moonlight's embrace.\n\nBare up my lullaby Winds of the Earth,\nthrough Cloud and through Sky and through Space.\nCarry the peace and the coolness of Night,\nand carry my sorrow in kind.\n\nLuna you're loved so much more then you know,\nforgive me for being so blind.\nSoon did that Elf take notice,\nthat others did not give her sister her due.\n\nAnd neither had she loved her as she deserved,\nshe watched as her sister's unhappiness grew.\nBut such is the way of the limelight,\nit sweetly takes hold of the mind of its host.\n\nAnd that foolish Elf did nothing to stop,\nthe destruction of one who had needed her most.\nLulay Moon Princess goodnight sister mine,\nrest now in Moonlight's embrace.\n\nBare up my lullaby Winds of the Earth,\nthrough Cloud and through Sky and through Space.\nCarry the peace and the coolness of Night,\nand carry my sorrow in kind.\n\nLuna you're loved so much more then you know,\nmay troubles be far from  your mind,\nand forgive me for being so blind....*", ""),
        StaticBook("Moonlight Dance", "Lucius Tython", "The sun submerged itself beneath the dimming salmon mountaintop to the sight of the harmonic moonlight begining its climb from the darkened earth. The air was amongst a thinning moisture that tingled the auromic smell of lingering fern. Perfectly placed \nfingers were present amongst the warmth of her grass pressed palm. Her teals eyes pierced the skin to which she glanced, enticing my very being. The charming aura surrounding such a very being pulled me closer to her ever so slightly. Returning the gaze I\npulled my lips closer to hers, watching the dimming sight of her illuminated lips under the moonlight as I found my eyes closing furthering my body towards hers. The warming sensation of her lips became that of a snug hearth under a wood burning fire. \nMy mind soon became detained in cadence of her lips on mine. A joyful ache overtook me as her arms found home upon my shoulders wrapping themselves around my neck. The slight tug of her arms took me onto the ground alongside her torso. The swing of her\naffection brought me amongst a trance of cherishment. The trance was broken by an unevitable pulling from my lips. My adorning eyes opened in a graceful haste to look upon a warming smile within her eyes. Almost as if it was a pleasant virus, I too felt a\nsmile manifesting at my mouth. The beating of our breath was only so slightly shadowed by the delicate rushing water of the creek. I could only take a moment of admiration towards her before I felt the forward pulling of her weakened hands among my shirt.\nThe scene faded into an Aengulic light as I was pulled against her chest.\n\n-Published on 17th of the Amber Cold, 1472 by Lucius Tython ((Kyle4924))", ""),
        StaticBook("Moonlight Dance", "Lord Lucius Tython", "The sun submerged itself beneath the dimming salmon mountaintop to the sight of a harmonic moonlight beginning its climb from the darkened earth. The air was amongst a thinning moisture that tingled the aromatic smell of lingering fern. Perfectly placed \nfingers were present among the warmth of her grass pressed palm. Her teal eyes pierced the skin to which she glanced, enticing my very being. The charming aura surrounding such a woman pulled me closer to her ever so slightly. Returning the gaze I pulled \nmy lips closer to hers, watching the dimming sight of her illuminated lips under the moonlight. I found my eyes closing, furthering my body towards hers. The warming sensation of her lips became that of a snug hearth beneath a wood burning stove. My mind \nsoon became detained in cadence of her lips on mine. A joyful ache overtook me as her arms found home upon my shoulders wrapping themselves around my neck. The slight tug of her arms took me onto the ground alongside her torso. The swing of her affection \nbrought me amongst a trance of cherishment. The trance was soon broken by the inevitable pulling from my lips. My adorning eyes opened in a graceful haste to look upon a warming smile within her eyes. Almost as if it was a pleasant virus; I too felt a\nsmile manifesting at my mouth. The beating of our breath was only so slightly shadowed by the delicate rushing water of the creek. I could only take a moment of admiration towards her before I felt the forward pulling of her weakened hands among my shirt.\nThe scene faded into an Aengulic light as I was pulled against her chest.\n\n-Written on 17th of the Amber Cold 1472, by Lucius Tython ", ""),
        StaticBook("Moot ((11/8/14))", "Arinislia", "6th of The Grand Harvest, 1480\n\n\n        Two of the Arch druids appeared to have attended this meeting, Arch druid Gi’garun, and Arch druid Leyun. Guides were Sequoia Druid Lillith and Mist Druid Earendur. The Spirit of Nature also attended as well.\n First Topic :\n\nArch druid Gi’garun brought to attention was the path that all druids should be taking, the path of the Aspects. This is a path that all druids follow to help preserve and maintain the balance in nature.\nIt was unfortunate that two druids were un-attuned before this meeting, the Metal Druid Sharr and the Pumpkin Druid Aphoros. We understand the reasons why they stray from the path of druidism, We understand the reasons why they stray from \nthe path of druidism, but they cannot keep their attunement and title for their actions will not only hurt themselves, but the Order as a whole. \n\nSecond Topic:\n\nThe announcement of my own attunement \nand joining the order, I became known as the Owl Druid.\n\nThird topic:\n\nThe removal of non-active dedicants. There are lots of unaccounted for dedicants \nand the dedicants grotto is completely filled with non-active members.. It is recommended the removal of the non-active ones and to move ‘ALL’ druids to the upper housing so there are spaces for the new dedicants. \nGuides and Arch druids are moved to the new Guide grotto. . It was also mentioned that a new dedicant grotto could be made to accommodate all the new dedicants.\nFourth Topic :\n\nThe stepping down of Arch druid Gi’garun. In his place, he appointed Lillith to be the new Arch druid. Now a guide spot is empty and a new Arch druid stands up to watch over the Order.\nFifth topic :\n\nPine Druid Gi’garun being made a Hierophant. \n\nSixth topic:\n\nThe Spirit of Nature warning the Order of an old threat that seems to have come back to the world.\n. In the pass it threatened Luminaire and the grove. An unnatural tree that grew beneath the city and strangled the life out of the land and spread corruption. The Snow Arch Druid Zeremdos seemed to know more about it, yet he is no longer with us. \nThe Spirit of Nature stated that it was placed by the Black Scourge and feels there is a fully sized one now in this world, growing larger as it devours plants.. It was said that Solace Druid Callax exploded to destroy the threat last time,\nyet now this sounds like on a much larger scale. We were also warned it is guarded by something so killing it will prove to be more of a problem.A way of finding it is the feeling of death from trees. Do not shrug it \noff, for it may lead to the tree itself. Also there might be books on this matter and how to destroy it.\n\nSeventh Topic :\n\nUpon the Moot ending, a suddenly strange dark light appear above the disc ball. \n. It flew into it before forming into a figure below on the tree itself. The image was the spirit of Vardak, giving a warning. . The one that captured the grove in Asulon has returned to try and reclaim the grove once more. \n. A witch named Kyral, who is now undead and wishing to reclaim the grove once more. The spirit also stated that in order to talk to him again, we must seek out shamans to do so. And with that, the spirit departed back to the spirit world.\nEighth Topic :\n\nThe shunning and banishment of Evark. His crimes included of the burning the Frost Druid Dain to death and killing many others in his destruction.\n The After Story~\n\nThe Moot was then ended and everyone parted their own ways. Arch druid Lillith headed back down to the guide where I encountered her for a short time before departing. \nForgetting to inform Lillith of a detail, I came back to the grotto to come across the witch herself, Kyral. She found Lillith and cornered her in the Guide Grotto. Pine Druid Gi’garun came up behind me and quickly engaged as Kyral conjured fire \nfire at Lillith and shot it at her. During the encounter, Gi’garun was burned in the face, but Lillith and I quickly subdued the witch.. Angered form within Lillith’s thoughts and was shown in her rash actions. \n. Gi’garun and I made her see through her clouded mind and come back to reason and thus, correct justice was passed. Kyral sentenced to death and thus was ripped apart by the very vines and branches that Gi’garun and I provided from the encounter. \nThough our victory would be short-lived, for we assumed she is an undead and thus will be back for us again. And as of now, Kyral is now kill on sight. \n\nI put this story of what happened after the moot in here \nhere for this is a lesson to be taught to all druids. Do not let anger and impure emotions cloud your reasoning and judgment. To truly protect and guide others, never start conflict, only defend and end it.\n\n~Owl Druid Arinislia", ""),
        StaticBook("Mountain Kings", "Durza Volkihar", "The Dwarves are a small but strong race capable of carving great cities into mountains. Most dwarves are miners or blacksmith. Their skills at forging are legendary usually creating amazing jewelery or weapons.\nDwarves have a great love of war, food and ale. They are quick to anger but have a great love for their friends and kin. They value honor over everything, It is said a dwarf will always keep his word and will never betray a oath.\nMost Dwarves live underground, prefering to mine eternaly for treasures beneath the earth, always wanting to expand their wealth. Dwarfs live much longer than humans but tend to die in battle before they can die of old age.\n Dwarves are small but very muscular. Most of them prefer to grow their beards extremely long. Apart from having incredible beards, they are extremely hairy. Female dwarves are extremely rare but it is hypothesised that the are to hairy to be\nnoticed.\nThe Dwarves speak the simple tounge of men but have added their own touch to the way they speak, usually prefering to shorten words rather than speaking the full length of normal words. The dwarves have a ancient language but the elders use it more.\nIf you share these views, come seek my symbol to find me.\n\n\n\n[!] A red eagle is drawn here.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mr & Mrs Carrot!", "Aylun'sae Tenna'tahu", "The Gallant Adventures of Mister and Missus Carrot!!\n\n  -Aylun'sae\n     Tenna'tahu\nDay One:\n The Carrot Patch\n\n The morning was a clear one, the season good for growing. Upon this fine morning, Mister Carrot stretched his roots and said to himself, \"Now isn't this a fine day!\" and with that he\nuprooted and went to start on his morning walk. \n  Upon this walk, he saw Mister Potato teaching his daughter how to starch. As a neighborly sort of fellow, Mister Potato called out, \"Hello Mister Potato! A fine day it is today!\"\n  To this, Mister Potato angrily replied, \"Yeah! A fine day to your skinny rooty self! Stay away from my daughter you orange fiend!\"\n  Mister Carrot didn't mind Mister Potato's angry outbursts. Usually they just meant he was being friendly. So on Mister\nCarrot went with his walk, and a fine walk it was.\nDay Two:\n  Mister Rabbit\n\n From time to time the garden patch would have visitors who would come from far and away to see the vegetables growing and raising their offshoots. Mister Rabbit was one of these fine fellows, and \nquite the self proclaimed vegetable afficionado he was!\n On one of the gallant mornings that Mister Carrot went for one of his delightful walks, lo and behold!! Mister Rabbit was meandering down his usual route of walking!\n Mister Carrot was quite beside\nhimself with emotion, \"What is this?? A Mister Rabbit?? In the gardens???\" It was quite the dilemma.\n Mister Carrot, being an unusually amiable fellow, of course, thought it best to give Mister Rabbit the appropriate welcome. And so, without further ado,\nMister Carrot found himself walking himself towards Mister Potato's usual garden bed.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mundane Healing", "hex37", "__Mundane Healing: The treatment of minor wounds with common herbs.__\n\nMatron’s Foreword: When I was studying medicine in Oren, a strange dwarf brought me with a book of medical recipes that used very common ingredients \nfor the treatment of various medical casualties, by an Aleya Darolien. One or two of them sounded interesting, though a large amount of them were, largely ridiculous, like one that claimed it possible to heal a major burst of an artery\nor internal organs with nothing but rose thorns and petals. In the interest of not wasting precious paper, I’ve recorded some of the more practical recipes in the book, and added one or two of my own, to make up for lost space.\n-Minor Cut (Matron)\nSimply apply a dose of  deceased Sentient-Slime to the affected area, and wrap a light bandage around it.  The slime helps stop the bleeding, and the mildly acidic nature of it is known to remove the taint from a body.\n-Headache (Aleya)\nBoil milk. Add honey while the milk boils. Take Dandelion stems and Rose thorns and put them in a thin satchel. Dip the satchel in the milk and let it infuse with the mixture for about 2 minutes. Slowly drink the milk.\n-Stomach Ache (Matron)\n\nBoil water. Once it hits a full broil, add honey until the liquid turns an amber color. Add tea leaves to a small bag, and let it soak in the mixture for about five minutes. Drink the tea slowly.\n-Soreness (Aleya)\n\nSimply apply rags with hot water to relax the muscles, and rest a lot.\n-Burns (Matron)\nSet your hands on some vinegar, and drench a clean bandage with it. Apply this bandage to the affected area, and wrap a dry bandage around this to keep the vinegar from getting absolutely everywhere.\nThe important thing about burns is to keep the affected area moist while it mends, as burned areas do not produce sweat properly. Vinegar is ideal for this, as it will keep the area moist, and not evaporate as water would. Be sure to check the burn wound\n daily until the skin can finally reform.\n-Fever (Aleya)\nKeep the victim’s body cool using cold water. Give him hot milk steeped with brown mushroom stems and ground rose thorns. It will fight off the disease. Keep the victim rested and feed him mushroom soup.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mundane Healing", "Isabella Deathsbane", "__Mundane Healing: The treatment of minor wounds with common herbs.__\n\nMatron's Foreword: When I was studying medicine in Oren, a strange dwarf brought me a book of medical recipes that used very common ingredients\nfor the treatment of various medical casualties, by an Aleya Darolien. One or two of them sounded interesting, though a large amount of them were, largely ridiculous, like one that claimed it possible to heal a major burst of an artery\nor internal organs with nothing but rose thorns and petals. In the interest of not wasting precious paper, I've recorded some of the more practical recipes in the book, and added one or two of my own, to make up for lost space.\n-Minor Cut (Matron)\nSimply apply a dose of  deceased Sentient-Slime to the affected area, and wrap a light bandage around it.  The slime helps stop the bleeding, and the mildly acidic nature of it is known to remove taint from the body.\n-Headache (Aleya)\nBoil milk. Add honey while the milk boils. Take Dandelion stems and Rose thorns and put them in a thin satchel. Dip the satchel in the milk and let it infuse with the mixture for about 2 minutes. Slowly drink the milk.\n-Stomach Ache (Matron)\n\nBoil water. Once it hits a full broil, add honey until the liquid turns an amber color. Add tea leaves to a small bag, and let it soak in the mixture for about five minutes. Drink the tea slowly.\n-Soreness (Aleya)\n\nSimply apply rags with hot wayer to relax the muscles, and rest a lot.\n-Burns (Matron)\nSet your hands on some vinegar, and drench a clean bandage with it. Apply this bandage to the affected area, and wrap a dry bandage around this to keep the vinegar from getting absolutely everywhere.\nThe important thing about burns is to keep the affected area moist while it mends, as burned areas do not produce sweat properly. Vinegar is ideal for this, as it keeps the area moist and not evaporate as water would. Be sure to check the burn wound\n daily until the skin can finally reform.\n-Fever (Aleya)\nKeep the victim's body cool using cold water. Give him hot milk steeped with brown mushroom stems and ground rose thorns. It will fight off the disease. Keep the victim rested and feed him mushroom soup.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~Written by Nienna Calm\n\nTranscribed by\nIsabella Deathsbane\nwith no edits.\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~", ""),
        StaticBook("Mundane Healing", "Pinsir99", "Mundane Healing\nBy Unknown\n\nCopied by Aerius Adurond.\nMatron’s Foreword:\nWhen I was studying medicine in Oren, a strange Dwarf brought me with a book of medical recipes that used very common ingredients for the treatment of various medical casualties, by an Aleya Darolien. \nOne or two of them sounded interesting, though a large amount of them were, largely ridiculous, like one that claimed it possible to heal a major burst of an artery or internal organs with nothing but rose thorns and petals.\nIn the interest of not wasting precious paper, I’ve recorded some of the more practical recipes in the book, and added one of two of my own, to make up for lost space.\n\n\nMinor Cut (Matron)\nSimply apply a dose of deceased sentient slime to the affected area, and wrap a light bandage around it. The slime helps stop the bleeding, and the mildly acidic nature of it is known to remove the taint from a body.\n\n\nHeadache (Aleya)\nBoil milk, Add honey while the milk boils. Take Dandelion stems and Rose thorns and put them in a thin satchel. Dip the satchel in the milk and let it infuse with the mixture for about 2 minutes. Slowly drink the milk.\n\n\nStomach Ache (Matron)\nBoil water. Once it hits a full boil, add honey until the liquid turns an amber colour. Add tea leaves to a small bag, and let it soak in the mixture for about five minutes. Drink the tea slowly.\n\nSoreness (Aleya)\nSimply apply rags with hot water to relax he muscles, and rest a lot.\n\n\nBurns (Matron)\nSet your hands on some vinegar, and drench a clean bandage with it. Apply this bandage to the affected area, and wrap a dry bandage around this to keep the vinegar from getting absolutely everywhere.\n\nThe important thing about burns is to keep the affected area moist while it mends, as burned areas do not produce sweat properly. Vinegar is ideal for this, as it will keep the area moist and not evaporate as water would.\nBe sure to check the burn wound daily until the skin can finally reform.\nFever (Aleya)\nKeep the victim’s body cool using cold water. Give him hot milk steeped with brown mushroom stems and ground rose thorns. It will fight off disease. Keep the victim rested and feed him mushroom soup.\n\n", ""),
        StaticBook("MUNGO!", "Mungo Proudfoot", "\n        ________\n       _|         |_\n       |_________|\n        | __   __ |\n        | ||_|   ||_| |\n        |    __    |\n        |________!\n\nMUNGO!", ""),
        StaticBook("MUSHROOMSnSHIT", "\"Rags\"", "I was given a freaky mushroom by a brother and then I ate it so my friendo Kal could get some info on the shit. I gotta say it aint that bad everythings real freaky and shit I'm seeing like sounds man Like I'm listenin to shit and the birds and crap and\nthey be makin motherfuckin color wind like I already knew bout the magic in the beautiful world but this is a whole fuckin new level of magic and shit. *there would be a very poorly drawn balloon here* Sorry I zoned out, But check that balloon brother \nI've got mad art skills like if there were an art school and shit I could be the motherfuckin teacher you ever wonder why balloons float? Like there aint anythything in them I'm freakin out thinking about it I just freaked myself out I'm gonna be right \nback. Ok I'm back yo I just went to talk to the guy sittin next to me he a chill dude but he don't like sharin his food, I wonder why he isn't usin his hands to eet i meen its reel creepy those utensiminals and shit like they sharp *you would note the bad\nspelling and grammar* *theres a bunch more scribbles along here* Hey I'm just thinkin it's kinda nice to write down my thoughts, makes me feel reel nice inside and all that. Cuas no one listens to me you feel? I mean the world is filled with so much hate \ni don't understand why people can't like love, like my friend the motherfuckin owlbear, he's sittin right next to me he's a cool dude he aint like the others the ones that don't listen...the ones that would be smitten by-*the ink would seem to fade*\n*the text from here on out would be blood red* Lost focus again that's a fuckin pretty flower I love it. I'm gon tell my friendo bout it when I get home, do you still need the rest of your body to smell or can you just use your head? Anyways I'll see you \nguys later I hope my friend back home can smell this flower...it's motherfuckin magic...", ""),
        StaticBook("Music Vol. 1", "Evelynn Shards", "-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n \nThe  Sound  Of  Music\n\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-\n \nSix songs each book.\nSelf made songs and there will be more soon!\n-------------------Written by Evelynn Shards\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Page 3: The Sun Parade\nPage 4: Into Nature's Beauty\nPage 5: Lights Will Guide Us\nPage 6: All Of Us \nPage 7: Praise Our Emperor\nPage 8: Fire\n-------------------\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-The Sun Parade\n\nIt shall start soon. Our king will praise our sister, the sun...\nAnd when the light shall raise, the sword will light up. Few people to hold back. Only one Elven day shall this happen. I-its the Sun. \n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Into Nature's Beauty\n\nIf you would walk into the wild and open forest. No human to see. Wide open space full of trees, animals everywhere to be seen. A small rabbit would run in the grass. An owl to fly over. Just too much..\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=- Lights Will Guide Us\n\nWhen night falls over the town. Lights will raise from upon the sky. Thou shall form one long line. Just for us to follow. They shall guide us home, and to a better place. Keep on searching to there...\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-All Of Us\n\nImagine if our God. Would be down here with us. Nobody to know anything. He built cities all over the world. Nobody to notice anything. Though everybody was happy. All of us were...\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-Praise The Emperor\n\nOh, misty forest, filled with ghosts and monsters. The plains ruled by our Emperor. May our Emperor be praised. Oh, praise the Emperor (x2) He makes us happy, gives us good advice. Who doesnt honour him?\n-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-            Fire\n\nI see fire. In the trees going over to the houses. Licking the air like a cat. Burning down towns and forests. The fire needs to be destroyed. It needs to stay small, and keep us warm..", ""),
        StaticBook("Musty pages", "Tuvya", "*a small folder of collected pages, some seem to have writing*\nMy Memoirs\n\nIt is strange to think about this life of mine, as young boy I could never dream I would be one to called by they name Carrion, nonetheless for fellows of all man to call I Emperor. As days of mine grow old, I feel I should write...\n         1\n\n\n    ....and thus was how I found vaults of Toveah the Goldman. In fashion of his genius, I have begun to make such vaults of my own to protect wealth for future lines of my blood. My dear Royal Enchanter, Mariane'a has taken an interest.\n     27\n\n\n\n             \n\n         ....construction has gone slower than would like with all safeguards necessary. Is good I have kept such secret. Dear baby boy of mine Yakov....\n             44\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLook where the Blessed Plate of St. Augustus was first forged....", ""),
        StaticBook("My love, Arin.", "Herun Athna", "My spirit once alone\nonce doubting to share. A knight alone in a body of gold; but divine ichor. Fate had its way. My heart was lost and chained.\n\nUntil she came; Her skin as lovely as gemstones, her hair the sun upon summer. Her step elegance manifest,\nan Aengul herself in mortal form. Purity and Elegance in utter perfection.\n\nIf fate had her way your eyes would've never met mine. Lost forever and past in time. Yet, fate hadn't her way. Your eyes met mine and we were whipped away.\nLong awaited, our hands will touch; yours fragile and silk, mine warm and rough.\n\nOur love kindred for one another; bound by hope and memory.\n\nLet us make our love offical.\n\nWill you marry me?", ""),
        StaticBook("My Nightmare", "Damien Menrow", "I've got another confession to make,\nso complicated let me try to explain.\n\nDon't want this feeling to go away,\nso it stays...it stays...it stays...\nIs it the way that you talk that's causing me to freak? Is it the way that you laugh that's making my heart beat?\n\nIs it the way that you kiss...\nIts gotta be the way you taste...you taste...\nYou're such a Gorgeous Nightmare...old habits never seem to go away.\n\nYou make me feel brand new yeah...\n\nWe resurrect it's like I've come back to life!\n\nI feel so alive! I feel so alive!\nFirst impressions are hard to erase,\netched in my mind and it just wont go away...\n\nMaybe I'm playing my cards way too safe,\nI've gotta change...change.......change...\nIs it the way that you feel against my body?\nIs it the way that you act so damn naughty?\n\nIs it the way that you shake...\nWhen your hips move to the Bass...the Bass...the Bass...\nYou're such a Gorgeous Nightmare...old habits never seem to go away.\n\nYou make me feel brand new yeah...\n\nWe resurrect it's like I've come back to life!\n\nI feel so alive! I feel so alive!\nYou're such a Gorgeous Nightmare...old habits never seem to go away.\n\nYou make me feel brand new yeah...\n\nWe resurrect it's like I've come back to life!\nI feel so alive!\nI feel so alive!\n\nI feel so alive!\nI feel so alive!", ""),
        StaticBook("My note of death", "Olivia Edwin", "By the time anyone gets this note, I will have passed, I decided that I could not live with the fact that I and Sigmar had to keep our relationship quiet, so, I have been suicidal and hung myself... I shall not be missed, Yours sincerely, Sapphy Kási.", ""),
        StaticBook("My story", "Zargloth", "((I know I'm an orc, but I'm gonna be writing as a human so yah, go to next page for book))\nAnd so it begins, my journey, my adventure, my Destiny!\nIt all starts off in the Temple.\nLittle did I know I was going on the Adventure I did.\n\nI stepped outside as I was blinded by the light I squinted my eyes open.\nI saw some Hobbits running away from an Orc, which was when I decided that \"This was the life\" I thought.\nI stretched my arms wide and walked down the stairs, A man in a robe stared at me so I smiled and headed off.\nI stepped down the stone stairs and headed pass Gimblewood.\nA man happened to say to me\n\"'Ey ye! 'f yer walk pass te'h bridge yer out 'f cloud temple!\"\n\nI nodded with a slight smile on my face.\nI kept on walking, excited to head on to Petrus, perhaps buy a house!\nAs I passed a small farm, I noticed of what looked like a Cottage on the side of the road.\nI decided to look in out of Curiosity, I anticipated to see an old fellar standing at \nthe door.\nBut instead it was a little skinny fellar with thin bones and lacked muscle.\nHe was leaning on the counter, he didn't know I was there.\nI knocked on the side of the door, slowly walking inside.\nHe looked up with a surprised and happy \nlook on his face.\n\"What can a'h get yer\" he asked with a joyful tone.\nI reached into my green robe pockets and pulled out some string, I had no minas.\nI chuckled and said \"I guess a'hm 'ere ter make friends\".\nHe extended his arm for a handshake, whilst doing it he said said \"Arthur Caulfield, you?\". I replied with a smile on my face, \"Corvo\".\n\nI decided to stay for a bit, examining the place and exploring the inn, in a way it felt like home!\nWell that was before a man came in with his daughter.\nHe looked at me strangley, so I joked\n\"She having an Ale?\" I was the only one laughing.\nThe room seemed quiet, Arthur smirked a little but never laughed.\n\"Shut up and don't butt in my conversation!\"\nHe told the daughter to stay and told me to come outside, I wasn't exactly jumping on the idea knowing that he was going to try and kill me.\n\nWe fought for a bit until a women spoke to me, so I turned and as I did I felt his hands across my neck.\nI didn't know what happened after that but I awoke in the Temple.\n\nI soon found out his name was Aepholis, the little cunt snapped my neck, Arthur had seen the whole thing, as well as the little girl.", ""),
        StaticBook("Mysterious Badge", "Tyrell Blavier", "*A large and decorated \"V\" lies in the center of the page. Around it lies repetition of the phrase \"Pax et Gloria. Safe.\"*\n\n       Agent One\n         P-APP\n          -Q\n          -TB\n          ", ""),
        StaticBook("Mzuden'a Knultu.", "[∆Scribe∆˙]†Spriggar Ireheart¥", "A sickness flows through the lands.\n\nThousands die in almost an instant.\n\nBodies line the streets, a plague keeps some in... storing what they can find to thwart the coming of death.\n\nThis plague is of tremendous proportions and knows no bounds of mercy or faith.\n\nIt comes without reason, and as the world comes to a halt, we wonder why countless suffer\n\nI address this issue to speak of a time where\nlife was not meaningless\n\nWhen thousands hung by a thread, to be cut by the sword of greed and vengeance\n\nWhen did knowledge become what we despise..\nStay safe.\n\n\n- Spriggar Ireheart\n\n24th Amber Cold\n1461 a1.6.3\n\"[To be found at the doorstep of friends\nabound]", ""),
        StaticBook("Nativitas", "Shady_Tales", "There were no planes of existence. There was nothing. Just the Void. And a bright shining light. It was not a light we could see, it was not really white either,but it would be the only way to explain the existance of such a Deity in contrast to the \nemptiness of the Void. None knows why, how or when this Deity existed. It is, in-fact, heresy to speculate on such matters. The all knowing God was this Deity. Truly none can describe the majesty of the Creator, the one and only. The Merciful Creator of \nthe seven skies. With his intent and only his intent, the World of Aegis was created. All he had to do was simply want it to occur and it happened. THe rolling seas, the proud mountains and vast landscapes, all in a second, created by God. The World was \nnot created in play or for fun, but as a theater for the inhabitants of the World to be tested in. And tested for what?Well, that is for us to speculate. Though I believe it is to test us for purity, how  hard we can fight temptation, and how clean and \ngood our actions be. The first mortal being to be created was a man.This man was sculptured with clay and water, but left hollow. Soulless, God's creation lay there for the other immortal beings to see.The Aengul and the Daemon, the two spirits created\nbefore the mortal beings and before the creation of Aegis. Not much is known about these two spirit races, but hopefully in the future we may know more.God then blew his own breath of existance into the hollow shell  and the Man lived. God then took the\nman and put him in the centre of all of Aegis, the same spot where The Cloud Temple now stands. God also made the Man a partner, from the exact same clay and the exact same breath, she was the first Woman.These simple beings could not talk, nor talk, nor \ndid they know much about the World. They roamed the world, enjoying its pre-historic beauty. Untouched, the fresh glades and dark thick forests were a wonder to set eyes on. Soon they had four children in the four corners of Aegis. Malin was the first of \ntheir sons, born in the deep forests of Aegis, he had blonde hair and sharp ears. Standing tall, he was the father of the Elves of Aegis. Krug was born in the scorching deserts of Aegis, fierce and relentless, he was the father of the Orcs. In the fresh \nmeadows of Aegis was born Horen, father of Humans. And lastly, in the deep underground caves of the World, Urguan was born, master of the Dwarves.\nWe do not know how they fathered sons and how their lines continued but perhaps God created a partner for\neach Son to continue their lines? Soon the first Man and Woman created by God departed Aegis and climbed the seven skies to meet with God their creator, for they had achieved their goal. The Sons began to explore the World of Aegis and each was stricken\nwith grief at the loss of their parents, seeking to fill the chasm of emotion inside them they began to create civilisation.\nAlthough none of the descendants of each Son had neither their long lives or splendour, they did carry their forefathers looks \nand traits. The Elves with Malin at their head travelled the lands and began naming all the inhabitants of this world, they were the first to create a tongue and began to name the trees and grass, the mountains and valleys across all of Aegis. At this\ntime, Malin, Urguan, Krug and Horen were not far apart in looks. Horen and Krug had darker hair than the other two and Krug was a bit larger in build. Malin had blonde hair and Urguan had red. They were all close companions, friends and had much love for \neach other and each others people.Urguan travelled into the deep underground of the earth, naming the beautiful ores and gems, sharing it with his brethren. Krug travelled the harsh deserts, testing his stamina and strength against the environment of\nAegis, and Horen began building the first squalid huts and villages in the valleys. The people lived for hundreds of years, in harmony and peace.Until the day where one of the great Daemons, great worshipers and squalid protectors of the void, kin spirits\nto the Aengul, defected.Iblees, who was once the greatest of all of God’s worshippers, was cast down into the mortal world, and so he was about to destroy the tranquility of Aegis forever. He came in the shape of a man. A gentle being who loved things\ndelicate, he was quickly befriended by Malin, Urguan and Horen. Krug had a great distrust of him but after many years of being convinced by his brothers, he finally accepted his company.\nLittle did Iblees know that this was all part of the Great God's\nplan to test the descendants of Man and Woman. Iblees wanted to destroy everything that God had designed and created, still not in a powerful physical form he began sowing seeds of evil in the minds of the Descendants. Prying on their needs and lusts.\nTo Malin, lover of nature, he promised him many children. He promised him that their laughter would resonate across their forest homes and that he would be content.\n Malin did not want anything more in this world than a bountiful amount of young to occupy\nthe barren forests.To Urguan, he promised infinite wealth of Gems and Ores, colours and crystals he could never imagine. To this, Urguan’s greed failed him. Horen, was anxious he would not live long enough to see his city completed and his lands increased\nHe needed more time, and Iblees struck the chord and promised immortality. Krug was the last to be tricked, Iblees tempted him with wealth, with food, with power. None interested Krug. Iblees was furious, he then challenged Krug in open combat in fury.\nThe whole world witnessed Ibleeses transformation into a hideous beast of the void. His skin turned into scales, fangs erupted and dark horns exploded from his head. He grew large wings and began to breath terrible fire. All stood in shock and horror. The\nbeast terrified all inhabitants of Aegis, animals fled and the Sons of the Descendants were too weak of blood to stand in front of such evil fury. Only Malin, Urguan, Horen and Krug stood in front of the terror. In Iblees’ right hand, a sword wrought in\nflame. Krug, naked and unarmed charged at The Daemon, throwing himself against the scalding scales of Iblees. He screamed in agony as his skin burned and decayed, becoming green and molten. Iblees was knocked off his feet, for the last time.\nHe stood up, heaving in rage and fury, flames exploded from the pores of his skin, he roared in anger and where he struck his sword the land collapsed and the ground erupted. And Lo, great was the Valour of Urguan and the strength of Krug as they fought \nthe beast.Powerful were the arms that carried Krug’s Axe and strong was Horens’ heart as they fought in open combat upon the plains of Aegis. Malin’s sword was fast and swift, smiting Iblees upon his breast and sides.For the thirty years the Descendants\nfought Iblees, the world became a war torn mess. The dead were defiled, summoned from their graves to follow Iblees’ mastery. The more mortal descendants died, the greater Iblees’ armys grew. It was a sad chapter in the World of Aegis, the brothers threw \nup their hands to God in prayer and in agony. Their creations destroyed, their children massacred. Woe to the Sons of Man and Woman. Krug grew bitter, his resentment of his brothers increased for befriending such a beast.Urguan grew greedy, he fought \nvalourously but began to hide his treasures underground. Malin feared for the lives of his children and did not leave his forest sanctuary. It seemed the world was destined for ruin. Iblees grew in strength and the Descendants weakened. Until one day. One\nday where the sound of horns could be heard from the far east. Beautiful horns echoing all across the skies of Aegis. God had heard their prayers. An army of Aengul and Daemons from the seven skies had appeared on the grand shores of Aegis. Their majesty \nand beauty so strong that all The Descendants could see was a flash of power white light erupt from the east. Only Malin with his strong sight could see the figures of light as they sped across the skies towards the armies of Iblees.\nIblees' soldiers\nburned where they stood, The thousands of skeletons and cadavers burst aflame and his living servants fled. There in the right middle of the world, he was cast down and chained by the Aengul and Daemon. A robed figure with brown hair and a black wreathed \napparation stood before him. They were a terrible image to look at, all shyed down and bowed in their majesty and glory. They stood before Iblees, knowing he was immortal and could not be killed, sentencing him to banishment to the void where his dark\nsoul would be forever trapped in torment. But..Before they could complete the sentence, Iblees stood tall for one last time. “You believe you may simply banish me from this plane and that my taint shall not last? I have touched this world with evil and it\nshall forever be part of the souls that inhabit it. Malin, I find no need to curse you, for so many of your kind have already willingly submitted to me now and will on my return in the distant future. The curse you will bear, is not of my doing, but of\nyour own. Even you have done my work, if you realize it or not.Urguan, your greed and lust shall overcome you, you are not worthy of the height God gave you, your descendants shall be short, squalid and ugly. You will always seek to find Gold and Gems in\nthe deep underground of the earth, your hunger never satisfied. Horen, the immortality you hope for will never arrive. Your longevity has already been stripped away thanks to the labors of the twisted heart of your brother Malin. You will age quickly and \ndie before you ever taste the fruits of your pointless labors. And you Krug, the most hated of the Descendants, you shall always have the lust of war. You are strong? Well the strength shall be used against your brothers, used to pillage and murder! Your \nlust for battle shall be unsatiated and your descendants shall grow ugly and heartless.\"As soon as his words were uttered, the world grew suddenly dark. A great roar erupted from Iblees’ mouth and in a blast of fire he was banished from the Mortal World. \nThe Descendants stood still for several moments, pondering over the words of Iblees. Malin, Krug, Urguan and Horen all knew naught of what the great Devil had spoken of when concerning Malin’s betrayal. Dismissing it as the lies of a demon propagated to\ncorrupt their souls, the brothers thought nothing further of Malin’s supposed treason. The robed figure stood before the four brothers. \"There is nothing in my power that may undo what that evil did. There is one thing I may do however, and that is to\nbless you. Malin, may your forests be a sanctuary of peace and your children long lived. Urguan, may your descendants be hardy, strong hearted and strong of mind. Krug, may your people show valour and honour where none exist. And Horen, may your people\none day explore the Seven Skies. I can do no more, for all my power is spent. We will go back to our master now, I believe your children may see us once more but long in the future.\" And with that, the great host of Aenguls and Daemons disappeared into\nthin wisps in the air.\n\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Natural Poem", "Draoen", "     Nature's Poem                                   The peaceful grass.\nThe blowing wind.\nBlooming flowers.\nAnd the tall trees.\nThe matters of them connect to one.\nNature, it's nature, for us all.\n\n   Linked Together\n\nA Druid serves nature.\nLinked to one.\nSame as the roots with trees.\nThe sun with plants.\nA family with support.\nAll is linked together.\n    Dandelion Fuzz\n\nBlooming with the wind\nNothing is sinned.\nThe fuzz flies and plants.\nNewcomers join to sprout and dance.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature", "Ierven Camoryn", "Nature\n=================\nBy: Ierven Camoryn\nNuts and beans\nAcorns and leaves\nTree-pecking birds\nUrchins with spines\nRhinos with horns\nEveryday!\nTrees with apples\nRocks with moss\nEvery tree hasa leaf\nEvery rock is stoney\nGrasshoppers\nRabbits\nAcorns\nSnakes\nSnails\nShrooms red \nHow brown?\nRed I said!\nOr brown?\nOr red!\nMaybe both.\nSpiders are cool\nPrickly sometimes\nI know this for sure\nDirt is their friend\nEverone                         misunderstands\nRory the poor spider", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature", "[[Dedicant]] Cillion Chase", "There is no place in this world as relaxing as the forest, the birds whisteling, no fighting, no conflicts.\nAnd yet, some people wich to destroy nature, taking away the peace from the land, gaining nothing. Like the birds whistle in the forest, they whist\nnowhere else. The leaves falling down the trees, the silence of the forest, such a silence wich cannot be found anywhere else.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature Healing", "The Sequoia Druid", "The Nature of Druidic Healing\n\n\nBy: Lillith Winterleaf, the Sequoia Druid\nDruidic Healing is a topic many are curious about, but few, even among Druids, wholly understand the cost on the one performing it.  Most Druids use living reagents to assist in the mending process, this is so that they can draw on the living essense \nwithin the reagent and draw less from themselves, but a skilled Druidic healer can also safely mend wounds without any reagents onhand.\n\nDruidic healing is, quite simply, an exchange of living essence from the healer to the patient.\nThe Druid focuses upon the living tissue around the wound and asks the patient's body to mend itself, giving of their own living essence to aid in the healing.\n\nThis is where the cost and risk of Druidic healing is, and you should heed it well.\nA druid is capable of amazing feats of healing at times, but all of our healing derives from ourselves and it is possible to cause lasting damage to oneself while healing the wounds of another. This is why living reagents are used, to lessen the need to \ndraw from oneself.\nI have personally healed a variety of wounds, from cuts and scrapes to sword clashes to broken bones to some far worse. They all draw from you to some extent, even when using reagents. \nSimple wounds you may not even notice the drain upon yourself, it depends how strong your healing has become, and how familiar you are with the process. Practice truly makes perfect in this regard.\nI am of the belief an example of a mistake is the best way to pass on a lesson, so I will share how I nearly lost my life while healing the King of Skravia back on Asulon. He and some of his soldiers had been lured out to the base\nof a rogue mage that had harassed their kingdom for a long while. When they gained the upper hand and cornered the mage, his trap was revealed, the walls of his base were lined with explosives, and he detonated them.\nThree of the soldiers died in the blast, the others dragged a severely injured but alive King back to Skravia and to my inn, knowing I was a healer. His wounds were great, part of his skull was visible on the top of his head,\nand he had shrapnel wounds all across his body, blood everywhere. There was nothing to do but try, I coated the wounds with a mixture of living slime and bonemeal, then began the healing process, \ndrawing upon the living essences as well as myself to stabilize and mend the kind King laying before me. It took nearly two hours, but his wounds sealed and he was alive and left in a coma for a few days.\nHowever, I had given of myself far more that I should have, my skin was pale, almost white, and my eyes had lost most of their color and I could hardly breathe. I am told I lost consciousness, I awoke five weeks later, my every limb sore, \nmy hair a shade lighter, but my skin almost back to its normal tone. A fellow Druid, Jena, had to come out to restore some living essence to me or I would have perished within the first day or two, I was so weakened.\nTake my mistake with you if you seek to heal, you give of yourself, there is a limit you will reach and you should not go beyond it or you risk your life.  Find your balance between personal sacrifice and reagents to ease your burden, \nyou almost always have something available to ease your personal strain, even if it is the grass around you or a planter of flowers outside the window, any living essence can ease your burden \nif the situation is a bit beyond your ability to heal safely with nothing on hand.\n\n\n~Lillith Winterleaf, the Sequoia Druid", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature Poem", "Cotman Harfoot", "Acorns o Acorns\nThey really are not \nvery fond of thorns\nThey are like nature\nand tend to be quite mature when you harvest them\nBut dont confuse them with other nuts \nBecause that'll make them go nuts\nJust remember,\n\nNuts are your friend\nNot food", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature Poems", "[Dedicant] Mith'Laku \"Mith\"", "The Pond\n\nA light pitter patter\nBreaks the night.\n\nA dragonfly,\nit rests for flight.\n\nThe Bullfrog croaks\nits final song,\nAs the moon begins to yawn.\n\nAll is quiet, all is still.\nFor the pond has had her fill.\n\nThe crane, it rests, its weary head, on the\nmakeshift water bed.\n\nEven the lark,\nits chest so bright,\nquiets its song, \njust for the night.\nAll is quiet, all is still.\nFor the pond has had\nher fill.\n\nBut suddenly a sharp\n\"HOO-WOO. HOO-WOO\".\nA flash of wings, \na tiny squeak.\nThe Owl grasps the \nmouse, so meek.\n\n\nThe Bullfrog lets loose a croak,\nbut soon falls down the old Crane's throat!\n\nThe meadowlark, it takes to flight, away and deep into the night.\n\nThe dragonfly it zips away, \nTo be seen another day.\nNone is quiet, none is still.\nEven the pond can't have her fill.\n\nFor each night brings, a cacophonic storm,\nfor that is Nature,\nin its purest form.\n\n-Mith\n\nThe Wind\n\nA measly plant, so small in grain, begins the lively train.\n\nThe seeds they blow,\nacross the field,\na crop that only a mouse can yield.\n\n\nThe Mouse swallows the seeds, and runs,\nfor he is not the only one, \nthat heeds the winds of old.\n\nThe Owl, he rides as darkness drops, \nthe wind his only ally throbs,\nand covers the noise his kill spills.\nBut back to the seed, for it grows so tall,\ncrop for one, \nstart for all.\n\nThe Deer, it grazes on the grass, grown by wind, and rain, at last.\nThe wind carries the scent to his nose,\na scent he all to well knows.\nThe Wolf, it hunts the little field deer. Relying on the wind for him to steer,\nin the right direction for his meal. \nHe lays down at night, his muzzle bloodied, thanking the wind, \nhis only buddy.\n\nFor this is how the wind is here, \nand there,\nand far, \nand near.\n\n-Mith", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Break", "Monk Scribe", "Nature's Break\n\nAs the sun arises,\nthe moon falls,\nas the warmth starts,\nthe cold fades,\nas the leaves are bore,\nthe frost thaws,\nAegis rotates.\n\n- Written by LaTerre Amant in Aegis.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Calling", "[Dedicant] Llumaria", "Nature calls through the wind and trees,\nIt calls with every buzzing bee.\nIt is to me as gulls to the sea.\nA most basic attraction, a harmony.\n\nThe rabbit grows by eating grass,\nBut grows to feed the teeming mass,\nOf predators that roam the ground,\nWho will someday become a mound.\n\nAnd from the fertile soil and dirt,\nNew life will surely come to spurt,\nTo help again, the rabbit grow,\nThus keeping on life's constant flow.\nThe way of life and death goes on.\nIt is natures call. It is nature's song.\nIt is to me as leaves on a tree.\nNature is a living part of me.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Life", "Lilliana", "Nature is but many things\nA guide who leads us\nA guard who protects us\nA mother who provides us\nThe guide in nature\nCarries us forth\nHe teaches us right and wrong\nHe carries us forth\nHe shows us how to hunt\nThe Guard in nature\nProtects us from danger\nHe shows us safe from unsafe\nHe teaches us how to be strong\nThe Mother in nature\nProvides us with life\nShe gives us food and love\nShe gives us hope\nNature is many faces\nNature is many lives\nEach life continues\nFrom root to leaf\nFrom nose to tail\nNature lives in all of us\nHer life is ours\nWithout her, we will never be the same", ""),
        StaticBook("Nature's Rule", "[Teen] Dedicant Yaehahn Signus", "The promise of life\nbring a few to this dense, mysterious forest.\nFor those that are unaware, Nature is the ruler of these lands.\nGentle, it will share its wisdom with the few that choose to listen.\nForgiving, it will bring back life to the dead\nand forgotten as the seasons cycle in their eternal dance.\nProtected, it will guard its inhabitants from those that only seek chaos and destruction. A fair warning, however, to those that wish to wander into Nature's welcoming arms,\ngive back the same \nwhich is given.\nBe gentle to the budding flower as it shyly awakens from its slumber.\nBe forgiving to the hungry birds that may take what they need from your hand.\nBe protective, for Nature can be vulnerable when its least expects it.\nChoose to listen,\nhear the rustling whispers of the trees as they laugh in the wind. \nAnd the silent deer moving into the distance, \nin search of a place to rest its head for another night.\nBare trees seem to rise towards the \nclouds,\nbathing in misted morning light.\nAnimals stalk silently in their territory, wary of both friend and foe.", ""),
        StaticBook("Natures Balance", "Rindir", "\n\n\n\n  Natures Balance\n          by: Rindir\nAlike the six descendant races,\nThere exists six trees to hold their places:\n\nOak,\nBirch,\nSpruce,\nJungle,\n\nAnd according to the tale,\n\nAcacia,\nAnd dark oak are said to hail.\n\nThe trees can be appointed to the six,\nAnd with that nature will achieve its balanced fix:\n\nOak to Human,\nBirch to Elf,\nSpruce to Dwarf,\nJungle to Kha’,\nDark Oak to Orc,\nand Acacia to Halfling.\n\nAnd with these six beautiful tree,\nThe rest of the world will see,\nThe wonderous forest created by thee.", ""),
        StaticBook("Natures Ode", "Terreni Decorum", "Morning springs with hope anew\n\nTo melt the chill of winters dew. \n\nThe great tree wakes from slumbers peace\n\nWhile blooms of color shows their fleece. \nNo more will winter's chill cause the world to still. \n\nWhy you ask , should spring flow in such a way\n\ntis the circle of which things grow, death of one another to sew\nThere you have the poem nature's ode \n\nSilence to follow while darkness calls, life stills beneath the shadows\n\nThe third page reached so no more shall follow. ", ""),
        StaticBook("Natures Way!", "Isengar Brockfall", "\n\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n         Journey to \n      The Forbidden Lands\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n\n\nBy Isengar Brockfall\nDay 1\n\n\nLife. Life is a mysterious thing don't you think?\n\nWhen that one special day leads up to a perfect moment and you want it to never end.\nI mean you probably want to know more about me, who am I? you might ask.\n\nWell i'm a Halfling, I have long brown hair, blue eyes and I am 67\nyears of age.\nWhich is obviously quite young for a halfling.\nWell I live in a Beautiful village called Gimblewood, a place of peace.\nUs Halflings tend to stay out of the big world, not knowing much about the famous Bandits or Orcs out to get us.\nI awoke and reached out for my small mug of clean water.\nA sense of joy came to my body as I slurped it up.\n\nI got out my squeaky bed and picked up my brown, long smooth pipe.\n\nI shoved some grated cactus green in the end, and held the pipe to my lantern.\nI walked across my spruce floor board and opened my door.\nA nice breeze hugged against my delicate face.\nI stood leaning on my door, glancing at every movement whilst smoking my pipe.\nEvery smoke I took, felt as if I were sucking in more energy.\nMy head got a buzzing sensation and I felt a relaxation in my body.\nI waved my pipe in the air and blew it until the smoke stopped.\nI placed it down on my cabenet on the outside of my door.\n\nI reached inside my burrow and grabbed my red silky jacket.\nIt has never had a stain on it and I like to keep that way!", ""),
        StaticBook("Needing a Task", "Lego_XBOX", "\"Good whatever time...\n\nI'm Vel'Aryla Kameki and my teacher has asked me to get a task from you. Whenever you have a moment... could you give me a task to do...?\"\n\n~Vel'Aryla Kameki", ""),
        StaticBook("Neo-Druidism", "Tristanm1", "Neo-Druidism\nBy Salmon Druid Petyr\nWith some modern druidic practices wandering astray, it has been necessary for me to scribe another text - that of Neo-Druidism. Throughout my travels and my meditative studies, I have had revelations unknown to others. It is important to understand that\nthere is a special type of balance that druids themselves remember to study - action and non-action. Let me clarify. Action is motion, the motion of restoring balance, and of being the hand of Nature. Non-action is meditation and silence; invisible\nmotion. You see, this is the only Way to truly understand Nature, the Universe, the \"It\". For, through non-action you may feel \"It\", and through action you may be \"It\".  Call this \"It\" what you want; most druids will call it Nature. What is important is\nto understand what Nature truly is, and how it relates to you. To reveal this is the goal of this tome.\nSection 1 - The Unity of Nature\nThe first fault in thinking is to make distinctions - those between you and Nature, between life and death, between good and evil.  Everything is Nature, despite what any given piece believes. A druid does not use nature: a\ndruid is merely a piece who realizes the profound truth. Through non-action, one may truly understand this concept and feel it course through them. However, while meditating in non-action, one also separates themselves from the flow; the Way; the path\nthat Nature chooses for its pieces. To rejoin with Nature, one must practice action. \nSection 2 - The Way of Nature\nNature does what it will, and while we realize this while meditating, sometimes we forget in action. We are merely moving along the river of life, the tides of balance. While we believe that we are restoring the balance, it \nis balance restoring itself in an everlasting self-conflict. Thus, through action, we are the hands of Nature, of balance, of the Way. One must not think of \"using\" Nature while playing their part, but of \"being\" Nature. This is a common mistake as well\namong young druids and non-druids. They think to restore the world by planting lots of trees... but that is not balance, nor is it even the way to achieve balance. Action alone is not the Way, for one forgets the purpose of action. Only with the mix of\naction and non-action can one achieve the goal: to have your cake and eat it, too.\nSection 3 - The Druid\nSo as we can now see, the Druid is simply the most important hand, the piece Nature uses to govern itself. To truly try to understand is a task that lies within meditation and non-action. However, there is a limit to the mental reach\nof the pieces, for as no one truly knows all of themselves, Nature does not know all of itself. This is a fairly important observation: that even though Nature is unified, the unification is marked by threadlines of dubiousness and doubt. The Neo-Druid \nshould not fret, however. There are uneven surfaces in every river. No tree has identical leaves. Just so, nothing, no \"It\", can be the symmetrical perfection one would like to see. Randomness and uniqueness are inherent in Nature, as defining as the\nunity itself. Paradoxical though it may seem, through balance of action and non-action, it shall be revealed to you, and only then will you understand the phrase...\n\n\n\"All is One\"", ""),
        StaticBook("Nether Star", "Cloven", "      Nether Star\n    Research notes\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~      Written by Elvi\n        Terryal\n\n *A detailed drawing of the object is on this             page*\nWhat is the object?\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nThe object appears to be a type of gem, rather purplish and resembling much like a heart. According to a few people, this gem is found within a foul creature wielding three heads, most likely one of Iblee's servants or\npart of his undead army. Could this possibly be the heart of this creature?\nFrom observance, the gem appears to be fairly normal, aside from resembling a little bit of a heart. However, I do feel a strange yet eerie aura radicating from it. Could there\nbe something within the gem or is it something used to store the creature's energy in? This energy, or perhaps the gem itself, could be used within Alchemy, magic and perhaps further. Hence why I am studying this object.\nResearch 1#\n~~~~~~~~~\nFrom what I know, this gem is from a creature with three heads, mostly known as a 'Wither', a dark creature that is said to be in league with Iblees. This gem comes from the Wither whenever someone would defeat it. \nHowever, it is unsure on what this item is used for. Could it be used as an Alchemy symbol (then what would it represent? What element would it be?)? Would this be used as part of magic, either light or dark magic? Or does it dive much deeper into \nsomething that perhaps we do not understand. That is something I am hoping to find out during my time researching it.\nFor the time been, I shall refer to the gem as the 'NetherStar' as it would be likely that the Wither is from the Nether and the gem does\nvagely resembles a star from some angles.\n\n\n-Written by Elvi Terryal\n\nDate: 12th of Sun's smile 1484\nTheory number one\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nThe energy theory\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nOne of the few theories I have came up with about this gem is about a potental energy hidden within.\nNow, think about the Wither. This gem can supposedly be its heart, what keeps it \nalive. That energy within can be a type of life source, something that keeps the wither alive. If we can somehow extract this energy from this gem, then we may be able to use this energy for either good or bad intentions, depending on who wants to use it \nand why. I believe a way of extracting this supposed energy can be through the use of magic, or someone who can extract energy from things and possibly can transfer the energy from the gem into something else.\nHowever, judging that the energy is from a \nwither, I cannot guarentee that this energy can be used for good purposes as it is from a very dark creature.\nI am planning on asking a mage of some sorts about this theory. However, I just need to know where one is and ask. Hopefully they will  explain\nabout the process and whenever or not it can be possible. I suppose only time will know.\n\n- Written by Elvi Terryal\n\nDate: 11th of Grand Harvest 1485", ""),
        StaticBook("Neutrality", "Aeran Winterleaf", "Neutrality\n~~~~~~~~\nThe trees fall\nBabies crawl\n\nTreaties exchanged\nNatue is unchanged\n\nFor it does not care\nAs it would appear\n\nNature does not care about your quarrels\nNor your battles\nIt cares not about pride\nIt has no side\n\nAs long as balance remains\nDruids need not complain\n\nNeutrality.", ""),
        StaticBook("New Elven L. V-1", "Zahir Darthirii", "In elven grammatical structure is often different to that of common. For one instead of placing an adjective before a noun it is placed after said noun. This becomes particularly important for the most basic of greetings.\nKarin'ayla - Goodmorning\nKer'ayla - Goodnight\nVan'ayla - Goodbye\n\nEach places the time of day before the adjective describing it. Noun and adjective are separated by an apostrophe.\n\nOther important words for the beginner in elven include race names:\n\nMali - Elf\nUruk - Orc\nValah - Human\nBortu - Dwarf\n\nIn order to form sub-races of elves, adjectives are taken to add more detail to the word Mali.\n\nMali'ame - Wood Elf\nMali'aheral - High Elf\nMali'ker - Dark Elf\n\nIn order to form sub-races of other cultures the suffix tali- may be used. This word most directly means 'part of'.\n\nTali'uruk - Goblin (Lit. Part Orc)\nTali'valah - Halfling (Lit. Part Human)\n\nOther basic phrases include exclamations including:\n\nAyla - Good\nSul'leyun - Brilliant (Lit. Beautiful Light)\n", ""),
        StaticBook("New Moon", "Zane Capette", "May thy light be fair to me!\nMay thy course be smooth to me!\nIf good to me is thy beginning,\nSeven times better be thine end,\nThou fair moon of the seasons,\nThou great lamp of grace!\nHe Who created thee\nCreated me likewise;\nHe Who gave thee weight and light\nGave to me life and death,\nAnd the joy of the seven satisfactions,\nThou great lamp of grace,\nThou fair moon of the seasons.", ""),
        StaticBook("NEWS", "Amoss", "NEWS:\n\nIBLEES, fearedevil God has escaped his cage in the north! All citizents are advised to stay within the city walls and to not venture on dangerous quests!!! STAY TUNED FOR MORE INFO", ""),
        StaticBook("No Songs", "Dio Astóre", "Fathers. Brothers. To here have come.\nVoice of creation whispers. Silence - a brittle glass in shattering - undone; tears away at flesh as reflection scattered 'fore feet and eye.\n\nStifled song for children longed and distant the hand that caresses so fondly the growing thing; sing for the green few.\nLonely star amidst far pallid heavens; mournful silver, dreaming of golden waves; dream itself of days golden gone.\n\nFathers and Mothers here are none.\nNot abyssal fire relieve the ear from the howling, unsettled, fretful and frightful; lurks in changing darks that not light alters.\n\nRemoved earth; chasms ravinous, wide and gaping maw stolen from sanctuary's prideful estate; precious the nothing-color wistful; falling crystal.\n\nSwooning, frays cannot delay. Bark cease! Delay. Embers for sand show no clear shapes in rejuvenation, exhultation: lame you our Father's shame!\n\nSlow wavering hope, fog shifting; alike twisting hidden-water shan't be red-stained - blossoming steel rose; foes begot by another.\n\nOh Mother! From hence forth lead us away, not astray to escape belated those doom fated in hatred grasped; abated for neither Son nor sun, or daughter's laugh.\n\nNo error in our unblighted spirit; merit with it go, we know and so under storm a sail we had set. Forget not these drums light.\nHum. Hum. Old life undone. Fathers and Brothers, Heroes and Mothers to here have come.\n\nBut no songs by children sung.\nNo songs.", ""),
        StaticBook("Nobility Vol 1", "Farley III Stafyr", "  On the Nature of\n      Nobility in an\n     Ignoble World\n\n        Written by\n Archduke Edward\n         Winter II\n\n          Edited by\nArchbishop Castus\nNobility is by nature an honor granted to the meritocratic few. Those with much to offer are given the land by which to realize their loyalty to the Crown, and the status to recognize them among the glorious peer group that is the nobility.\nThis is the basis upon which nobility is created.\n\nThere have been inglorious moments, when those thought to be honorable and the great showed themselves as lustful and slothful. This is not the nature of nobility.\nNobility is to be a pure state, by which the great of the country serve those who need serving. The nobles are not but lords above the inferior, but have an innate feudalistic duty to protect and further the interests of their vassals, noble and ignoble.\nThe true noble of Oren is to be virtuous, honorable, racially pure, devout to the Church, and loyal to his liege and crown. The true noble aims to amass prestige, not power. To make a name for one's noble house as a truly noble one is to glorify one's \nancestors and arrange a future for one's progeny. The honorable lord or lady, or those of their kin, should endeavour to embody the following tenets of nobility:\n\n•Proper etiquette in the home and in the public eye.\n•Serving promptly and amply the just needs of their liege and Crown.\n\n•Acting always in a manner befitting of one's noble status.\n\n•Just and kindly treatment of one's vassals, noble or ignoble.\n•Just and kindly treatment of all which one meets, noble or ignoble.\n\n•Diplomatic attitude towards one's peers and neighbours, friend and foe alike.\n•Knowledge of happenings within the kingdom, present and past.\n\n•Devout and worshipful observance of the Creator and his Church.\n•Honesty and oath-worthiness in all instances.\n\n•Good Management of one's estate and demesne, physical and moral.\n\n•A hunger for power, but instead for honor.\nTo make a name for one's noble house as a dwelling of sin is to dishonor one's family. To besmirch one's family name is a personal dishonor second to none in a noble's life. The honorable lord or lady, or the kin of those, should endeavour to avoid \nthe following:\n\n•Wasteful consumption or gluttony which drains the coffers.\n\n•Speech or action unbefitting of a noble in the public eye.\n•Sodomy or scandal which paints a stain upon one's name.\n\n•Mistreatment of one's vassals, noble or ignoble.\n\n•Lust for power, which causes discord and shows one unfavourably.\n•Maligning of others, noble or ignoble, without just cause.\n\n•Failure to amply serve the just needs one's liege and Crown.\n\n•Irreverence to the Church which marks one as heretical.\n•Mismanagement of one's own demesne which proves ineptitude.\n\n•Breaking of one's oaths which marks one as dishonest.\nThe following of this counsel will render one an honorable and noble lord or lady befitting of his title or his family's status. The observing of this document is sure to generate great prestige for one's house as a family deserving of its\nstatus, titles, and holdings, and similar counsel should be given to those children born to a noble house. To rid Oren of sinful and improper nobles, there must be an effort to create virtuous and honorable nobles.\nThis must be an effort not by the state to enforce honor among nobles, but by nobles to encourage honor among one's kin and peers.\n\nDeus Vult\n\n-Archduke Edward Winter II", ""),
        StaticBook("Nobility.", "Napoleon Fournier d'Avenese", "NOBLE CUSTOMS AND A GREATER STATE\n_____________________________By Napoleon Fournier d'Avenese\n_____________________________Squire To His Lordship of Aldersburg\n_____________________________Hello, sires, of this nation. Clergy, gentry, nobility, and all \nothers interested. This book is to explain the concept of Fuedalism, and lay out edicts for the perfect fuedal monarchy. It is also to explain to nobles and higher clergly how to behave and interact with the lower orders and their respective lieges. \nI sincerly hope that you enjoy this book, for I am probably to print it and produce many at the store of Jacob Chapel. If any would wish to see me about this piece, I am usually in the Avenese Estate near Pibblebonk or #I Waterfall Avenue, Aldersburg.\nI.The Concept of Fuedalism___________________                                This chapter shall be short for it should be known to all. \n\nThere is no commonly accepted modern definition of feudalism, at least among scholars.\nIt is commonly, and simply\ndefined, as the nobility, in exchange for protection, swear fealty and provide labour and military power in exchange for counties and duchies. In some fuedalistic socities, kings and emperors were little more than grand dukes, with the meeting, the king\nand the nobles together having the power. However, fuedalism does seem to be on the decline, due to the absoulitist nature of the imperial monarchy. In fuedal society, the heirarchy could be described as:\nEmperor\nKing\nArchduke\nPrince\nGrand Duke\nDuke\nCount\nLord Mayor\nMayor\nGentry\nSmall Landowners\nFree Subjects\nPeasants\nII.Edicts of Nobility.\n\nI.He must be gregaroius and socially competant, and speak in a noble and \"posh\" manner (as some blackguards would call it)\n\nII.He must know the code of chivalry, save for the paragraph which says he must serve the welfare of all. \nHe is free to do as he wishes with his peasants and other subjects, mistreating them is discouraged but it is not an edict to not do so.\n\nIII.He must distrivute his land amoung the gentry and other landowners and delegate tasks to his courtiers.\n\n\nIV.He must mantain a full court, and hear the advice of his courtiers, for they are gentlemen and women, and their word should be respected. \n\nV.A nobleman must seek the consel of his liege before making a major decision, including marriages. However, if \na monarch would abuse their power, it is both their right and duty to fight against the tyranny and give more power to the nobility. Just as one delegates tasks and wealth to courtiers, a king must do so with his nobles. The monarch and his nobles must be\nin balance.\n\nVI.A noble must obey the head of house except in exceptional circumstances. \n\nVII.A noble must be an advocate for the fuedal system in every aspect, organizing his army in this way.\nVIII.He must not assosciate with peasantry or other men of the lower order.\n\nIX.The head of house should not occupy himself with martial prowess or entrance into the clergly-this is the duty of the younger sons.\n\nX.He must instead focous on his \nadministrative skills.\n\nXI.He must seek to be pious and faithful, for a noble should get to the fifth heaven at least.\n\nXII.As well as this, he must be able to attend mass and read the Holy Scriptures regularly, and teach it to his subjects, inspiring his\nlevies with such things.\n\nIII.Principles of A Stable State________________________\nI have read many books which stress the importance of absoloute monarchy. Of despots, and absoloute power. However, I think this not be true. I think it prudent that a \ntable of nobles, all the lords of the realm, decide together, with the ruler holding meeting regularly with noblemen. The reason absoloute monarhcy is bad is because it is brilliant if given to an able leader, an able emperor. But, if it falls under the \ncontrol of a usurper, his nobles are powerless and even then, if he is a mere bad monarch, and no usurper, the effect is much the same. The tyrant will abuse his power and inspire not with his wisdom but with his power and intimidation, many gallant \nnoblemen will be imprisoned.\n\nFurthermore, a noble should have a similar system with his gentry. He must seek their consel before making a move, and must build a good relationship with his gentry, who will then indeed serve their lieges gratefully and \nproesperity will come from the estates and martial prowess from the sons and brothers of these landed gentry. \n\nSuch a relationship should also be given to a noble's wife, who may be given administrative and diplomatic tasks. Of course, according to the \ncode of chivalry, we should not risk the lives of the ladies. \n\nHonour is an important aspect, and a duke or above should be able to call the crown out on matters of honour. Whilst not going so far as dueling, as a royal's death would cause instability, \nnoble must be a paragon of honour and hope, and must make sure his liege is. And vice versa. One should improve the other, with the monarch seeking the consel of the nobles and the nobles seeking the guidance and loyalty to their liege.\n\nIn order to keep \nfuedal order and keep the loyalty of the subjects, absoloute monarchy must stop. \n\nNo one but dukes and above should swear direct alligience to the emperor. The dukes and above must, with the counts swearing to them, the minor nobles doing to them, the \ngentry, the peasants etc. This also, suprisingly, helps the monarch, for he has no unworthy subjects unneedily wasting his time. An emperor must be in a high, distant place, the dukes closer, the counts closer and all the way down to the small landowners,\ndirectly taking responsability for serfs. This is the ideal fuedal government, the one which embodies fuedalism the most, and the greatest enforcement of fuedalism. \n\nBut you may ask, why would the monarch ever agree to this? Well, that is a simple \nquestion, sire, and for that, I have the answer.\n\nIn return for the greater power, the nobles must give a greater amount of troops, mostly levies, to the cause. If we focous on conscripting the levies in times of war, and follow this system in which \nvassals must provide more, perhaps we can double the size of our forces. \n\nI have also written a book documenting my principles on the organization of the army. \n\nIt is to be printed soon. If you enjoy this, please read that.\nAnd with that, sires, I bid you aideu.\n\n\n\n__________________________________________________________[!]A sketch of a miltiaman in a gambeson and with spear and shield is seen here[!]", ""),
        StaticBook("Norgaladir", "Norgaladir Nólatári", "Hi could I recieve a ferrum sword with unbreaking (whatever level you would like) and sharpness. I will pay 200 minas upon delivery", ""),
        StaticBook("Northal, Hanseti", "Aetosion", "The Lands of Northal in Anthos, A Compendium of Environmental and Historical Information\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\nby The Rt. Hon. Edward, second of his name, of the noble House Winter of the lands herein writ. \nNorthal --- A History\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\n       Chapter I\nNorthal, previously within the demesne of the Kingdom of Hanseti, was never properly settled. In fact, the past human habitation of Northal is so entirely unremarkable that the writer isn't even sure the purpose of this passage. Previously unsettled, and \nonly recognized as arctic wasteland \"Wall-Adjacent\", it was unused by its lords, left as ice and stone. It was not until the modern lords and ladies of House Winter, and their hardy bannermen and vassals approached the steep and snowy cliffs, and said,\n\"Yes, this is land. It can be tamed like any other,\" and so it was. The tundras remained just as glacial, but the indomitable human spirit prevailed against the elements. The County of Northal was chartered by his grace, the Duke Codrik Green, for his new\nfound vassal, the Rt. Hon. Lord Robert Winter, son of Harold.\n\nNorthal --- A Land\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\n       Chapter II\nThe lands of Northal stretch from the old Teuton bridge due east of the Castle Greywyn, to the open seas of Valence off the eastern coast of the Kingdom of Oren. Defining the Southward border is the sharp shift in flora and fauna notable where snow begins\nto fall south of the wall. The northern border is, of course, the North Icewall. Built by whatever force for whatever reason, no human who has dared summit it has come back entirely intact. These borders, effective by edict of his grace, the Duke Codrik\nGreen, determine the bounds of the land. \nNorthal --- A Climate\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~\n      Chapter III\nThe climate of Northal is the most entirely inhospitable south of the Wall. Any man, elf, or dwarf idiotic enough to build their home within these lands comes out a member of a hardened folk. The lands are infertile and barren, a layer of permafrost rests\njust beneath the soil, requiring intensive labour to render what sparse deposits of nutrient poor soil there are in Northal, usable for planting. However, it is still somehow managed, but what magics of a massed peasantry it cannot be fathomed. The \navailable land to be built upon is rocky and irregular. To traverse it is difficult, and to establish is even moreso. Excluding the pockets of flatland within Northal (namely, Ice Lake, Snowy Pass, and Bridge Gully), all other locales must be mined into \nwith a strong iron pick to break through the frozen stone and ice. It is postulated that these rare pockets are the result of glacial movements centuries past, which carved out basins into the terrain, and later melted to water the fields of Ager. The \nhigh temperatures of the land are rivaled only by the peaks of the Silver Mountains of Furnestock, and at only at one month during the year in the height of summer does the temperature rise above freezing, enough so for the ice of Northal to sweat and run\nthin streams down the cliffs into Ager, the Greymarsh, the Raev, and Norfolk. Lower down the mountains these streams gather into stillwater pools, which will continue to provide irrigation for southward farmlands, even when the water in the high up lands \nof Northal remains frozen. It is said that if you piss towards the Castle Greywyn in the dead of night, from any peak in Northal, that the ghost of the Hochmeister Mirtok will freeze your piss into a spear and the winds will blow it back into you, running\nyou through with your own piss. A nastier death a man could never fathom. The mountains of Northal are home to the frozen corpses of hermits, and the bundled men of the Winter, and none other.\nAn Author's Note.\n~~~~~~~+~~~~~~~~Father, why didn't you move us to Salvus. There is a complete lack of immoral Southeron noblewomen. Send help.\n-The Rt. Hon. Edward Winter II\n\n*The Snowflake Sigil\nof House Winter*", ""),
        StaticBook("Northman's Stash", "Ulfrík de Pepe", "-=The North Stash=-\n\n\nVenture to the land where the beards grow strong and the midgets grow small.\n\nMain city is not your friend. The falls are flowing smoother.\nFollow the rocky bridge until you have reached the shroom land.\n\nLeave the bottom! Kazimir's pass can be followed two ways, the wrong way, or the right way!\nThe light is blinding.. Make it stop when it is seen. Hills are made to be climbed... \n\nReach the peak for that is the true location.", ""),
        StaticBook("Note", "Izier Vincrute", "Resia I believe there is love for everyone. However I will respect your wishes and not pursue you. However I would very much like you to accompany me for the festival. \n\nSorry for the gift... I know its not much.\n\n~Izier\n*The crystal that he offered before is laid in the chest. A small leather cord is tied around it forming it into a necklace. The crystal shined in the light sparkling and twinkling*", ""),
        StaticBook("Note to Aria", "Glacio Nereus", "Dear Aria,\nI know I only knew you for a little bit so far. You're really pretty, and pretty funny, also pretty damn fiesty. I like that, I like that a lot, almost as much as I like you. I also want to date you, officially. That'd be neat if you said yes.\nI'm not the best writer, so this'll probably sound kind of simple or dumb. I would tell you all of this myself if I saw you, but this is just in case somebody else sees you, and not me personally. I'm sorry if you don't like that it isn't in person, but I\ndo really, really like you for how long we've known eachother. I want to get to know you better, spend more time with you, and get closer to you. \n\n[!] A heart is drawn, and a frowny face is drawn next to it.[!] Check one off. -Glacio", ""),
        StaticBook("Note to Dak'ir", "[Sorcerer] Nauron", "Dear Dak'ir and Alakagh,\n\nI will be back shortly to the city. For now I have took on a personal mission to gather any intel at all of Iblees and plans, even the littlest bit of information. I will not appear as I was before, and will be \nwearing an iron mask in case of any bad characters. I will come back to serve my duty as a part of the Div'Cruan as shortly as possible. \n\nVan'Ayla\n-Nauron", ""),
        StaticBook("Note to Phaedrus", "Turin", "*the letter is written in dark green ink, probably concocted of some forest plant.*\n\"Phaedrus,\nI joined this force with eager eyes and a hopeful disposition. I feel I have done my duty as a guard well, be it patrolling the Elven lands or marching behind \nyou in battle. \nI have been with you for nearly four years now, and I have seen a shift in you, an unpleasant one. I obeyed your each and every command, but I am done. I see now that we are no different from the Silver... The Bronze is merely just a new\nhead fit poorly upon the same chicken. I fought for freedom, yet I see Elvenkind slaughtered for their beliefs. Revenge was not what I thought your leadership would bring. We were treated as lesser by the Aheral, but what do we treat them as now?\nMalin would frown upon this \"Era of the Chosen.\" I frown upon it as well. \nI do see that you will remain leader, perhaps for many years. Think upon what I have written, I hope it will point out flaws that I see. Maybe you will pay them heed, maybe you \nwill not. But remember why my brothers have died in battle, remember what they fought for so that YOU might be in power. Do not make their spilt blood be for nothing.\n-Turin\"\n*a badge is laid on the paper, as well as a shiny leaf\nmade of metal. It seems to be cracked in half, with red paint flowing from the cracks, \"I hope you see what I see from this, llir.\" is written by it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Note-Block Organ", "The Musician", "    How To Tune A\n  Note-Block Organ\n\nA note-block organ is a simple instrument, actually composed of several complex boxes which produce a single note each. These boxes can be tuned to produce most any note on the scale.\nIn order to tune each box, one must turn a crank once to the right for each step up the octave.\n\nFor simplicity's sake, this book only records the C major scale, but their range begins at F# below C, and can be turned 24 times before returning to F#.\nIt is interesting to note that the box's tone changes depending on what material they sit upon. Wood produced a deep, resonating sound, while simple dirt and most other materials creates a sound that is the closest to an actual organ or harp.\n     C MAJOR SCALE\n\nC: 6 turns\nD: 8 turns\nE: 10 turns\nF: 11 turns\nG: 13 turns\nA: 15 turns\nB 17 turns\nC: 18 turns", ""),
        StaticBook("NOTES", "Yeam'lar", "I Av Foind A Bak Way Inta Da City, But I Ned Tiome To Get It Wide Enough. I Av Becom A Citezen Ta Da City And  Et es noice Ear. Da Flowers Ar Liv An Da Birds Sing Wen Me Round. I Avent erd From Ye Fo Wile An Ma Wonder Is Ye Stil No Im Ear. Da Folks Ar Nic\nDo nut want ta kill moy, Oh No, I Av Ta Go, *Blood Is Spattered On The Page.", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes on 'Allah'", "Jan", "'Allah turned to the sky, and it had been smoke. He said to it and to the earth to come. They combined, the stars and the planets, the Heavens too. Allah created the sun, the moon, and the planets, each with their own individual courses.'\n'Allah created the heavens and the earth, and all that is between them, in six days.'\n\n'After this, he settled on the throne of the heavens.'\n\n'He created everything in this life'\nAnd thus spake a Qalashite, as attested by Jan Velimir Vtak on 13th of Amber Cold, anno 1506.\n\nⰭⱀⱁⱎⰵⰽ Ⰲⰵⰾⰹⰿⰹⱃ Ⰲⱅⰰⰽ\n\nThey observe five pillars of worship:\n\nA profession of faith, wherein they recognize no god nor prophet but their own,\nA ritual of prayer, which demands they bend knee five times a day at marks of day and night,\nThird is a mandate of fasting,\nthey observe a month of abstinence from drink of 'hasheesh', and do not eat during day hours.\nThe fourth is a mandate of charity to the poor.\nThe fifth is a mandate of pilgrimage, where they travel to somewhere to pray and feast.\nThese 'pillars' are called such as the Qalashites believe them to be foundation for a metaphorical \"house\". This figurative system of \"pillars\" is sometimes configured into the building of their temples.\nThe hierarchy of the Qalashite religion is ingrained in their politics, with their leading clergyman representing their country as 'caliph' or king of sorts.\n\nThe Qalashites should be distinguished higher than mere elven pagans. Their faith mandates\nneither bloodshed nor lechery, but their ways should not be trusted all the same, and are still a heresy to the True Faith and the name of Godan.\n\nGodani Jest Wielki!\n\nJan", ""),
        StaticBook("Notes on Inwe", "Tristanm1", "Notes on Inwe\nAuthor Unknown\nJoma, 20th of The Second Seed, 1316: \n\nThe Original Dark Elves of Inwe seemed to live within the forest, having trees for homes. In conflict with their orc neighbours, they hid themselves amongst the thick darkwood trees and found shelter and\npeace here. \n\nTheir houses are made of simple wooden constructions, preferring to live in the inside of thicker trees. Despite this preference, they were not keen on climbing, and dug cavernousdwellings below their houses as\npossible shelter and storage.\n\nThe grove within Inwe was the reason the Dark Elves split from Malinor. They worshipped the moon over the sun. This is evident in seeing their sacred pool, as it was built so moonlight would reflect in it during\ntheir nightly gatherings. \n\nIn time, Dark Elves learned to weave forests and perhaps even draw the moon's power into magical abilities. Their chantings of old seem to echo within the pool still. It was possible that the Dark Elves\nhad an order much like the Druids, but this is unconfirmed.\nTolta, 24th of The Second Seed, 1316 : \n\nThere is a strange sort of energy wafting this place. It surrounds the pool like mist. I feel it strongest in the mornings, as the dew hangs from the leaves and the moon's touch is still recent upon\nthe pool.\n\nIt is revitalizing, like waking from a long sleep. Some traces of the Mali'Ker magic might still linger here.\nIrba, 4th of Sun's Smile, 1316: \n\nI have found an old tome amongst the ruins of the Dark Elven settlement. It denotes that the Dark Elves in Inwe were hunted by a people they called 'Marauders', a group that finally found and attacked the village.\nThis may have been the cause of their disappearance, but that's anything but confirmed. \n\nThis book is not written in old elvish, but in common Aegis tongue. We might be able to derive a time period from this, or a taboo for using elvish in\nwritten work. Either way, the journal uses the word 'Velulaei', which possibly means 'Moon'. They speak of the moon as if she is an entity, like a goddess or a spirit. I wonder if this could be more than silly superstition of a primitive race.\nSabet, 28th of Snows Maiden, 1316: \n\nWe are starting to uncover multiple grotto's that may have served as cave dwellings and hiding spaces for the dark elves. These caverns seem damp and cramped, and I would abhor spending just\none night in one of them. The original inhabitants must have used them out of necessity, perhaps a fear of being found if they spend too much time above the surface.\n\nOne of the caverns is situated directly below the largest tree of\nthe village, baring its roots and the water that feeds it. I wonder if the elves tampered with this pool of water in some way, in order to have the tree grow to its unusual vastness.\n\nSpending too much time in this grotto makes me ill at ease, my senses\nurging me to leave.. This feeling might be the remnant of an ancient spell ment to keep intruders out. If this is true, I can only assume that within this grotto is something the former owners did not want to be found.", ""),
        StaticBook("Oakhearth", "Noach Hughes", "The History of Oakhearth\n-------------------\n\nOakhearth was founded on 18th of Snow's Maiden, 1468.\n\n------------------\n\nNoach Hughes was the first person to build a house in Oakhearth.\nNoach started to explore the surrounding areas and found out that there were lots of ruins and remainings of old settlements.\n\nYears later more people started to settle down in Oakhearth,\nAnd soon another three houses were built along with a Tavern.\n\nOakhearth's existence is rarely known due to the remote location and very few visitors come across the hamlet.\nChapter 2.\n-------------------Ruins of Oakhearth's Forest and Heath.\n-------------------\n\nThe remainings of a forgotten cemetery near Oakhearth and several Dolmens in Oakhearth's Heath are still present and their dark.\nhistory still lures within them.\n\nThe ruins of a small chapel can be found in Oakhearth's Heath. Excavation research revealed that the chapel was pillaged and destroyed by a undead raid somewhere in 1471.\nThe remainings of the Lychbane Cemetery are the reminder of the dark and eery influence of Cultists and Undeads in Athera.\n\nRumour has it that the cemetery is haunted by the restless citizens whose graves were robbed.\nThe Barrows of Wolfheze Heath.\n\nSeveral Barrows can be found in the Heath of Oakhearth.\nRich noblemen are buried with their riches and treasures to ensure a safe transition to the hereafter.\nChapter 3\n-------------------\nCavern of Tranquillity -------------------Deep beneath Oakheart are the remainings of a underground river flowing through the mountain.\nMoss and fungus have started to grow in the cavern and\ngrass is slowly spreading too.\nMushrooms and Herbs growing bountiful and insects are crawling everywhere.", ""),
        StaticBook("Observations", "[Dedicant] Thickfeet", "\n\n     Observations\n            on\n        Enchantments\n\n      \n\n\n\n\nWritten by\nLethius Thickfeet\nAs my learnings during my druid dedicancy continue, it has come to my attention the unnatural tendencies of the magical realm.\n\nIt its known that the source of most arcane magics is the great endless Void.\n\nIt is also believed by some the damaged cause by such usage is severe and slowly affects the greater realms that we live in.\n\nI was tasked to witness this in person. \n\nI spent a few days looking to the locals of Kal'Archon for\na regular magic user. Unforunately I found noone in the safety of the newly built city.\n\nOf course within the Druid Grove itself that kind of search would be futile.\n\n\nSurprisingly my search led me right under my nose.\n\nI was sifting through old belongings and came across a small journal I had purchased decades before.\n\nAmong my collection stood a very unique\nitem. A mage's journal had described the usage and discoveries she had made involving Enchantments.\n\nI glanced through the pages where a short collection of glyphs and its attributes were described in detail.\nIt was clear to me that such magical imbuement must have come from the same source as most Arcane magics.\n\nWhat I found was not wrong, and what found me was unnatural in every way.\nI began by searching the city markets for the magical reagents needed to perform enchantments.\n\nAfter collecting a few cheap tools and swords, the natural emerald stone which takes most precidence in the profession, and a mixture of other\npowders and artifacts required, I set out into the mountains to find a secluded and safe place to examine and study.\n\nThe first few days of attempts yielded nothing of value. \n\nMost attempts ended with absolutely no\nchange to the item in question. Some attempts the enchantments didn't hold but I had started to gain progress.\n\nFor most of those days, deep meditation and rebalance of the area is how I kept my focus. For every single attempt I \nmeditated to find signs to stop my continued attempts. It seemed to me that nature itself approved of me finding out the dangerous truth of the Arcane.\n\nI also repaid my attempts with various planting of flowers and herbs. Thankfully my work in gathering\nleft me with the stores to do so.\n\nEventually my labor bore fruit. I managed to enchant an old sword with a magical sharpness. It was almost as if the enchantment itself took precidence to the edge of the old blade. Ahead of the blade\nwas a magical barrier that cut through most things much easier than the obvious dull blade would have ever done alone. \n\nI rested that night after planting a healthy sapling nearby.\nThat night was some of the worst sleep I could have imagined. I dreamed of a darkness swallowing all around me. I couldn't outrun it, and eventually it over came me as I ran for my life.\n\nI awoke in the middle of the night\nknowing that something dark was coming due to the actions I had taken in those mountains. \n\nThe sounds of the area were too silent. The life of the area had fled and I could feel an unnatural emittion from the sword.\nI picked up the sword with both hands and felt the great weight atop the used hilt.\n\nI turned to see that a group of undead abominations had found me.  Running up the hill I felt the sweat and pain in my legs as I gained hieight but not distance.\nTaking a few wild swings I was able to stop a few of them as I ran. \n\nThe feeling of futility overcame me as the more I used the sword, the more monsters I had seemed to see. \n\nWhat had I unleashed? This was obviously \nno intent behind the enchantment, but I was starting to believe the author of that journal might have known exactly what they were doing when writing down a 'Sharpness' enchantment.\n\nI had been tricked and I was paying the price.\nI began slashing more wildly at everything in sight.  If I could wear out the blade and break it, I knew the enchantment would cease.  \n\nWhile this included some of the monsters around, I also slashed at the stoney earth.\nThe clangs of metal and stone probably agitated more of these monsters and the pain seared in my arms with every swing.\n\nI thought of death and how I had truly deserved it by naively jumping into a magic I didn't understand. \nA piercing wail filled my ears and a satisfying relaxation overcame me as the weight was lifted from my hands.\n\nThe enchantment had ended and the old sword was scrap at my feet.\n\nAs I stood watching the creatures come\nfor me, I decided to earn my fate. \n\nMy pulse filled my hearing, my body shook with fright, and I realized my folly.\n\nI closed my eyes and asked the aspects for forgiveness as the footsteps came closer and closer...\nA sudden silence filled my ears as powerful as a dragon's roar.\n\nIt felt like an age passed before I gained the courage to open my eyes. \n\nI sat on my knees completely astonished. As swiftly as the creatures appeared,\nthey had suddenly dispersed into the darkness.\n\nI spent a few days longer within the trees just thinking of the events. I hid the remainder of the items I purchased around the area hoping they would be lost with time.\nOnce I returned to the Grove I immediately penned what you have just read.  I hope that my story can help in those that come after me. \n\nDo not delve into what we know least about without guidance and carefulness. \nThe void contains a power that we are simply unable to wield elegently and it is not to be toyed with.\n\nI pray that my actions do not reflect poorly on the order, nor did I do too much damage to nature itself.\nDedicant\nLethius Thickfeet\n\n13th of the Grand Harvest.\n\n1457", ""),
        StaticBook("Observations I", "Lorar Minskin", "Observations and Their Benefits\n-------------------Written by Lorar Min.\n-------------------  As you look around do you notice the fate of the people around you? The tick tock of the clockwork and the innerworkings of the life they live is set in stone. \nIt is to the point of which it seems that even the biggest actions seem to have no effect in pulling people out of this loop. Now, It'd be safe for me to say that you are probably one of those people who dilly-dally around and are stuck in one preset path\nin which you feel that you have no control over. Infact It'd also be safe for me to say that you, yourself, the person that is reading this is so clueless that they don't even realize that they're stuck in this loop. You need help, fast.\n Unlike others\nbefore you, these events don't need to happen. They don't need to be one event piling on another and another to the point of no return. You, yes you have a chance to stop this. At this point I'd say you're quite confused on what I'm talking about. I'm \n talking about your old friend who is predetermined every single, every single day I tell you to do the same exact thing in which he does the same exact tasks until the point of no return. That no returning point being death. I'm talking about changing \nwhat you're doing. For better or worse, for the danger, for the fun, for the neverending amazement of not knowing what is going to happen next. Unlike your ancestors you can change what your future may hold. Perhaps your grandfather was a carpenter and \nyour father was also a carpenter and by golly-gee you better be a carpenter. That, that itself is what I'm talking about. Do not follow what is preset for you, stand out.\n You've got one life and you better make it worth living because who knows what \ntommorrow may hold. Take that last step, eat that extra roll, ask to court that special lady, do whatever you feel like you may never have another chance to do. Because who knows, you might not be there the next day to do it.\n\n-My First Book, Lorar", ""),
        StaticBook("Observations v1.", "snowshovel", "A Record of Magical Sightings in the Witch Woods of Lenfarthing.\n\nRecorded by Dedicants Eldon and Beth.\nOn their travels to the With Woods of Lenfarthing, the subject of many tales of magical anomalies, the Dedicants mentioned came upon a strange sight. This create appeared to the Dedicants as a faintly glowing blue orb which floated and could vanish at\nwill. Although one could be commonly seen, the Dedicants also observed a group hovering around one, larger orb. The Dedicants can only assume these were unnatural beings created by magical events. More records may be taken at a later date.", ""),
        StaticBook("Odd Events C1", "Sofetios Jayamen", "       Odd Events\n\n            ~By Sofetios\n                  Jayamen\n\n        Prologue\n\nMy name is James Geedin. My family recently settled in a new land without a name, so I call it Genway.  We were lucky enough to stumble upon an old house. \nWhoever built it, I don’t know. Some odd stuff seems to happen here, but I’m determined to stay at my new home. This is my story.\n       Chapter One\n\n         The Light\n\nI woke up in the middle of the night with chills running down my spine. I was around 13 at the time. I went downstairs to get something to drink.\n That’s when I noticed it. I looked out the window and noticed a light in the distance at the woods. Though it wasn’t yellow like a fire, it was white. I thought about checking it out, but decided I should go to bed instead.\nThe next day in the morning, I went downstairs to have breakfast, but the thing was, my parents weren’t awake. That was unusual, so I went upstairs to their room and knocked on the door. \nWhen I knocked on the door it simply opened slowly from the force of my knock. I looked in, but no one was there. I spent the rest of the day looking for them, but to no avail. \nSoon it became nighttime and I was in the kitchen. That’s when I noticed it again, the same light as before. This time I went out towards the light. I came to the forest clearing where the light was coming from.\n There was a small mirror in the center, angled towards my house. Then I noticed something, the white light wasn’t coming from the mirror, It was coming from the attic window of my house being reflected by the mirror. \nI saw a word carved into the mirror. It said “Truth”. I looked back towards the house. Could my parents be were that light is? I took the mirror and made my way back the house. \n\n\nI got to the house and made my way upstairs to look for a way up into the attic. I looked different places until I got to my parents room. I walked in and there was a small white light coming from the bookcase in there, going around the books. \nI walked up to the bookcase and looked through the cracks between the books. There was a white stairway that lead upwards.  I shifted the bookcase over and started walking up the white stairs. Until I got to a white trapdoor above me.\n cautiously, I lifted it up a crack and looked in the attic room.  There were two statues that looked just like…  I then opened the trapdoor fully and rushed into the white room towards the two statues.\n\n\n They were exact replicas of my parent, but they had faces looked like they were scared of something. Suddenly there was the sound of the trapdoor shutting behind me. \n\n\nI looked back to see something in white robes that covered its whole body and a light coming from under its hood. \n“Who are you?” I said scared. It simply stood there. I looked back towards my parents for a second and back again. \n Then it was right in front of me. Startled I fell back and the mirror dropped on the floor face up but didn’t break. I got up and backed up. It didn’t move.\n\n\n Then I noticed something. In the reflection on the mirror on the ground, where the thing was supposed to be was a person. The person in the reflection had light coming from its eyes. \nI took out my short sword. Getting ready to fight whatever this was. I lunged at its chest, but as it made contact with the light coming from it, the blade turned white. I dropped my sword from it happening. \nI was about to panic when I remembered the mirror, then got an idea. Keeping my eyes on the person, I picked up the mirror, careful not to get in the light. \nThen with one hand, I slid it under the light coming from the person’s hood, trying to aim it back at it. Suddenly the light coming from under the hood disappeared. Then the person in the robes simply fell over, solid as a stone. \nI turned to the statues of my parents. I wasn’t sure what to do. I talked, I yelled, but there was no response. I had never felt so alone before. My parents turned into stone, not knowing how to turn them back to normal. \nBut then that made me realize something. Something I should have thought from the start. \n\nWho had left the mirror in the woods?\n\nEnd of Chapter One\n\nThank you for taking the time to read this book! I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing, please let me know by bird or in person!\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))\n“Fear mainly occurs in the absence of knowledge.” ~ Sofetios Jayamen\n\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Fiandria", "Rindir", "\n\n\n\n\n     Ode to Fiandria\n          by: Rindir\nOde to the great white stag adorned here.\nAnd to the wonderful marvel of those without fear.\n\nOde to the bears, and elk, and 'coons.\nAnd to all the marvelous life that looms.\n\nOde to Fiandria.\nOde to the enormous trees so tall.\nIn hopes that they will surely never fall.\n\nOde to the great mystical forest.\nAnd to the flowers loved by a magnificent florist.\n\nOde to Fiandria.\nOde to the wildlife that comes and goes.\nAnd to the many plants that grows.\n\nOde to the friends that we may make.\nIn hopes we will always cherish their wake.\n\nOde to Fiandria.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Nature", "Pine Druid Gi'Garun", "An Ode to Nature\n    by The Pine Druid\n\nWe walk upon her soil,\nWe gaze upon her trees.\n\nWe're surrounded by all kinds of things, \nThat sprouted from her seeds.\nWhen one steps out of one's front door,\nOne will see her work.\n\nHer prowess great and beautiful,\nNo dark or evil mirk.\n\nWhat is it that I speak about?\n\nWhat is it that I praise?\nThe lands we roam,\nThe streams that foam.\n\nThe trees so tall,\nThe rain that falls.\n\nAll of these are of her make, for they are her and her alone. The make of two of love and great.\n\nThat be Nature.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Nature", "Dedicant Gi'Gárun", "An Ode to Nature\n   By Gi'Gárun\n\nWe walk upon her soil,\nWe gaze upon her trees.\n\nWe're surrounded by all kinds of things,\nThat sprouted from her seeds.\nWhen one steps out of one's front foor,\nOne will see her work.\n\nHer prowess great and beautiful,\nNo dark or evil mirk.\n\nWhat is it that,\nI speak about.\n\nWhat is it that I praise?\nThe land we roam,\nThe streams that foam.\n\nThe trees so tall,\nThe rain that falls.\n\nAll of these are of her make, for they are her and her alone. The make of two of love and great.\n\nThat be nature.", ""),
        StaticBook("Ode to Youth", "\"Caroline Hightower\"", "Caroline V. Hightower\n\n\"Ode to Youth\"\n\n__________________\n\nNo Heart, no Spirit - Lo! Cadaverous crowds!\nO Youth! Pass me thy wings,\nAnd let me o'er the dead earth soar;\nLet me vanish in delusion's clouds,\nWhere many the Zeal begets a wonder\nAnd grows a flower of novelty up yonder,\nAdorned in Hope's enamelings.\n\nWho by his elder age shall darkened be\nHis toilsome forehead to the ground bent\nlow,\nLet him no more perceive or know\nThan his thus lowered selfish eyes may see.\n\nYouth! Up and over the horizons rise,\nAnd smoothly penetrate\nWith Thy all-seeing eyes\nThe nations small and \ngreat.\n\nLo there! The space of dearth,\nWhere putrid vapors in the chaos wrestle:\n'Tis Earth!\nUp from the waters where the dead wind blows\nA shell-clad Reptile rose.\nHe is his own rudder, \nsailor and vessel.\nHe often dives and rises up with little trouble,\nFor some smaller brutes he craves,\nThe waves cleave not to him nor he to the waves;\nAnd suddenly he bumps upon a rock and bursts like a bubble.\nNobody knew his life,\nand of his death nobody wists.\nEgoists!\n\nOh Youth! The ambrosia of life be Thine\nWhen I with friends do share time so sweet\nWhen youthful hearts at heav'nly feasting meet\nAnd golden threads around them all\nentwine.\n\n/En masse/, Young Friends!\nIn happiness our ends.\nStrong in unison, reasoned in rage:\nMove on, Young Friends!\nAnd happy he that perished in the strife\nIf for the others he'd prepared the stage\nOf fame and honored life.\n/En masse/, Young Friends!\nThough steep and icy be our path\nThough force and frailty guard the door:\nWhen force is used, with force respond and wrath;\nWhile young, upon our frailty wage a war.\n/\nWho, as a child, detached foul Hydra's head,\nIn Youth, shall strangle Centaurs even;\nSnatch victims from the Devil dread,\nAnd for the laurels march t' Seven Skies.\n\nUp and reach the places out of sight,\nBreak that to which the brain can do no harm!\nYouth! Mighty as an eagle's is Thy flight,\nAs a thunderbolt - Thine arm!\n\nHey, arm to arm! by chains\nLet's bind the earth around;\nTo one focus bring\neach sound,\nTo one focus spirits bring and brains!\n\nMove on, Thou Clod! Leave the foundations of the world!\nWe'll make Thee roll where Thou hast never rolled,\nWhen finally vanished from Thee the mold,\nGreen years shall be\nonce more, Thy sails unfurled.\n\nSince in the land of darkness and of night,\nThe Elements have fallen out;\nBy a simple \"Let there be\", due t' Seven Skies' might,\nThe world of things is made;\nGales are blowing,\nshelters give no shade,\nAnd soon the stars will brighten Heaven all about;\n\nWhile in the land of men a night so dumb,\nThe elements of Will are yet at war;\nBut Love shall soon burst forth like fire;\nOut of the dark, the world of Soul will come,\nIn Youth's conceived desire,\nBy friendship braced forever more.\n\nThe ice, so long unmoved, is bursting now,\nWith superstitions that have dimmed the light.\nHail, Dawn of Liberty! Oh, Long live Thou!\nThou carriest the Redeeming Sun so bright.\n\n___________________", ""),
        StaticBook("Ok", "Viktoriya Vladov", "Additions to Petrusia Household:\n\n1. A large single bed.\n2. Children's bedroom/playroom.\n3. Updated kitchen and seating.\n4. Updated bathroom.\n5. Remodeled office space and master bedroom.\n\n6. Reworked balcony seating and tables.\n7. Updated fireplace with mount for hunting trophies.\n8. Paintings that tie the room together.\n9. Library to hold Viktoriya's articles.", ""),
        StaticBook("Okar", "Cheza Stirling", "Okar Magic: \n\nFirst I shall note the cons of becoming someone's okar. \n\nYou will find yourself becoming overly cautious, afraid of any possible threat to your mage. Breaking the bond or betraying said mage is unthinkable. \nYou live to protect them. You can never harm them, nor cause harm to come to them.\n\nHealing becomes slower for the okar. Their body no longer able to recover quickly on it's own although healing magics seem to be just as effective as normal.\nWhen you bond to a mage you lose your magic. You also lose the ability to ever learn it. For the rest of the okar's life they will find the void unreachable.\n\nIf this hasn't put you off from this path continue reading. Next we list the pros to the magic.\nThe okar can sense the wellbeing of their mage, but not the location. Emotions are also sensed, letting the okar know the moment there is even possible trouble. Happy mage means happy okar. \n\nHowever this can backfire. A sad mage can depress their okar.\nIt depends on the type of bond just how strong these emotions and feelings are. \n\nAn okar gains a heightened sense of hearing, scent, and sight. Able to hear better. Smell better. See sharper. All of these help it in combat.\nHowever there is a drawback here as well. The tastebuds become more dull and bland. The sense of touch becomes minimal.\n\nYour favorite foods will no longer taste as divine as they once did. The touch of soft silk no longer different from rough stone.\nNow, on to the ritual itself.\n\nFirstly the mage must be a mage, they must have a mana pool to draw from. \n\nFind a wolf and skin it alive. This sounds horrible but it must be done this way. \n\nQuickly tan the hide, obviously a source of heat is helpful here. But don't burn the hide. \nAs it tans collect a small bowl of the okar's blood, one of the mage's blood, and then one bowl with their blood mixed together.\nWhen the hide is finished carve the following symbol into it carefully. \n\n*A circular symbol is drawn here, check the forum guide to see the exact one.*\nIn the center of the symbol the okar must use their own blood to write their name. Then using the blood of the mage they write the mage's name. \n\nThis must be an act of their own free will. It cannot be forced or else the magic will fail.\nOnce this is complete use the mixed blood to seal the names with a second symbol. \n\nThis symbol depends on the bond chosen.\n\nNow we will go over the possible bonds.\nA bond of loyalties: The okar can sense the mage but the mage can not sense the okar, this is the best way if the mage wishes to keep their mind clear at all times from the emotions of their bonded.\n\n*The symbol for this bond is then drawn.*\nA bond of friends:  You can sense each other but it's dulled. There is more privacy, you can keep those deep feelings hidden and just let the stronger ones be felt. \n\n*This bond's symbol is then drawn.*\nA bond of lovers:  You both sense each other, your physical, mental, and emotional state.  This is the most invasive version as you really get no privacy from the other, they always know what you feeling.\n\n*Once more the bond's symbol is drawn.*\nA bond of Criminals:  The mage can sense the criminal, emotional, mental and physical, this means they can tell if their Okar is lying to them. It's really the only way to keep someone under control that you don't want to trust. \n*The symbol is drawn.*\nFinally, let it be noted that if the okar wishes to serve another mage they can do so. A transfer.\n\nOnce the magic is completed there is no going back. You will be an okar until you die the final death.\nBut you can choose who to serve. \n\nUsing the same wolf hide simply simply write the new mage's name with their own blood and using mixed blood draw the symbol of transfer.\n\n*Said symbol is drawn into place.*\nThis however usually leaves a mage with an empty spot in their soul and mind, yet this heals over time.  If you are to transfer your Okar, the mage you wish to transfer to may not have a current Okar, nore may you rebond one within the next elven week.", ""),
        StaticBook("Okar Contracts", "Teerz", "Okar Magic\n\nWritten by Al'ria Ay'm'al.\nOh today do I have a tale to please the eyes!  Oh yes!  I shall inform you of the studies I did of a Woman and her Okar, it was delightful!\n\nThe Okar was named Lan, his mage Tulatha.\n\nOf course they started as lovers\nbut eventually that wasn't enough, Tulatha found the book on Okars and decided to do a ritual.  Since she was already a mage she was able to do this!  No non mages where able to use this magic.  Isn't that amazing?\n\nThe test started out\nnormally, she had Lan fetch a live wolf and skin it.  A dead wolf skinned just didn't do anything.  After the skin was tanned it was carved deeply with a sybool, using a dull bone.\n\n*A large symbol drawn in what looks like a fur is sketched here*\nAfter that Tulatha had to mix her blood and Lan's as well as keeping a bowl of his and hers seperate.  \n\nLan wrote his name in the center in his blood.  Tulatha did her name in her own blood as well.  Then they chose a symbol.\nA Bond of Lovers \n*The symbol sits here*  They both feel what the other feels, extremely invasive\n\nA bond of Loyaltys\n*The symbol lays here*\nThe okar can only feel the mage.\nA bond of Friends\n*The symbol*\nThe Okar and mage can feel eachother, but in a lesser sense.\n\nA bond of Criminals.\n*The Symbol*\nThe mage senses the Okar.\n\n\nThe bond Transfer.\n*The symbol*\nThis is made in a new symbol on the same fur to transfer mages, you need the blood of the old mage and cross it out, the blood of the new mage, and the blood of the okar.\nThe symbol must be writen in the blood of both the Okar and the Mage.  Once this is done Tulatha channeled her mana into it and passed out.\n\nShe said she could feel every emotion Lan felt!  Its so exciting!\nAfter Effects.  \n\nLan became more cautious with his Mage around.\n\nHis wounds healed Slower.\n\nHe lost his sense of taste and most of his touch sense.  His hearing, eyesight, \nspeed, and smell increased alot.  \n\nLan also couldn't touch the void afterwords.\n\nWhen Lan killed himself Tulatha went insane before killing herself.\n\nBefore this we discovered a few \nthings.  One can not bond more than one Okar.\n\nAn Okar can be transfered but there is a period in which they can't rebond after.\n\nAn Okar can NEVER learn magic.\nIf one or the other dies the other always feels it, and tends to go crazy till they are brought back....if they are brought back.\n\nThats all I can really tell you, for those who do this, good luck.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old AdunianVolII", "SortedJarhead", "     Ancient Adunian \n           Vol II    \n           \n       Written  by\n         Monk Argyll\n\n-- -- -- -- -- -- --\n           OOC:\n\nThis is part two of a n IC knowledge book. This is only useful if you've read the first one IC.\nAll rules from the first book apply. After your character studies this for 1-2 IC years, you will know all Ancient Adunian. (2-4 weeks IRL MINIMUM)", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Ash Tree", "Damien Menrow", "My dear Father died in a faraway war,\nleft Mother and I quite without hope.\n\nSo we used our last Minas to leave our fine city,\nand away to a house on a farm.\nAnd on this farm on the thorniest edge,\nstood an Ash Tree as old as the Earth.\n\nWith branches so gnarled and twisted and black,\nthat my every bone cried out to climb.\nBut no, \"Oh no!\" said our funny old neighbor,\nwringing her hands like two rags.\n\n\"Don't you go near it, little Mali'ker, for her roots are bad all the way down.\"\nI thought it silly but did as she told,\ntill Mother went missing one eve.\n\nSo sad had she been that I feared the worst,\nand went down to the old Ash Tree.\nAnd now here I stand but here there is nothing,\nnaught but a flowering weed.\n\nBut the breeze I swear I could smell,\nthe scent of my Mother's perfume.\nYes the Wind I declare does carry upon it,\nthe scent of my Mother's perfume...*", ""),
        StaticBook("Old fat spider", "[Poet] Garry Bogger", "       Halfling Poems\n\n1.           Old fat spider           spinning in a tree\n\nOld fat spider spinning in a tree!\nOld fat spider can't see me!\nAttercorp! Attercorp!\nWon't you stop,\nStop your spinning and look for me?\n\nOld Tomnoddy, all big body,\nOld Tomnoddycan't spy me!\nAttercorp! Attercorp!\nDown you drop!\nYou'll never catch me up your tree!\n\nLazy Lob and crazy Cob\nAre wearingwebs to wind me\nI am far more sweet than other meetBut still cannot f\nind me!\n\n\nHere am I, naughty little fly;\nYou are fat and layzy.\nYou cannot trap me, tough you try in your cobwebs crazy.\nMay the hair on yer toes never fall off!!!", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Journal", "Unknown", "2nd of Sun's Smile, 1500\nKuro has begun teaching me magic, as he had agreed to do back in Kaphro, Guarger also seems much more stable, and it seems he would like to rekindle our old relationship...I'm not sure if I have the heart to tell him...\n15th of The Amber Cold, 1500\nThings are becoming incredibly uncomfortable between Guarger and I...I have to tell him soon, this can't keep going on.\n5th of Snow's Maiden, 1501\nI told Guarger, he took it well, I also met a nice elf named Sana, we spoke for a while and then had tea, I quite enjoyed talking to her, I think I'll invite her around again soon.\n16th of Snow Maiden, 1501\nSana has moved in with me, and Kuro has explained The Void to me, he also gave me a flower and asked me to love him, which confused me, but it's Kuro, so I'll just roll with it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Journal", "MrWizardGorlock", "The Fire\nBy The Wanderer of the World\n\nThe Fire, bright as the Star\nThe Fire, deadly as war\nShining so brightly\nAs it Devours Air\nIt looks ever so fair\nThe Fire, warming as a hug\nThe Fire, A spark of hope\nthat is as deadly as you.\nA Light in the Darkness\nBy the Wanderer of the World\n\nLight, shining so bright\nDark, lurking like a shark\nYou light my way\nOut of the Caves\nInto the Fields\nAnd I follow your way\nOut of the Dark\nand into a new Day\nThe Wanderer\n\nTraveling Across lands far and wide.\nMoving through life like a slide.\nHe never stops, but always observing.\nNever participating on the side.\n((a picture of a man above a crowd is here))\nHe looks for a reason.\nHelping others, never himself as it's treason.\nHe forgot himself, forever lost,\nUpon the paths, like a fading season.\n\n((a drawing of spring overtaking the last snowflakes of winter is here))\nHis name lost and unknown.\nHe has been given many, but none he owns.\nThe Wanderer, there like starlight.\nFaded and grey, always out of sight.\n\n((a man is faded into a black background, barely a differentiating between the two colors, but still there))\nHe wanders, ever searching\nNo reasons, just observing.\nA life of solitude, always alone.\nHe looks for friends, but never finding.\n\n((a man is here among many, all have their back to him))\nDeath evades him.\nLife forgets him.\nAlone with an untold story\nBecause Nobody will remember him.\n\n((a group of people pass a man, darkness covers the left side and light on the other, he stands between.))\nEmotions, he gains.\nBut over time he has learned to feign.\nHappy but sad, he prays\nHoping Aspects take these reins.\n\n((a man cries into his legs, a few water splotches dot the page from 'tears'))\nHe needs someone, a friend.\nHe wants a lover, someone to tend.\nBut he walks alone upon the world.\nNow he sighs and stares as he meets his end.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Man's Dream", "MerekM", "\n'There once was a dream,\nIn this man's heart,\nThat life was never sullied,\nInstead was cherished,\nWould not be perished,\nAnd brought to eternal flame.'\n\n'A keep they built,\nSo it was felt,\nA burden they all could carry,\nBut darkness fell,\nIt did compel,\nA burden for none to bury.'\n\n'New burden made,\nSo lightness fade,\nAnd the man was who to blame.'\n\n\n\n\n-Poem rewritten by Merek Moonstrike. Original creator: Gladius Moonstrike.", ""),
        StaticBook("Old North V.I", "Alexander Bedevere", "Tales From The Old North.\n\nPrologue:\nThe Undead are facinating creatues. A true spectical to behold, when you see thier power. Braved by many, but feared by many more. They have acute senses and strange rituals, leaving them surrounded by \nmystery. But no aspect of them is shrouded in more mystery then the North. The critters whom once lived there are unlike any other. Animals, infected with the taint of Iblees or something straight from the Nether itself. It's horrifying for most but if \nyou're like me, it's exciting. Enchanting, the creatures of the North are truely. The things you find are spectacular. So grab a chair. I'm about to tell you about my ventures to the long forgotten North, when eldritch things still roamed there, under the\nwatchfull eye of Iblees. Where to start.\n\nVeins of the Snow:\nThe North is just as dangerous, if not even more, underground. With miles and miles of icy caves, there's the reason it goes unexplored. The only thing that awaits a traveller down there, \nis death. And so it's no supprise that when traveling there, you watched your step. But it's not that easy. You've got snow, ice, all of which could drop you 100ft at a moments notice. Unfortunately, that's exactly what it did to us. But however, in good \nfortune, what I found down there, were things no human, elf or dwarf had ever seen. I fell deep into the heart of the North, and traveled through the veins.\nIt began as a scouting expedition I was conduicting. Something part of a larger venture. It\nwas I, and a human friend of mine. Together we traveled through a tight mountain pass when we came across a ravine, constucted as if god himself had come down to strike a cut into this land. Deeper then the eye could see, and seemed so perilous that it\nwas easier to avoid it altogether. But my partner insisted. He suggested we take the time to cut out a large tree nearby and use it to cross. Looking back on it now, it was a poor choice, but going around or god forbid over the adjacent mountains would \ntake another week. And in this weather, another week, we could not spare. It took a day to chop it down, but when it came down, it fell fast. And right over the ravine. We secured the one side that we could, and decided to cross immediately. The bridge \nseemed sturdy enough, and we were no strangers to crossing on logs, but something seemed off. Midway through my gamble I turned at the sound of screaming. The undead were assulting our basecamp, and shook the land with thier elemental forces. It rocked us\nright out from under our feet, and soon we were doomed to fall. But fate is a funny thing. Doom did await us, but doom would not be the one to end us. Narrowly, to say the least, I manged to survive the fall. With my pick I dug into what I could\nreach, and while I did nearly rip my arm off, it slowed me down enough to walk away with a merely a broken arm, and some minor cuts and things. My parter was not so lucky. He was crushed under the log, and that wasn't mentioning the other injuries he had \naquired. I hadn't known him well, but I did honor him with a small wooden cross at the site. I spent the first night at the bottom of the ravine, curled up for warmth, and trying my best to light a torch to see. The air was thin, and somewhat fragile. \nLike holding a thin sheet of porcelain in your hands, trying to be careful not to hurt it or damage it. Or waste it in my case. By the time morning came I was thirsty, and freezing. I had to escape as soon as possible. Luckily, I could light a torch with \nmore ease now that the light of day was showing, and I could see what I was doing perhaps a bit better. The first thing I met was the howl of the wind, and the numbness I did feel when entering the many caves that surrounded me. I had roughly three days \nof supplies, and I needed for them to count. \nMany of the caves I encountered were dead ends, or barren, but others, I found strange signs. Once I found a great tunnel leading downwards into darkness, seemly created by some large worm. Other times I\nstumbled across scores of ancient symbols. Strange, alien text of which I could make no recollection. That's when I found the withers. Some call them skeletons, and I could see why, but they do have flesh. They are animals, think linky creatures of the\nNorth. Horrifying things, truely. Perhaps easy to take down one, with a sword and shield in hand, but to take five, seven or even ten at a time was suicide with my arm in it's current condition. I swear they stalked me in the tunnels, crawling on thier \nfours through the viens of the North. Perhaps they were fighters at the surface, but observes undergrounds. It was too dangerous to find out. I spent my nights huddled in a corner, eyes locked onto the entrance, and for good reason. Withers don't quite \nserve the undead as they do chaos. From what I saw they are thier own peoples, addicted purely and wholely to death. Or something greater. I'll give you an example of one of these odd occurances. Once, under the cover of darkness I stumbled upon one of \nthier resting places. I'm not sure if I can call it a home, but the creature was praying to a shrine. That he seemed to have created. With skulls of many beasts of varying complexions. How odd of these creatures to pray, I thought. And it's a good\nquestion, what need of any god does a mindless animal have. Perhaps these were not withers but I swear, they sure do look like them. The difference I suppose is the way they act. I've never seen one conduct itself in such a way on the surface.\nIt was on the second morning, that I found a way out. Though not in a way I suspected. Screaming, yelling of banshees echoed in the caves' walls. At first I thought I was mad, but as I followed the sound, it got louder. At this point I was hopelessly lost\nand seeing anyone would more then likely be a good thing. I came across a stone wall, from which the screams were coming from. There was a small crack in the wall of which I hardley observe. A man, on a stone table being vexxed by another\nfigure, cloaked in red. I almost wanted to call out due to my sheer joy of seeing a face that wasn't stark black darkness, but I held my excitement. The man was being tortured, clearly, and I certainly wished not to be the next. I had to find a way in.\nPerhaps above. I had spotted a ladder from the chambers, that I would assume went upwards. I scowered the perimeter when it wasn't blocked by ice and found merely a wall, roughly a story above the chambers of which I had just seen. I took my pick and \nbegan digging. Quiet at first but as the day progressed, I drew less cautious. It was in the darkness of the third night that I was kidnapped, still attempting to create a hole big enough for me to fit. I was knocked out cold, and could feel myself being \ndragged away. Perhaps to death, or maybe something more.\n\nVeins of the Snow II:\nI should've died that day. But so keenly I evaded fate, that it still amazes me. People aren't kidnapped by cultists everyday so it was somewhat new to me. All I \ncould see were the hands that fed me and the ceiling, with an open roof, that taunted me. I could smell the fresh air. I could breath again. But these pleasantries certainly did not outweigh the suffering. Days of cutting, sharp blades digging into me. \nThe burning lotion applied before and the salt applied after. Day in and day out I was thier new toy. One that they played with and one that they loved. Immense pains everywhere. And they relished in my cries for help. I could feel myself fading. How long\nhad it been? Weeks? Months? I had forgotten by now. Slipping into oblivion I wept, calling into a void of nothingness. At this point I had given up. Closed my eyes and let the blood run. But a stroke of luck saved me from such an ugly fate. It was after \nmuch time in that cold room that it happened. I could hear scratching a scratching on the walls, moans and groans coming from beyond. Perhaps, I thought, it was just another one of the masters' tricks. In the North there are plently of devious liars.\nBut no. I could hear stone shaking. The walls beating and breaking. What was it? Was it a god? Was it a creature of the Nether? Perhaps, but what fate was worse then my current? I closed my eyes and braced. A loud crash, and crumble. And as the ceiling \nbegan to fall, I went out cold.\nI awoke to a blue sky and empty stomach. A light head, and stinging cheeks. My binds had been crushed and it seemed that the entire facility was collapsed. As I rose, I scanned the landscape. Ontop of a mountain. I had gone\nup an entire mountain. How odd and how lucky I was, I thought, and still think today. I trudged through the snow to find a pile of black, corpses. The Withers. They seemed dead enough, but why here? Why converge here? Perhaps that wasn't the biggest \nquestion on my mind, since I did happen to ignore it for a time. I took what food I could find, what clothes I could find and started a fire. The cultists tattoo'd me all over. With scars rather then ink. A much less preferable choice, but I suppose it \ngot the job done. For them atleast. Camp that night was bloody cold, but I cant say I didn't enjoy looking around at the night sky once more. That night the gods were gracious enough to entertain me with dancing lights. They enchanted the sky\nwith every color imaginable. But one thing still boggled me. What were the withers after? Why go so deep? For what? I awoke late, but before heading out to trek home, a place of which I long awaited, I had to check. One by one I pushed the bodies out from\nthe pile. Each time getting closer and closer to something. What was it? I had to know. I had to. What had granted me my freedom? In the end all that remained was a corpse, one of my captors. But in his hand, of which had been shut tight, he held a small \nbox. Reaching down, I slipped it from him. Opening it up, I found a red crystal, with a glowing auora. It gave me chills to even hold it but it was warm. Firm. Alive and breathing. A dark relic. I am no fool. These things belong in the North. These things\nstay in the North. I took the crystal to stone, and crushed it. Beating out every last bit of it's unholy vibe. \n\nI returned home. Safe, but weary. I had finally left the North, but it would not be my last time there.\n\nI still have dreams about the \nstone. Even now. What awaited me, on my return?\n\nRead\nTales of the Old North Vol. II", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Notebook", "-", "*An old notebook of sorts*\nDay 8: \nAlas, it seems to be all labor and no fruit! I have experimented with the greatest reagents I could find: Dragon bone, entire fins of Hydra, and the beaks of Hippogryphs! It seems to be a wild goose chase! \nEverytime I try to incorporate these greater reagents into anything, I simply end up with overly efficient healing potions. BAH! I’ve taken to simply using these failed attempts at the Panacea for creating more homunculi… strange, alien homunculi.\nThey seem to mock me. I fear them, I had my manservant lock them into the basement while they were still confused in the beginning moments of their creations. \n\nDay 14:\n    More and more failed attempts. This is ridiculous. I use more powerful reagents, yet they are so powerful that now I cannot produce potions anymore… just fiends and deviants.\nThey cram every single cell and cranny of this blasted mansion. I hear their screams of agony, they mutters and senseless murmurs. I cannot sleep.\n\nDay 20:\n    Finally! A revelation! It turns out that the more organic reagents I use, the more impure the potion is! This is why those pesky homunculi kept on appearing. \n\nIf I perhaps use a different base, I can create potions instead of these blasted fiends. I had a lot of them burned and buried out in the crypts. I kept the deviants.\nThey took too much money and too much precious reagents that could have had a better use.\nDay 21:\n    Almost immediate success! I could not sleep last night for my anticipation kept on making me get out of bed. I labored all night, and finally got a potion: \na beautiful golden liquid that almost pulses with light! It looks angelic… perhaps I have dispelled this myth!\nForget getting rich: my name will go down in history as the Alchemist who discovered the Panacea! I will be showered with gifts by every nation!\n\nI will keep this recipe secret, however. Money is not such a bad thing after all… and humans would be thirsting after this potion. Now, only to test it.\nDay 23:\n    As I write this, I contemplate on testing this potion out on my manservant. I am worried, however. I am waiting for the tea to finish boiling… and I endeavour with myself to decide whether I should slip some of this brew into the tea or not.\nNo, that it preposterous! I cannot afford to harm anyone! What if this potion is just a homunculizing tool? What if it turns Remy into one of those blasted Dopplegangers my colleagues have talked about?!\nI need to find something to test this potion on… oh, cursed fate! For every thing I resolve, another problem has to arise! ", ""),
        StaticBook("Old Raev Letters", "Laura Randuins", "((lol m8 u bought\na book full of russian\nletters enjoy learning\npo-russki you shekel meister :^) ))\n\nOld Raev Alphabet\n  _____\n /       \\\n |         |\n |         |\n |______|\n |         |\n |         |\n |         |\n |         |\n\nAH\n_______\n|\n|\n|\n|______\n|         \\\n|          |\n|          |\n|______/\n\nB\n______\n|        \\\n|         |\n|         |\n|_____/\n|        \\\n|         |\n|         |\n|_____/\n\nV\n_______\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n|\n\nG\n  _____\n  |       |\n  |       |\n  |       |\n /       |\n/_____,\\\n|          |\n|          |\n|          |\n\nD\n_______\n|\n|\n|\n|______\n|\n|\n|\n|_______\n\nYE\n  *    *\n_______\n|\n|\n|\n|______\n|\n|\n|\n|_______\n\nYOH\n\\   |   /\n \\  |  /\n  \\ | /\n   \\|/\n   /|\\\n  / | \\\n /  |  \\\n/   |   \\\n\nŻ\n   ____\n  /     \\\n  |       |\n         .|\n      _./\n        \\\n         .|\n  |       |\n  \\___/\n\nZZ\n|      /|\n|     / |\n|    /  |\n|   /   |\n|  /    |\n| /     |\n|/      |\n\nEE\n  \\  /\n   \\/\n|       /|\n|      / |\n|     /  |\n|    /   |\n|   /    |\n|  /     |\n| /      |\n|/       |\n\nY\n|   /\n|  /\n| /\n|/\n|\\\n| \\\n|  \\\n|   \\\n\nK\n    _____\n    |       |\n    |       |\n    |       |\n    |       |\n    |       |\n _/       .|\n\nL\n|\\            /|\n| \\          / |\n|  \\        /  |\n|   \\      /   |\n|    \\    /    |\n|     \\  /     |\n|      \\/      |\n\nM\n|          |\n|          |\n|          |\n|______..|\n|          |\n|          |\n|          |\n|          |\n\nN\n     __\n   /    \\\n  /      \\\n /        \\\n |          |\n \\        /\n  \\      /\n   \\__../\n\nOH\n________\n|           |\n|           |\n|           |\n|           |\n|           |\n|           |\n|           |\n|           |\n\nP\n______\n|        \\\n|         |\n|         |\n|_____/\n|\n|\n|\n|\n\nR\n   _____\n  /       \\\n /         \\\n |\n |\n |\n |\n \\         /\n  \\____../\n\nS\n________\n      |\n      |\n      |\n      |\n      |\n      |\n      |\n\nT\n\\        /\n \\      /\n  \\    /\n   \\  /\n    \\/\n    /\n   /\n_/\n\nOO\n     _|_\n   /  |  \\\n  /   |   \\\n /    |    \\\n |     |     |\n |     |     |\n \\    |    /\n  \\   |   /\n   \\_.|._/\n      .|\n\nF\n\\        /\n \\      /\n  \\    /\n   \\  /\n    \\/\n    /\\\n   /  \\\n  /    \\\n /      \\\n/        \\\n\nH\n|          |\n|          |\n|          |\n|          |\n|          |\n|______..|_\n            |\n            |\n\nTS\n|           |\n|           |\n|           |\n|_______.|\n           .|\n           .|\n           .|\n           .|\n\nCH\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|____|____|\n\nSH\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|      |      |\n|____|____|_\n               |\n               |\n\nSHCH\n__\n   |\n   |\n   |__\n   |   \\\n   |    |\n   |__/\n\nHARD SIGN\n|      |\n|__   |\n|   \\ .|\n|    | .|\n|__/ .|\n\nY'\n|\n|__\n|   \\\n|    |\n|__/\n\nSOFT SIGN\n_____\n       \\\n        |\n  ____|\n        |\n        |\n____../\n\nEH\n       __\n|     /   \\\n|    /     \\\n|__..|       |\n|    |       |\n|    \\     /\n|     \\__/\n\nYU\n     ___\n    /    |\n    |     |\n    \\__..|\n        /|\n       / |\n      /  |\n     /   |\n\nYA", ""),
        StaticBook("Olo's Riddles", "Olo Smials", "~-~    Olo Smial   ~-~\n~-~-~   The   ~-~-~\n~-~    Big Book   ~-~\n~-~   Of Riddles  ~-~\n~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~            12th of\n     Malin's Welcome\nThis thing all things devours:\nBirds, beasts, trees, flowers;\nGnaws iron, bites steel;\nGrinds hard stones to meal;\nSlays king, ruins town,\nAnd beats high mountain down.\nWhat has roots as nobody sees,\nIs taller than trees\nUp, up it goes,\nAnd yet never grows?\nNo-legs lay on one-leg, two legs sat near on three legs, four legs got some.\nAn eye in a blue face\nSaw an eye in a green face.\n'That eye is like to this eye'\nSaid the first eye,\n'But in low place\nNot in high place.'\nAlive without breath,\nAs cold as death;\nNever thirsty, ever drinking,\nAll in mail never clinking\nIt cannot be seen, cannot be felt,\nCannot be heard, cannot be smelt.\nIt lies behind stars and under hills,\nAnd empty holes it fills.\nIt comes first and follows after, \nEnds life, kills laughter.\nThirty white horses on a red hill,\nFirst they champ,\nThen they stamp, \nThen they stand still.\nA box without hinges, key or lid,\nYet golden treasure inside is hid.\nVoiceless it cries,\nWingless flutters,\nToothless bites,\nMouthless mutters.\nI am taken from a mine, and shut up in a wooden case, from which I am never released, and yet I am used by almost all children.\n\nWhat goes round the house and in the house but never touches the house? \nWhat is it that you can keep after giving it to someone else?\n\nWhat walks all day on its head?\nWhat gets wet when it's drying?\nWhat appears once in a minute, twice in a moment, but never in a thousand years? \nWhat is round as a frying pan, deep as a bathtub, and still all the oceans in the world couldn't fill it up? \nThere were five men going to church and it started to rain. The four men who ran got wet and the one that remained still stayed dry.\nhe more you take, the more you leave behind. What are they?\nHe who has it doesn't tell about it. He who takes it doesn't know about it. He who knows about it doesn't want it. What is it?\nBrothers and sisters have I none but that man's father is my father's son.\nWho spends the day at the window, goes to the table for meals and hides at night?\nI fasten it and it walks. I unfasten it and it stops.\nWhat goes round and round the wood but never goes into the wood? \nI have a little house in which I live all alone. It has no doors or windows, and if I want to go out I must break through the wall. \nAlmost immediately after the father came into this world, the son was seen dancing on the roof.\nThere are four brothers in this world that were all born together. The first runs and never gets tired. The second eats and is never full. The third drinks and is always thirsty. The fourth sings a song that is never finished. \nA cloud was my mother, the wind is my father, my son is the cool stream, and my daughter is the fruit of the land. A rainbow is my bed, the earth my final resting place, and I can be a nuisance to people. \nPoke your fingers in my eyes and I will open wide my jaws. Cloth, leather, cardboard or paper, I greedily devour them all.\nAnswers\n1. Time\n2. Mountain\n3. Fish on one-legged table, man sits on three-legged stool, cat gets the bones.\n4. Sun shining on daises which are growing in a field.\n5. Fish\n6. Dark\n7. Teeth\n8. Egg\n9. Wind\n10. Lead in a pencil\n11. The sun\n12. Your word\n13. A nail in a horseshoe\n14. Towel\n15. The letter M\n16. A sieve\n17. Dead man and 4 Bearers\n18. Footsteps\n19. Coffin\n20. My son\n21. Fly\n22. Sandal\n23. The bark of a tree\n24. A chick in an egg\n25. Fire and Smoke\n26. Water, Fire, Earth\n27. Rain\n28. Shears\nWritten by Olo Smials\n(( and these guys \n\nhttp://www.berghuis.co.nz/abiator/unit/hobbit/ch/riddles.html\n\nhttp://www.berghuis.co.nz/abiator/humour/riddles1.html#oneb\n\n                        ))", ""),
        StaticBook("On \"Racism\"", "[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "\n\n\n\n  \n    On \"Racism\"\n\n   by Lucion Sullas\n\nDuring my initial days of Haelun'or, I took great care in writing a treatise upon the more common questions critical of Mali'aheral society. This book contained rebuttals to these questions for the sake of education, and the sake of the ignorant\npeoples beyond our community. This is a reprint of sorts (though not truly, as the original manuscript was lost in Asulon) that contains more ignorant questions, extended arguments, and superior literacy. That said, this introduction is long enough.\nLet us begin.\nA human once told me,\n\"Purity is of the heart, not of the mind and body.\"\n\nLet us ignore the obvious conclusion that a heart is merely a muscle that pumps blood through the body, with no importance to purity other than filling its\nmandatory biological role. The human presumably refers to the heart as the beholder of an abstract concept of \"goodness,\" so we shall argue that point. \n\n\"Goodness,\" the act of being \"good,\" is a fundamentally flawed concept. There are\nthings that are innately evil, such as the dread-demon Iblees, profligates of Dark Magics, and other varying monsters who tout goals of domination and destruction. These are certainly cruel. But what can truly be considered \"good?\". Good (and at times\n\"evil\") are subjective in their very nature. Even the words of the much-revered Aenguls are only a matter of perspective. Let us say that in a Valah village, a family is taxed heavily by their local Lord. This lord treats them the same as any other\nfamily, but this one is particularly poor, and can barely survive the long winters. One day, the father in desperation robs a nobleman, and kills him. The pauper did not take pleasure in the act, and had not intended the nobleman to die. This allows him\nto feed his long-suffering children. Would this act be good, or evil? If one situation can be skewed in such a way, then, logically, others must also be flawed. \n\nThe maehr'sae hiylun'ehya teaches that purity is a combination of mind,\nand body. It is something only mali'aheral are capable of, and can never be allowed to fester beneath the guidance of the ignorant. To advance our people, Mali'aheral must enhance and strengthen their minds. To understand nature's workings is\nto understand our existence and its natural place in the true-world. To this end, our people are gifted with extraordinary intelligence, and it allows us a greater capacity to absorb information, and create our own. \n\nIn advancing our people, we complete the maehr'sae hiylun'ehya. The heart has no purpose in true purity.\nA wood elf once asked me, \n\"If you are not racist, why do you not allow intermarriage between your people and other races?\"\n\nThe other races are, as commonly said, \"lesser\" than true, pure mali'aheral. This is not a malicious slur\naimed at them for amusement or as an emotion-fueled outburst of hatred. This is simply observable fact. Traits that commonly bring the ruin of other societies, for example; greed and violence, are barely prevalent within our community. Furthermore, our\naforementioned heightened mental faculties make such traits almost non-existent. Our blood ensures this superiority, and every muddying of our family lines with inferior specimens dulls preferred Mali'aheral traits. This makes the completion of the \nmaehr'sae hiylun'ehya more difficult with every corrupted generation. Were we to allow rampant, foolish breeding, then the maehr'sae hiylun'ehya would never be completed, and our kind would languish in the mediocrity of the other Mali.\nA Dwarf once asked me,\n\"If you have no prejudices, why do you allow so few outsiders within your society?\"\n\nOutsiders are difficult to trust. There are a worthy few of them accepted into our numbers, but these have proven their \ncompliance and worthiness to our people. To why we bar outsiders in general, will be answered in an allegory--one that will be far simpler to grasp for those not of our people. \n\nImagine yourself the owner of a fabulous dwelling, filled with\ndelights unknown to your envious neighbours. You enjoy your life amongst the trinkets and comfort items that you gathered, and rest in a fine wicker chair in the sitting room. Suddenly, a person you have never met walks through your door, without so much \nas a knock, and begins to walk about \"Ooh\"ing and \"Ahh\"ing at the many delights and placing her dirty fingers upon your property. Confused and concerned, you ask the obvious question of \"What are you doing?\" The stranger, with a smile, explains that she\nthinks your home beautiful, and is looking around. You, in turn, explain to her that you don't want unannounced people in your home. With that, she becomes angered, and declares that you're \"greedy\" for keeping your property to yourself. Such things\nshould be for everyone to see! You explain to her that she is free to do as she pleases with her own home, but that you prefer your privacy, thank you very much. \n\nThe stranger disappears, and begins spreading  rumours amongst your fellow\nneigbours of your greed, and how you \"hate people.\" Suddenly these neighbours also begin to barge their way in, and look about. Sometimes, things go missing. You in turn build a high wall around your home, and though they continue to voice their anger\nat your actions, they are no longer a nuisance. It should not be a necessity to do this, but the nature of your neighbours makes it so.\n\n\nA dark elf once asked me, \n\"Your people insult ours so viciously! Why would you be so cruel?\"\n\nMisinterpretation on the part of those whom we \"insult.\" If this occurs so commonly, why have these victims not re-evaluated the\nreasons behind our words? What we say always has merit. This is what they do not understand. They choose defensiveness over recognising their faults, and therefore never adapt and improve themselves. Thusly, our people will \"insult\" them again, and will\nlikely continue to do so until their missteps are recognised. Such is only befitting a proper Mali, and for their own good.\nA Goblin once asked me (version interpreted from orcish babble),\n\"If you are superior, how is it we orcs have slaughtered you in battle countless times?\"\n\nActs of combat and physical strength do not imply superiority. Violence is one of the\nbasest and most simplistic natural urges that any animal is capable. It can stem from any dark emotional outburst, and those that practice them commonly cannot be fully trusted. To use yet another example -- were a giant spider to attack and kill an Uruk,\nwould the spider therefore be superior to the orc?\nSuch as it is, these questions irk me so, especially when my associates cannot answer them. May you be further enlightened, my reader.\n\nMaehr'sae hiylun'ehya.", ""),
