        StaticBook("Petrus6", "Cloven", "Eviction Policy\n\nIf you do not pay taxes, you are subject to immediate eviction.  If you wish to reclaim your home or have any questions about your eviction, they should be directed towards the Lord Steward", ""),
        StaticBook("Pick Up Line", "Elayne", "I wake up to your warmth of breath,\n\nRealising you're with me.\n\nI feel your arms tighten around,\n\nAnd draw a smile onto me.\nI turn around to face your front,\n\nFeeling blessed a millionth time.\n\nI stare at your sleeping self,\n\nAs I murmur a thankful pray.\nWhen I needed, you were there,\n\nTo help me up and walk me through.\n\nI held on tight to your hands,\n\nAs you pull me closer to you.\nI found something that I had lost,\n\nA love so pure and so serene.\n\nI kiss your brow slow and soft,\n\nIn my arms I hold you close.\nTears would form when you're close,\n\nA clear smile to change the means.\n\nI see your eyes open up,\n\nAnd gaze into mine.\nI feel your hands brush my cheeks,\n\nAnd pull me closer under the sheets.\n\nWe both grin at a sync,\n\nToss this poem and let's pass time.\n\n-=Lain=- <3 <3 <3", ""),
        StaticBook("Pigmen", "[Ghost] The Eternal Librarian", "\n\n\n  A Brief History\n     of Pigmen:\n   Aegis - Kalos\n\n  by Sunshine Druid\n         Verden\n\nThe appearance of Zombie Pigmen was first documented during the Second Iblees Invasion, several hundred years ago. These unnatural monsters were used by the Fallen Daemon as powerful soldiers during his war of conquest against the mortal races of\nAegis, where their group mentality and shear ferocity was crippling to the defenders. Unlike other lesser Undead, the Zombie Pigmen possessed an intellect far superior to any common Zombie or Skeleton. THey would always be seen in possession of\njagged, golden swords and very rarely were encountered alone.\n\nTheir speed was almost on par with that of the living, a trait that allowed few the luxury of escaping. After the invasion was ended and the Dark One had been sealed away for good, many \nstrange things were observed by the population of Aegis. While the mindless Zombies and Skeletons continued to attack anyone on sight, the Pigmen did not. They had become passive, almost completely indifferent to the world around them.\nInstead of mindlessly killing, they began to aimlessly roam the world, staring blindly at the things around them as if they weren’t truly there. After Aegis had crumbled and sank into the sea, many thought they would never see the creatures again.\nAnd, indeed, they didn’t. For a time. But, again, the Pigmen emerged from the unknown, and they were furious. For many years the Pigmen were found roaming the darkest corners of Asulon and attached anyone foolish enough to get close. The reason for this\nis not clear, an many theories have arisen to explain. However none have been proven. Yet another strange event was observed on the island of Elysium.\n\nA large volcano near the temporary Elven settlement erupted and spewed lava over the\nMalinor farms; and the Pigmen rode it down the slope. They were, thankfully, peaceful this time; but they appeared in numbers never seen before. Hundreds of them began to blindly wander from their lair, spilling over the border into the Orcish deserts.\nThere they were destroyed not by men, nor by beast, but by cacti. The creatures tore themselves to shreds over the next few months as they walked through the cacti farms, and they would still if it wasn’t for the piles of their dead brethren impeding\ntheir movement.\n\nSince arriving in Anthos there has only been one reported case of Pigmen. This was a very small issue, where only three or four Pigmen would wander around a border wall in the Wilds. A logical fpi,tao, was placed at this\nsite, and all has been quiet since then.\n\nIf the Pigmen are here on Anthos, they are hiding very well.", ""),
        StaticBook("Pile of Letters", "Seth", "\"Oh Seth, I am truely overjoyed to learn that you are still a vigilant guard of the silver cihi. Perhaps you shall incite rebellion and ensure that we can be together once more. The Malinorian acts shall come back, as I will reunite with you one day my-\n\"-love. \nRemember, our love cannot be broken by 'purity'.\n-Alirya Kaen'aum\n===================[!] Small dots of ink litter the page, and the corners of the letter seem to be slightly darker than the rest of it. [!]\n\"Seth,\nI have arranged a meeting with the Dark Elven woman you fancy, these letters are getting harder to smuggle into the city. Once you are done, please pay the woman before you depart from the place. And ensure you are not followed. Remember,\n\"-I do not want to get in trouble for one of my clients.\"\n-Johnny\n===================[!] This letter is sealed with the mark of the Raine Academy, it imprinted in a red wax seal which lies on the bottom of the page. [!]\n\"Darling,\nIt happens, t- . -., ci-,. -\n[!] The rest of the letter appears to be burnt, the flames avoiding the top of the letter. [!]\nAssassination of the Sohaer which controls us, the Sohaer which destroys our race with thoughts of purity and that other crap. We must love one another as we used to, or, I have attempted that. The elves look at me, but I cannot articulate the feelings-\n-of hatred I feel. My position in the military will soon finalise the attempt at the Sohaer's life. Perhaps at the wedding of ----------------------------------\n[!] The rest of the letter is scribbled out hastily. Now a jumble of quill swipes. [!]", ""),
        StaticBook("Pine Tree Art", "Archdruid Gi'Garun", "*An elaborate painting of a Pine tree is here*\n\n             --\n           -----\n         ------- \n       ----------\n     ------------ \n    -------------\n   --------------\n  ---------------\n -----------------\n            [ ]", ""),
        StaticBook("Pine Trees", "Pine Druid Gi'Garun", "Pine Trees\n   By The Pine Druid\n\nWhen one be walking,\nBout thee's lands.\nOne may wander dunes of sand.\n\nOr maybe even caverns deep,\nAnd view the beauty found beneath.\nWhen one begins to feel cold,\nThey start to miss the sands of gold.\n\nBut when one thinks to open eye,\nThey see the beauty way up high.\nTrue beauty lies above you see,\nIt stands as such, a mighty tree.\n\nA towering canopy tall and fine,\nThe tree I speak of is the Pine.\nWhen time is right, and tree is sound,\nIt drops it's pinecones to the ground.\n\nThe needles that it grasps above,\nAre there not to be grabbed or shoved.\nBut rather be a tool to use,\nAnd not a weapon to abuse.\n\nThe tree I speak of is no quarry,\nSo harm it and you shall be sorry.\nSo when thou next goes near a Pine,\nBe sure to heed these words of mine.\n\nRespect the tree so tall and fine,\nAnd feel the kindness of the Pine.", ""),
        StaticBook("PInkee Buk", "Reyna'Dom", "*The page has been torn out, along with the next two pages. The whereabouts of the missing pages are unknown*\n*The page is torn out*\n*This page is also torn out, though the next pages seem mostly intact*\nlliran'ame elmali'nor'leh ito marsirame'ne, eldruin'leh ito marsirame'ne kae'leh ito marsirame. Malin evare eltalareh sirame. Eltaliamean vallumeyae Ehiereyae elhael fer'linan'mali'ehya ehiereyae'yem\nelareh'sirame narneyae ito yuln'hael'ame'ehya ehiereyae'yem elareh'sirame kaean'leh evareh mali'mali'nor\n\nElokarir'ame\n~Wall\n\nillyth eltalareh'taynei \nil'anuh'wy\n~Stairs\n\nelyuln'oem\n~Sewer pipe\n\nelasimlum uhiere elhael yuln'sirame\n\nelyuln'hael\n~Other pipe\n\n\nelyuln'nuit\n~Another pipe\n\naverir iylmahnihto ernne'onarne\n~Pillar\n\nelsanan Malin ito narne\n~Pillar 2, Side 1\n\nelame ito narne\n~Pillar 2, Side 2\nElokarir'ame ito narne.\n~Pillar 2, Side 3\n\nelasimlum uhiere elhael yuln'sitame\n~Fountain", ""),
        StaticBook("Play Orc", "BuubHozh Hefner", "       PlayOrc\n\n\n*The cover page features a caricature of a bunny with rows and rows of teeth*\nZlutz Uv De Munth\n\n*The following ten pages feature women with disfiguring scars in extremely high quality armor.*\nPruper Tuzk Care\n\n*Listed here is a long and suprisingly informed article about polishing tusks to shine. According to the article, \"Bitchez Luv Shinee Tuzkz\" *\n BuubHozh Hefner\n\n*This article, accompanied by a drawing of an old uruk wrestling two female uruk and a Lur Wolf at once, depicts the life of a fictitious Uruk mating tychoon named BuubHozh Hefner.*\nMojo ztrength, Real ur Fayke?\n\n*Depected in a picture for the article is a battle-scarred, armored elven woman lifting an entire boulder above her head.*\n*The rest of the article is about telekinetics.*\n  De Bear Flattin\n     Ztandurd\n*Below is a drawing of a scarred, scantily clad orc woman snapping a bear's neck with her bare hands. It folds out into 4 pages.*\n\"If Zhe can nub flat a bear wid hur bare handz, zhe iz nub Hozh Mayte!\"\n      Flat - Ztickz\n      Agh Lat!\n*The featured weapon this issue seems to be the famous White Rose Arbelest, accompanied by a picture of a scarred human woman firing it.*\n*To one side of it is a column comparing serrated and normal battleaxes.*\n Lettur Frum De           Zlutz\n *There's a long (and likely fictional) letter below from a (presumably) female author about how she cant stand an uruk who doesn't smell like the blood of their enemies.*", ""),
        StaticBook("Plot 1 Quota", "[Legion Commander] Fili Grandaxe", "Bread-4 Stacks\nCarrots-6 Stacks\nPotatos-6 Stacks", ""),
        StaticBook("Plot Agreement", "Baron Gustaven I", "   ~The Hold of Fellbor~\n\nBy placing your signature on this document you pledge allegence to Count Beros Grandfell-Grandaxe-Tunnelsmasher-Treebeard Jarl of Kal'Fellbor, You vow to never take up arms agaist his lordship, and you vow to pay your weekly tax.\nYour signature insures that you will have a plot under Jarl Beros that will be iron clad untill the Jarl revokes your permissions\n\nX: Jarl Beros \n\nX: Gustaven av Huwaard the First", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem", "August", "Black or brown, big or small, \n\nHaving coarse hair, or hair that is soft,\n\nLily keeps each of them in their warm stall,\n\nExcept one, who caught a cold and coughed.\nThe poor horsey was sick as can be!\n\nBut Lily did her best to help..\n\nThe horse was so full of misery,\n\nAnd it made many sad sounds, including a yelp.\nWith all the knowledge and love she could summon,\n\nShe ventured into the stall, and some time did pass. \n\nA figure knocked at the door, saying \"May I come in?\"\n\nAnd Lily, in reply,\ngave him sass.\n\nThe two cared for the horse, and over time..\n\nThe horsey recovered! All was well!\n\nIf only this poet could more skillfully rhyme!\n\n\nThen he won't have to utter a solemn farewell..\n\n..to his writing career, which is coming to a close, I fear.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem", "[Dedicant] Anthony Hearthal", "In the meadows of asulon\nThere are many plants and trees\nAnd most of the grass grows up, passed your knees\nIn the deepest sea of the world\na massive fish did wonder\nwhy men would dive so far down under\nWell the truth is simple they just wanted to plunder\n\nLike magic trees and humming bees the world also has many secrets, where it hides treasure, the beauty of nature and soulmates that one desires.", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem Of Death", "Virgo the Venalicius", "It does not matter wether your the happiest person... Or the saddest beggar.\n\nThe biggest giant or the most terrible creature. \n\nWe are all the same in the eyes of The Reaper.\n- Virgo", ""),
        StaticBook("Poem to Kalenz", "Ikur Seregon", "*A letter is delivered, sealed with wax.*\nDearest Kalenz Uradir,\nI do think it is time to tell you that you are dear\n\nCertain am I of your purity,\n\nYet of your love for me, there is an insecurity\nWe could progess as fortold in the maehr'sae hiylun'ehya\n\nEnding at a butterfly, and beginning as gastraea.\n\nI do hope your love will be reciprocated\n\n\nYour response is anticipated\n\n\n(Do so by planting a poppy in front of the entrance of the clinic)\n", ""),
        StaticBook("Poetry", "Sofetios", "         Poetry\n\n          ~ By Sofetios\n                Jayamen\n        The Lake\n\nI wander upon a path by a lake,\nWondering where it’s all gone.\nEvery day I used to come here,\nFrom the morning to the evening's yawn.\n\nEvery now and again I wonder,\nWhy the days have passed.\nAnd think very deeply,\nWhy they don't seem to last.\n\nDay by day, night by night,\nThe Lake becomes smaller still.\nThe thoughts of it become thinner,\nEven to my strongest will.\n\nIt has become ten times smaller and ten times shallower,\nEven thought it has not changed.\nTo other people it might be normal,\nbut to me it is considered strange.\n\n        The Dreamer\n\nThe Dreamer,\nThe one who dares not be the same,\nFor in dreams mysteries unravel,\nAnd endless space you do travel,\nAs your dreams you try to tame.\n\nAs the Dreamer,\nDreams above the sky,\nAnd looks down below,\nTo the people that show,\nWho know not what lies that high.\n\nUnawakened, the Dreamer,\nIn a maze of thoughts,\nWandering the mists,\nWith endless twists,\nAs the path on minds paper he jots.\n\nThough be wary dreamer,\nAs thoughts tend to run away,\nOnly the ones you really need,\nAnd the ones you often feed,\nAre the ones that often stay.\n\n        The Cobbler\n             and\n         The Crow\n\nI was working in the night,\nAs I bent over a shoe,\nI was working very hard,\nto finish before the morning dew.\n\nI was just about finished,\nWhen what was this I hear?\nA tapping, slight tapping,\nAnd it seemed to be so near.\n\nI turned to the window,\nAnd what did I see?\nA crow tapping at my glass,\nStaring at me.\n\n\"Go away.\" I said,\nSternly as I did stand.\n\"Don't you know I have work to do?\"\nThough I know it didn't understand.\n\nBut the crow kept tapping at the glass,\nAnd I began to get mad,\nThis crow just would not stop,\nNot even a tad.\n\nI turned to my unfinished shoe,\nTo work as long as I could bear,\nBut then I noticed something missing,\nThat would stop me from completing the pair.\n\nThe golden lace that I needed,\nFor this order to be complete,\nI couldn't find it anywhere,\nAs the glass the crow did beat.\n\nI checked my table, checked my pockets,\nAnd then to my surprise,\nThere was a hole in one,\nThat was just the right size.\n\nI would never finish this shoe,\nBy the morning light,\nNow things were dire,\nand my situation was quite tight.\n\n\"I must have dropped it,\nOn the way back from the store!\"\nBut it was too far I thought,\nAs my confidence tore.\n\nBut then I realized \"What's this?\"\n\"Where was the tapping I couldn't bare?\"\nI looked to the window,\nAnd the crow wasn't there.\n\nI went to the window,\nAnd what was in its place?\nIt was simple and divine,\nThe golden lace.\n\nI opened the window,\nAnd took it with care,\nBecause of who gave this gift,\nI was certainly aware.\n\nI went to my table,\nAnd finished the shoe.\nBut then thought of the crow,\nIf I only knew!\n\nI then went to bed,\nWith not much I could say,\nI only did hope,\nThe crow would come back some day.\n\n         Friend\n           or\n         Enemy?\n\nEverywhere I am\nI hide from your sight,\nIf it's in the day,\nAnd especially at night.\n\nI am one of sleep's worst enemies,\nAnd one of fear's best friends,\nI grow ever more larger,\nAs your will begins to bend.\nIn the day I am more friendly,\nEspecially when its hot and I'm in sight,\nBut when it turns to night time,\nI'm sure to give you a fright.\n\nI am a mimic of sorts,\nI try to copy you while you walk,\nI even mimic other people,\nAlthough I cannot talk.\n\nEven when you go about the day,\nI skulk and kind of stalk,\nFear or fear not, I am always here,\nI am the shadow on which you walk.\n\n         Imprint\n\nI stand in a forest,\nOne that I know quite well,\nWhere the trees are tall,\nAnd they never do fall,\nAt least that's what I like to tell.\n\nI came here,\nWhen I was a boy,\nUsually in the day,\nBut any time I would say,\nThe forest was my favorite toy.\n\nI came here again,\nA few years ago,\nIt was the same place I knew well,\nAs far as I could tell,\nThere was nothing different to show.\n\nI open my eyes now,\nTo see the trees,\nMy eyes see an empty place,\nAs if leaving no trace,\nThough this is not what my mind sees.\n\n   An Uncommon Sight\n\nI go about my day,\nDoing the same things as before,\nThe same old road,\nFrom my humble abode,\nNot realizing it is a bore.\n\nI walk ever onward,\nKnowing all my tasks by heart,\nA straight narrow trail,\nWith nothing new to avail,\nAll I do is my part.\n\nThen one day as I'm going past,\nA park once again,\nI see a boy,\nwithout even a toy,\nwho couldn't be past ten.\n\nThe boy was doing something abnormal,\nsomething I hadn't seen before,\nHe was looking in the sky,\nNot even blinking an eye,\nthey didn't even seem sore.\n\nI wondered if there was a hawk,\nOr something in the sky,\nI didn't hear a thing,\nOnly the birds that always sing,\nSo I did wonder why.\n\nI looked over,\nto the boy sitting by,\nHe was being bullied by his peers,\nAnd was starting to go in tears,\nJust for looking in the sky.\n\nI was about to go over,\nTo try to help if I could,\nbut the parents went first,\nBefore I feared the worst,\nTo help as they should.\n\nI walked on my way home,\nWondering why what I saw came to pass,\nI had a thought why,\nso I looked to the sky,\nand saw it in it's great mass,\n\nAs I looked to the sky,\nI was transfixed,\nI hadn't done this in a while,\nAnd it did make me smile,\nAs past and current memories mixed.\n\nI saw the vast expanse,\nIn all its mystery,\nI watched the clouds in the sky,\nAs they changed shape and passed by,\nAs they had for all of history.\n\nSuddenly someone laughed to my left,\nEven thought it was mild,\nSomeone looking at me,\nLaughing at what I appeared to be,\nJust as the others had done to the child.\n\n         Courage\n\nIt is a thing you use\nWhen you can't go to the end,\nWhen all seems lost\nAnd you can't defend.\n\nYou use it as\nIt combines with your will,\nAll you need to do is\nLet it give its fill.\n\nThe choice is yours,\nGive in or go,\nThough in the end\nOnly you will know.\n\nSee with your spirit\nAnd not with fear,\nfor then you will know\nThat courage is here.\n\nThe End\nThank you for taking the time to read my poetry. I will be writing more in the future!\n\nIf you like my writing let me know! By Bird or in person.\n\n~Sofetios Jayamen\n((Sofetios))\n\"Everything you think you know all has to do with what you believe.\" ~Sofetios Jayamen", ""),
