        StaticBook("Tippen's Root", "Sol Azirus", "     Tippen's Root\n            and\n         it's uses\n\n\nBy: Sol Azirus\n\n   Acknowledgements:\n\n-Lemik Rexiureus:\n\nFor supplying Tippen's root and brewing of Tippen's Root Potion\nIndex:\n\nCh. 1: Introduction\nCh. 2: Description\nCh. 3: Location\nCh. 4: Harvesting\nCh. 5: Origin Of Name\nCh. 6: Uses\nI. : Medical Use\nII. : Alchemical Use\nIII. : More on Potion           of Tippen's Root\n-------------------Chapter 1: Introduction\n-------------------\n\nIn this book I will be explaining what Tippen's Root is, it's uses and how to identify it\n-------------------Chapter 2: Description\n-------------------\n\nTippen's Root is a yellow herb spotted with dark yellow spots. Tippen's Root can be spotted by the purple flowers from which it grows.Tippen's Root is known for it's odor and bitter taste\n-------------------Chapter 3: Location\n-------------------\n\nTippen's Root is commonly found in forests and around dead trees and animals.\n-------------------Chapter 4: Harvesting\n-------------------\n\nTo harvest Tippen's Root, just dig or pull out the purple flower and wash it off, then cut off the roots where it meets the stem\n-------------------Chapter 5: \nOrigin of Name:\n-------------------\n\nTippen's Root was named after the botanist and alchemist that discovered it\n-------------------Ch. 6: Uses\n-------------------\n\nTippen's Root is mainly used for medical and alchemical purposes\nI. Medical Use:\n\nTippen's Root is used by physicians and doctors to create a salve which can instantly stop bleeding, needless to say, Tippen's Root is very usefull and has saved countless lives\nRecipe: Tippen's Root Salve\n\nIngredients:\n\n*Lard or Slime\n*Tippen's Root\n\nStart by melting down the lard(or slime) till it has taken the form of a liquid. While that is busy you can begin to chop up the\nTippen's Root (after washing it of course!)\ninto fine pieces, once the base is ready throw in the Tippen's Root and whatever juices escaped during the chopping process then leave the mixture to cool. Once it has solidified it will be ready for use\nII. Alchemical Use:\n\nTippen's Root is an alchemical reagent mainly used in mending potions, infact, it is believed to be in any and all forms of healing potions\n\n\nTippen's Root symbolises:\n\n*Life\n*Regrowth\n*Resilience\n*Strength\n*Endurance\n\nTippen's Root can be used to make a usefull potion:\nRecipe: Potion of Tippen's Root\n\nBase: Distilled Water\n\nReagent: Tippen's R.\n\nFor the Potion of Tippen's Root you will need to create a base of distilled water, start doing this by heating normal water\nand grounding some coal down into a powder. Once this is done add the coal powder to the water and stir till all the powder has dissolved. Once this is done you will have a base to work with, chop up Tippen's Root into fine pieces and add it to the base.\nOnce everything has dissolved and the color of the liquid has changed, let it cool down and the result will be a usefull mending potion.\n\nIII. More on Potion of\n     Tippen's Root:\n\nHow to Apply:\n\nCommonly drunken \nfor effect however it can be poured into the wound\n\nEffects:\n\nUsefull for stopping bleeding of minor wounds to slightly bigger wounds.\nNote:\n\nIn this form the Potion of Tippen's Root is quite weak and has the potential to become greater as other reagents are added\n(I would suggest using a reagent which symbolises water)", ""),
        StaticBook("Tippen's Root", "Rathrafhir Golldir", "      Tippen's Root\n\n\nTippen’s Root represents a Moderate Earth Symbol when used in alchemy.\nTippen’s Root, named after the botanist that discovered the small, yet bitter, herb is quite common in denser forests and around hills.\n\nWithin dense forests, it can be found dug in deep around fallen trees and on hillsides.It can be identified by the fascinating purple flowers that tend to grow around it, along with its distinct appearance with numerous dark, yellow spots covering the wr-\n-nkled, ruddy herb. The uses of this herb are actually quite fascinating, for an old remedy uses it in a rather foul-smelling balm that stops bleeding nigh-instantly of some of the deepest cuts. I even observed local lumberjack stop the bleeding of a rath\ner deep wolf bite with the use of this balm, although death from blood loss appears far more favorable than inhaling the stench of this remedy.With that said it can be used for this balm of course,but in the hands of an alchemist, it can be used in\nrepresentation of earth and it's  nigh-necessary in a strong healing potion.\n\nStrength and endurance also appear to be traits of this plant and therefore they would imbue a potion  with these traits as well, for it grows like a weed and is terribly hard\nto get rid of.", ""),
        StaticBook("To a good friend", "JamesG6", "For There\nAmongst the stars\nAnd the gods\nShall two hearts join\nAnd take hold of eachother\nForever more\nIn timeless Oblivion\nthey looked through\nEachother, seeing\nbut not seeing\n\n\nShe woke that day to the sound of rain a rush of wind upon the pane of glass that led to outer world as coffee brewed downstairs she turne ", ""),
        StaticBook("To Casper", "Iren Granel", "To Casper:\nIm sorry.\nI feal as though all i've been doing is making things worse for you....\nAll my things are in the chest behind you.\nI love you...\nGoodbye....", ""),
        StaticBook("To Conjure", "Archibald Vallero", "       To Delve\n  Into Conjuration\n_-_-=_=_=+=_=_=-_-_\n\n  To begin, I assume the person reading this has some basic grasp of how to connect to the void and or how to cast a spell. Essentially, I assume the one reading this is a mage.\nIf one is not a mage, then this is not exactly the book you should continue reading.\n\n   Now, if the one reading this truly wishes to delve further into conjuration and the concepts surrounding the combination of \n-different types of evocations, such as fire and water or earth and fire. Etcetera. \n\n     Let's begin. Conjuration comes in two forms, morphon and perennial. Morphon refers to the conjuring of beasts and their hybdrids.  \nThe conjuration of such beasts ranges from a simple tadpole to an irregularly sized bull or wolf or anything of a certain weight that is considered too much mass to contain. A drake would be an example of a beast one would die simply trying to conjure. \nIn order to conjure such beasts, one must kill them. That is the one law. That is how it always has been. There is no way around the blood and organs. To be able to conjure one's desired beast one must kill it and study its internal and external features.\nAfter memorizing and picturing the beasts internal organs and external aspects, after a bit of practice, one could conjure it (given your link to the void is sufficient enough). Once one is adept enough and skilled in the conjuring of two beasts, a hybrid\nof the two beasts is capable. For example, a wolf with bull horns, or a squirrel with the feet of a bird, albeit impractical. The next form of conjuration comes into the place of plants and foliage and other greenery.  It's quite simple, really, One could\n-conjure a flower, or a tree, perhaps. These plants that are conjured can be grown through certain objects such as gates or walls if the force behind forming them is great enough, and can create holes through softer materials. Stone? No. Metal? No. The \n-act itself of evoking something such as a tree itself does not produce enough 'force' to puncture harder materials, but it does still carry force with it. The act of existing means that something produces or exerts a force on the natural world around us.\n   One thing one should also consider is the limitations behind conjuring. A caster is not capable of conjuring their beast or their greenery out of their eye's sight. The point at which you conjure your familiar/object must be in a place which you can \n-see. \n\nNow, there is a third form of conjuration which consists of the combination of evocation and morphon conjuration. This form is known as primordial conjuration. Basically, these conjurations are your regular creatures and plants\n-combined with the features and attributes of another evocation one has learned and mastered. A fire evocationist with knowledge of conjuration could evoke a bird composed of flame or a cat made of fire as well. This can be applied to other elemental -\n-evocations and conjuration when combined. \n\n -Archibald Hariman Avery Vallero", ""),
        StaticBook("To Deathsbanes", "Charlie Wick", "Dear Mr and Mrs Deathsbane, \n\nMy name is Doctor Charles Wick. I am writing from your tavern. I have just spoken to a kind man working here, and it seems that you two are the owners of this tavern, 'The Wandering Inn'. It is a lovely \nplace. May I speak to you about a job, here? I would like to apply as the tavern doctor, due to many injuries in this area. \nMany bandits roam the roads here, and I feel\nit is important to have\na doctor staitoned here. I would only ask 5 minas a day, and \nmy being there would likely bring more buisness to the tavern, with injured men coming. I would only need a small room to do medical work. We can discuss terms, if this is okay with you, at a later date. Please write back. I will be giving this letter to\n\none of your employees to deliver to you. Thank you for taking the time to read this letter.\n\nFrom,\nDoctor Charles Wick", ""),
        StaticBook("To Echo Grace", "Duke Lucius Tython", "Dear Echo,\nYou are a little bitch.\n-Lucius Tython", ""),
        StaticBook("To Enepay", "Nafis Yar", "Dear Enepay,\n\nI'm sorry I have to leave you once again. I feel as though I am trapped in this city. I have thoughts of creating a family and laying down the blade when I'm here. But that is not me. And I have a mama and papa to avenge.\nI will miss our talks of smashing women, my Qali. Shokran for bringing me to such a beautiful city. Shokran jazeelan for letting Zahrah stay with us. Please take good care of her - and you were right. I do like her a lot. I was hoping to ask her to love \nme as much as I love her when I became a man. And that maybe in time she and I would be married by our glorious Caliph. I don't think this can happen anymore. I tasted the fire water and my mentor Zhulik has slain the Prince. They might try to kill us, \nEnepay. I'm scared. I say I do not fear death but in reality I do. So I ask you for a few things. Take care of Zahrah and look out for her, please visit my new mama across the street, and pray for me my Qali. Should I return, I hope to return a man who \nknows Allah on a personal level and knows the truths of loyalty and friendship. That knows the difference between right or wrong. I love you, my Qali. You're everything I want to be and I look up to you. I don't even remember if my real mama and papa \nloved me. But I'm grateful to call you family, my Qali. \n\nFarewell,\n\nNafis Yar.", ""),
        StaticBook("To Father Johan", "Guru Pinya", "~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\n\nThe Letter of Guru Pinya to the Leaders of the Church of the Order of St. Amyas.\n\n             I\n          --I--\n             I\n          --I--\n             I\n\n~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~+~\nBrothers of the Church of the Order of St. Amyas. I write to you not in hate, but in love for your wellbeing. My name is Guru Pinya, I am a firm believe of the Creator, an author of theological books, a teacher of doctrine, and a Church militant. As the \nspiritual leaders of Leuvarden, I must point out that the Ordermen under your sect have been committing great hypocrisy. These men go around torturing people of different faith. Yes, you did indeed read correctly, I said torturing. Not only did I have\nnegative feeling towards this group of organized barbarians, I told them of their errors in the way of the Lord. First I noted to them that your Cathedral is not the house of God, it is the house of worship, and I told them that saying such things sugges-\n-ted that the Creator is incompetent and is limited to such a small and humane building. Next I told them of their error in calling their superiors 'Lord'. This title is meant for the One True Lord, the Creator. Also, I might mention that your parishioner\nshould not call you Father, but they should call you Teacher, or Guru like myself. I also taught them of their error in conversion. These barbaric men kill unbelievers, in a crucifixion, which desecrates the sacred symbol known as the Lorraine\nCross. Anyways, they shouldn't even be killing! The Lord commanded that no one should kill, for it is the Lord's decision to take anyone's lives! One Ordermen claimed that torturing unbelievers was saving them from eternal damnation! Not only do bring \nabout the followers of the True Faith, but the make the Lord seem evil, because these men try to use scripture from the Holy Scrolls to support their view points! Now I must tell you what they did to me. Two men came up to me and told me to give them a \npamphlet I had written about their barbaric heresy. I gave it to them, and they began to assault me, not only with force, but with words. They then dragged me into the river, and plunged my head in for periods at a time. Once I began to drift into \nunconsciousness, they decided that since I had written the pamphlet, they should cut off my right hand, which explains why my handwriting is so sloppy. Then they said since I was so opinionated, they would cut out my tongue, which is why I write a letter\nto you. In all sincerity, please correct you flock, for they are misguided by the lust of violence.\n\nPeace be with you, and glorify the world by your light.\n \n           -Guru Payina\n\n\n\n\n\nm\ne\nm\ne\ns", ""),
        StaticBook("To Giliath", "Luxeanna", "Dear Giliath,\n\nI'd like to take this opportunity to thank you for your dedication, leadership and guidance of the Bards Guild. You have generated the momentum to keep moving this guild forward and the wisdom to keep it moving in the right direction. It is\nan understatement to say that we benefited from your contributions, because you did so much more for us. \n\nWhen you left, the hole that the lack of your presence immediately came to light. To step into this role feels like steering something heavy.\nThe Bards' Guild operates under a plan which sets it's direction,and all of that was done by you. I willdo everything that I can to steer this guild into a path you had set out for us.\n\nSee you again, friend.", ""),
        StaticBook("To Lelien", "Mithradites", "\n\n     To the One for\n      Whom I Yearn\nEyes of striking emeralds,\n\nbroken heart, soon to be mended.\n\nOne can give only his love,\n\nto earn your own, currently suspended.\n\nWhen in this Tomb, the light touched naught,\n\nluminescence came from only our fires.\n\nBut when I saw you approach that day,\n\nthe chamber lit as if strewn with a thousand pyres.\n\nOne cannot take without permission,\n\nand you shan't give without desire.\n\nI shall not push, nor take your love,\n\nbut without you with me, my emotions expire.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nOne is not a poet.", ""),
        StaticBook("To Lucius Visaj", "Abigail", "-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-\n\nTo: Lucius Visaj\nFrom: Abigail Massey\n\nDear Lucius,\nI am able to show you the basics of the arts of Alchemy, furthermore you should know that even I as an alchemist do not know of every\nsingle potion that there is to brew in Athera. The answer is a yes from me, but you must find me whenever I am avaliable. Please meet me at the clinic near the gates of the city, or you can send a bird.\nSincerely,\nAbigail Massey\n-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-", ""),
        StaticBook("To Missus Carrot", "Pern", "Missus Carrot,\n\nI was so delighted to find that you had picked up my letter earlier! Have you forgiven me?? It's been so long since I've seen you I fear the oceans may flood from my tears! If you wish to not see me... could you possibly\nsend a letter? Or just one word of your well being? I worry so much! Please take care of yourself Missus Carrot! I promise I will never eat you again! That was a terrible thing of me to do!\n\nWith much love,\n    Pern", ""),
        StaticBook("To Resia", "MetaSol", "To my dearest beloved,\n\nYou said much in your letter, of how you onced loved me and how you held me dearly. IT seems that you held several years of turmoil in your heart; and while some days you may have felt a deep love for me and others-\nyou held nothing but discontempt. I wish you had always known that every day I ensured in my heart I truly held the title of Mr. Los'iirae in my ideal heart and my love for you beside it.\n\nHate? I cannot hate you, for hate implies you have dishonored me-\nin a way I cannot forgive. All I have ever felt was love for you and in this I have diservice you; If your happiness means away from me, as your own person Resia Sae'lantae then I give onto you that choice of your own.\nI made a promise to you, and I hope I can keep it even if the memory of it and so much more may leave me in heart and mind.\n\n\"Má thagann mé, beidh tú ar ghabháil fós dom? Gheall mé tú ba mhaith liom teacht fós tú\"\n\n\nMeta Los'iirae\n\n\"Never may the sun forget the rose that it given onto it's light.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("To tristin", "Caranor", "Things to notice when you march to Dun'Sildur; \n\nThe force of the hole will pull you up, get have thing with you to keep yourself on the ground.\n\nThe one who controls it is the Lord of Undru, you'll have to kill him.", ""),
        StaticBook("To You  [ .CB]", "Colette Blake", "Dear,\n\nI am not the first person you loved. You are not the first person I looked at with a mouthful of forevers. We have both known loss like the sharp edges of a  knife. We have both lived with lips more scar tissue than skin.\nOur love came unannounced in the middle of the night.\nOur love came when we'd given up on asking love to come.\nI think that has to be part of the miracle.\n\nThis is how we heal.\nI will kiss you like forgiveness.\nYou will hold me like i'm hope.\nOur arms will bandage and we will press promises between us like flowers in a book.\n\nI will write sonnets to the salt of sweat on your skin. I will write novels to the scar of your nose. I will write a dictionary of all the words I have used trying to\ndescribe the way it feels to have finally found you.\n\nAnd I will not be afraid of who you are, and your scars.\n\nI know sometimes\nIt's still hard to let me see you\nin all your cracked perfection,\nbut please know:\n\nwhether its the days you burn more brilliant than the sun\n\nor the nights you collapse into my lap\nyour body broken into a thousand questions,\nyou are the most beautiful thing i have ever seen.\n\n\n\n\nI will love you when you are a still day.\n\nI will love you when you are a hurricane.\n", ""),
        StaticBook("To: Taynuel", "Laura", "    Dear Taynuel,\n\n   In the short time that you've been my guide, you've shown me nothing but unwavering, true kindness that I would only expect from a lifelong friend, from making me a fishing rod, to baking me bread, to giving me a blessed apple, to \ngiving detailed lessons on what it means to be a Druid in a way only you can explain. In this way, I feel that I owe it to you to pay you back in my own special way, so you may expect random delivieries of food or pastries in the time to come, as my way\nof saying thank you.\n\nI won't be taking no as an answer. ツ\n\n       ❤ Laura", ""),
        StaticBook("Tobo", "Tobo", "~*~Living Simply~*~\n\nChewing on bamboo\n\nWithout a care in the world –\n\nThe Sun slowly sinks\n~*~ Daily life as...~*~\nRolling in the branches Enjoying all the fun Dancing about so proud\nPulling at the bamboo And defending his tree Never going to far from home\nDanger lurks everywhere.\nAll is well... for now\n~*~Cycling Wind~*~\n\nThe wind goes with it.\nSeemingly, always to fit.\nIt goes without a care.\nYet, it is always fair.\nNature is like wind.\nA cylce, never to end.\n  ~*~ Connected ~*~\n\nAll together, one.\n\nWe stand proud and just as we are bound.\n\nAll one, together.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome: Air Evo", "Uradriethiel", "Air Evocation\nThis book was created by Master Air Evocatonist Alatar of the Mage Academy\n\nRecopied By\nUradriethiel\nTable of Conents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\nChapter 1: \nAccording to legend and study, the void is an infinite plane of potential thought and energy surrounded by absolute darkness. Since complete darkness is rather hard to imagine, think about a blank canvas.\nHypothetically, anything can be drawn out of the void if it does not contradict nature. The first steps of becoming a mage is learning to understand the void and it's 'blank' \nconcept. Meditation is the most efficient and common way, as I have found.\nLearn how to blanket yourself in a blank state of mind and prepare to paint your canvas.\n\nChapter 2:\nWind and air are essences to all life around the realm of existance. You breathe it into your very self.\n\nWith the art of air evoction, you manipulate it. Wind is unpredictable and one\nof the best studied branches of arcane magics, You must experience the element in it's true form before you can even think about summoning it.\n\nIn order to achieve this, I suggest moving to a  region of high altitude and feel the wind rush over your very body. Experience how pressure seeps through the cracks of all substance. Taste it, feel it, smell it.\nAnother useful experiment is placing something in the air that can float. Watch how the wind carries the item and gently cradles it back down to the earth or\ncarries\nit away\n\n\nChapter 3:\nAfter you have learned all there is to know about your element, and your meditation is firm; you may begin your attempts in summoning your element from the void. Firstly, you must find a place of peace.\nNext, use your blank canvas to imagine air, allow the thought to fill you and the energy of yourself to course through your very blood. Through combining all of this. A small amount of wind may be conjured.\n\nWarning: You may pass out a few times uponfirst connection.\n\nChapter 4: After you have mastered basic connection, you can move onto some of the key spells in an air evocationist's arsenal.\nThe first and most obvious, is the force push. Essentially, a novice air evocationist musters all of his strength for one large\nand forceful push at the enemy. Secondly, and slightly more defensive, is the protective tornado.\nA skilled air evocationist can create a spiraling current of wind that could potentially keep most objects out of your sphere. That concludes this tome. \n", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome: Air Evo.", "Violetoin", "Air Evocation\nThis book was created\nby Master Air\nEvocationist Alatar of\nthe Mage Academy.\n\nRecopied By \nVioletoin\n\nTable of Contents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\n\nChapter 1:\nAccording to legend\nand study, the void is\nan infinite plane of\npotential thought and\nenergy surrounded by\ncomplete darkness.\nSince complete\ndarkness is rather\nhard to imagine, think\nabout a blank canvas.\nHypothetically,\nanything can be drawn\nout of the void if it\ndoes not contradict\nnature. The first steps\nof becoming a mage is\nlearning to understand\nthe void and it's 'blank'\nconcept. Meditation is\nmost efficent and\ncommon way, as I\nhave found.\nLearn how to blanket\nyourself in a blank\nstate of mind and\nprepare to create\nyour canvas.\n\nChapter 2:\nWind and Air are\nessences to all life\naround the realm of\nexistance. You\nbreathe it into your\nvery self.\nWith the art of air\nevocation, you\nmanipulate it. Wind is\nunpredicatable and one\nof the best studied\nbranches of arcane\nmagics. You must\nexperience the\nelement in it's true\nform before you can\neven think about\nsummoning it.\nIn order to achieve\nthis, I suggest moving\nto a region of high\naltitude and feel the\nwind rush over your\nvery body. Experience\nhow pressure seeps\nthrough the cracks\nof all substance.Taste it,\nfeel it, smell it.\nAnother useful\nexperiment is placing\nsomething in the air\nthat can float. Watch\nhow the wind carries\nthe item and gently\ncradles it back down\nto the earth or carries\nit away.\n\nChapter 3:\nAfter you have\nlearned all there is\nto know about your\nelement, and your\nmeditation is firm; you\nmay begin your\nattempts in summoning\nyour element from the\nvoid. Firstly, you must\nfind a place of peace.\nNext, use your blank\ncanvas to imagine air,\nallow the thought to\nfill you and the\nenergy of yourself to\ncourse through your \nvery blood. Though\ncombining all of this.\nA small amount of\nwind may be conjured\nWarning: You may\npass out a few times\nupon first connection.\n\nChapter 4:\nAfter you have\nmastered basic\nconnection, you can\nmove onto some of the\nkey spells in an air\nevocationists arsenal.\nThe first and most\nobvious, is the force\npush. Essentially, a\nnovice air evocationist\nmusters all of his\nstrength for one large\nand forceful push at\nan enemy. Secondly,\nand slightly more\ndefensive, is the\nprotective tornado.\nA skilled air\nevocationist can\ncreate a spiraling\ncurrent of wind that\ncould potentially keep\nmost objects out of\nyour sphere. That\nconcludes this tome.\n\nFor any other\nquestions, feel free to\nvisit the Mages' Guild.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome: Air Evo.", "The Scribe", "Air Evocation\nThis book was created by Master Air Evocationist Alatar of the Mage Academy.\n\nRecopied by The Scribe\nTable of Contents\n\nChapter 1: The Void\n\nChapter 2: All About Wind\n\nChapter 3: Summoning\n\nChapter 4: Basic Spells\nChapter 1:\nAccording to legend and study, the void is an infinite plane of potential thought and energy surrounded by complete darkness. Since complete darkness is rather hard to imagine, think about a blank canvas.\nHypothetically, anything can be drawn out of the void if it does not contradict nature. The first steps of becoming a mage is learning to understand the void and it’s ‘blank’ concept. Meditation is most efficient and common way, as I have found.\nLearn how to blanket yourself in a blank state of mind and prepare to create your canvas.\n\nChapter 2:\nWind and Air are essences to all life around the realm of existence. You breathe it into your very self.\nWith the art of air evocation, you manipulate it. Wind is unpredictable and one of the best studied branches of arcane magics. You must experience the element in it’s true from before you can even think about summoning it.\nIn order to achieve this, I suggest moving to a region of high altitude and feel the wind rush over your very body. Experience how pressure seeps through the cracks of all substance. Taste it, feel it, smell it.\nAnother useful experiment is placing something in the air that can float. Watch how tthe wind carries the item and gently cradles it back down to the earth or carries it away.\n\nChapter 3: \nAfter you have learned all there is to to know about your element, and your meditation is firm; You may begin your attempts in summoning your element from the void. Firstly, you must find a place of peace.\nNext, use your blank canvas to imagine air, allow the thought to fill you and the energy of yourself to course through your very blood. Though combining all of this. A small amount of wind may be conjured. \nWARNING: You may pass out a few times upon first connection.\n\nChapter 4: \nAfter you have mastered basic connection, you can move onto some of the key spells in an air evocationists arsenal.\nThe first and most obvious, is the force push. Essentially, a novice air evocationist musters all of his strength for one large and forceful push at an enemy. Secondly, and slightly more defensive, is the protective tornado. \nA skilled air evocationist can create a spiraling current of wind that could potentially keep most objects out of your sphere. That concludes this tome.\n\nFor any other questions, feel free to visit the Mages' Guild.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tome: Water Evo.", "Arayon Drakon", "Go and meditate in a ice biome you fool!", ""),
        StaticBook("Torn journal", "Skeletal writings", "*The book before you appears to be some kind of journal, however its writings are inelligable, save for a few words.\n\n\"Kavir\", \"Grond\", and \"Vlokon\" are repeated many times.*\n*The final page of the journal appears to be stained with blood, but a faint drawing of can be made out, depicting five glimmering swords surrounding an ominous dark skull*", ""),
        StaticBook("Torn Page", "Ancient Dwarf", "[!] This page has been torn from it's respective tome.\nScratching on the walls. That's all I've heard for the last.. what? I can't count the days any longer. All of my kinsmen have gone missing. I'm all that's left. Khorvad preserve me, for I fear I shan't escape his place alive. Footsteps... I hear footsteps", ""),
        StaticBook("Torn Page", "Sprat", "What you speak of implies great ignorance and lack of understanding for what Enlightenment my coil has been endowed with. Due to my nature of divinity, as the Matriarch of Pain, the mortal construct of pleasure is a falsehood to me. In the past ages of my\nbeing have I come to know unfathomable satisfactories, but one must understand that their comfort is that of a dieing and dim light. It--. . ..\\ ", ""),
        StaticBook("TOTTA: Book I-2", "SapphireShard", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist\n-----------\nElements of Alchemy\n    \nVolume I-2 of V\nThe Elements:\n--------\nFire\n--\nEarth\n--\nWater\n--\nAir\n--\nSkeptics often approach me with a wholly ignorant conundrum, and that is “how can you trace every spell and element back to these four basic elements.”\nBut what they do not realize is that an understanding of what is meant by these four elements is required before that question can be answered and in fact,\nif that understanding is acquired then this question will never be asked.\nThese four basic, rudimentary elements, Fire, Earth, Water, and Air, are what every potion, oil, elixir, and concept, in Alchemy of course, is based upon.\nthey must look deeper than the surface. I shall go into each of these elements in detail, so that you, and budding alchemist or simply a well-read individual,\nthey must look deeper than the surface. I shall go into each of these elements in detail, so that you, and budding alchemist or simply a well-read individual,\nmay understand how Air may both aid someone’s speed, as well as their perception of their surroundings.\nFire \n=== \nThe Destroyer, the Unmaker, the Cleanser.\nWe will begin with one of the most rudimentary and used element, Fire. Fire can represent many a feeling, action, and state and because of this, it is often one of the most useful elements of the Alchemist’s arsenal.\nFire can represent rage, anger, hatred, as well as bravery, courage, and gusto. Through this a brew can be crafted to make someone unquestionably and savagely furious, but it could also craft a brew to strengthen one’s will,\nand allow them to do what other men would shrink away in fear at. These feelings, these emotions can be invoked by the element of Fire within a potion, but feelings are not the only thing that fire can produce, actions are another.\n\n Actions are often the most recognized aspect of fire, and by this I mean the action of burning, consuming, and destroying.\nAlchemist’s Fire, a handy inferno in a bottle, is an example of this aspect of Fire, for within it is the action of destruction. This action can be further expanded upon with burning oil placed upon a weapon,\nbut if one were to use this example they would also have to include the element of pain, another feeling that fire an invoke. State is aspect that Fire can take one within an alchemist’s potion and this is often represented as Chaos.\nIt is true that Chaos is often something alchemist’s fear, for it is something athat often ruins a potion, but it is yet another aspect of Fire that must be recognized.\nChaos, being the state that it is, may seem like it has little place in a potion but brilliant minds and powerful reason can bring it to use just the same as the first farmer realized a stone and a stick,\ntwo completely different objects, could help them till their fields by putting them together. As this is not a book on the philosophy of alchemy, or solely on the element of fire, I will not go into depth on how Chaos can be used.\nThe teacher must leave something for the student to ponder.\n\nEarth\n===\n\nThe Enduring, The Lifegiver, the Fortifying\nWith the conclusion of Fire, the other elements will not have as long as description for most of the fundamentals of the elements are covered in how they can represent different things,\nI will simply go over what they may represent, starting with Earth.\nEarth is a truly powerful element for it may represent metals, strength,\nthe notion of being stalwart, solidity, firmness, flora, animals, and countless other, quite obvious, subjects. Earth is often used for potions that increase one’s endurance,\nor strength, and it is a must in potions that toughen one’s skin. In fact, a strong series of representations of Earth are required to perfect a barksin, or stoneskin potion.\nProtecting oneself is not the only use for the element of Earth, for it is also used in antidote potions, disease-curing potions,\nand, of course, fertilizers. Antidotes use this element for it can represent animals, and among those elements are snakes and spiders,\noften associated with poison. Of course a symbol of Water is also required for a proper antidote, but this will be addressed later on.\nMany of the uses of the element of Earth are quite obvious, but a always, if one wishes to master the field of Alchemy they must learn creativity is their greatest friend,\nand using elements in odd and interesting ways can be useful, and quite detrimental, in numerous ways. A final note on the element of Earth is how it is associated with the fable of Transmutation.\nNow, as a scholarly and skeptic individual, I have doubts upon transmutation, its existence, and possibility. This will be described more in depth, but know that the element of Earth is the greatly represented by metals,\nfor metals are merely ores in their purest forms.\nWater\n===\n\nThe Base, the Purifier, the Healer.\nWater is the element that seems to get the most amount of use due to how it is a base for many a potion and it is true, for if someone wants a liquid spell they want to use the Element of Water.\nOften known as the “base” element, it truly lives up to its name for observations, and personal experience, lead all to believe it is necessary for life. This base element, is often associated with flowing,\nliquids, balance, peace, calm, order, life, and rebirth. One requires water for any potion that one plans on drinking, and any oil that one plans on applying for to have a liquid,\none must use the Element of Water. Now that and understanding on how Water is a base, we must go into how it is used beyond simply supporting the other elements.\nWater’s uses are varied, for it can be used both in antidote as well as poisons, showing that it is quite the versatile element. One may also use it to create a potion of calmness, or perhaps a potion of healing due to its association with life.\nBut one can also use this beautiful element in the creation of an oil to coat a weapon, or to coat and strengthen armor. This is not the same as enchanting armor or weaponry, mind you, for doing that is often permanent and requires magic.\nThrough oils, one may reap similar benefits of enchantment but instead it is only temporary and anyone with basic sense can apply them. The purest base, and often representation of water, is Aqua Vitae or “Water of Life,” which is best made\nwhich is best made by distilling wine or other liquors\nAir\n===\n\nThe Nimble, the Traveller, the All-Seeing.\nOur final element, the element of Air, is not the least by any stretch of imagination but is often the hardest one to put into representation and use in a potion. Now this element is often associated with agility, quickness, travel, feet, perception,\nand even flight, it can be quite difficult to embody the element of Air into a physical item due to our basic fault of lacking understanding of what we cannot see. But with reason and the opening of one’s eyes,\nthe ways in which to represent Air are quite obvious in our everyday world.\nItems that can be used to represent air, and subsequently put through your tools to produce a potion,\nare rather easy to find during your average day but of course, as with all the elements, purer ingredients are often quite rarer. One representation that you may have already thought of is a mere feather,\nwhich is often the most common representation of Air. This, of course, is not the purest representation of the element but it will often due for your more basic potions centered around this element.\nAnother, more outlandish representation, are rabbits’ feet. Rabbits are often seen as quick and agile critters, and as Air takes on the aspect of agility and quickness, it becomes quite obvious how the feet of a rabbit and Air intermingle.\nWith that said, it becomes rather easy to find out representations of Air through simply looking and observing the world around you. But do not fret if you are still hard-pressed to find these symbols,\nthe next chapter will explain herbs that will best aid you in the process of finding symbols for all the elements.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tower, v?", "Unknown", "\"Captain!\" the man called out in disgust. \"Why are you here… Why do you do this? Betray your own people in the name of this monster?\" The captain scoffed, looking down at him from above. \"I have served Horen for nearly all my life. I received nothing\nbut a meager pittance in exchange! I risked my life on a daily basis to defend the Capital, and I am repaid with nothing. Only kind words and thoughts. I never grew in power when I was a soldier...but when I turned mine eyes towards the powers of magic,\nand the Mages, well...\"   The Captain laughed heartily now, throwing his head back before leaning forward to look down upon the man scornfully. \"The Mages are weak. I chose my own magic to use. I found forbidden tomes, researched and plotted, waiting\nfor the perfect opportunity to strike. I learned the arts well, better than any of the Guild would know. Now, I am ready to strike against you filthy humans and join in Iblees' service. You fools are too weak, too cowardly. Too afraid to deal with\nsomething you do not understand, or learn about something your forefathers have deemed 'forbidden.' Hah! You are just a cowardly lot who will die easily at my hands, as Horen will die at the hands of Iblees!\"", "")
    )

    private fun _ATHERA_7(): List<StaticBook> = listOf(
        StaticBook("Town Registery", "Gregor", "All Govermental Buildings in Ambrose:\n\nGreat hall\nOffice & Chambers\nRoyal Residence Tower Statue of the Steed\nChurch\nDocks\nShipbuilding dock\nGatehouses.\nCommon Residences.\nFarmlands\nSmithy\nThese are all registered buidlings within Ambrose's Control.\n \n\nThese Buildings are found in or around Ambrose and the lord of Ambrose and his court have absolute authority over them.", ""),
        StaticBook("Translocators", "Archmage Haadi", "               ~*~\n      On the creation\n    of translocators\n                  -\n\n\n\n              \\o/\n              -O-\n              /o\\\n[!] Along the page you see many strange markings and drawings. They look to be rough copies, at best. There appears to be some sort of archway built of stone, below it, several odd circular schematics, or perhaps a map. Next to the map, the phases of the\nmoon trail along.\n\n[OOC] View in browser:\n\n tinyurl.com/slatrop\n-------------------\nAn odd name, indeed. Though, the actual device is far more mysterious and eldritch in nature. Translocators, Lori Oathcast refers to them as. Crumena took to calling them closets, as per their birthplace, the closets within Rasmot's magical porta-potty.\nRegardless, the process for creating these translocators is a fairly complex ritualistic one that involves both a skilled Void Translocationist with knowledge of the subject, and a fairly powerful enchanter capable of binding the spell to the locations\nin question.\n---\nCreation\n\nAn enchanter holds the magic to the spot. One who knows how to send matter to the Void and bring it back again must have learnt to create what is like a 'gateway' into the Void. More than just the item itself, but anything that touches it. Even\nair. Then again you do it on the other end and the mage attempts to pull the block back through the Void and the enchanter binds that to a doorway or pillar or something. In short, one must reach into the Void and pull out an open space.\nMaintenance\n\nAfter time, it will need a mana supply or one end will fizzle out before the other and create an endless hole. It's advised then that you have the enchanter available fairly often to refill whatever large mana gem or crystal you wish to power\nthe translocators' magic with. As far as i'm aware, this would be the only option, unless one would be able to get ahold of Kalameet's notes on the mana obelisk. For the ignorant, this is large pillar in which sums of mana are stored for use. The mana\ncannot be pulled out by any means by a magi to fuel their own spellcasting, but instead it exerts energy needed to power enchantments in the area. I'd elaborate further, though i'm only going off of what i've seen myself.", ""),
        StaticBook("Traveler's Guide", "Illmori", "Nice and Bad Places\nGood=10\nNice=9\nPretty good=8\nPretty nice=7 \nOkay=6\nWell=5\nNot so Nice=4\nNot so good=3\nUgh=2\nEww=1\nWorse=0 \n\nNerezza - 3\nPetrus - 8\nHaelun'or - 9\nCerulin - 9 \nDul'Sildure - 7\nAkovia - 5\nWerdenberg - 8 \nViridian Inn - 3\nDwarven City - 7\nCloud Temple - 7 \nUruk Lands - 6\nDwarven City with ships - 2\nConyon lands - 5\nBronze Elven Lands - 7\n\n-=-=-\n\nTourist's Guide : \n\nForest Walks : Cerulin, Haelun'or, Petrus, Nerezza, Akovia, Dwarven city with ships\nSwimming : Cerulin, Dul'Sildur, Dwarven city with ships, Bronze elven lands\nEarning Tan : \nUruk Lands, Dul'Sildur, Petrus\n\nParty and Events \n(One to Ten scale used) \nNerezza - 2\nPetrus - 10\nHaelun'or - 8\nCerulin - 4\nDul'Sildur - 7\nAkovia - 3\nWerdenberg - 5\nViridian Inn - 8\nDwarven City - 3\nCloud Temple - 7\nUruk Lands - 3\nDwarven City With Ships - 1\nConyon Lands - 3\nBronze Elven lands - 7\n\n-=-=-\n\nDescriptions : \nNerezza - Unpeaceful Cold Kingdom (Bad for elves) \nPetrus - Friendly Central\nKingdom. May have some problems there sometimes. \nHaelun'or - A beautiful, but spooky city, high racism rates\nCerulin - A Beautiful, but spooky city, high control rates\nDul'Sildur - Scary, but interesting castle. Good for walks or adventures. Have a lot \nof secrets\nAkovia - Small Kingdom, few citizens, not so fun\nWerdenberg - Small City, lots of citizens, pretty fun\nVirdinian inn - Inn with lots of things happening in it. Good for scandals or fights\nDwarven city - Absolutely boring place, but beautiful \nsomehow. \nCloud Temple - The. Center. Of. The. World. \nUruk Lands - Hot and dry places, bad peoples, lots of fights\nDwarven city with ships - What The Nether \nConyon lands - Pretty boring, but interesting too lands. \nbronze elven lands - ...", ""),
        StaticBook("Treating Wounds", "Kristoff Herrick", "     Wound healing\n\nMy time in the Order of Saint Amyas did teach me a few things, healing wounds is one of them. \nLet's start by steps\nStep 1- Cleaning the wound. \nAlthough that might sound simple, water will not do, for a gash or\ndeeper wounds you'll need something with alcohol in it, like beer or something of the sorts. (ofcourse you need to take the dirt off first if it has dirt in it). \nStep 2- Suttering the wound. \nFor that you'll need a needle and some thread.Sounds easy \nright? Well not really, you need a steady hand and you also need to do it by these...Let's called them sub steps.\nSub Step I Heat up the needle. You need to do that incase of the needle carring any illness.\nSub Step II Clean the thread.You need to do\nit beacause you don't want dirt on your wound.\n \nSun Step III-Suttering You start at well the start of the gash or deep wound (Warning this does NOT apply for small wounds.) \nYou'll then sutter like your sewing a rag, a messy and bloody rag\nthat screams like a little girl, non the less a rag. \n\nStep 3-Bandaging\nA bandage can be almost anything, a old rag (As long as it's clean) A bed sheet (As long as it's clean) etc. Then you just wrap it around the wound. Done\nNote: If there's any sign of infection after or  before you done this process you'll be better off cuting off the leg.", ""),
        StaticBook("Trebuchet Basics", "[Steward] Boaz Colin", "How to operate a Trebuchet:\n\nPlace a leather mat under the cannon hook.\n\nRoll a boulder on the mat.\n\nHook the mat around the boulder and attach it on the hook.\nRelease the Trebuchet lever.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tree Task", "[Dedicant] Barry Winterleaf", "\n       Task Notes\n\nI have found a calm area to plant the sapling, just outside of lillith's grove, firstly i have set up a camp, a fire and a tent is all i need for now, i have also planted the sapling close by, so i can keep an eye on it\nI will start caring for it in the morning, for now i will rest.\n\nI have brought certain equipment with m to help with the trees growth, i will list them on the next page.\nEquipment\n\n10 bags of Fertilizer.\n\nA bucket, for water.\n\nFencing, for protection.\n\n\n\nI estimate using 2 bags of Fertilizer every 2 months, with maybe some left over. I will begin logging how i care for the tree in the morning.\nThe first month.\n\nStarting off, i have planted the sapling just outside of where i have set up a tent, for the first few days i have started to sprinkle some Fertilizer around the sapling, along with pouring a little water in the soil.\nI even told it a story. i am not sure if it would help or not but, i havne't spoken to anyone in days, it was nice to speak.\n\nLightning had struck close by in the night, it woke me up, a fire was close, small and easy to put out, it wasn't to close.\nThe sapling seems to have properly rooted itself into the ground, its stirdy and i can see some change in its growth.\n\nI have gotten into the routine of giving the sapling a little fertilizer and a little water every two days, this seems to keep\nthe sapling growing at a steady rate, the first month is almost over and the sapling is slowly showing good results in its growth.\n\nThe second month\n\nSince the sapling has now rooted into the dirt i have stopped giving it water as the\nroots should be able to pull water from the soil, i will continue to spread fertilizer every two days however.\n\nA few more days have passed, the sapling is showing rather promising results in its growth, the fertilizer is helping greatly.\nI sat by the sapling for awhile, humming a song that my mother used to sing to me when i was a boy, if it could hear me, i hope it enjoyed it.\n\nI have gone through my second bag of fertilizer, i sprinkled the last of the bag this morning. the tree\nis making good progress and i find the area very peaceful and relaxing.\n\nI find myself fending off a few monsters in the night, they seem to have increased in number, they get close to the tree but are more interested in me, so i fight back.\nThe Third month\n\nThe tree is making great progress now, i would say it is around the same size as me now and i feel a sense of accomplishment whenever i tend to it. i have a few more bags of fertilizer on me which should last the remaining time i have.\nSpreading the fertilizer has also caused some grass and plants to grow in the area, along with the tree the area is starting to look rather nice.\n\nI am spending most nights fighting off monsters now, they come in small waves\nand are easy to handle but with the increase in the amount it is getting a little hard to sleep. something is causing more to come, maybe me being here is causing more to come, but its best to not think upon this and focus on the tree.\nI have few bags of fertilizer left and i think it may be time to stop spreading it, i think the tree can go without it now, i will let a few weeks pass and then look at the results.\nThe Fourth month\n\nThe tree has grown taller than me now, it is nice to see it growing without fertilizer, i think this is the final month i will need to be here, i will spend a some time here and talk to the tree, if it can hear me, i hope it enjoyed\nmy company for the time i have been with it.\n\nI have watched over the tree now for a total of just under four months, i am going to spend just a few more days with the tree, and then leave to speak with Arin to tell her i think i have completed my task\nall in all this has been a nice experience and to be honest i am a little sad to see it come to an end, and this will be the last thing i write before heading out to speak to Arin, i hope the tree enjoyed me here as much as i have enjoyed caring for it.", ""),
        StaticBook("Trial of Imarssa", "Asul'athri Parir'tailyu", "Imarssa Sillonaine is hereby charged with attempted murder, assault and battery, and extreme insubordination in regards to Elsillumiran, as well as letting in the unscrupulant ata and avernan. Pariran'tir include Elwyn, Braxus Ni'leya, and Mediliyur.\nElwyn begins questioning. First question: How do you plead to the list of charges?\n\nImarssa: Not guilty, except for that of insubordination.\n\nElwyn asks Ibar Izalith, witness whom alleged that she had made an\nattempt to kill him, as to how many witnesses he had. He answered one, being Earendur. She then asked him to recount his story.\n\nIbar: She came to me sword drawn, saying they needed to chat. She advances towards him, but stops when Earendur leaves the \ninn. She stops, and says, \"It's a shame he arrived.\" He inquired as to whether she had meant to kill him, to which she said, \"Perhaps next time.\"\n\nElwyn asks Imarssa to make defence. Ibar interrupts, withdraws claim as to the Sohaer having issued the \ncommand to Imarssa. Begins directly slandering Elsohaer, Elwyn silences him. Imarssa begins.\n\nImarssa: I approached Ibar, fully armoured, and told him I wished to speak privately. He refused, and I inssisted, and then Earendur came out to\nsay he wished to speak with me. Before Ibar left, he stated that he believed me to have intention of killing him. I responded with a sarcastic \"Definitely.\"\n\nElwyn asks her as to what question she wishd to ask. Imarssa replied that she had wished to take \nlessons for elmaehr'sae hiylun'ehya. Elwyn asks why this would require the unsheathing of a sword. Imarssa replies that it was the entire time sheathed.\n\nBraxus begins line of questioning.\n\nBraxus: How many \ntimes have you been a citizen, and how many times have you been expelled?\n\nImarssa: Three and two respectively.\n\nMediliyur begins interrogation. She asks for a recount of her final crime, insubordination. \nImarssa begins recounting story.\n\nImarssa: I allowed Tarlok into elcihi after recieving word that he would exchange information on a possible coming attack in exchange for a chance to go through his purity trials.\n\nBraxus interjects, clarifying that these were against his direct orders. Interrogation continus.\n\nMediliyur: Was this action cleared with any official, such as oem'Medi'ir?\n\nImarssa: Tarlok admitted to having no\ntrue information and was hoping that I would play along, as he believes me still his sister. When he entered, I moved to apprehend him against Braxus' orders, as he is a fire evocationist. I was attempting to stop him from connecting to the void in \nbreaking his concentration through pain. After an extended amount of warning from Braxus, I removed him from elcihi.\n\nPresider Okarir'tir Asul'athri requests that they reach a verdict. Elwyn request for time for discussion,\nrequest was granted. Three Pariran'tir parlay quietly, attempting to reach their verdicts.\n\nVerdict is reached.\n\nBraxus names the accused guilty, as does Mediliyur and Elwyn Punishment is noted to be \nbanishment and branding, with no chance of appeal or return.", ""),
        StaticBook("Tristan The Bard", "Aepholis", "The soft breeze touches Tristan's skin, as he wakes up in his lower class bed, small windowcrack open. Tristan gets swiftly up on his feet, as he'd grab his favorite turquise blue shirt, buttoning up, as he fetches his black pair of trousers. \nHe'd grab his colorful royal blue coat from a hanging rack, along with his feather trilby, heading to the local inn, flute slinged over his right shoulder.  As he heads to the local inn of Aldersberg, whistling a small tune, a nice smirk on his face.\nAs he enters, the inn seems crowdy, the brightness moody and candles lighting the place. He nods firmly to Ciera, aswell as two other lovely ladies. He orders a glass of red wine from Ciera, handing her the sufficient minas.\nHe slowly gulps it down, as he brings the flute up to his puffy lips, a soft and gentle melody starting to play. Three burly mates arch a brow at Tristan, shaking their heads disapprovingly.\nOne raises, walking forward to Tristan.\nTristan nods to the lad, as his flute is suddenly pulled away from his lips, drool connecting between. Tristan frowns, as he'd hear a crack, the man breaking the pipe in half. A elegant man, puffing on his pipe raises, heading to the scene.\nHe'd nod to the burly man, of the name Tor, as he'd put a hand gently on Tristan's shoulders, shaking his head, grip on his gold-tipped cane tightening slightly, as he'd give Tor a hateful and mean look, before walking away with Tristan.\nTor grumbles, sending a kick for the back of the elegant man's knee, causing him to fall facefront, making quite the ruckus. The man chuckles, as he'd raise, dusting dust off his fine clothing, tilting his head at Tor, smirking widely.\nTristan gulps worriedly, as he'd step a bit back, glancing between the two. The elegant man turns again, as Tor sends a punch for the back of his head, grabbing a bottle from a table, breaking it on the edge of the table itself.\nThe elegant man is hit in the back of the head with a fist, as he'd swiftly turn on his heel and send the gold-part of the cane into Tor's jaw, a loud crack being heard. Tor cringes exremely, falling to the ground, as the elegant man turns yet again. \nThe two burly chaps raise from their seats, one unsheathing an axe, while the other draws a dagger. The elegant man frowns slightly, as this will be a challenge. He notes that a few of the customers have left the establishment.\nHe glances at Ciera, that remains behind the counter, a slight frown on her small face. He'd take a step back, as the axe wielding mate charges at him, putting a foot infront of him, causing him to fall onto a table.\nThe lad holding the dagger approaches slowly, sending a slash for the elegant man's chest, as the man would send his cane onto the dagger wielder's temple. The dagger chap falls down to the ground, unconsious, as the elegant man turns.\nThe axe wielding chap charges, as he'd send a punch for the elegant man's nose. The elegant chap is punched, stumbling back, before sending the tip of his cane into the attacker's adam's apple, causing him repeated coughing, before he'd fall down, dead.\nThe elegant man turns to Tristan, before he'd glance at all the three defeated men, darting his eyes at Ciera, letting out a simple \"Apologies.\" as he'd grasp onto the collar of each one of them, throwing them out. Tristan grins happily at the elegant lad\nThe elegant man nods to Tristan, glancing down at the flute, frowning slightly, before he'd nod to Ciera and Tristan, heading out by himself, shutting the door, of course.\n-This novel is dedicated to the innkeeper herself, Ciera Lefevre. May she enjoy the reading of this fiction, and give me an honest opinion.\n\n-Aepholis Celadithel.\n*An image of a lurky, slender and tall blonde-haired elf, with orange eyes, covers this page, the inn being his surroundings.*", ""),
        StaticBook("TTOTWA: Book I", "SapphireShard", "The Tales of a Travelling Alchemist \n------------\nThe Fundamentals of Alchemy\n \nVolume I of V\nForeword\nI have heard many a man ask why one, such as myself, would take such interest in the art of alchemy when there are spells that could do just the same, if not better, a job than an apothecary’s potion.\na job than an apothecary’s potion.\nMy response to this statement is rather simple, whereas one must have a certain gift for spell-slinging, rune-craft, and spirit-speaking,\none mustn’t need these frivolous commodities to use a balm to mend a wound,\nshroud themselves in a nigh-invisible cloak with the swig from a bitter bottle, or set someone ablaze with bottled flame. Alchemy is not merely reproducing a wizard’s spell,\nbut it is shaping the spell in a way for the common man’s use. While magic still has its place in our world, Alchemy allows those without the gift to reap the same benefits as any mage might.\nChapter 1: The Fundamentals of Alchemy \n---------------\nThe Principle and Law of Equivalent Exchange:\nAs I was providing my services to a rather quaint hamlet, which also provided me information of the local flora and more of the same legends I always here of, I was approached by a man of common repute, and he produced a sarcastic, yet thought \nand he produced a sarcastic, yet thought provoking, question.\nThis question was, and I will attempt to reproduce the dialect as best as my phonetic ability will allow, “ ‘ow come, Mister, ‘hat ye’ can make all ‘dis ‘onderful stuff\nNow as shameful as his speech may be, the question remains the same. How is it, that alchemists are capable or reshaping the spells of sorcerers for the common folk out of, seemingly, nothing?\nThe answer to this question is simple to state, but fairly difficult to grasp, and this is the Principle of Equivalent Exchange. Much like the money one gives to the merchant for the apple or slab of meat, one cannot have something for nothing.\nAs the saying goes, “there is no such thing as a free brunch,” there is no such thing as a free potion. This fundamental rule applies to old alchemy, through the use of symbols and rituals, far more than what I call “Modern” alchemy.\nAlthough I will not get into ancient alchemy, I will state why this rule applies to Modern Alchemy. To craft a potion, for example a bottle of alchemist’s fire, one requires the materials. Much like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something \nMuch like a spell casted by a wizard, one cannot make something out of nothing.\nKnow that to make a potion, and the possibilities are truly endless, one must take plants, objects, and other representations of what they wish to\nother representations of what they wish to make.\nFor example, alchemist’s fire could be best produced with an -inked out-, a mixture of -inked out-, -inked out-, and a dash of -inked out-. Why would one need these items?\nThe -inked out- provides the basic mixture of what we will make, and of course the better quality of the -inked out- the better quality of the Alchemist’s Fire, but of course we will get further into this in a later chapter.\n-inked out-, a -inked out- that grows most often in deserts, is often -inked out- and often associated with -inked out-. This provides an superb symbol for the element of -inked out-. -inked out-, another powerful symbol of -inked out-,\nmixes well with the crushed -inked out- to produce something truly strong. The-inked out-, an optional portion, is another symbol of -inked out-, albeit both weaker than -inked out- or\n-inked out- for both a -inked out- and -inked out-’s fire burns far greater than -inked out-.\n\nNow then, although you have these symbols of flame, rage, and warfare, one mustn’t simply toss objects into the metaphorical cauldron. Precision is necessary, as always.\nOne must measure their mixture carefully, or they will give too much. And giving too much will tip the scales of Equivalent Exchange, causing unpredictable results. Why is this you might ask?\nWhen balance is achieved through Equivalent Exchange, a mixture of the elements for the bottled spell of your desire, than all is well.\nBut when you disrupt this balance and push on the world, taking and forcing in too much of one or more element then the world will push back. Of course a word of caution is required,\nfor one may think taking less is the best option when balance is not a certainty. This is a common misconception for not providing enough of one element will result in the same conclusion, imbalance and chaos.\nThis can be easily associated with cooking, too much of one ingredient will spoil the recipe, not enough of an ingredient will produce similar results. Of course it could also produce blander results,\nand in that case a potion may not be spoiled but it will simply be weak. This is preferable to exploding, of course.\nPreparation and You:\nNovice Alchemists often question me, what advice would you provide the budding alchemist, what sip of wisdom could you provide one who desires to tap your fountain of knowledge? .\nThe answer to this is rather simple, preparation is key to any potion. What is meant by this is that to mix a potion that you have not mixed before, or do not mix often, you must have all the facts and supplies before you begin.\nThis is best provided in a short list of required procedures if one wishes to keep their eyebrows, fingers, and life.\n1. Have all the Facts\n2. Know the Recipe\n3. Clean and check all your instruments are in working order\n4. Ensure you have plenty of whatever ingredients you are using\n5. Keep a Calm Dispostion\nI believe the only topic that requires explanation is “Have all the Facts” and by this I mean that one should not mix a potion they know nothing about.\nEven having the recipe is not enough to craft a potion safely, but knowledge of its origins, ingredients, and uses are crucial. Although budding alchemists often overlook this step, it may save your life one day.\n((OOC Footnote: Due to the constraints of MC books, the Elememts of Alchemy must be made into a seperate book. It is titled \"The Tale of the Travelling Alchemist: Elements of Alchemy\"))", ""),
        StaticBook("Tulatha's Diary", "Teerz", "Tulatha's Diary\n\nVolume 10\nDay 1\n\nI went hunting with Lan today, we ended up finding a huge cavern within was a book it was locked in a chest, I barely managed to avoid setting it ablaze, luckly Lan was there and saved the book from the fire.  I wonder whats inside.\nDay 2\n\nApparently we found a magic book!  I have lan out gathering the materials, we need a wolf, blood from myself and lan, as well as a bone for carving.  I plan on getting the girls together to help with this.  As we are a cult.\nDay 3\n\nTurns out we had to skin the wolf alive, the blood was astounding, however Lan quickly tanned it for me.  I wish the wolf had been dead for the ritual, but it wasn't allowed.  Now we must wait for the hide to finish tanning.\nDay 5\n\nUgh, there we go its finally done!  Lan's carving a symbol inside the corner of the hide.  *A symbol lies here*  We have to go through the book to see what we must do now.  It seems we need other symbols.  We have a choice of \nthese  *Several symbols sit here with descriptions*  I chose the one of lovers.  Lan agreed to it.  Julia and her lover are coming over to do their own ritual, they have their own wolf skin they made sure they did it when they where alive.\nDay 6\n\nAlright, we have everything ready, we must make the symbol, in our blood, we then need to write our own names in our own blood and I must channel mana into the middle symbol.  *A diagram is showed here*\nWish us luck.\nDay 10\n\nI was out for a few days, I can sense Lan, its amazing!  I can tell what he's feeling, he can sense me!\n\nIf I hurt myself he jumps slightly and glares at me once in a while, its funny.\nDay 11\n\nLan went out today, didn't tell me why.  My friend was silly and tried to go bind two Okars...thats what my Lan is, to herself, it failed, apparently you can only have one.\nDay 15 \n\nLan finally came back, he's wounded badly, his wound's don't seem to be healing...I don't understand.  I'm going to store you away for a while.  I'll use you as a note book dealing with Okars.\nDay 300\n\nIts been nearly a year since I bound Lan to me as my Okar, he's overly careful, he never goes out to cause trouble with me, his wound's don't heal proper.  He can barely taste, and feel.  But he can still hear, see, and smell better than\nI can, I don't know why fate has handed me these cards.  We got in a fight with some people, he kept them off of me, being faster as well and a skilled swordsman.  He kept me safe, however when he was hurt it hurt me just not as much, he got mad later \nafter the bodies lay fallen across the floor.\n\nHe saw how his pain had hurt me as well, and he left.  \n\nBack to the box you go.\nDay 421 \n\nToday, I woke up, dread filled me, I didn't know what was going on, then the pain hit me.  Its still there, I can't find Lan, it feels like my soul has been ripped out.\nDay 434\n\nIts been a while, I foud out what happened, Lan killed hims....it was my fault.  The monks won't return him.  Why, do they hate me?  He should be returned to me....hush little baby, everything will be ok.\n*The pages are filled with a madwomans ramblings.*\nDay 500,\n\nI write this, Okar's are a curse, don't meddle in what you think you know...this is my final Entry....goodbye.  I will go the way Lan went' and drink a posion.  Goodbye cruel world.\n\nSincerly \nTulatha", ""),
        StaticBook("Two Doors", "Dolan Velethen", "Two doors, both opaque in feature, stood in front of two staggly men. On the left stoff Veren, a human whose life was reached half. To his right stood Hijol, a wood elf younger than a fox out of season.\n\n\nIn between them was a sign that read \"Choose carfully\". Hijol inspected the sign and claimed that the sign was closer to the right door than the left one.\nAfter some long discussion the two decided that they must both choose the same door. They felt tired and rested.\nThe next marrow Hijol woke to fine two sleeping children. Both girls of unrecognizable race. He awoke one and quickly told her his tale. \"The right door is correct\" He notes that the sign has dissappeared. \"There was a sign that read 'There must be only\none right door'\".\n\nMeanwhile Veren awoke to find the other girl. Afyer he told her she must not trust Hijol, the two groups conjoined.\n\nFighting words came out. In one corner stood anger and spite.\nThe other stoff curriosity and fear. The winner, only the one brave enough to turn a knob,\n\nThey grew weary and slept another night. The next marrow Hijol's girl and Veren's girl awoke to find themselves alone and, adults.\nIn Hojol's girl's mind she had two facts, she must choose a door with Veren's girl, and it has to be  the right one. Veren's girl was to scared to speak to Hijol's girl.\n\nHijol's girl only had one option, in her mind, she must take out veren's girl to\ncontinue...\n\nHer fists hit her face. Her blood tained her tunic. Fist meet fist, and death was quick.\n\nHijol's girl turned the right knob slowly. On the other side was horrer. Both doors went to the same place. A small\nbedrock room with a sign that read \"Life isn't going to bend it'self to your perceptions.\"", ""),
        StaticBook("UA Vol. I", "Luthon Leyu'ilum", "Unearthed Arcana, Vol. I\n\nThe Void\n\nBy Luthon Leyu'ilum\n-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-\n\n\"The Void is nothing, yet everything. Like our imagination, it may be dull and empty; or it can be full and colorful.\"\n-Arcane Proverb\nChapter One: Existance\n\nThe Void is an empty plane of existance where things may be created with the use of ones imagination and willpower.\n\nThe Void, as a whole, is a plane where nothing exists unless a mage wills it into existance.\nFor one to connect to the void a basic line of communication must be established. This line of communication is generally gained through meditation and rigorous mental practice.\n\nDue to the difficult nature of sustaining this line of communication,\nnovice mages can have their connection severed even by minor distractions such as loud noises, sudden changes in temperature, stray thoughts, bright flashes of light, strong smells, etc.\n\nThey may otherwise sustain their connection until drained of mana,\nwhich we will move onto in volume two, causing the mage to suffer greatly and, in some cases, die.\n\nMasterful mages, however, are capable of holding their focus throughout various condition, usually only suffering a loss of connection when struck or shot.\nChapter Two: The Effects\n\nConnecting to the void, as mentioned in chapter one, is a long and arduous process. Channeling voidal energy is well known to have averse effects to the healh of the mage doing so.\n\nThese effects include, but are not entirely\nlimited to as follows.\n\nWeakness: The issue that affects every mage is that of physical weakness. This flaw manifests in both the musculature and immune system of the mage, eating away at muscle tissue and causing a higher rate of disease and infection.\nIt is for this reason that mages do not wear armor, a suit of heavy plate or leather adds additional physical exertion to the mental exertion of casting a spell, resulting in either the failure of the arcane spell, or the exaustion of the user to a point\nof unconciousness. This also loans somewhat to ones degredation of martial skill, using a blade or bow is difficult. To compound this with the weakness posed by being a mage causes the task of achieving a master level in martial skill to be impossible.\nIt is hypothesized that this phenomenon is due to the daemon Iblees, who resides within the void. For the mages life energy and power must go somewhere, and to absorb the inherent life and mana of the sentient races into himself is his goal.\nAnother hypothesis is that some energy is lost in the midst of the communication between the mage and The Void. This implies that some of the mana the mage uses to illusion, evoke, conjure, or alter is never returned to the material plane. A scary idea.\nThe third, and final, hypothesis presented in this volume is the idea that The Void is simply not meant to be interacted with naturally.\n\nThis idea implies that The Void's energy is poisonous or draining, and that its use simply taints the body.\nFrailty: This is an extremely common fault in mages. Their pain tolerance and fortitude is dramatically less than the average of their race and sub-race.\n\nBone is more easily broken in mages than it is in commoners or other combatants, and their blood\ndoes not clot as quickly, resulting in more blood loss per wound. This is why mages do not participate in combat, or leave the confines of their homes or respective towers often. A vicious cycle considering this usually prompts mages to excersize less, \nand thus become more weak than The Void would normally cause.\n\nThe theories for frailty remain the same as the theories for weakness.\nChapter Three: Connection\n\nTo connect to The Void is no easy task. As mentioned in chapter one and two. The process is that of complete control of ones own mind through the sheer power of will. \n\nTo re-iterate the proverb mentioned on the cover of\nthis\nvolume \"The Void is nothing, yet everything.\"\n\nIt is impossible to focus on everything at once, and thus mages have taken to focusing on nothing at all. This is how they envision the void and connect to it.\n\nFocusing on nothing is no easy task, however.\nThere is no way to teach connection to the void, only to guide students through the proper form of meditation.\n\nStudents seeking to connect to the void should find a quiet and isolated spot that is both comfortable and of average temperature.\nFrom there the student should sit, this makes it easier to focus, as ones legs will likely tire during meditation.\n\nThe student must then clear his or her mind completely of any thought or feeling. This is by far the most difficult part of the process.\nTo do so successfully, you must hone your will. How will is strengthened varies from individual to individual, but know this; it will not be an easy process.\n\nOnce connected, you will experience extreme pain. Many faint, and many die.\n\n-End Vol. I", ""),
        StaticBook("UA Vol. II", "Luthon Leyu'ilum", "Unearthed Arcana Vol. II\n\nMana\n\nBy Luthon Leyu'ilum\n-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-~-\n\n\"If spells were precious ore, buried deeply beneath the earth, mana would be the pickaxe with which they are brought to the surface.\"\n-Arcane Proverb\nChapter One: Mana\n\nWhen a mage is connected to The Void, he or she may create whatever they like within it. However, to draw a spell into the world or to apply it to someone or something, a mage must use mana.\nMana is as invisible as the air; it flows through our world like floating streams, and is a part of every living thing.\n\nMages, counting themselves among the living, have learned to use mana to draw from The Void.\n\nBy giving the energy of life, one can\nwork thaumaturgic wonders in the material plane; altering reality to suit their wishes.\n\nThough mana in this world is bountiful, you cannot manipulate the various lines of mana residing in the world or in creatures around you.\nOnes personal mana is,\nas mentioned in the proverb, much like a tool. The tool has its limits before it crumbles and becomes useless. The primary difference is that this tool is a part of you, an extension of yourself; and in breaking it you become fatigued.\n\nThe overuse of\nmana is a common theme among novices, who do not understand how to conserve or gauge their energy. In rare cases, even master mages push themselves beyond their normal capabilities, resulting in an exceptionally powerful spell followed by unconciousness\nor, in rare cases, death.\n\nEverything on the material plane naturally creates mana while growing or resting, including mages. Fostering ones health while learning or practicing magic will cause ones mana pool, covered in chapter two, to regenerate far \nmore quickly than one who does not. However, where does the expended mana go?\nThis leads us to two different prophecies, constantly at ends with eachother.\n\nThe Flowing Waters:\n\nThe flowing waters dictates that, in using mana, you expend it into the\nuniverse, thereby adding to the amount of magic housed within the material plane.\n\nThe Sacred Waters:\n\nThe sacred waters dictate that when you use mana it is consumed by The Void and never to return, thereby subtracting from the\ntotal amount of magic in the material plane.\n\nOne suggests that magic is infinite, while the other suggests it is finite. Mages tend to believe the former, using their magic in common tasks to add to the amount of magic and, thus, increase their\nabilities and the ability of those around them.\n\nThe latter is believed by Druids and Clerics for the most part, giving an interesting double meaning to the term \"Sacred Waters\". They see mages as a draining force, and themselves as agents of balance\nthrough the healing of nature and the spread of their diety's magic. Both parties believe\ntheir diety's power infinite, and thus a replenishing force to the drain imposed by mages.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nChapter Two: Mana Pools\n\nMana pools are how much mana one has in his or her person. This determines how grand ones spells can be, and the duration ones spells can be held in the material plane.\n\nMana pools are governed by two factors; the skill of the \nmage, and the type of magic that he or she is using.\n\nSkill is self explanatory; the longer a mage trains in magic, the more efficient he or she will be with his or her mana when using spells.\n\nThe second is less so. Different archtypes and subtypes of\nmagic have different demands on their users; with certain spells doing little but causing a twitch or blink, and others causing great tremors in the mage or creating great light within ones irises.\n\nThe commonly accepted list of magical scaling is as\nfollows, from lowest to highest amount of mana used. \n-Illusion, Light Illusion\n-Illusion, Cognitism\n-Illusion, Mind Magic\n-Evocation, Fire\n-Evocation, Water\n-Evocation, Air\n-Evocation, Earth\n-Evocation, Electrical\n-Conjuration, Perennial\n-Alteration, Tekelinesis\n-Evocation, Arcane Shielding -Conjuration, Morphon\n-Evocation, Arcane Evocation -Alteration, Translocation\n-Conjuration, Primordial\n-Alteration, Transfiguration\nInterestingly enough; a mage's mana pool can be unnaturally boosted by learning one of the more challenging subtypes first.\n\nA mage who began as a transfigurationist will have a greater supply of mana than a mage who began as an illusionist.\nOnly certain very exceptional individuals, however, have been capable of boasting the\narts of alteration, conjuration or arcane evocation as their first. Many who try fail, and many who fail die.\n\n\"A mystical energy that surges through the body of every\nliving thing just as blood runs through the four races' veins. At the hands of The Creator our world is filled with mana that ties everything to The Void and The Heavens from which he wove the beginnings of our universe. It exists in a constant ebb and\nflow and is perhaps the thing that makes every living thing so secretly beautiful. The blossoming of a flower amidst the bitter winds of winter are a testament to the magical qualities of mana, that even beyond the sun's rays and the nutrients absorbed\nfrom the ground there is energy within that frail creature to perform something of wonder.\"\n\n-Anonymous Mage", ""),
