        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "Hit         List\nQuavinir Signus\n-Goldensaint2", ""),
        StaticBook("Writable Book", "", "The Soldier's Siren\n\n-===-=========-===-A short story by \nMarvin Firemeadow.\n\nThe Sun's smile glared down upon the high walls and roofs of Petrus. The air was dry and gritty, and each wind washed a torrid, dry sensation over the city dwellers.\n\nA single guard was sited on the wall. He leaned over the parapet and stared\n\ndown into the green water of the moat. It seemed clearer, less murky than usual. The bottom was evident, vaguely, between shifting clouds of grit and grime.\n\nA lily pad idly floated across the moat where the guard watched. His eyes followed it,\n\none point of interest in the dreary moat. The flower had withered away, tinges of necrotic brown were all that was left of its lilac color.\n\nHe sighed slightly, rearing himself to stand and stretching his broad shoulders. Beads of sweat\n\nglistered on his forehead, for it was far too hot to bear a helm of any sort.\n\nHe turned around to look at the overshadowing citadel of the palace. The sun blazed just behind a stone tower, crowning the palace in a circlet of\n\nof light. The guard covered his eyes and twirled back to the moat.\n\nHe wiped his brow with a gloved hand, and blinked the blindness out of his eyes. The moat slithered to the east, giving a sonorous murmur as the water's belly chafed the\n\nearth.\n\nFrom the alcove of the gatehouse sprung something, someone, twirling and rocking on a boxy canoe. She bore impunity to the filthy water, wearing a miraculously white sundress. The pygmy boat fought valiantly to keep her upright.\n\nShe raised her sun-hat as she drifted across the water, raising a sweatless brow at the soldier who stared at her. \n\nShe traipsed her fishing rod across the moat water and brought it back into her boat. She raised a hand to wave at the\n\nguardsman.\n\nHe tilted his head, unsure what to make of her gesture, but nevertheless gave her a wave in return. She hooded herself with the sun-hat and set herself back to fishing.\n\nThe soldier watched her fish for a long\n\nwhile, transfixed by her artful sailing. The moat was more or less barren of fish. It was an artificed body of water, but the soldier had no heart to tell her this.", ""),
